const e="72 chapters",t=[{id:"chapter-46",number:46,title:"N이었어요 / N였어요 · N인데 / N인데도",goal:"Turn role nouns into past identity: 학생이었어요, 회사원이었어요.",biteCount:10,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 학생~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"학생",romanization:"haksaeng",en:"student",pos:"noun",nuance:"학생 is a common identity word. Use 이에요 after 학생 because it ends in a consonant."},sentence:{ko:"작년에는 학생이었어요.",en:"I was a student last year."},target:"학생",options:["student","office worker","employee; staff member"],note:"Core role noun for N이었어요/였어요, N이라서, and N이니까."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"회사원",romanization:"hoesawon",en:"office worker",pos:"noun",nuance:"회사원 is 會社員 ('company-member'), literally a member of a company, and it is the neutral catch-all a salaried employee of a private firm gives when asked 무슨 일 하세요? Contrast it with 직장인, which means 'a working person' in general (anyone with a paid job, including teachers or nurses): every 회사원 is a 직장인, but not every 직장인 is a 회사원. Also separate it from 사업가, the owner who runs the business — a 회사원 is employed by someone else. The pitfall for English speakers is twofold: 회사원 ends in a consonant so the copula is 회사원이에요, never 'office worker'-style 회사원예요; and don't translate 'I work at a company' as a job title — to name the job you simply say 회사원이에요 rather than literally rendering the English sentence."},sentence:{ko:"지금은 회사원인데도 계속 공부해요.",en:"Even though I am an office worker now, I keep studying."},target:"회사원",options:["student","office worker","employee; staff member"],note:"Better fit than a random job word because it explains a busy adult schedule."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"직원",romanization:"jigwon",en:"employee; staff member",pos:"noun",nuance:"직원 (職員, 'duty-personnel') is the plain, everyday word for anyone who works at a company, shop, bank, or office — the person on the other side of the counter helping you. The synonym contrast to nail: 직원 is generic 'staff' regardless of rank, while 사원 (社員) specifically names the entry-level rung on the corporate ladder, and 종업원 sounds older/more clerical (waitstaff, hired help). In daily life 직원 is overwhelmingly the natural choice — 직원한테 물어보세요 (ask a staff member) is a sentence you'll use weekly. Register is neutral and safe for everyone. The English-speaker pitfall is twofold: first, don't call the customer a 직원 — the 직원 is the one who WORKS there, not the visitor (that's 손님); second, English says 'I asked the staff' with no particle, but Korean must mark the person with 한테/에게: 직원에게 물어봤어요."},sentence:{ko:"저는 직원인데요, 담당자는 아니에요.",en:"I am an employee, but I am not the person in charge."},target:"직원",options:["employee; staff member","student","office worker"],note:"Useful for soft background with 인데요."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"작년",romanization:"jangnyeon",en:"last year",pos:"noun",nuance:"작년 means last year. Both 작년에 만났어요 and the particle-less 작년 만났어요 can occur; 에 is useful when clearly marking the time point, but it is not obligatory in every sentence."},sentence:{ko:"작년에는 학생이었어요.",en:"I was a student last year."},target:"작년",options:["student","office worker","last year"],note:"Time anchor for past identity."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"올해",romanization:"olhae",en:"this year",pos:"noun",nuance:"올해 is the native Korean word for the present calendar year and slots straight in front of a verb, normally WITHOUT 에: 올해 졸업해요 (I graduate this year), not 올해에. It anchors the three-year set 작년 (last year) – 올해 (this year) – 내년 (next year). There is a Sino-Korean synonym 금년 (今年, 'present year'), but it is stiff and limited to formal writing or speeches; in conversation 올해 is the only natural choice. Two English-speaker pitfalls stand out. First, the 에 habit: because English says 'in this year' learners over-add 에, but native usage drops it after 올해. Second, the look-alike trap with 오늘 ('today') — both start with 오, yet 올해 is a whole year and 오늘 is a single day, so 'I'm twenty this year' is 올해 스무 살이에요, never 오늘."},sentence:{ko:"올해는 회사원이에요.",en:"This year I am an office worker."},target:"올해",options:["student","office worker","this year"],note:"Pairs naturally with 작년 when comparing past and present identity."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"주말",romanization:"jumal",en:"weekend",pos:"noun",nuance:"주말 is Sino-Korean, 週末 — 週 'week' + 末 'end', literally 'week-end', and its opposites are 평일 平日 'weekday' and 주중 週中 'midweek'. It refers to Saturday and Sunday as a block. The contrast worth noting is 주말 vs 휴일: 주말 is specifically the weekend, while 휴일 休日 is any day off including public holidays — so a Wednesday holiday is a 휴일 but not a 주말. Habitual partners: 주말에 쉬다/놀다, 이번/지난/다음 주말, 주말마다, 즐거운 주말, and the farewell 주말 잘 보내세요. The English-speaker pitfall is the particle: learners borrow 'at/on' loosely and write 주말에서, but 에서 marks a LOCATION or a source ('from'); the time-when always takes 에 → 주말에. A second subtlety: for 'this weekend' use 이번 주말 (이번 = the current instance), not 오늘 주말; and 다음 주말 'next weekend' vs 다음 주 주말 can differ, so keep 다음 주말 for the upcoming Sat–Sun."},sentence:{ko:"주말이니까 한 시간이라도 공부해요.",en:"Since it is the weekend, let's study even if just for one hour."},target:"주말",options:["weekend","student","office worker"],note:"Time noun that naturally supports 니까 and 라도 practice."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"작년에는 학생이었어요?",en:"Were you a student last year?"},hl:"학생"}],canDo:"Say what something was in the past (학생이었어요 · 주말이었어요)",id:"chapter-46-b1",chapterId:"chapter-46",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N이었어요 / N였어요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N이었어요 / N였어요",sub:'"It was an N" (past of 이다)',lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"어제는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"제",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"생일",mid:"이었어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"어제는 제 생일이었어요.",en:"Yesterday was my birthday.",romanization:"eojeneun je saengirieosseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"그",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"사람은",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"제",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"친구",mid:"였어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"그 사람은 제 친구였어요.",en:"That person was my friend.",romanization:"geu sarameun je chinguyeosseoyo."}],rule:{name:"N이었어요 / N였어요",rows:[{when:"Noun ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"N + 이었어요",ex:"학생 → 학생이었어요 (was a student)"},{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"N + 였어요 (shortened from 이었어요)",ex:"친구 → 친구였어요 (was a friend)"},{when:"Casual 반말 past",add:"N이었어 / N였어",ex:"주말이었어 / 친구였어 (was the weekend / was a friend)"},{when:"Negative past (was not N)",add:"N이/가 아니었어요",ex:"학생이 아니었어요 (wasn't a student)"}]},more:{func:'Says what something was in the past — an identity, role, or category that was true before now: "It was my birthday," "She was a student," "Yesterday was the weekend." It is just 이다 put into the past polite form.',funcLead:'Says what something was in the past — an identity, role, or category that was true before now: "It was my birthday," "She was a student," "Yesterday was the we…',keyPoint:{label:"이었어요 vs 였어요 — just a sound rule",body:"Both are the same past form of 이다. After a consonant, keep the full 이었어요 (학생이었어요). After a vowel, 이었어요 shrinks to 였어요 (친구였어요). The meaning is identical — only the spelling and sound change, based on the noun's final letter."},pronunciation:"학생이었어요 links across the boundary and sounds like [학쌩이어써요]; 친구였어요 sounds like [친구여써요].",pitfall:{wrong:"학생였어요",right:"학생이었어요",explanation:"Learners grab 였어요 because it is shorter, but 였어요 only attaches to vowel-final nouns. 학생 ends in a consonant, so it needs the full 이었어요. Choose the ending by the noun's last letter, not by what is easier to say."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Last year I was a student too.",sentence:"작년에는 저도 학생___.",options:[{text:"이었어요",ok:!0},{text:"였어요",ok:!1}],explanation:"학생 ends in a consonant → 이었어요; 작년 (last year) sets the past time."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Yesterday was my birthday."',tokens:["어제는","제","생일이었어요."],correct:"어제는 제 생일이었어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "That person was my friend."',tokens:["그","사람은","제","친구였어요."],correct:"그 사람은 제 친구였어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"학생이었어요",ok:!0},{text:"학생였어요",ok:!1}],explanation:"Learners grab 였어요 because it is shorter, but 였어요 only attaches to vowel-final nouns. 학생 ends in a consonant, so it needs the full 이었어요. Choose the ending by the noun's last letter, not by what is easier to say."}],canDo:"Say what something was in the past (학생이었어요 · 주말이었어요)",id:"chapter-46-b2",chapterId:"chapter-46",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 평일~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"평일",romanization:"pyeongil",en:"weekday",pos:"noun",nuance:"평일 is Sino-Korean 平日 (平 'flat/ordinary' + 日 'day'), literally an 'ordinary day' — Monday through Friday, the regular working days, set against 주말 (weekend) and 휴일 (holiday/day off). The dividing line vs 주중 (週中, 'inside the week'): 평일 simply names the ordinary days as a category, while 주중 frames those same days as the span 'during the week' and pairs naturally with 주말 as a matched contrast (주중에는 바쁘고 주말에는 쉬어요). 평일 is neutral and used by everyone in any register, from a weather report to a friend's text. It habitually appears as 평일 + time-of-day (평일 아침/낮/저녁) and with 에/에는. The English-speaker pitfall: 'weekday' in English can mean 'any single working day' OR the whole work portion of the week, but in Korean 평일 leans toward the category of ordinary days, so for a duration like 'during the week I'm busy' Koreans more often say 주중에는 바빠요; also don't write the near-homophone 휴일 (rest day) by mistake."},sentence:{ko:"평일이라서 오래 못 해요.",en:"Because it is a weekday, I cannot do it for long."},target:"평일",options:["weekday","student","office worker"],note:"Useful contrast with 주말 for schedule-based reasons."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"아침",romanization:"achim",en:"morning; breakfast",pos:"noun",nuance:"아침 is a native Korean word that does double duty: the early part of the day (roughly sunrise to mid-morning) and the breakfast meal — the same way English 'morning' differs from the meal but Korean merges the breakfast sense into the time word. The grammar disambiguates: with 에 (아침에) it's the time, with 을 (아침을 먹다) it's the food. Contrast 아침 vs 오전: 아침 is the felt, lived early morning, while 오전 午前 is the neutral clock-AM you put on timetables — so 아침에 일어나요 (waking life) but 오전 9시 회의 (a scheduled item). Habitual partners: 아침에 일어나다/운동하다, 아침을 먹다/거르다 ('skip breakfast'), 아침 일찍, 매일 아침. The big English-speaker pitfall is using 오전 for the breakfast meal — 오전 is only the clock period and can never name food, so 'eat breakfast' must be 아침을 먹다, never 오전을 먹다. Also don't force 에 onto the meal sense: you eat 아침을, you don't 'eat at morning'."},sentence:{ko:"아침이라도 괜찮아요?",en:"Would even morning be okay?"},target:"아침",options:["student","office worker","morning; breakfast"],note:"Works as a fallback time when the better time is unavailable."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"시간",romanization:"sigan",en:"time; hour",pos:"noun",nuance:"시간 works with 있어요/없어요 and is essential for scheduling."},sentence:{ko:"한 시간이라도 공부해요.",en:"Let's study even if it is just one hour."},target:"시간",options:["time; hour","student","office worker"],note:"A high-value noun for 라도 because learners often negotiate minimum time."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"질문",romanization:"jilmun",en:"question",pos:"noun",nuance:"질문 is Sino-Korean 質問 — 質 'substance/inquire into' + 問 'ask', literally 'to inquire and ask', i.e. a question put to someone. The set verb is 질문을 하다 (or the single verb 질문하다); the person you ask takes 에게/한테 (or honorific 께 for a teacher): 선생님께 질문을 하다. Its natural partner is 대답/답(答) — a 질문 is answered by a 대답. The big redundancy trap for English speakers is 질문을 물어보다 ✗: 물어보다 already means 'to ask', so this literally says 'ask a question-asking' — say either 질문을 하다 or just 물어보다, never both. Distinguish 질문 (a question seeking information/an answer) from 문제(問題, a 'problem' or exam item) — '3 questions on the test' are 문제 세 개, but 'I have a question for you' is 질문이 있어요. Note answering takes 에: 질문에 답하다, not 질문을 답하다."},sentence:{ko:"질문이라도 하나 해 보세요.",en:"Try asking at least one question."},target:"질문",options:["student","question","office worker"],note:"A small action that fits the minimum-option meaning of 라도."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"할인",romanization:"harin",en:"discount",pos:"noun",nuance:"할인(割引) is Sino-Korean — 割 'cut' + 引 'pull (down)' — literally 'cut and pull down', i.e. knock money off a price. The key contrast for learners is 할인 vs 세일: 할인 is the abstract act/amount of reducing a price on an item ('10% off', a discount), while 세일 (from English 'sale') names a store-wide selling event (백화점 세일 a department-store sale). So a 세일 contains many 할인s. Watch the voice carefully: from the price's point of view you say 할인이 되다/할인되다 ('it gets discounted'); from your point of view as buyer you say 할인을 받다 ('I receive a discount'); and to actively ask for one you say 할인해 주세요 ('please discount it for me'). The English-speaker pitfall is firing 할인해요 at a clerk hoping it means 'give me a discount' — bare 할인해요 just states 'someone discounts', so it lands as a flat description, not a request. Add 주세요/돼요 to make it work. Common partners: 할인되다, 할인받다, 할인 행사 (discount event), 할인 쿠폰."},sentence:{ko:"학생이라서 할인을 받을 수 있어요.",en:"Because I am a student, I can get a discount."},target:"할인",options:["student","discount","office worker"],note:"Shows why 학생 is not just an identity word but a real reason in daily life."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네. 평일에는 바쁘니까 아침에만 조금 해요.",en:"Yes. Since weekdays are busy, I only do a little in the morning."},hl:"평일"}],canDo:"Set the scene or mark mild contrast on a noun (회사원인데 · 주말인데도)",id:"chapter-46-b3",chapterId:"chapter-46",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N인데 / N인데도",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N인데 / N인데도",sub:`"it's N, and… / even though it's N"`,lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"오늘은",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"주말",mid:"인데",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"어디",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"갈까요?",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"오늘은 주말인데 어디 갈까요?",en:"It's the weekend today, so where shall we go?",romanization:"oneureun jumarinde eodi galkkayo?"},{tokens:[{pre:"주말",mid:"인데도",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"회사에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"주말인데도 회사에 가요.",en:"Even though it's the weekend, I go to work.",romanization:"jumarindedo hoesae gayo."}],rule:{name:"N인데 / N인데도",rows:[{when:"Noun (any ending) + background / lead-in",add:"N + 인데",ex:"학생인데… (I'm a student, so/and…)"},{when:`Noun + "even though it's N"`,add:"N + 인데도",ex:"주말인데도 (even though it's the weekend)"},{when:"Past noun background",add:"N + 이었는데 / 였는데",ex:"친구였는데 (we were friends, but…)"},{when:`Polite sentence-ender (soft "it's N, but…")`,add:"N + 인데요",ex:"여기 제 자리인데요. (Um, this is my seat…)"}]},more:{func:`Attaches to a noun to set the scene before the main clause: "It's the weekend, so…/and…" (인데), or to mark a mild contrast "even though it's N" (인데도). 인데 softens a statement and leads into the rest; 인데도 adds "despite that."`,funcLead:`Attaches to a noun to set the scene before the main clause: "It's the weekend, so…/and…" (인데), or to mark a mild contrast "even though it's N" (인데도).`,keyPoint:{label:"인데 vs 인데도",body:`인데 is neutral: it just hands you a noun-fact as a lead-in, and context decides whether it means "so" or "but." 인데도 is clearly concessive — it always means "even though it's N," and the next clause is something surprising. Add 도 only when you want the firm "despite" feeling.`},pronunciation:"인데 sounds like [인데] with no change; 인데도 sounds like [인데도], the 도 stays plain.",pitfall:{wrong:"학생는데",right:"학생인데",explanation:"인데 comes from 이다, so a noun needs the 이 (학생 + 이 + ㄴ데 → 학생인데). Learners drop the 인 and stick 는데 straight onto the noun as if it were a verb. 는데 is for verbs and adjectives; 인데 is the noun version."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"It's my first time, so please be kind to me.",sentence:"저는 처음___ 잘 부탁해요.",options:[{text:"이었어요",ok:!1},{text:"였어요",ok:!1},{text:"인데",ok:!0}],explanation:"처음 (first time) + 인데 sets up the polite request."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"We're in the same class, but I don't know their name.",sentence:"같은 반___ 이름을 몰라요.",options:[{text:"이었어요",ok:!1},{text:"였어요",ok:!1},{text:"인데",ok:!0}],explanation:`인데 here leans contrastive — "it's the case that…, yet…"`},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "It's the weekend today, so where shall we go?"`,tokens:["오늘은","주말인데","어디","갈까요?"],correct:"오늘은 주말인데 어디 갈까요?",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Even though it's the weekend, I go to work." (주말인데도 시작 · Start with 주말인데도.)`,tokens:["주말인데도","회사에","가요."],correct:"주말인데도 회사에 가요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Set the scene or mark mild contrast on a noun (회사원인데 · 주말인데도)",id:"chapter-46-b4",chapterId:"chapter-46",index:3},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N이라서 / 라서",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N이라서 / 라서",sub:'"because it is N"',lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"오늘은",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"주말",mid:"이라서",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"가게가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"쉬어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"오늘은 주말이라서 가게가 쉬어요.",en:"Because it's the weekend today, the shop is closed.",romanization:"oneureun jumariraseo gagega swieoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"저는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"초보",mid:"라서",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"천천히",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"할게요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"저는 초보라서 천천히 할게요.",en:"Because I'm a beginner, I'll go slowly.",romanization:"jeoneun chobolaseo cheoncheonhi halgeyo."}],rule:{name:"N이라서 / 라서",rows:[{when:"Noun ends in a consonant",add:"N + 이라서",ex:"학생이라서 (because I'm a student)"},{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"N + 라서",ex:"친구라서 (because we're friends)"},{when:"More formal / written-sounding reason",add:"N + 이어서 / 여서",ex:"주말이어서 (because it's the weekend)"},{when:"Reason for a noun negative",add:"N이/가 아니라서",ex:"가족이 아니라서 (because I'm not family)"}]},more:{func:`Gives a reason that is a noun fact: "Because it's the weekend, the shop is closed," "Because I'm a beginner, it's hard." It is the 이다 version of the reason ending -아서/어서.`,funcLead:`Gives a reason that is a noun fact: "Because it's the weekend, the shop is closed," "Because I'm a beginner, it's hard." It is the 이다 version of the reason end…`,keyPoint:{label:"이라서 vs verb -아서/어서",body:`For verbs and adjectives you say -아서/어서 (먹어서, 좋아서). For nouns you must run the reason through 이다 → 이라서/라서. So "because it's a student" is 학생이라서, never 학생어서. Reach for the noun form whenever the reason is a noun.`},pronunciation:"학생이라서 sounds like [학쌩이라서]. The [쌩] is just how 학생 is always said — the ㄱ받침 of 학 tenses the 생; 이라서 itself adds no new tensing.",pitfall:{wrong:"주말이어서 가게가 쉬어요 (said casually)",right:"주말이라서 가게가 쉬어요",explanation:"이어서/여서 is correct grammar but sounds formal or bookish in speech. In everyday conversation Koreans overwhelmingly say 이라서/라서. Both are right; pick 이라서/라서 to sound natural when you're talking."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Because it's a weekday, there's nobody around.",sentence:"평일___ 사람이 없어요.",options:[{text:"이라서",ok:!0},{text:"라서",ok:!1}],explanation:"평일 (consonant-final) + 이라서 gives the reason."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Because I'm not family, I can't go in.",sentence:"가족이 아니___ 못 들어가요.",options:[{text:"라서",ok:!0},{text:"이었어요",ok:!1},{text:"였어요",ok:!1}],explanation:"Noun negative reason: 아니다 → 아니라서."},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Because it's the weekend today, the shop is closed."`,tokens:["오늘은","주말이라서","가게가","쉬어요."],correct:"오늘은 주말이라서 가게가 쉬어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Because I'm a beginner, I'll go slowly."`,tokens:["저는","초보라서","천천히","할게요."],correct:"저는 초보라서 천천히 할게요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Give a noun-based reason (주말이라서 · 주말이니까)",id:"chapter-46-b5",chapterId:"chapter-46",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N이니까 / 니까",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N이니까 / 니까",sub:'"since it is N, so…"',lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"오늘은",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"주말",mid:"이니까",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"같이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"공부해요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"오늘은 주말이니까 같이 공부해요.",en:"Since it's the weekend today, let's study together.",romanization:"oneureun jumarinikka gachi gongbuhaeyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"우리는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"친구",mid:"니까",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"괜찮아요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"우리는 친구니까 괜찮아요.",en:"Since we're friends, it's okay.",romanization:"urineun chingunikka gwaenchanayo."}],rule:{name:"N이니까 / 니까",rows:[{when:"Noun ends in a consonant",add:"N + 이니까",ex:"주말이니까 (since it's the weekend)"},{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"N + 니까",ex:"친구니까 (since we're friends)"},{when:"Leading a suggestion / command",add:"N이니까 + ~해요/하세요",ex:"주말이니까 같이 공부해요 (it's the weekend, so let's study together)"},{when:"Noun negative reason",add:"N이/가 아니니까",ex:"애기가 아니니까 (since you're not a baby)"}]},more:{func:`Gives a reason with a "since you know this, therefore let's…" feel — strong for backing up a command, suggestion, or decision: "Since it's the weekend, let's study," "Since it's my birthday, I'll treat you." It is the 이다 version of -(으)니까.`,funcLead:`Gives a reason with a "since you know this, therefore let's…" feel — strong for backing up a command, suggestion, or decision: "Since it's the weekend, let's s…`,keyPoint:{label:"이니까 vs 이라서",body:`Both mean "because it's N," but 이니까 is the one you use before commands and suggestions (조용히 하세요, 같이 공부해요) and when the reason is shared or obvious. 이라서 is more about plain cause and sounds off before a command. Rule of thumb: order or invite → 이니까; just explain → 이라서.`},pronunciation:"주말이니까 sounds like [주마리니까]; 까 is written with ㄲ, so it is always tense.",pitfall:{wrong:"주말이라서 같이 공부해요 (before a let's-suggestion)",right:"주말이니까 같이 공부해요",explanation:`Before a suggestion (같이 공부해요) or command (하세요), Koreans use 이니까, not 이라서. 이라서 is for plainly stating a cause; it sounds unnatural right before "let's…" or an order. When your sentence ends in a suggestion or command, switch the noun reason to 이니까/니까.`}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Since it's my birthday today, I'll treat you.",sentence:"오늘은 제 생일___ 제가 살게요.",options:[{text:"이니까",ok:!0},{text:"니까",ok:!1}],explanation:"이니까 supports the speaker's decision (제가 살게요)."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Since it's a weekday, please go to bed early.",sentence:"평일___ 일찍 자세요.",options:[{text:"이었어요",ok:!1},{text:"였어요",ok:!1},{text:"이니까",ok:!0}],explanation:"이니까 + command 자세요 — a natural pairing that 이라서 avoids."},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Since it's the weekend today, let's study together." (오늘은 시작 · Start with 오늘은.)`,tokens:["오늘은","주말이니까","같이","공부해요."],correct:"오늘은 주말이니까 같이 공부해요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Since we're friends, it's okay."`,tokens:["우리는","친구니까","괜찮아요."],correct:"우리는 친구니까 괜찮아요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Offer a fallback option with N이라도/라도 (커피라도 마셔요)",id:"chapter-46-b6",chapterId:"chapter-46",index:5},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N이라도 / 라도",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N이라도 / 라도",sub:'"even just an N / at least N"',lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"주말이니까",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"한",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"시간",mid:"이라도",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"같이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"공부해요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"주말이니까 한 시간이라도 같이 공부해요.",en:"Since it's the weekend, let's study together even if just for one hour.",romanization:"jumarinikka han siganirado gachi gongbuhaeyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"바쁘면",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"커피",mid:"라도",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"한잔해요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"바쁘면 커피라도 한잔해요.",en:"If you're busy, let's at least grab a coffee.",romanization:"bappeumyeon keopirado hanjanhaeyo."}],rule:{name:"N이라도 / 라도",rows:[{when:"Noun ends in a consonant",add:"N + 이라도",ex:"한 시간이라도 (even just one hour)"},{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"N + 라도",ex:"커피라도 (coffee, at least)"},{when:"Offering a fallback choice",add:"N이라도 + 할까요? / 드세요",ex:"물이라도 드세요 (at least have some water)"},{when:'"even on/at N" (time noun)',add:"시간 noun + 이라도/라도",ex:"주말이라도 (even on the weekend, at least)"}]},more:{func:`Marks a noun as a not-ideal-but-acceptable fallback: "Let's study even just one hour," "Have at least some water." It says "this isn't the best option, but it'll do." It is the 이다 form behind the 라도 fallback marker.`,funcLead:`Marks a noun as a not-ideal-but-acceptable fallback: "Let's study even just one hour," "Have at least some water." It says "this isn't the best option, but it'…`,keyPoint:{label:'라도 = "not the best, but okay"',body:"이라도/라도 picks a noun that isn't your first choice but is good enough: 한 시간이라도 (even just one hour), 커피라도 (at least a coffee). Use 이라도 after a consonant and 라도 after a vowel — the same sound split you see in 이라서/라서 and 이니까/니까."},pronunciation:"한 시간이라도 sounds like [한 시가니라도]; 커피라도 sounds like [커피라도], no special change.",pitfall:{wrong:"한 시간라도",right:"한 시간이라도",explanation:"시간 ends in a consonant (ㄴ), so it needs 이라도, not 라도. Learners default to the shorter 라도 everywhere, but 라도 only goes on vowel-final nouns (커피라도). Check the noun's last letter first, just like with 이라서 and 이니까."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"If you're hungry, at least have some bread.",sentence:"배고프면 빵___ 드세요.",options:[{text:"이라도",ok:!0},{text:"라도",ok:!1}],explanation:"빵 (consonant-final) + 이라도 offers a fallback food."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"If you have no time, do a little even just in the morning.",sentence:"시간 없으면 아침에___ 조금 해요.",options:[{text:"라도",ok:!0},{text:"이었어요",ok:!1},{text:"였어요",ok:!1}],explanation:'아침에 + 라도 — "even at/in the morning" as a fallback time.'},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Since it's the weekend, let's study together even if just for one hour." (주말이니까 시작 · Start with 주말이니까.)`,tokens:["주말이니까","한","시간이라도","같이","공부해요."],correct:"주말이니까 한 시간이라도 같이 공부해요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "If you're busy, let's at least grab a coffee."`,tokens:["바쁘면","커피라도","한잔해요."],correct:"바쁘면 커피라도 한잔해요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Say what something was in the past (학생이었어요 · 주말이었어요)",id:"chapter-46-b7",chapterId:"chapter-46",index:6},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Two learners are scheduling study time while explaining role and time with 이다 forms.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"작년에는 학생이었어요?",en:"Were you a student last year?",romanization:"jangnyeoneneun haksaengieosseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네, 지금은 회사원인데도 계속 공부해요.",en:"Yes, even though I am an office worker now, I keep studying.",romanization:"ne, jigeumeun hoesawonindedo gyesok gongbuhaeyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"회사원이라서 한국어가 더 필요해요?",en:"Because you are an office worker, do you need Korean more?",romanization:"hoesawoniraseo hangugeoga deo piryohaeyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네. 평일에는 바쁘니까 아침에만 조금 해요.",en:"Yes. Since weekdays are busy, I only do a little in the morning.",romanization:"ne. pyeongireneun bappeunikka achimenman jogeum haeyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"그럼 주말이니까 한 시간이라도 같이 공부해요.",en:"Then since it is the weekend, let's study together even if just for one hour.",romanization:"geureom jumarinikka han siganirado gachi gongbuhaeyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'Since it is the weekend, let's meet in the morning.'",tokens:["주말이니까","아침에 만나요"],correct:"주말이니까 아침에 만나요",explanation:"-니까 gives a reason that supports the next decision. 주말이니까 sounds like “since it is the weekend.”"},{kind:"drill",prompt:"누가 한 말일까요? · Who said this?",sentence:"그럼 주말이니까 한 시간이라도 같이 공부해요.",options:[{text:"민서",ok:!0},{text:"지훈",ok:!1}],explanation:"Then since it is the weekend, let's study together even if just for one hour."},{kind:"drill",prompt:'무엇이 들어갈까요? · "Were you a student last year?"',sentence:"작년에는 ___이었어요?",options:[{text:"회사원",ok:!1},{text:"학생",ok:!0},{text:"직원",ok:!1}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Say what something was in the past (학생이었어요 · 주말이었어요)",id:"chapter-46-b8",chapterId:"chapter-46",index:7},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 회사원인데도 계속 공부해요",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"회사원인데도 계속 공부해요",chunks:["지훈은 작년에는 학생이었어요. 올해는 회사원인데도 한국어를 계속 공부해요. 회사원이라서 회의와 이메일에서 한국어가 더 필요해요.","평일은 바쁘니까 아침에만 조금 공부하고, 주말이니까 한 시간이라도 친구와 같이 공부해요. 시간이 많지 않아도 역할 명사와 시간 명사를 잘 고르면 이다 표현을 자연스럽게 쓸 수 있어요."],translation:"Jihun was a student last year. This year, even though he is an office worker, he keeps studying Korean. Because he is an office worker, he needs Korean more in meetings and email. Weekdays are busy, so he studies only a little in the morning, and since it is the weekend, he studies with a friend even if just for one hour. Even without much time, choosing role nouns and time nouns well makes the 이다 forms feel natural.",qas:[{q:"지훈은 작년에 무엇이었어요?",a:"학생이었어요."},{q:"지훈은 왜 한국어가 더 필요해요?",a:"회사원이라서 회의와 이메일에서 한국어가 더 필요해요."},{q:"주말에는 얼마나 공부해요?",a:"한 시간이라도 친구와 같이 공부해요."}]},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"지훈은 작년에는 ___이었어요.",options:[{text:"학생",ok:!0},{text:"직원",ok:!1},{text:"질문",ok:!1}],explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"올해는 ___인데도 한국어를 계속 공부해요.",options:[{text:"회사원",ok:!0},{text:"직원",ok:!1},{text:"질문",ok:!1}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Set the scene or mark mild contrast on a noun (회사원인데 · 주말인데도)",id:"chapter-46-b9",chapterId:"chapter-46",index:8},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence correctly says 'I was a student last year'?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"작년에는 학생이었어요.",ok:!0},{text:"작년에는 학생했어요.",ok:!1},{text:"작년에는 학생였어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"학생 ends in a consonant, so the past copula is 학생이었어요."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Choose the soft explanation: 'Because I am an office worker, I need Korean.'",sentence:null,options:[{text:"회사원이라서 한국어가 필요해요.",ok:!0},{text:"회사원이라도 한국어가 필요해요.",ok:!1},{text:"회사원인데도 한국어가 필요해요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"이라서 gives a cause based on what someone is. 라도 means fallback, and 인데도 means contrast."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence gives a reason that leads straight into a study plan?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"주말이니까 한 시간이라도 공부해요.",ok:!0},{text:"주말이라도 한 시간이에요.",ok:!1},{text:"주말이었어요 한 시간 공부해요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"주말이니까 gives the reason, and 한 시간이라도 gives the minimum study amount."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence marks a minimum acceptable option?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"한 시간이라도 공부해요.",ok:!0},{text:"한 시간만 공부해요.",ok:!1},{text:"한 시간이라서 공부해요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"이라도 means 'even if only / at least'. 만 means only, and 이라서 gives a cause."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"작년___ 학생이었어요. (Last year, I was a student.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"에",ok:!0},{text:"에서",ok:!1},{text:"를",ok:!1}],explanation:"작년에 marks the time when the identity was true. 에서 would mark an action place, not a year."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence gives a soft noun background?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"저는 회사원인데 한국어가 필요해요.",ok:!0},{text:"저는 회사원이라도 한국어가 필요해요.",ok:!1},{text:"저는 회사원뿐 한국어가 필요해요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"N인데 gives background before the main point. 이라도 and 뿐 do not fit this explanation."}],canDo:"Give a noun-based reason (주말이라서 · 주말이니까)",id:"chapter-46-b10",chapterId:"chapter-46",index:9}]},{id:"chapter-47",number:47,title:"저희 가족은 ~ 명이에요 · 형 / 오빠 / 누나 / 언니",goal:"Describe family size with 저희 가족은 ~ 명이에요.",biteCount:10,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 저희~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"저희",romanization:"jeohui",en:"our / my (humble, polite)",pos:"pronoun",nuance:"저희 is not colder than 우리; it is socially careful. Use it when the listener is outside your group and the situation is polite. Use 우리 with close friends, family, and many natural everyday phrases like 우리 엄마."},sentence:{ko:"저희 가족은 네 명이에요.",en:"There are four people in my family."},target:"저희",options:["our / my (humble, polite)","family","counter for people"],note:"Core word for polite family introductions."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"가족",romanization:"gajok",en:"family",pos:"noun",nuance:"가족 is the Sino-Korean word 家族 ('house' 家 + 'clan/group' 族), so it names the whole family unit as a collective, never a single relative — for one member you must say 가족 한 명 or name the relationship (형, 누나). The near-synonym 식구 also means family but literally means 食口 'mouths to feed', stressing the people who live and eat under one roof, so 식구 feels warmer and more domestic ('우리 식구 다섯이에요') while 가족 is the neutral standard term that also covers relatives living apart. Register is neutral and safe in any setting. The big English-speaker trap is two-fold: 'big/small family' is 가족이 많다/적다 (many/few), never 가족이 크다 (that sounds like physically large bodies); and Koreans say 우리 가족 ('our family') where English forces 'my family', so the 우리 is idiomatic, not a mistake."},sentence:{ko:"가족이 몇 명이에요?",en:"How many people are in your family?"},target:"가족",options:["parents","father","family"],note:"Pairs with 명 for family size."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"명",romanization:"myeong",en:"counter for people",pos:"counter",nuance:"명 is the neutral people counter. In everyday family counts use native-number forms such as 한 명, 두 명, 세 명, and 네 명; use 분 when respectfully counting other people in an appropriate context."},sentence:{ko:"저희 가족은 다섯 명이에요.",en:"There are five people in my family."},target:"명",options:["our / my (humble, polite)","counter for people","family"],note:"People take 명, not 개."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"부모님",romanization:"bumonim",en:"parents",pos:"noun",nuance:"부모님 is Sino-Korean 父母 ('father' 父 + 'mother' 母) plus the honorific suffix 님, so the respect is baked into the word itself and it is the everyday polite default — there is no need to add anything to make it courteous. The bare root 부모 exists but is abstract and institutional ('부모의 역할' the role of parents, '부모 교육' parenting education); aimed at a real person it sounds cold, so for your own or anyone's parents you say 부모님. Because 님 marks the referent as worthy of respect, every verb around it must climb to its honorific tier: 있다→계시다, 주다→드리다, 집→댁, 에게→께, and the topic/subject can even take 께서. The classic English-speaker pitfall is pairing 부모님 with plain verbs ('부모님이 있어요', '부모님한테 줬어요'); grammatically the words exist, but to a Korean ear it clashes like saying 'Her Majesty ate fast' — you must use 계세요 and 드렸어요/께."},sentence:{ko:"부모님께서도 결혼식에 오세요.",en:"My parents are coming to the wedding too."},target:"부모님",options:["family","parents","father"],note:"Respected family noun plus 께서."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"아버지",romanization:"abeoji",en:"father",pos:"noun",nuance:"아버지 is the respectful, neutral word for 'father,' used in polite conversation, in writing, and whenever you speak about your dad to others. Its near-synonym 아빠 is the warm home word ('dad/daddy'): children and many adults call him 아빠 to his face, while 아버지 sounds more grown-up, dignified, or formal — the gap is exactly the English 'dad' vs 'father.' One step higher is 아버님, reserved for someone else's father or your father-in-law. Because a father is an elder, 아버지 naturally pulls honorific machinery: the subject marker 께서, the dative 께, and -(으)시- verb endings (읽으세요, 주무세요). Two English-speaker pitfalls: the copula is 아버지예요 (vowel-final 지 → 예요, not '아버지이에요'); and possession uses 우리 아버지 ('our father'), since 내 아버지 sounds oddly cold to Korean ears."},sentence:{ko:"아버지께서도 오세요.",en:"My father is coming too."},target:"아버지",options:["father","family","parents"],note:"Use 아버지 in polite introductions."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"어머니",romanization:"eomeoni",en:"mother",pos:"noun",nuance:"어머니 is the respectful, neutral word for 'mother,' used in polite speech, in writing, and when talking about your mom to others. Its near-synonym 엄마 is the affectionate home word ('mom/mommy') that people usually call her directly at home, while 어머니 carries more formality and respect — the same distance as English 'mom' vs 'mother.' The higher honorific 어머님 is for someone else's mother or your mother-in-law. As an elder, 어머니 routinely takes honorific particles and endings: 께서 for the subject, 께 for 'to,' and -(으)시- verbs (하세요, 주무세요). Watch two English-speaker traps: the copula contracts to 어머니예요 after the vowel 니 (not '어머니이에요'); and you give things to her with the honorific 어머니께 ... 드리다, not the plain 어머니한테 ... 줘요. Possession is likewise 우리 어머니, since 내 어머니 sounds unnaturally distant."},sentence:{ko:"어머니께서 오셨어요.",en:"My mother came."},target:"어머니",options:["mother","family","parents"],note:"Use 어머니 in polite introductions."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"가족이 몇 명이에요?",en:"How many people are in your family?"},hl:"가족"}],canDo:"Say how big my family is politely (저희 가족은 ~ 명이에요)",id:"chapter-47-b1",chapterId:"chapter-47",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · 저희 가족은 ~ 명이에요",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"저희 가족은 ~ 명이에요",sub:"Saying How Many People Are in Your Family",rows:[{when:"Counting people, use a native-Korean number + 명",add:"한/두/세/네/다섯… + 명",ex:"네 명 (four people), 다섯 명 (five people)"},{when:"Numbers 1–4 change shape right before a counter",add:"하나→한, 둘→두, 셋→세, 넷→네",ex:"두 명, 세 명, 네 명 (NOT 둘 명 / 셋 명)"},{when:"Closing the sentence after 명",add:"명 + 이에요 (명 ends in a consonant, so 이에요)",ex:"저희 가족은 네 명이에요."}],examples:[{ko:"저희 가족은 네 명이에요.",en:"There are four people in my family.",romanization:"jeohui gajogeun ne myeong-ieyo.",note:'저희 (humble "our") + 명 is the standard polite way to give family size; the native number 네 is used before 명.'},{ko:"저희 가족은 다섯 명이에요. 부모님, 형, 누나, 그리고 저요.",en:"There are five people in my family: my parents, older brother, older sister, and me.",romanization:"jeohui gajogeun daseot myeong-ieyo. bumonim, hyeong, nuna, geurigo jeoyo.",note:'Shows how to break the number down into members; 저요 ("and me") naturally closes the list.'}],more:{func:'To tell someone how big your family is, politely. 저희 is the humble form of 우리 ("our"), used to be modest in front of a listener, and 명 is the counter for people. You name the group (저희 가족은 = "my family"), then give the count with a native-Korean number + 명 + 이에요.',funcLead:"To tell someone how big your family is, politely.",keyPoint:{label:"명 vs. 분 for counting people",body:"명 is the neutral people-counter and is correct for your own family: 저희 가족은 네 명이에요. When you respectfully count other people (elders, guests), use 분 instead: 손님 세 분 (three guests). You always count your OWN family with 명, never 분, because you don't elevate your own in-group when speaking to others."},pronunciation:"가족은 links across the 받침: 가족 + 은 → [가조근]. The same happens in 가족이 → [가조기]. The final ㄱ of 가족 slides onto the following vowel.",pitfall:{wrong:"저희 가족은 넷 명이에요.",right:"저희 가족은 네 명이에요.",explanation:"Native numbers 1–4 take a special shortened shape before a counter: 하나→한, 둘→두, 셋→세, 넷→네. English speakers tend to keep the standalone form (넷) and say 넷 명, but before 명 it must become 네 명."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "There are four people in my family."',tokens:["저희","가족은","네","명이에요."],correct:"저희 가족은 네 명이에요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"저희 가족은 넷 명이에요.",ok:!1},{text:"저희 가족은 네 명이에요.",ok:!0}],explanation:"Native numbers 1–4 take a special shortened shape before a counter: 하나→한, 둘→두, 셋→세, 넷→네. English speakers tend to keep the standalone form (넷) and say 넷 명, but before 명 it must become 네 명."}],canDo:"Say how big my family is politely (저희 가족은 ~ 명이에요)",id:"chapter-47-b2",chapterId:"chapter-47",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 형~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"형",romanization:"hyeong",en:"older brother (male speaker)",pos:"noun",nuance:"형 (Sino-Korean 兄 'elder brother') is the word a MALE uses for an older brother, and crucially it is also a warm address term a younger man uses for any slightly-older male he is close to — an older friend, schoolmate, or colleague — so '민수 형' can mean 'Minsu, my big-brother-figure', not a blood relative. The matched set is gendered by speaker: a female says 오빠 for the same older-brother relationship, while 누나/언니 cover older sisters — so the single word leaks the speaker's gender, something English's flat 'older brother' never does. Register is casual-but-respectful: 형 is itself an honorific address (you call him 형, never his bare name), and you speak up to him. The dominant English-speaker pitfall is gender: a woman must never call her older brother 형 — that is 오빠 — and second, don't assume 형 always means kin; in daily life it is most often the affectionate title for an older male friend."},sentence:{ko:"저는 형이 한 명 있어요.",en:"I have one older brother."},target:"형",options:["family","parents","older brother (male speaker)"],note:"Used by a male speaker."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"오빠",romanization:"oppa",en:"older brother (female speaker)",pos:"noun",nuance:"오빠 is the word a FEMALE uses for an older brother, and it is the most socially loaded kinship term in Korean: a woman also calls a close older male friend, a senior, or especially a boyfriend 오빠, so '오빠' on its own can mean 'my big brother', 'my guy friend', or 'babe' depending on context — hence 친오빠 exists to specify the actual sibling. Its gendered counterpart is 형, used by a MALE for the same older-brother relationship, so 오빠 vs 형 splits by speaker, and the word instantly reveals the speaker is a woman. Register is casual and affectionate, used with -요 forms, and 오빠 is itself the address term (never his bare name). The major English-speaker pitfall is gender — a man must say 형, never 오빠 — and the cultural trap: because 오빠 also means 'boyfriend' in romantic use, calling a male friend 오빠 can imply flirtation, so learners should know the connotation before using it freely."},sentence:{ko:"저는 오빠가 한 명 있어요.",en:"I have one older brother."},target:"오빠",options:["family","parents","older brother (female speaker)"],note:"Used by a female speaker."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"누나",romanization:"nuna",en:"older sister (male speaker)",pos:"noun",nuance:"누나 is the word a MALE uses for an older sister, and like 형 it doubles as a warm address title a younger man uses for any older woman he is close to — an older female friend, senior student, or, famously, a beloved older woman ('누나' in K-pop). Its gendered partner is 언니, which a FEMALE uses for the very same older-sister relationship, so 누나 vs 언니 is split purely by who is speaking, and the word betrays the speaker's gender in a way English 'older sister' cannot. Register is casual but deferential: 누나 is itself a respectful address (you call her 누나, not her name) and you speak up to her with -요 forms. A very natural collocation is 도와주다 'help (as a favor)'. The headline English-speaker pitfall is gender: a man must say 누나 and a woman 언니 for an older sister — swapping them is an immediate giveaway — and remember 누나 is also how a younger man fondly addresses an older woman, not only a sibling."},sentence:{ko:"저는 누나가 한 명 있어요.",en:"I have one older sister."},target:"누나",options:["family","parents","older sister (male speaker)"],note:"Used by a male speaker."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"언니",romanization:"eonni",en:"older sister (female speaker)",pos:"noun",nuance:"언니 is the word a FEMALE uses for an older sister, and it extends warmly to any close older woman — an older female friend, a senior at school or work — and is also the friendly way younger women address a female shopkeeper or restaurant server ('언니, 여기요!'), so it ranges from blood sister to polite stranger. Its gendered counterpart is 누나, used by a MALE for the same older-sister bond, so 언니 vs 누나 splits purely by the speaker's gender, and the word reveals that the speaker is a woman — a distinction English 'older sister' simply lacks. Register is casual and affectionate, used with -요 endings, and 언니 itself serves as the address (you say 언니, not her name). The chief English-speaker pitfall is gender: a man must say 누나, never 언니, for an older sister; and don't be surprised to hear women call a saleswoman 언니 — it is friendly social lubricant, not a claim of kinship."},sentence:{ko:"저는 언니가 한 명 있어요.",en:"I have one older sister."},target:"언니",options:["family","older sister (female speaker)","parents"],note:"Used by a female speaker."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"동생",romanization:"dongsaeng",en:"younger sibling",pos:"noun",nuance:"동생 (Sino-Korean 同生 'born of the same' parents) means a younger sibling and, unlike the older-sibling words, is NOT split by speaker gender and does NOT itself reveal whether the sibling is male or female — to specify you prepend 남 (男) for 남동생 'younger brother' or 여 (女) for 여동생 'younger sister'. It also extends to any younger person you are close to (a junior at work or school), mirroring how 형/오빠/누나/언니 work upward; the system is asymmetric — elders get gendered, speaker-dependent titles, while juniors share the one neutral 동생. Register: unlike the older-sibling titles, 동생 is NOT used as a direct address — you call a younger sibling by name, and 동생 is only how you refer to them. The key English-speaker pitfall is assuming 동생 means 'younger brother'; it is sex-neutral, so without 남/여 it could be either, and you should never address someone as '동생' the way you'd say '형' — call them by name instead."},sentence:{ko:"저는 동생이 한 명 있어요.",en:"I have one younger sibling."},target:"동생",options:["family","parents","younger sibling"],note:"Works regardless of the speaker's gender."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"할아버지",romanization:"harabeoji",en:"grandfather",pos:"noun",nuance:"할아버지 is your grandfather on either side, and casual speech uses it for both the father's and the mother's father; 외할아버지 (maternal) or 친할아버지 (paternal) pin down the side when it matters. It is 할- (a prefix for the generation above) + 아버지 'father', and like 할머니 it can respectfully address any elderly man you know. The spelling trips beginners because of the liaison: it is one solid word, 할아버지, written together — never 할 아버지 — and the ㄹ slides onto the next syllable so it sounds like 하라버지. The deeper pitfall for English speakers is honorifics: a grandfather outranks you, so the verb climbs to the honorific level (읽으세요, 주무세요, 진지 드세요), and 밥 politely becomes 진지. Say 우리 할아버지, mirroring 우리 할머니, rather than the textbook-literal 제 할아버지."},sentence:{ko:"할아버지께서 오세요.",en:"My grandfather is coming."},target:"할아버지",options:["grandfather","family","parents"],note:"Good noun for practicing 께서."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"저희 가족은 다섯 명이에요. 부모님하고 형, 동생이 있어요.",en:"There are five people in my family. I have my parents, an older brother, and a younger sibling."},hl:"동생"}],canDo:"Pick the right older-sibling word for my own gender (형 · 오빠 · 누나 · 언니)",id:"chapter-47-b3",chapterId:"chapter-47",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · 형 / 오빠 / 누나 / 언니",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"형 / 오빠 / 누나 / 언니",sub:"Older-Sibling Words That Depend on YOUR Gender",rows:[{when:"A male speaker's older brother",add:"형",ex:"(남자가) 우리 형 (my older brother)"},{when:"A female speaker's older brother",add:"오빠",ex:"(여자가) 우리 오빠 (my older brother)"},{when:"A male speaker's older sister",add:"누나",ex:"(남자가) 우리 누나 (my older sister)"},{when:"A female speaker's older sister",add:"언니",ex:"(여자가) 우리 언니 (my older sister)"}],examples:[{ko:"(남자) 우리 형은 회사에 다녀요.",en:"(male speaker) My older brother works at a company.",romanization:"(namja) uri hyeong-eun hoesa-e danyeoyo.",note:"A boy calls his older brother 형; 오빠 would be wrong here for a male speaker."},{ko:"(여자) 우리 오빠는 키가 커요.",en:"(female speaker) My older brother is tall.",romanization:"(yeoja) uri oppa-neun kiga keoyo.",note:"A girl calls her older brother 오빠 — same person, different word from a boy's 형."}],more:{func:'To name an older brother or older sister. Korean has no single word for "older brother" or "older sister" — the word you pick depends on whether YOU (the speaker) are male or female. A man and a woman use completely different words for the very same person.',funcLead:"To name an older brother or older sister.",keyPoint:{label:"The word encodes the SPEAKER's gender, not the sibling's",body:"Picture a 2×2 grid. Older brother: 형 (said by a male) vs. 오빠 (said by a female). Older sister: 누나 (said by a male) vs. 언니 (said by a female). The same older brother is 형 to his younger brother and 오빠 to his younger sister at the same time. Younger siblings have no gendered split — they are just 동생 (남동생 = younger brother, 여동생 = younger sister)."},pronunciation:"오빠's ㅃ is a tense, slightly held stop. 언니 → [언니] keeps both ㄴ sounds clear. 형은 → [형은], with the ㅇ (ng) carrying over softly; no tensing here.",pitfall:{wrong:"(a male speaker) 우리 오빠는 키가 커요.",right:"(a male speaker) 우리 형은 키가 커요.",explanation:'English has one word, "older brother," so learners grab whichever Korean word they heard first — often 오빠 from K-pop. But 오빠 is only for FEMALE speakers. A male speaker must say 형. Always ask first: who is talking, a man or a woman?'}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "(male speaker) My older brother works at a company." ((남자 시작 · Start with (남자.)',tokens:["(남자)","우리","형은","회사에","다녀요."],correct:"(남자) 우리 형은 회사에 다녀요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "(female speaker) My older brother is tall."',tokens:["(여자)","우리","오빠는","키가","커요."],correct:"(여자) 우리 오빠는 키가 커요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"(a male speaker) 우리 형은 키가 커요.",ok:!0},{text:"(a male speaker) 우리 오빠는 키가 커요.",ok:!1}],explanation:'English has one word, "older brother," so learners grab whichever Korean word they heard first — often 오빠 from K-pop. But 오빠 is only for FEMALE speakers. A male speaker must say 형. Always ask first: who is talking, a man or a woman?'}],canDo:"Pick the right older-sibling word for my own gender (형 · 오빠 · 누나 · 언니)",id:"chapter-47-b4",chapterId:"chapter-47",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 할머니~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"할머니",romanization:"halmeoni",en:"grandmother",pos:"noun",nuance:"할머니 is your grandmother on either side, and in plain conversation Koreans use it for both the father's and the mother's mother; add 외- (외할머니) or 친- (친할머니) only when you must mark the side. It is built from 할- (an old prefix meaning a generation above) + 어머니 'mother', and it doubles as a warm, respectful way to address ANY elderly woman you feel close to, much like English 'grandma' for a kindly neighbor. The big trap for English speakers is grammar of respect, not vocabulary: a grandmother is a high-status elder, so the verb itself must be honorific — say 할머니는 사세요 / 주무세요 / 드세요, never the flat 살아요 / 자요 / 먹어요. Pair it with 우리 할머니 ('our grandmother'), not 제 할머니, because Korean treats family as shared. Compared with the polite stranger-address 아주머니/아주머님 (a middle-aged woman), 할머니 implies real old age, so misjudging the age can sting."},sentence:{ko:"할머니께 안부를 전해 주세요.",en:"Please send my regards to your grandmother."},target:"할머니",options:["family","parents","grandmother"],note:"께 marks a respected receiver; 께서 marks a respected subject."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"결혼식",romanization:"gyeolhonsik",en:"wedding ceremony",pos:"noun",nuance:"결혼식 is the wedding CEREMONY (식 = 式 'ceremony/rite'), as opposed to 결혼 (結婚, 'tie + marry'), which is the marriage or the act of getting married — so 결혼했어요 means 'I got married,' while 결혼식에 갔어요 means 'I attended a wedding.' That 결혼/결혼식 split is the #1 trap for English speakers, since English 'wedding' blurs both. Compared with the everyday synonym 예식 (禮式, 'ceremony of courtesy'), 결혼식 is the plain default and 예식 sounds slightly more formal/venue-flavored (hence 예식장). The ceremony itself is famously short (30–60 minutes); the socially weighty part is the buffet meal afterward, which your cash gift 축의금 helps cover. Habitual collocations: 결혼식에 가다/참석하다, 결혼식을 올리다, 결혼식장. Take 에 for attendance — 결혼식을 가다 is a common particle error."},sentence:{ko:"부모님께서도 결혼식에 오세요.",en:"My parents are coming to the wedding too."},target:"결혼식",options:["family","wedding ceremony","parents"],note:"A natural event where family members are introduced politely."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"오다",romanization:"oda",en:"to come",pos:"verb",nuance:"오다 is from the speaker’s point of view. If someone moves toward you or your place, 오다 is usually right."},sentence:{ko:"어머니께서 오셨어요.",en:"My mother came."},target:"오셨",options:["to come","to call by phone","our / my (humble, polite)"],note:"오다 becomes 오세요/오셨어요 when honoring the subject."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"전화하다",romanization:"jeonhwahada",en:"to call by phone",pos:"verb",nuance:"전화하다 is noun + 하다, so 전화 안 해요 is a natural short negative."},sentence:{ko:"부모님께 자주 전화해요.",en:"I often call my parents."},target:"전화해",options:["to come","our / my (humble, polite)","to call by phone"],note:"Useful family-contact verb; 전화드려요 is the more respectful version."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"부모님께서도 결혼식에 오세요?",en:"Are your parents coming to the wedding too?"},hl:"결혼식"}],canDo:"Refer to my parents respectfully (부모님 · 아버지 · 어머니)",id:"chapter-47-b5",chapterId:"chapter-47",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · 부모님 / 아버지 / 어머니",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"부모님 / 아버지 / 어머니",sub:"Respectful Words for Parents",rows:[{when:"Both parents together, respectfully",add:"부모 + 님 (honorific suffix)",ex:"부모님 (one's parents)"},{when:"Father — respectful vs. casual",add:"아버지 (respectful) / 아빠 (casual)",ex:"저희 아버지 vs. 우리 아빠"},{when:"Mother — respectful vs. casual",add:"어머니 (respectful) / 엄마 (casual)",ex:"저희 어머니 vs. 우리 엄마"},{when:"Someone else's parents (extra polite)",add:"아버님 / 어머님 (add ~님)",ex:"친구 어머님 (a friend's mother)"}],examples:[{ko:"저희 부모님은 부산에 사세요.",en:"My parents live in Busan.",romanization:"jeohui bumonim-eun busan-e saseyo.",note:"부모님 + the honorific verb 사세요 (from 살다) elevate the parents — the natural pairing for elders."},{ko:"저희 아버지는 선생님이세요.",en:"My father is a teacher.",romanization:"jeohui abeoji-neun seonsaengnim-iseyo.",note:"아버지 (not 아빠) is the respectful choice when speaking to people outside the family; ~세요 honors him."}],more:{func:'To talk about your parents respectfully. These are the polite nouns for family elders. 부모님 = "parents" (with the honorific suffix ~님), 아버지 = "father," 어머니 = "mother." They contrast with the casual 아빠 (dad) and 엄마 (mom) that children use at home.',funcLead:"To talk about your parents respectfully.",keyPoint:{label:"아버지/어머니 (respectful) vs. 아빠/엄마 (casual)",body:'Use 아버지/어머니 when you describe your parents TO other people, in writing, or in any polite setting — they show respect. Use 아빠/엄마 to ADDRESS your own parents at home or among close family, like English "Dad/Mom" vs. "father/mother." 부모님 always carries ~님 and has no casual short form.'},pronunciation:"부모님은 links the 받침: 부모님 + 은 → [부모니믄], the ㅁ carrying onto 은. 선생님이세요 → [선생니미세요] in the same way.",pitfall:{wrong:"저희 엄마는 의사예요. (in a polite self-introduction)",right:"저희 어머니는 의사세요.",explanation:'English uses "my mom" comfortably even in semi-formal speech, so learners say 엄마 everywhere. In Korean, 엄마/아빠 sound childish when you introduce your family to strangers or elders. Switch to 어머니/아버지 (and 부모님 with ~님) to be polite.'}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "My parents live in Busan." (저희 시작 · Start with 저희.)',tokens:["저희","부모님은","부산에","사세요."],correct:"저희 부모님은 부산에 사세요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "My father is a teacher."',tokens:["저희","아버지는","선생님이세요."],correct:"저희 아버지는 선생님이세요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"저희 엄마는 의사예요. (in a polite self-introduction)",ok:!1},{text:"저희 어머니는 의사세요.",ok:!0}],explanation:'English uses "my mom" comfortably even in semi-formal speech, so learners say 엄마 everywhere. In Korean, 엄마/아빠 sound childish when you introduce your family to strangers or elders. Switch to 어머니/아버지 (and 부모님 with ~님) to be polite.'}],canDo:"Refer to my parents respectfully (부모님 · 아버지 · 어머니)",id:"chapter-47-b6",chapterId:"chapter-47",index:5},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N께서 V-(으)시-",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"N께서 V-(으)시-",sub:"Honoring a Family Elder Who Does the Action",rows:[{when:"Marking an honored subject",add:"이/가 → 께서 (은/는 → 께서는)",ex:"아버지가 → 아버지께서"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel + honorific",add:"stem + ~세요",ex:"가다 → 가세요, 오다 → 오세요"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant + honorific",add:"stem + ~으세요",ex:"읽다 → 읽으세요, 앉다 → 앉으세요"},{when:"Special honorific verbs (must be memorized)",add:"있다→계시다, 먹다→드시다/잡수시다, 자다→주무시다",ex:"할머니께서 주무세요 (Grandma is sleeping)"}],examples:[{ko:"할아버지께서 신문을 읽으세요.",en:"My grandfather reads the newspaper.",romanization:"harabeoji-kkeseo sinmun-eul ilgeuseyo.",note:"할아버지께서 marks the honored subject; 읽다 (consonant stem) → 읽으세요 with the ~으세요 form."},{ko:"어머니께서 방에 계세요.",en:"My mother is in the room.",romanization:"eomeoni-kkeseo bang-e gyeseyo.",note:"있다 has the special honorific 계시다, not 있으세요 — use 계세요 for an elder's location/presence."}],more:{func:"To honor the person doing the action — usually a family elder like a parent or grandparent. Two pieces work together: 께서 replaces the subject marker 이/가 (or 은/는) to lift up the subject, and the verb takes the honorific infix ~(으)시~, which surfaces as ~세요/~으세요 in polite speech.",funcLead:"To honor the person doing the action — usually a family elder like a parent or grandparent.",keyPoint:{label:"Honor the subject in the verb",body:"The verb ending ~(으)시~ is the core way to honor the person doing the action. The honorific subject marker 께서 often accompanies it in careful speech, while ordinary 이/가 can also appear with an honorific verb depending on context. You can be humble about yourself with 저희 while honoring an elder: 저희 어머니가 만드세요 and 저희 어머니께서 만드세요 are both possible."},pronunciation:"읽으세요 → [일그세요]: the ㄺ cluster splits, ㄱ moving onto the 으. 계세요 → [게세요] in casual speech (the ㅖ often flattens to ㅔ). 께서 keeps its tense ㄲ at the start.",pitfall:{wrong:"아버지께서 저녁을 먹어요.",right:"아버지께서 저녁을 드세요.",explanation:"Learners may add 께서 but leave the verb plain. The verb should honor the elder too, and conventional lexical honorifics such as 드시다, 계시다, and 주무시다 are the natural choices in careful family descriptions."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "My grandfather reads the newspaper."',tokens:["할아버지께서","신문을","읽으세요."],correct:"할아버지께서 신문을 읽으세요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "My mother is in the room." (어머니께서 시작 · Start with 어머니께서.)',tokens:["어머니께서","방에","계세요."],correct:"어머니께서 방에 계세요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"아버지께서 저녁을 드세요.",ok:!0},{text:"아버지께서 저녁을 먹어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Learners may add 께서 but leave the verb plain. The verb should honor the elder too, and conventional lexical honorifics such as 드시다, 계시다, and 주무시다 are the natural choices in careful family descriptions."}],canDo:"Honor a family elder who does the action (아버지께서 ~하세요)",id:"chapter-47-b7",chapterId:"chapter-47",index:6},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A learner introduces family and checks sibling terms before a friend's wedding.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"가족이 몇 명이에요?",en:"How many people are in your family?",romanization:"gajogi myeot myeongieyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"저희 가족은 다섯 명이에요. 부모님하고 형, 동생이 있어요.",en:"There are five people in my family. I have my parents, an older brother, and a younger sibling.",romanization:"jeohui gajogeun daseot myeongieyo. bumonimhago hyeong, dongsaengi isseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"형은 남자가 쓰는 말이죠?",en:"Hyeong is the word a male speaker uses, right?",romanization:"hyeongeun namjaga sseuneun marijyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네. 여자가 부르면 오빠라고 해요. 누나와 언니도 달라요.",en:"Yes. If a female speaker says it, she says oppa. Nuna and eonni are different too.",romanization:"ne. yeojaga bureumyeon opparago haeyo. nuna-wa eonnido dallayo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"부모님께서도 결혼식에 오세요?",en:"Are your parents coming to the wedding too?",romanization:"bumonimkkeseodo gyeolhonsige oseyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네, 어머니께서 오시고 아버지께서도 오세요.",en:"Yes, my mother is coming, and my father is coming too.",romanization:"ne, eomeonikkeseo osigo abeojikkeseodo oseyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'My family has four people.'",tokens:["저희 가족은","네 명이에요"],correct:"저희 가족은 네 명이에요",explanation:"저희 가족은 politely sets the family as the topic. People are counted with 명, and four before a counter is 네."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"누가 한 말일까요? · Who said this?",sentence:"저희 가족은 다섯 명이에요. 부모님하고 형, 동생이 있어요.",options:[{text:"지훈",ok:!0},{text:"민서",ok:!1}],explanation:"There are five people in my family. I have my parents, an older brother, and a younger sibling."},{kind:"drill",prompt:'무엇이 들어갈까요? · "How many people are in your family?"',sentence:"___이 몇 명이에요?",options:[{text:"부모님",ok:!1},{text:"아버지",ok:!1},{text:"가족",ok:!0}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Say how big my family is politely (저희 가족은 ~ 명이에요)",id:"chapter-47-b8",chapterId:"chapter-47",index:7},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 저희 가족은 다섯 명이에요",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"저희 가족은 다섯 명이에요",chunks:["지훈은 회사 동료에게 가족을 소개했어요. '저희 가족은 다섯 명이에요. 부모님하고 형, 동생이 있어요.'라고 말했어요. 지훈은 남자라서 자기보다 나이 많은 남자 형제를 형이라고 불러요.","여자가 같은 사람을 부르면 오빠가 돼요. 나이 많은 여자 형제도 남자가 말하면 누나, 여자가 말하면 언니예요. 친구 결혼식 이야기를 할 때는 '어머니께서 오시고 아버지께서도 오세요'라고 말했어요. 가족 이야기는 단어 선택과 높임 선택이 같이 가야 자연스러워요."],translation:"Jihun introduced his family to a coworker: 'There are five people in my family. I have my parents, an older brother, and a younger sibling.' Because Jihun is male, he calls an older male sibling hyeong. If a female speaker refers to the same person, the word becomes oppa. For an older female sibling, a male speaker says nuna and a female speaker says eonni. When talking about a friend's wedding, he says, 'My mother is coming, and my father is coming too' with honorific subject language. Family talk sounds natural when word choice and respect level work together.",qas:[{q:"지훈의 가족은 몇 명이에요?",a:"다섯 명이에요."},{q:"남자가 나이 많은 남자 형제를 부를 때 어떤 말을 써요?",a:"형이라고 해요."},{q:"지훈은 부모님에 대해 어떤 높임 표현을 썼어요?",a:"어머니께서 오시고 아버지께서도 오세요."}]},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"지훈은 회사 동료에게 ___을 소개했어요.",options:[{text:"가족",ok:!0},{text:"할아버지",ok:!1},{text:"할머니",ok:!1}],explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"'___ 가족은 다섯 명이에요.",options:[{text:"저희",ok:!0},{text:"할아버지",ok:!1},{text:"할머니",ok:!1}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Pick the right older-sibling word for my own gender (형 · 오빠 · 누나 · 언니)",id:"chapter-47-b9",chapterId:"chapter-47",index:8},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence describes family size naturally?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"저희 가족은 네 명이에요.",ok:!0},{text:"저희 가족은 네 개예요.",ok:!1},{text:"저희 가족은 네 잔이에요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"명 counts people. 개 counts things, and 잔 counts cups or glasses."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"A male speaker is talking about his older brother. Which word fits?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"형",ok:!0},{text:"오빠",ok:!1},{text:"언니",ok:!1}],explanation:"A male speaker calls an older brother 형. A female speaker calls an older brother 오빠."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"A female speaker is talking about her older sister. Which word fits?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"언니",ok:!0},{text:"누나",ok:!1},{text:"형",ok:!1}],explanation:"A female speaker calls an older sister 언니. A male speaker calls an older sister 누나."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence honors the mother as the subject?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"어머니께서 오셨어요.",ok:!0},{text:"어머니께 오셨어요.",ok:!1},{text:"어머니가 왔어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"께서 marks the respected subject, and 오셨어요 is the honorific past of 오다."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence uses 께 as a respected receiver, not a subject?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"할머니께 안부를 전해 주세요.",ok:!0},{text:"할머니께서 안부를 전해 주세요.",ok:!1},{text:"할머니가께 안부를 전해 주세요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"께 means to a respected person. 께서 means the respected person is the subject."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어머니___ 오셨어요. (My mother came.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"께서",ok:!0},{text:"께",ok:!1},{text:"를",ok:!1}],explanation:"어머니 is the respected subject, so use 께서. 께 marks a respected receiver instead."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"A male speaker is talking about his older sister. Which word fits?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"누나",ok:!0},{text:"언니",ok:!1},{text:"오빠",ok:!1}],explanation:"Male speakers use 누나 for older sister. 언니 is from a female speaker’s point of view."}],canDo:"Refer to my parents respectfully (부모님 · 아버지 · 어머니)",id:"chapter-47-b10",chapterId:"chapter-47",index:9}]},{id:"chapter-48",number:48,title:"Symptom frame · V-아/어야 해요",goal:"Describe symptoms with body part or symptom + 이/가 + 아프다/나다.",biteCount:13,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 증상~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"증상",romanization:"jeungsang",en:"symptom",pos:"noun",nuance:"증상(症狀)은 '병 증(症)' + '모양 상(狀)', 즉 '병의 모양·꼴'이라는 뜻으로, 병 때문에 몸에 드러나는 기침·열·통증 같은 '겉 신호'를 가리킨다. 헷갈리기 쉬운 이웃 단어가 둘 있다. 하나는 병 자체인 질병(disease)으로, 증상은 그 질병이 밖으로 내보이는 표시일 뿐이다. 또 하나는 통증(pain)인데, 통증은 아픈 느낌 하나를 가리키고 증상은 통증·발열·기침을 모두 아우르는 더 넓은 말이다. 말투는 격식·비격식을 가리지 않는 중립어라 환자도 의사도 똑같이 쓰며, 병원에서는 '어떤 증상이 있으세요?'가 첫 질문일 만큼 핵심어다. 영어 화자의 함정은 'I feel a symptom'을 직역해 '증상을 느끼다'만 고집하는 것인데, 한국어에서는 '증상이 있다 / 나타나다 / 심해지다 / 사라지다'처럼 증상을 주어로 세워 변화를 말하는 어법이 훨씬 자연스럽다."},sentence:{ko:"증상이 언제부터 있었어요?",en:"When did the symptoms start?"},target:"증상",options:["symptom","hospital / clinic","pharmacy"],note:"Clinic intake word."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"병원",romanization:"byeongwon",en:"hospital / clinic",pos:"noun",nuance:"병원 can mean hospital or clinic in everyday Korean."},sentence:{ko:"배가 아파서 병원에 왔어요.",en:"My stomach hurts, so I came to the clinic."},target:"병원",options:["symptom","hospital / clinic","pharmacy"],note:"In Korean, 병원 often covers neighborhood clinics too."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"약국",romanization:"yakguk",en:"pharmacy",pos:"noun",nuance:"약국 is where medicine is dispensed or sold. 병원 is where you see the doctor; 약국 is where you usually receive the medicine afterward."},sentence:{ko:"처방전을 가지고 약국에 가요.",en:"I take the prescription to the pharmacy."},target:"약국",options:["symptom","pharmacy","hospital / clinic"],note:"Clinic-to-pharmacy flow."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"목",romanization:"mok",en:"neck; throat",pos:"noun",nuance:"목 covers both the neck and the throat, so 목이 아프다 most often means a sore throat from a cold, NOT neck pain — assuming it only means 'neck' is the main English-speaker pitfall (for true neck/shoulder stiffness Koreans more often say 목이 뻐근하다 or specify 목덜미). Thirst is the fixed phrase 목이 마르다 ('the throat dries'), where 마르다 is 르-irregular → 말라요, 말랐어요; do not invent 목이 건조하다. A swollen throat is 목이 붓다 (ㅅ-irregular → 부어요), the same swelling verb as 발이 붓다, and going hoarse is 목이 쉬다 (lit. 'the throat rests'). Idiomatically 목이 빠지게 기다리다 means to wait so eagerly your neck stretches out ('wait with bated breath'). A throat/neck symptom marks the part with 가 (목이 아파요)."},sentence:{ko:"목이 아파요.",en:"My throat hurts."},target:"목",options:["symptom","neck; throat","hospital / clinic"],note:"In symptom talk, 목 often means throat."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"배",romanization:"bae",en:"belly; stomach",pos:"noun",nuance:"배 means the belly/stomach (the body region), and the same syllable also means 'pear' and 'boat/ship' — three homonyms split only by context. Hunger and fullness are locked set phrases that ALWAYS keep 배: 배가 고프다 (hungry) and 배가 부르다 (full); you cannot say 나는 고파요 alone, which is the top beginner error. Both adjectives are irregular: 고프다 is ㅡ-irregular (고파요, 고팠어요) and 부르다 is 르-irregular (불러요, 불렀어요). Note 배 here is the colloquial stomach for aches and hunger, distinct from the Sino-Korean 위 (胃, the actual stomach organ) used in medical contexts like 위염 (gastritis). A culturally important idiom: 배가 아프다 also figuratively means to be jealous of someone's good fortune ('my stomach hurts' = green with envy), so context decides literal pain vs. envy."},sentence:{ko:"배가 아파요.",en:"My stomach hurts."},target:"배",options:["belly; stomach","symptom","hospital / clinic"],note:"Most natural first sentence for stomach pain."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"환자",ko:"네. 처방전을 받고 약국에 가서 약을 기다릴게요.",en:"Okay. I will take the prescription, go to the pharmacy, and wait for the medicine."},hl:"약국"}],canDo:"Say what hurts with body part + 이/가 아프다 (목이 아파요)",id:"chapter-48-b1",chapterId:"chapter-48",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · Symptom frame",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"Symptom frame",sub:"body part + 이/가 아프다",rows:[{when:"Body part ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"+ 이 아파요",ex:"목 → 목이 아파요 (my throat hurts)"},{when:"Body part ends in a vowel",add:"+ 가 아파요",ex:"머리 → 머리가 아파요 (my head hurts)"},{when:"Asking 'where does it hurt?'",add:"어디 + 가 아파요?",ex:"어디가 아파요? (where does it hurt?)"},{when:"Past tense (it hurt)",add:"아프 → 아팠어요",ex:"어제부터 배가 아팠어요 (my stomach has hurt since yesterday)"}],examples:[{ko:"머리가 아파요.",en:"My head hurts.",romanization:"meoriga apayo.",note:"머리 ends in a vowel, so it takes 가; 아프 + 아요 → 아파요."},{ko:"목이 아프고 열도 나요.",en:"My throat hurts and I also have a fever.",romanization:"mogi apeugo yeoldo nayo.",note:"목 has a 받침, so it takes 이; 열이 나다 is the natural way to say 'have a fever.'"}],more:{func:"Say what hurts at the clinic. Korean makes the body part the grammatical subject, marked with 이/가, and 아프다 (to hurt / be sore) describes its state. This is your answer when the doctor asks 어디가 아파요? (Where does it hurt?).",funcLead:"Say what hurts at the clinic.",keyPoint:{label:"The body part is the SUBJECT, not the object",body:"Use 아프다 for physical body pain (머리가 아파요). Korean makes the body part the subject with 이/가 — it 'hurts' by itself. There is no 'I have a pain' verb that takes the body part as an object, so never mark it with 을/를 here."},pronunciation:"",pitfall:{wrong:"나는 머리를 아파요.",right:"(저는) 머리가 아파요.",explanation:"English says 'I hurt my head' with the head as object (를). In Korean the body part is the SUBJECT (가), and 아프다 describes its state. Never mark the body part with 를 here."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "My throat hurts and I also have a fever."',tokens:["목이","아프고","열도","나요."],correct:"목이 아프고 열도 나요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"(저는) 머리가 아파요.",ok:!0},{text:"나는 머리를 아파요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"English says 'I hurt my head' with the head as object (를). In Korean the body part is the SUBJECT (가), and 아프다 describes its state. Never mark the body part with 를 here."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"빈칸을 채우세요 · Complete the form — Body part ends in a consonant (받침)",sentence:"목___",options:[{text:"가 아파요",ok:!1},{text:"이 아파요",ok:!0}],explanation:"목 → 목이 아파요 (my throat hurts)"},{kind:"drill",prompt:"빈칸을 채우세요 · Complete the form — Body part ends in a vowel",sentence:"머리___",options:[{text:"가 아파요",ok:!0},{text:"이 아파요",ok:!1}],explanation:"머리 → 머리가 아파요 (my head hurts)"}],canDo:"Say what hurts with body part + 이/가 아프다 (목이 아파요)",id:"chapter-48-b2",chapterId:"chapter-48",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 열~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"열",romanization:"yeol",en:"fever",pos:"noun",nuance:"열 (熱, the hanja for 'heat') is a fever, and the must-learn collocation is 열이 나다, where 나다 literally means '(something) comes out/arises' — Korean conceives a fever as heat 'coming out' of the body, so you never say 열을 가지다 ('have/possess a fever'), a direct translation of English 'have' that sounds completely unnatural. You can also say 열이 있다 ('there is fever') as a calmer alternative. The level of a fever is described with 높다/낮다 (열이 높다 = high fever), not 크다/작다, and once treated, 열이 내리다 ('the fever comes down') is the natural way to say it subsides — paralleling how prices or temperature 내리다. To take someone's temperature is 열을 재다 (재다 = to measure), the same verb as 키를 재다 (measure height). Don't confuse this 열 with the homonym 열 meaning 'ten' (열 개 = ten items); context separates them."},sentence:{ko:"아침부터 열이 나요.",en:"I have had a fever since this morning."},target:"열",options:["fever","symptom","hospital / clinic"],note:"Use 열이 나다 for having a fever."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"기침",romanization:"gichim",en:"cough",pos:"noun",nuance:"기침 is a native Korean word (not Sino-Korean), and it works in two equally natural patterns that differ subtly in viewpoint: 기침이 나다 frames the cough as something that 'comes out' on its own — built with 나다, the same verb in 열이 나다(run a fever), 콧물이 나다(runny nose), 재채기가 나다(sneeze) — stressing it's involuntary; whereas 기침을 하다 frames it as an action you perform, useful when you mean someone coughs deliberately or repeatedly. English speakers raised on 'have a cough' often try 기침을 가지다 ('possess a cough'), which is flatly wrong — Korean never 'has' a symptom with 가지다; symptoms either 나다 (come out) or you 하다 (do) them. Don't confuse 기침 with 재채기 (a sneeze): 기침 is a throat/chest cough, 재채기 is the explosive nose sneeze, and they are not interchangeable. For severity use 심하다 (기침이 심하다 = a bad cough), not 크다."},sentence:{ko:"기침 때문에 잠을 못 잤어요.",en:"I could not sleep because of the cough."},target:"기침",options:["symptom","cough","hospital / clinic"],note:"Good noun for 때문에."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"감기",romanization:"gamgi",en:"a cold",pos:"noun",nuance:"감기 (感氣, 'feeling/affecting + air/energy') is the Sino-Korean word for a common cold, the everyday illness with cough, runny nose, and sore throat — distinct from the heavier 독감 (毒感, 'poison-cold' = influenza/the flu), which is a serious, feverish illness, so don't call the flu just 감기. The critical grammar point is the particle: you 'catch' a cold with 감기에 걸리다, using 에, because 걸리다 ('get caught by/hung on') takes a location-like target — 감기를 걸리다 with 을 is the number-one English-speaker error. Cold medicine is taken with 먹다 (감기약을 먹다, lit. 'eat medicine'), never 마시다 ('drink'), even for liquid syrup. The phrase 감기 조심하세요 ('be careful of cold') is a warm, very common parting line when the weather turns cold, much like 'stay warm / don't catch a cold.'"},sentence:{ko:"감기 때문에 병원에 왔어요.",en:"I came to the clinic because of a cold."},target:"감기",options:["symptom","hospital / clinic","a cold"],note:"Common named cause in clinic talk."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"약",romanization:"yak",en:"medicine",pos:"noun",nuance:"Korean commonly says 약을 먹어요 for taking medicine."},sentence:{ko:"약은 식후에 드셔야 해요.",en:"You have to take the medicine after meals."},target:"약",options:["medicine","symptom","hospital / clinic"],note:"Core pharmacy noun."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"처방전",romanization:"cheobangjeon",en:"prescription",pos:"noun",nuance:"처방전 is not the medicine itself. It is the document or order that lets the pharmacy prepare the medicine."},sentence:{ko:"병원에서 처방전을 받았어요.",en:"I got a prescription at the clinic."},target:"처방전",options:["prescription","symptom","hospital / clinic"],note:"Bridge between clinic and pharmacy."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"환자",ko:"아침부터 열이 나고 목이 아파요. 기침도 나요.",en:"Since this morning I have had a fever and a sore throat. I also have a cough."},hl:"기침"}],canDo:"Describe a symptom 'coming out' with 이/가 나다 (열이 나요, 기침이 나요)",id:"chapter-48-b3",chapterId:"chapter-48",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-아/어야 해요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-아/어야 해요",sub:"must / have to (medical instruction)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"약을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"하루에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"세",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"번",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"먹",mid:"어야",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"해요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"이 약을 하루에 세 번 먹어야 해요.",en:"You have to take this medicine three times a day.",romanization:"i yageul harue se beon meogeoya haeyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"내일",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"다시",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"와야",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"해요",post:"?",hit:!0}],ko:"내일 다시 와야 해요?",en:"Do I have to come back tomorrow?",romanization:"naeil dasi waya haeyo?"}],rule:{name:"V-아/어야 해요",rows:[{when:"Verb stem has ㅏ/ㅗ vowel",add:"+ 아야 해요",ex:"받다 → 받아야 해요 (have to get / receive)"},{when:"Verb stem has other vowel",add:"+ 어야 해요",ex:"먹다 → 먹어야 해요 (have to take / eat)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"하 → 해야 해요",ex:"검사하다 → 검사해야 해요 (have to get tested)"},{when:"Casual spoken alternative",add:"-아/어야 돼요",ex:"쉬어야 돼요 (have to rest)"}]},more:{func:"Give or receive a medical instruction: something you must do. The doctor uses it for what's necessary (약을 먹어야 해요 — you have to take the medicine), and you use it for what you must do (쉬어야 해요 — I have to rest).",funcLead:"Give or receive a medical instruction: something you must do.",keyPoint:{label:"해요 vs 돼요",body:"-아/어야 해요 and -아/어야 돼요 both mean 'must / have to.' 돼요 is a touch more conversational and very common in speech; 해요 feels slightly more standard. In a clinic you will hear both."},pronunciation:"",pitfall:{wrong:"약을 먹어 해요.",right:"약을 먹어야 해요.",explanation:"Learners drop the 야. The obligation meaning lives entirely in -야; without it the sentence is broken. Always keep -아/어 + 야 + 해요/돼요 together."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"You have to rest well.",sentence:"푹 쉬___.",options:[{text:"어야 해요",ok:!0},{text:"해요",ok:!1},{text:"아야 해요",ok:!1}],explanation:"쉬다 → 쉬어야 해요; 푹 is the set adverb that pairs with resting."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"You have to get a blood test.",sentence:"피 검사를 받___.",options:[{text:"어야 해요",ok:!1},{text:"아야 해요",ok:!0}],explanation:"받다 has ㅏ, so 받아야 해요; 피 검사 = blood test."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Do I have to come back tomorrow?"',tokens:["내일","다시","와야","해요?"],correct:"내일 다시 와야 해요?",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"약을 먹어야 해요.",ok:!0},{text:"약을 먹어 해요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Learners drop the 야. The obligation meaning lives entirely in -야; without it the sentence is broken. Always keep -아/어 + 야 + 해요/돼요 together."}],canDo:"Describe a symptom 'coming out' with 이/가 나다 (열이 나요, 기침이 나요)",id:"chapter-48-b4",chapterId:"chapter-48",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 식후~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"식후",romanization:"sikhu",en:"after meals",pos:"noun",nuance:"식후 is concise and label-like. In casual speech you can also say 밥 먹고, but at a clinic or pharmacy 식후 is the expected word. 식후 usually pairs with 에 or a time amount: 식후에, 식후 30분."},sentence:{ko:"하루 세 번 식후에 드세요.",en:"Take it three times a day after meals."},target:"식후",options:["symptom","after meals","hospital / clinic"],note:"Medicine timing word."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"하루",romanization:"haru",en:"one day; a day",pos:"noun",nuance:"하루 is the native Korean word for a single 24-hour day and lives inside several fixed phrases you should memorise as chunks: 하루 종일 (all day long), 하루에 ... 번 (... times a day), and the doubled 하루하루 (day by day, one day at a time). Here 하루 is pure native counting, which is why it heads the irregular native day-count series 하루(1)·이틀(2)·사흘(3)·나흘(4) rather than taking 일(日) the way calendar dates do. Two English-speaker pitfalls. First, the rate construction: 'once a day' is 하루에 한 번, with 에 carrying the 'per' meaning — never 하루 번. Second, do not let 하루 drift into meaning 'today'; 하루 is 'a day' as a length or an unspecified day ('one day...'), whereas 'today' is the separate word 오늘. So 'I worked all day today' combines both: 오늘 하루 종일 일했어요."},sentence:{ko:"하루 세 번 드세요.",en:"Take it three times a day."},target:"하루",options:["symptom","one day; a day","hospital / clinic"],note:"Frequency word for medicine instructions."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"의사",romanization:"uisa",en:"doctor",pos:"noun",nuance:"의사 is Sino-Korean 醫師 ('medicine' 醫 + 'master/teacher' 師), so the word literally frames a doctor as a master of medicine — which is why Koreans almost reflexively append 선생님 (의사 선생님) when addressing or referring to one, the way English keeps 'Doctor' as a title. Saying bare 의사 to or about a specific physician can sound flat or even slightly disrespectful, like calling them 'the doc'. Distinguish 의사 (the licensed physician) from 의자 'chair', which differs by a single vowel and trips up beginners constantly, and from 환자 'patient' and 간호사 'nurse', the other people in the room. The natural verb is 환자를 보다 ('see patients') or 진료를 보다/받다, never the English-style '의사를 하다'; a doctor 의사가 되다 ('becomes' a doctor), with the goal marked by 가, not 을."},sentence:{ko:"의사 선생님께 증상을 말했어요.",en:"I told the doctor my symptoms."},target:"의사",options:["symptom","hospital / clinic","doctor"],note:"의사 선생님 is common in real clinic speech."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"약사",romanization:"yaksa",en:"pharmacist",pos:"noun",nuance:"약사 (藥師) combines 藥 'medicine/drug' with the professional 師 'master' (the same dignified 師 in 의사 doctor, 교사 teacher) — literally 'a medicine-master', the licensed pharmacist who dispenses drugs and counsels on dosage at a 약국 (藥局, 'medicine bureau'). A culturally important point for English speakers: in Korea the 약사 actively recommends over-the-counter remedies, so walking into a 약국 and asking the 약사 directly is a normal first move for a headache, cold, or upset stomach — you don't always need a doctor. Keep the obvious pairs straight: 의사 (醫師) diagnoses and writes the 처방전 'prescription', then the 약사 fills it; both share 師 but they are different jobs. Also distinguish the person 약사 from 약 'medicine' and 약국 'pharmacy'. Habitual verbs: 약을 짓다 'fill/compound medicine', 약을 추천받다 'get a medicine recommended', 약사한테 물어보다. English-speaker pitfall: the vowel-final stem takes 약사예요, not 약사이에요; and don't say 약사를 만나다 for routine advice — 약사한테 물어보다 (ask the pharmacist) is the natural collocation."},sentence:{ko:"약사에게 식후에 먹는지 물어봤어요.",en:"I asked the pharmacist whether to take it after meals."},target:"약사",options:["pharmacist","symptom","hospital / clinic"],note:"Pharmacy instruction source."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"쉬다",romanization:"swida",en:"to rest",pos:"verb",nuance:"쉬다 is used for taking a break, resting, or being off work/school."},sentence:{ko:"오늘은 집에서 쉬어야 해요.",en:"You have to rest at home today."},target:"쉬어",options:["to rest","symptom","hospital / clinic"],note:"Core recovery action."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"의사",ko:"감기 때문에 그런 것 같아요. 약은 식후에 하루 세 번 드셔야 해요.",en:"It seems to be because of a cold. You have to take the medicine three times a day after meals."},hl:"식후"}],canDo:"Explain the visit with cause-result -아서/어서 and N 때문에 (아파서 왔어요, 감기 때문에)",id:"chapter-48-b5",chapterId:"chapter-48",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A/V-아서/어서",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"A/V-아서/어서",sub:"symptom causing a result",rows:[{when:"Stem has ㅏ/ㅗ vowel",add:"+ 아서",ex:"아프다 → 아파서 (because it hurts)"},{when:"Stem has other vowel",add:"+ 어서",ex:"먹다 → 먹어서 (because I ate)"},{when:"하다 words",add:"하 → 해서",ex:"심하다 → 심해서 (because it's bad / severe)"},{when:"Symptom 나다 (fever / cough)",add:"나 + 서 → 나서",ex:"열이 나서 (because I have a fever)"}],examples:[{ko:"배가 아파서 병원에 왔어요.",en:"My stomach hurt, so I came to the clinic.",romanization:"baega apaseo byeongwone wasseoyo.",note:"아프다 → 아파서; the classic reason-for-visit sentence."},{ko:"열이 나서 학교에 못 갔어요.",en:"I had a fever, so I couldn't go to school.",romanization:"yeori naseo hakgyoe mot gasseoyo.",note:"열이 나다 → 나서; a symptom leading to an inability."}],more:{func:"Link a symptom to its consequence: 'because X, Y happened.' This is your go-to for explaining the visit: 배가 아파서 왔어요 (my stomach hurt, so I came). The 아서/어서 clause is the cause; the second clause is the result.",funcLead:"Link a symptom to its consequence: 'because X, Y happened.' This is your go-to for explaining the visit: 배가 아파서 왔어요 (my stomach hurt, so I came).",keyPoint:{label:"아서/어서 vs 때문에",body:"Both mean 'because.' Use V-아서/어서 to chain a state or action straight into its result in one flowing clause. Use N 때문에 when the cause is a NOUN. With 아서/어서 you cannot put a command or suggestion in the result clause — for that use -(으)니까."},pronunciation:"",pitfall:{wrong:"열이 나서 약을 드세요.",right:"열이 나니까 약을 드세요.",explanation:"You can't end an 아서/어서 result clause with a command or suggestion. 'You have a fever, so take medicine' needs -(으)니까, not 아서/어서."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "My stomach hurt, so I came to the clinic." (배가 시작 · Start with 배가.)',tokens:["배가","아파서","병원에","왔어요."],correct:"배가 아파서 병원에 왔어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I had a fever, so I couldn't go to school." (열이 시작 · Start with 열이.)`,tokens:["열이","나서","학교에","못","갔어요."],correct:"열이 나서 학교에 못 갔어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"열이 나서 약을 드세요.",ok:!1},{text:"열이 나니까 약을 드세요.",ok:!0}],explanation:"You can't end an 아서/어서 result clause with a command or suggestion. 'You have a fever, so take medicine' needs -(으)니까, not 아서/어서."}],canDo:"Explain the visit with cause-result -아서/어서 and N 때문에 (아파서 왔어요, 감기 때문에)",id:"chapter-48-b6",chapterId:"chapter-48",index:5},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N 때문에",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N 때문에",sub:"because of (a symptom / illness)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"감기",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"때문에",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"목이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"아파요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"감기 때문에 목이 아파요.",en:"My throat hurts because of a cold.",romanization:"gamgi ttaemune mogi apayo."},{tokens:[{pre:"열",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"때문에",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"잠을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"못",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"잤어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"열 때문에 잠을 못 잤어요.",en:"I couldn't sleep because of the fever.",romanization:"yeol ttaemune jameul mot jasseoyo."}],rule:{name:"N 때문에",rows:[{when:"Cause is a noun",add:"N + 때문에",ex:"감기 → 감기 때문에 (because of a cold)"},{when:"Sentence-initial 'that's why'",add:"그래서 / 그것 때문에",ex:"그래서 왔어요 (that's why I came)"},{when:"Person as cause",add:"사람 N + 때문에",ex:"동생 때문에 (because of my sibling)"},{when:"To say 'thanks to' (positive)",add:"N + 덕분에",ex:"약 덕분에 (thanks to the medicine)"}]},more:{func:"State a NOUN cause: 'because of my cold / fever / headache.' When the reason is a noun — a named symptom or illness — use N 때문에: 감기 때문에 일을 못 했어요 (I couldn't work because of a cold).",funcLead:"State a NOUN cause: 'because of my cold / fever / headache.' When the reason is a noun — a named symptom or illness — use N 때문에: 감기 때문에 일을 못 했어요 (I couldn't wo…",keyPoint:{label:"때문에 (negative cause) vs 덕분에 (positive cause)",body:"Both attach to a noun. 때문에 marks a cause, usually neutral or negative (감기 때문에 — because of a cold). 덕분에 means 'thanks to' and is positive (약 덕분에 나았어요 — I got better thanks to the medicine)."},pronunciation:"",pitfall:{wrong:"아파서 때문에 병원에 왔어요.",right:"아파서 병원에 왔어요. / 통증 때문에 병원에 왔어요.",explanation:"때문에 attaches to a NOUN only. You cannot stack a verb-form (아파서) in front of 때문에. Either use 아파서 alone, or put a noun like 통증 (pain) before 때문에."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I couldn't work because of a headache.",sentence:"두통 ___ 일을 못 했어요.",options:[{text:"해요",ok:!1},{text:"아서",ok:!1},{text:"때문에",ok:!0}],explanation:"두통 (headache) noun + 때문에."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I can't sleep because of the cough.",sentence:"기침 ___ 잠을 못 자요.",options:[{text:"해요",ok:!1},{text:"아서",ok:!1},{text:"때문에",ok:!0}],explanation:"기침 (cough) noun + 때문에; a line straight from the clinic."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "My throat hurts because of a cold." (감기 시작 · Start with 감기.)',tokens:["감기","때문에","목이","아파요."],correct:"감기 때문에 목이 아파요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I couldn't sleep because of the fever." (열 시작 · Start with 열.)`,tokens:["열","때문에","잠을","못","잤어요."],correct:"열 때문에 잠을 못 잤어요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Say what a symptom kept me from doing with 못 + V (잠을 못 잤어요)",id:"chapter-48-b7",chapterId:"chapter-48",index:6},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A/V-(으)니까",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"A/V-(으)니까",sub:"since this is so, do this (advice)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"열이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"계속",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"나",mid:"니까",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"물을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"많이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"마시세요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"열이 계속 나니까 물을 많이 마시세요.",en:"Since the fever keeps up, drink a lot of water.",romanization:"yeori gyesok nanikka mureul mani masiseyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"약을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"먹었",mid:"으니까",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"좀",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"쉬세요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"약을 먹었으니까 좀 쉬세요.",en:"Since you've taken the medicine, get some rest.",romanization:"yageul meogeosseunikka jom swiseyo."}],rule:{name:"A/V-(으)니까",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"+ 으니까",ex:"먹다 → 먹으니까 (since you eat / ate)"},{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ 니까",ex:"아프다 → 아프니까 (since it hurts)"},{when:"ㄹ stem (ㄹ drops)",add:"ㄹ 빠짐 + 니까",ex:"만들다 → 만드니까 (since you make it)"},{when:"나다 (fever / cough)",add:"나 + 니까 → 나니까",ex:"열이 나니까 (since you have a fever)"}]},more:{func:"Give advice or instruction grounded in a fact: 'since X is the case, do / let's do Y.' Unlike 아서/어서, the second clause CAN be a command or suggestion — perfect for a doctor's advice: 열이 나니까 약을 드세요 (since you have a fever, take the medicine).",funcLead:"Give advice or instruction grounded in a fact: 'since X is the case, do / let's do Y.' Unlike 아서/어서, the second clause CAN be a command or suggestion — perfect…",keyPoint:{label:"니까 lets you give a command; 아서/어서 doesn't",body:"The big difference from 아서/어서 is the second clause. With -(으)니까 the result can be a command (-세요), a suggestion (-아요/-(으)ㅂ시다), or a request. So whenever a doctor's 'because…, do this' sentence ends in an instruction, you must use 니까, not 아서/어서."},pronunciation:"",pitfall:{wrong:"열이 나서 약을 드세요.",right:"열이 나니까 약을 드세요.",explanation:"Both 아서/어서 and 니까 mean 'because,' so learners reach for 아서/어서 by habit. But when the result is a command or suggestion (약을 드세요), only -(으)니까 is grammatical."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Since your throat hurts, drink warm water.",sentence:"목이 아프___ 따뜻한 물을 드세요.",options:[{text:"니까",ok:!0},{text:"으니까",ok:!1}],explanation:"아프다 → 아프니까; advice based on a stated fact."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Since the clinic is closed today, let's go tomorrow.",sentence:"오늘은 병원이 문을 닫았___ 내일 가요.",options:[{text:"해요",ok:!1},{text:"으니까",ok:!0},{text:"아서",ok:!1}],explanation:"닫다 → 닫았으니까; result is a suggestion (가요), which 니까 permits."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Since the fever keeps up, drink a lot of water."',tokens:["열이","계속","나니까","물을","많이","마시세요."],correct:"열이 계속 나니까 물을 많이 마시세요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Since you've taken the medicine, get some rest."`,tokens:["약을","먹었으니까","좀","쉬세요."],correct:"약을 먹었으니까 좀 쉬세요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Say what hurts with body part + 이/가 아프다 (목이 아파요)",id:"chapter-48-b8",chapterId:"chapter-48",index:7},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · 열이 나다 / 기침이 나다",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"열이 나다 / 기침이 나다",sub:"symptom 'comes out' with 나다",rows:[{when:"Symptom noun ends in a consonant",add:"+ 이 나다",ex:"열 → 열이 나요 (I have a fever)"},{when:"Symptom noun ends in a vowel",add:"+ 가 나다",ex:"재채기 → 재채기가 나요"},{when:"Cough",add:"기침 + 이 나다",ex:"기침이 나요 (I have a cough)"},{when:"Past tense (had the symptom)",add:"나 → 났어요",ex:"어제 열이 났어요 (I had a fever yesterday)"}],examples:[{ko:"아침부터 열이 나요.",en:"I've had a fever since this morning.",romanization:"achimbuteo yeori nayo.",note:"열 + 이 나다; 아침부터 (since this morning) sets the start time."},{ko:"기침이 나고 콧물도 나요.",en:"I have a cough and a runny nose too.",romanization:"gichimi nago konmuldo nayo.",note:"기침이 나다 + 콧물이 나다 chained with -고; the classic cold combo."}],more:{func:"Describe symptoms that arise — fever, cough, runny nose — with the symptom noun + 이/가 + 나다. This is the most common way to list these cold symptoms at the clinic; forms such as 열이 있어요 can also describe their presence.",funcLead:"Describe symptoms that arise — fever, cough, runny nose — with the symptom noun + 이/가 + 나다.",keyPoint:{label:"나다 symptoms vs 아프다 pain",body:"Symptoms that 'come out' — 열(fever), 기침(cough), 콧물(runny nose) — use 이/가 나다. A body part that aches — 머리, 목, 배 — uses 이/가 아프다. Mixing them up (열이 아파요 ✗, 머리가 나요 ✗) is the giveaway, so memorize which noun pairs with which verb.",label2:""},pronunciation:"",pitfall:{wrong:"열이 있어요 is always wrong.",right:"열이 나요. / 열도 있어요?",explanation:"열이 나요 is the most useful default when reporting that a fever has developed. 열이 있어요 or 열도 있어요? can naturally describe or ask about the presence of a fever, especially during a medical check. Teach the preferred symptom pairing without banning the presence expression."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I've had a fever since this morning." (아침부터 시작 · Start with 아침부터.)`,tokens:["아침부터","열이","나요."],correct:"아침부터 열이 나요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I have a cough and a runny nose too."',tokens:["기침이","나고","콧물도","나요."],correct:"기침이 나고 콧물도 나요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"열이 나요. / 열도 있어요?",ok:!0},{text:"열이 있어요 is always wrong.",ok:!1}],explanation:"열이 나요 is the most useful default when reporting that a fever has developed. 열이 있어요 or 열도 있어요? can naturally describe or ask about the presence of a fever, especially during a medical check. Teach the preferred symptom pairing without banning the presence expression."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"빈칸을 채우세요 · Complete the form — Symptom noun ends in a consonant",sentence:"열___",options:[{text:"이 나요",ok:!0},{text:"가 나요",ok:!1}],explanation:"열 → 열이 나요 (I have a fever)"}],canDo:"Describe a symptom 'coming out' with 이/가 나다 (열이 나요, 기침이 나요)",id:"chapter-48-b9",chapterId:"chapter-48",index:8},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · 못 + V",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"못 + V",sub:"can't do it (symptom blocking an action)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"기침",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"때문에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"잠을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"못",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"자요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"기침 때문에 잠을 못 자요.",en:"I can't sleep because of the cough.",romanization:"gichim ttaemune jameul mot jayo."},{tokens:[{pre:"열이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"나서",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"학교에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"못",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"갔어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"열이 나서 학교에 못 갔어요.",en:"I had a fever, so I couldn't go to school.",romanization:"yeori naseo hakgyoe mot gasseoyo."}],rule:{name:"못 + V",rows:[{when:"Plain verb",add:"못 + 동사",ex:"자다 → 못 자요 (can't sleep)"},{when:"Past tense",add:"못 + V-았/었어요",ex:"가다 → 못 갔어요 (couldn't go)"},{when:"하다 verb (못 goes inside)",add:"N + 못 + 해요",ex:"일하다 → 일을 못 해요 (can't work)"},{when:"Written / formal alternative",add:"V + -지 못해요",ex:"자다 → 자지 못해요 (cannot sleep)"}]},more:{func:"Say you were unable to do something because of a symptom: 'I couldn't ___.' Put 못 right before the verb: 잠을 못 자요 (I can't sleep), 학교에 못 갔어요 (I couldn't go to school). This is how you describe what the illness stopped you from doing.",funcLead:"Say you were unable to do something because of a symptom: 'I couldn't ___.' Put 못 right before the verb: 잠을 못 자요 (I can't sleep), 학교에 못 갔어요 (I couldn't go to s…",keyPoint:{label:"못 = couldn't (blocked); 안 = didn't (chose not to)",body:"못 means you were UNABLE — something (a symptom) prevented you: 못 잤어요 (couldn't sleep). 안 means you simply didn't / chose not to: 안 잤어요 (didn't sleep). At the clinic you almost always want 못, because the illness is what stopped you."},pronunciation:"",pitfall:{wrong:"잠을 못 안 자요.",right:"잠을 못 자요.",explanation:"Don't stack 못 and 안 together — pick one. Use 못 for 'unable to' (a symptom blocked you). Also keep 못 directly before the verb (못 자요), and for 하다 verbs split the noun off: 일을 못 해요, not 못 일해요."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"My throat hurts, so I can't eat.",sentence:"목이 아파서 밥을 ___ 먹어요.",options:[{text:"못",ok:!0},{text:"해요",ok:!1},{text:"아서",ok:!1}],explanation:"못 + 먹어요; a common reason given at the clinic."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I couldn't work yesterday.",sentence:"어제 일을 ___ 했어요.",options:[{text:"해요",ok:!1},{text:"못",ok:!0},{text:"아서",ok:!1}],explanation:"일하다 splits: 일을 못 했어요 — 못 goes before 하다."},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I can't sleep because of the cough." (기침 시작 · Start with 기침.)`,tokens:["기침","때문에","잠을","못","자요."],correct:"기침 때문에 잠을 못 자요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I had a fever, so I couldn't go to school." (열이 시작 · Start with 열이.)`,tokens:["열이","나서","학교에","못","갔어요."],correct:"열이 나서 학교에 못 갔어요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Explain the visit with cause-result -아서/어서 and N 때문에 (아파서 왔어요, 감기 때문에)",id:"chapter-48-b10",chapterId:"chapter-48",index:9},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A clinic visit moves from symptoms to medicine instructions and follow-up advice.",lines:[{speaker:"의사",ko:"어디가 아파서 오셨어요?",en:"Where does it hurt? What brings you in?",romanization:"eodiga apaseo osyeosseoyo?"},{speaker:"환자",ko:"아침부터 열이 나고 목이 아파요. 기침도 나요.",en:"Since this morning I have had a fever and a sore throat. I also have a cough.",romanization:"achimbuteo yeori nago mogi apayo. gichimdo nayo."},{speaker:"의사",ko:"감기 때문에 그런 것 같아요. 약은 식후에 하루 세 번 드셔야 해요.",en:"It seems to be because of a cold. You have to take the medicine three times a day after meals.",romanization:"gamgi ttaemune geureon geot gatayo. yageun sikhue haru se beon deusyeoya haeyo."},{speaker:"환자",ko:"기침 때문에 잠을 못 자면 다시 와야 하나요?",en:"If I cannot sleep because of the cough, should I come back?",romanization:"gichim ttaemune jameul mot jamyeon dasi waya hanayo?"},{speaker:"의사",ko:"네. 열이 계속 나니까 물을 많이 마시고 쉬세요.",en:"Yes. Since the fever is continuing, drink a lot of water and rest.",romanization:"ne. yeori gyesok nanikka mureul mani masigo swiseyo."},{speaker:"환자",ko:"네. 처방전을 받고 약국에 가서 약을 기다릴게요.",en:"Okay. I will take the prescription, go to the pharmacy, and wait for the medicine.",romanization:"ne. cheobangjeoneul batgo yakguge gaseo yageul gidarilgeyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I have a fever and a cough.'",tokens:["열이 나고","기침이 나요"],correct:"열이 나고 기침이 나요",explanation:"Korean uses 나다 for fever and cough symptoms. -고 links the two symptoms naturally."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"누가 한 말일까요? · Who said this?",sentence:"감기 때문에 그런 것 같아요. 약은 식후에 하루 세 번 드셔야 해요.",options:[{text:"의사",ok:!0},{text:"환자",ok:!1}],explanation:"It seems to be because of a cold. You have to take the medicine three times a day after meals."},{kind:"drill",prompt:'무엇이 들어갈까요? · "Since this morning I have had a fever and a sore throat. I also have a cough."',sentence:"아침부터 열이 나고 목이 아파요. ___도 나요.",options:[{text:"증상",ok:!1},{text:"기침",ok:!0},{text:"병원",ok:!1}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Say what hurts with body part + 이/가 아프다 (목이 아파요)",id:"chapter-48-b11",chapterId:"chapter-48",index:10},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 약은 식후에 하루 세 번 드세요",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"약은 식후에 하루 세 번 드세요",chunks:["민수는 아침부터 열이 나고 목이 아파서 동네 병원에 갔어요. 의사 선생님은 감기 때문에 그런 것 같다고 말했어요. 기침이 심해지는 바람에 어젯밤에는 잠도 거의 못 잤어요.","민수는 처방전을 받아서 약국에 갔고, 약사에게 복약 방법을 들었어요. 약은 식후에 하루 세 번 먹어야 하고, 열이 계속 나니까 물을 많이 마시고 쉬어야 해요. 증상이 나아지지 않으면 다시 병원에 와야 해요."],translation:"Minsu had a fever and sore throat from the morning, so he went to a neighborhood clinic. The doctor said it seemed to be because of a cold. Because his cough got worse, he barely slept the night before. Minsu got a prescription and went to the pharmacy, where he heard the medicine instructions from the pharmacist. He has to take the medicine three times a day after meals, and since the fever is continuing, he has to drink a lot of water and rest. If the symptoms do not improve, he has to go back to the clinic.",qas:[{q:"민수는 왜 병원에 갔어요?",a:"열이 나고 목이 아파서 병원에 갔어요."},{q:"기침이 심해지는 바람에 무엇을 못 했어요?",a:"잠을 거의 못 잤어요."},{q:"약은 언제, 얼마나 먹어야 해요?",a:"식후에 하루 세 번 먹어야 해요."}]},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"민수는 아침부터 열이 나고 목이 아파서 동네 ___에 갔어요.",options:[{text:"병원",ok:!0},{text:"배",ok:!1},{text:"쉬다",ok:!1}],explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"의사 선생님은 ___ 때문에 그런 것 같다고 말했어요.",options:[{text:"배",ok:!1},{text:"쉬다",ok:!1},{text:"감기",ok:!0}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Describe a symptom 'coming out' with 이/가 나다 (열이 나요, 기침이 나요)",id:"chapter-48-b12",chapterId:"chapter-48",index:11},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which symptom phrase is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"열이 나고 목이 아파요.",ok:!0},{text:"열을 하고 목이 있어요.",ok:!1},{text:"열에게 목이 아파요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"열이 나다 and 목이 아프다 are natural symptom frames."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence names a symptom as the cause?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"기침 때문에 잠을 못 잤어요.",ok:!0},{text:"기침이 때문에 잠을 못 잤어요.",ok:!1},{text:"기침으로 때문에 잠을 못 잤어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"N 때문에 attaches directly to the noun."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which instruction means 'You have to take the medicine after meals'?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"약은 식후에 드셔야 해요.",ok:!0},{text:"약은 식후에 드실 리가 없어요.",ok:!1},{text:"약은 식후에 드시더라고요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"식후에 gives timing; 드셔야 해요 gives a necessary instruction."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence blames worsening symptoms for a bad night's sleep?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"기침이 심해지는 바람에 잠을 못 잤어요.",ok:!0},{text:"기침이 심해지는 덕분에 잠을 못 잤어요.",ok:!1},{text:"기침이 심해지는 중에 잠을 못 자야 해요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"는 바람에 fits an unwanted cause that disrupted sleep."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence reports a current pharmacy action?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"약국에서 약을 기다리는 중이에요.",ok:!0},{text:"약국에서 약을 기다렸어요.",ok:!1},{text:"약국에서 약 때문에 기다리니까요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"V-는 중이에요 reports an action in progress."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"목___ 아파요. (My throat hurts.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"이",ok:!0},{text:"을",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"아프다 describes the body part that hurts, so 목 takes 이. 목을 아파요 is a common learner error."}],canDo:"Explain the visit with cause-result -아서/어서 and N 때문에 (아파서 왔어요, 감기 때문에)",id:"chapter-48-b13",chapterId:"chapter-48",index:12}]},{id:"chapter-49",number:49,title:"N이/가 있어요 · N이/가 없어요",goal:"Ask about 보증금, 월세, 관리비, and what is included.",biteCount:13,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 원룸~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"원룸",romanization:"wollum",en:"studio apartment",pos:"noun",nuance:"원룸 literally comes from 'one room', but in Korean it is a housing category, not just any one room. It usually means a small self-contained studio with a bathroom and kitchenette."},sentence:{ko:"이 원룸은 보증금이 얼마예요?",en:"How much is the deposit for this studio?"},target:"원룸",options:["studio apartment","real estate; real-estate office","deposit"],note:"Core real-estate viewing noun."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"부동산",romanization:"budongsan",en:"real estate; real-estate office",pos:"noun",nuance:"When Koreans say 부동산에 가요, they usually mean the neighborhood office, not an abstract real-estate market. For the person, say 공인중개사 or 중개사."},sentence:{ko:"집을 구하기 위해 부동산에 갔어요.",en:"I went to a real-estate office to find a place."},target:"부동산",options:["studio apartment","real estate; real-estate office","deposit"],note:"In daily speech, 부동산 often means the office."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"보증금",romanization:"bojeunggeum",en:"deposit",pos:"noun",nuance:"보증금 is not rent. It is a deposit paid upfront and normally returned after the lease, depending on the contract and room condition."},sentence:{ko:"보증금은 500만 원이에요.",en:"The deposit is 5 million won."},target:"보증금",options:["studio apartment","deposit","real estate; real-estate office"],note:"Must be asked together with 월세."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"월세",romanization:"wolse",en:"monthly rent",pos:"noun",nuance:"월세 is the monthly rent payment. 관리비 and utilities may or may not be included, so ask 따로예요? and 포함돼 있어요?"},sentence:{ko:"월세는 70만 원이에요.",en:"The monthly rent is 700,000 won."},target:"월세",options:["studio apartment","real estate; real-estate office","monthly rent"],note:"Monthly payment, separate from deposit."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"전세",romanization:"jeonse",en:"large-deposit lease",pos:"noun",nuance:"전세 is not simply 'deposit'. The deposit itself is 보증금; 전세 is the lease type built around a very large deposit."},sentence:{ko:"전세보다 월세 원룸을 찾고 있어요.",en:"I am looking for a monthly-rent studio rather than jeonse."},target:"전세",options:["large-deposit lease","studio apartment","real estate; real-estate office"],note:"Lease type contrast."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"손님",ko:"이 원룸은 보증금이 얼마예요?",en:"How much is the deposit for this studio?"},hl:"원룸"}],canDo:"Say what a home has or lacks with N이/가 있어요/없어요 (주차장이 없어요)",id:"chapter-49-b1",chapterId:"chapter-49",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N이/가 있어요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N이/가 있어요",sub:"saying what a home has",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"집에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"방이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"세",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"개",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"있어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"이 집에 방이 세 개 있어요.",en:"This house has three rooms.",romanization:"i jibe bangi se gae isseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"우리",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"아파트에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"엘리베이터",mid:"가",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"있어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"우리 아파트에 엘리베이터가 있어요.",en:"Our apartment has an elevator.",romanization:"uri apateue ellibeiteoga isseoyo."}],rule:{name:"N이/가 있어요",rows:[{when:"Noun ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"+ 이 있어요",ex:"방 → 방이 있어요 (there is a room)"},{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"+ 가 있어요",ex:"엘리베이터 → 엘리베이터가 있어요 (there is an elevator)"},{when:"Yes/no question",add:"있어요? (rising tone)",ex:"주차장이 있어요? (Is there parking?)"}]},more:{func:"Use 있어요 to say a room or feature exists in a home. It is the everyday way to describe what an apartment or house comes with: a balcony, an elevator, parking, and so on.",funcLead:"Use 있어요 to say a room or feature exists in a home.",keyPoint:{label:"있어요 vs 이에요/예요",body:"있어요 means 'there is / it has' (existence). 이에요/예요 means 'it is' (identity). 방이 있어요 = 'there is a room'; 방이에요 = 'it is a room.' For listing what a home has, you almost always want 있어요."},pronunciation:"있어요 links the 받침 ㅆ into the next vowel: [이써요]. So 방이 있어요 runs together as [방이 이써요].",pitfall:{wrong:"이 집은 방 있어요.",right:"이 집은 방이 있어요.",explanation:"English drops the subject marker ('the house has rooms'), but Korean normally needs 이/가 on the thing that exists. Don't omit the particle before 있어요 in careful speech."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"There is a big supermarket near the house.",sentence:"집 근처에 큰 마트___.",options:[{text:"있어요",ok:!1},{text:"가 있어요",ok:!0}],explanation:"있어요 also covers 'there is X nearby,' a common housing detail."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Is there a balcony?",sentence:"베란다___?",options:[{text:"이 있어요",ok:!1},{text:"가 있어요",ok:!0}],explanation:"A rising tone turns the statement into a yes/no question renters actually ask."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "This house has three rooms." (이 시작 · Start with 이.)',tokens:["이","집에","방이","세","개","있어요."],correct:"이 집에 방이 세 개 있어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Our apartment has an elevator." (우리 시작 · Start with 우리.)',tokens:["우리","아파트에","엘리베이터가","있어요."],correct:"우리 아파트에 엘리베이터가 있어요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Say what a home has or lacks with N이/가 있어요/없어요 (주차장이 없어요)",id:"chapter-49-b2",chapterId:"chapter-49",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 관리비~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"관리비",romanization:"gwallibi",en:"maintenance fee",pos:"noun",nuance:"관리비 is Sino-Korean: 管理費, literally 管理 'management' + 費 'expense', i.e. 'management/maintenance fee'. It is the monthly building-upkeep charge covering shared costs — cleaning, security (경비), the elevator (엘리베이터), corridor lighting, and very often part of the utilities (수도세, 난방비, sometimes 인터넷) — and crucially it is billed ON TOP OF 월세 (monthly rent), which blindsides many newcomers. Always ask what the 관리비 covers and whether it's 포함 (included) or 별도 (別途, 'separate'), because a temptingly cheap 월세 can hide a fat 관리비. Like other recurring charges it 나오다 (comes out) and you 내다 (pay) it. Pitfalls for English speakers: first, do not confuse 관리비 (the monthly, non-refundable maintenance fee) with 보증금 (保證金, the large refundable deposit you get back when you leave) — they are completely different money. Second, do not assume rent already includes it the way some Western 'all-bills-included' rents do; in Korea 관리비 is usually a separate line. Third, distinguish 관리비 (shared building costs) from the individually-metered 공과금 like personal 전기세 — though some 관리비 bills bundle a portion of utilities, so reading the 관리비 고지서 (statement) itemization matters."},sentence:{ko:"관리비는 따로예요?",en:"Is the maintenance fee separate?"},target:"관리비",options:["maintenance fee","studio apartment","real estate; real-estate office"],note:"Core cost question."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"따로",romanization:"ttaro",en:"separately",pos:"adverb",nuance:"따로 is not only about money, but housing learners need it for separate fees and separate payments."},sentence:{ko:"관리비는 따로예요?",en:"Is the maintenance fee separate?"},target:"따로",options:["separately","studio apartment","real estate; real-estate office"],note:"Small word that changes total cost."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"포함되다",romanization:"pohamdoeda",en:"to be included",pos:"verb",nuance:"포함되다 focuses on what is included in a price or category. Do not use it like English 'include' with the fee as active subject unless you restructure the sentence."},sentence:{ko:"인터넷이 관리비에 포함돼 있어요.",en:"Internet is included in the maintenance fee."},target:"포함돼",options:["to be included","to sign / make a contract","to move house"],note:"Included item is the subject."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"계약서",romanization:"gyeyakseo",en:"contract document",pos:"noun",nuance:"계약서 is the document, while 계약하다 is the action of making/signing the contract. Do not blur them when asking questions."},sentence:{ko:"이사하기 전에 계약서를 확인해 두세요.",en:"Check the contract before moving."},target:"계약서",options:["studio apartment","real estate; real-estate office","contract document"],note:"Prepared action before signing or moving."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"계약하다",romanization:"gyeyakada",en:"to sign / make a contract",pos:"verb",nuance:"계약하다 is broader than housing, but in this chapter it should stay tied to real-estate contracts. 계약서를 확인하다 happens before 계약하다."},sentence:{ko:"오늘 계약할 수 있어요.",en:"You can sign the contract today."},target:"계약할",options:["to be included","to move house","to sign / make a contract"],note:"Real-estate action after checking the listing."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"손님",ko:"관리비는 따로예요? 인터넷도 포함돼 있어요?",en:"Is the maintenance fee separate? Is internet included too?"},hl:"관리비"}],canDo:"Describe a home with adjective + noun (넓은 방, 깨끗한 집, 조용한 동네)",id:"chapter-49-b3",chapterId:"chapter-49",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N이/가 없어요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N이/가 없어요",sub:"saying a home lacks something",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"방에는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"창문",mid:"이",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"없어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"이 방에는 창문이 없어요.",en:"This room has no window.",romanization:"i bangeneun changmuni eopseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"건물에는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"엘리베이터",mid:"가",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"없어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"이 건물에는 엘리베이터가 없어요.",en:"This building has no elevator.",romanization:"i geonmureneun ellibeiteoga eopseoyo."}],rule:{name:"N이/가 없어요",rows:[{when:"Noun ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"+ 이 없어요",ex:"창문 → 창문이 없어요 (there is no window)"},{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"+ 가 없어요",ex:"엘리베이터 → 엘리베이터가 없어요 (there is no elevator)"},{when:"Yes/no question",add:"없어요? (rising tone)",ex:"엘리베이터가 없어요? (Is there no elevator?)"}]},more:{func:"없어요 is the negative of 있어요: it says a feature does NOT exist. In housing talk it covers a missing room, no parking, no elevator, or 'there is no extra fee.'",funcLead:"없어요 is the negative of 있어요: it says a feature does NOT exist.",keyPoint:{label:"있어요 ↔ 없어요",body:"These are a fixed pair, not 있어요 + 안. To say a home lacks something, use 없어요, never 안 있어요. 방이 있어요 (has a room) ↔ 방이 없어요 (has no room)."},pronunciation:"없어요 links the cluster ㅄ so only ㅅ carries over: [업써요]. So 창문이 없어요 sounds like [창무니 업써요].",pitfall:{wrong:"이 집은 주차장이 안 있어요.",right:"이 집은 주차장이 없어요.",explanation:"There is no '안 있어요.' The negative of 있어요 is the separate word 없어요. Reach for 없어요 directly instead of negating 있어요 with 안."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"There's no extra cost besides the deposit.",sentence:"보증금 외에 추가 비용___.",options:[{text:"이 없어요",ok:!0},{text:"가 없어요",ok:!1}],explanation:"A reassuring rental phrase: 없어요 = 'there is no (extra fee).'"},{kind:"drill",prompt:"There are no empty rooms right now.",sentence:"지금은 빈방___.",options:[{text:"가 없어요",ok:!1},{text:"이 없어요",ok:!0}],explanation:"빈방 'empty room' + 이 없어요 = a common reply from a landlord."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "This room has no window." (이 시작 · Start with 이.)',tokens:["이","방에는","창문이","없어요."],correct:"이 방에는 창문이 없어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "This building has no elevator." (이 시작 · Start with 이.)',tokens:["이","건물에는","엘리베이터가","없어요."],correct:"이 건물에는 엘리베이터가 없어요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Describe a home with adjective + noun (넓은 방, 깨끗한 집, 조용한 동네)",id:"chapter-49-b4",chapterId:"chapter-49",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 거실~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"거실",romanization:"geosil",en:"living room",pos:"noun",nuance:"거실 is the shared 'living/common' room of a Korean home — the social hub where the family relaxes together and where guests are received, almost always centred on a 소파 and a TV. It is Sino-Korean: 居室, literally 居 'to dwell/reside' + 室 'room', so the feel is 'the room you live in (together)'. Contrast it with 응접실 (應接室, a formal parlour for receiving visitors), which sounds old-fashioned and upper-class today, and with 방, the generic word for any closed 'room/bedroom'; 거실 specifically names the open central area. Register is fully neutral — children and adults alike say 거실. The big English-speaker pitfall is location particles: because a TV simply sits there you say 거실에 텔레비전이 있어요 (에 = existence), but because you *do* something there you switch to 거실에서 쉬어요 (에서 = action). Mixing these up (거실에서 텔레비전이 있어요) is the single most common error."},sentence:{ko:"거실이 넓어요.",en:"The living room is spacious."},target:"거실",options:["studio apartment","living room","real estate; real-estate office"],note:"Layout noun."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"부엌",romanization:"bueok",en:"kitchen",pos:"noun",nuance:"부엌 is the warm, native-Korean everyday word for the room where you cook and wash dishes — the heart of the home where the smell of food lives. Its nearest synonym is the Sino-Korean 주방 (廚房, 'cooking-room'): 주방 is the neutral, technical label you see on apartment floor plans, restaurant signs, and in compounds like 주방용품 (kitchen goods), whereas 부엌 carries a homier, more personal feeling — 우리 집 부엌 sounds natural, 우리 집 주방 sounds like a brochure. Both are everyday and polite; there's no status caution. It collocates with 요리하다, 치우다, and 부엌 살림. The classic English-speaker pitfall is the final consonant: it ends in ㅋ (부엌), so the spelling is fixed even though it's pronounced [부억] before a pause — never write 부억. The second trap is particle choice: use 부엌에서 for an action happening there, but 부엌에 for where something simply sits (부엌에 냉장고가 있어요)."},sentence:{ko:"부엌에 냉장고가 있어요.",en:"There is a refrigerator in the kitchen."},target:"부엌",options:["kitchen","studio apartment","real estate; real-estate office"],note:"Appliance/location sentence."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"화장실",romanization:"hwajangsil",en:"bathroom / restroom",pos:"noun",nuance:"화장실 is the normal public word for bathroom/restroom."},sentence:{ko:"깨끗한 화장실이 중요해요.",en:"A clean bathroom is important."},target:"화장실",options:["studio apartment","bathroom / restroom","real estate; real-estate office"],note:"Inspection detail."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"공인중개사",romanization:"gonginjunggaesa",en:"licensed real estate agent",pos:"noun",nuance:"공인중개사 (公認仲介士) is a long but transparent compound: 公認 'officially recognized' (公 public + 認 acknowledge) + 仲介 'mediation/brokerage' (仲 go-between + 介 intermediary) + 士 certified professional — so literally 'an officially-licensed brokerage professional', the agent who arranges 전세, 월세, and home sales, and the person you inevitably deal with when renting in Korea. The number-one English-speaker confusion is place vs person: 부동산 (不動産, lit. 'immovable property') colloquially names the agent's office or the property itself, so 부동산에 가다 = 'go to the agent's office', but the human is the 공인중개사 — never call the person 부동산. The 공인 'officially licensed' prefix matters in formal/contractual contexts; dropping it to just 중개사 is loose. In everyday speech people also warmly say 중개사님 or 사장님 to the agent's face. Collocations: 공인중개사 사무소 'realtor office', 공인중개사 자격증 'realtor license', 집을 구하다 'find a home'. Note 공인중개사 ends in a vowel, so it would be 공인중개사예요, not 공인중개사이에요."},sentence:{ko:"공인중개사에게 관리비를 물어봤어요.",en:"I asked the licensed agent about the maintenance fee."},target:"공인중개사",options:["studio apartment","licensed real estate agent","deposit"],note:"The person helping with the contract."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"이사하다",romanization:"isahada",en:"to move house",pos:"verb",nuance:"이사하다 specifically means relocating your residence — changing the home you live in — built from Sino-Korean 移徙 (移 'shift', 徙 'relocate') plus 하다. Contrast it sharply with 옮기다, which is just physically moving an object from one spot to another (책상을 옮기다 'move the desk'); you never use 옮기다 for changing homes, and you never use 이사하다 for shifting furniture. It splits into 이사 가다 (move away, focus on departure) and 이사 오다 (move in, focus on arrival near the speaker). Being a 하다-verb, the negative wraps the noun: 이사 안 해요 / 이사 못 해요, NOT 안 이사해요. The biggest English-speaker pitfall is treating it like English 'move' as a transitive verb — Koreans don't say '집을 이사해요'; the home is implied, so just 이사해요 is complete."},sentence:{ko:"다음 달에 이사할 수 있어요.",en:"I can move next month."},target:"이사할",options:["to be included","to sign / make a contract","to move house"],note:"Moving schedule verb."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"손님",ko:"이사하기 전에 계약서를 확인해 두면 되죠?",en:"I should check the contract before moving, right?"},hl:"이사하"}],canDo:"State a housing condition with -(으)면 (싸면 계약할 거예요)",id:"chapter-49-b5",chapterId:"chapter-49",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A-(으)ㄴ N",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"A-(으)ㄴ N",sub:"describing a home (넓은 방)",rows:[{when:"Adjective stem ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"+ 은 + N",ex:"넓다 → 넓은 방 (a spacious room)"},{when:"Adjective stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ ㄴ + N",ex:"깨끗하다 → 깨끗한 집 (a clean house)"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem (drop ㄹ, add ㄴ)",add:"ㄹ→ㄴ + N",ex:"멀다 → 먼 곳 (a faraway place)"}],examples:[{ko:"넓은 방을 찾고 있어요.",en:"I'm looking for a spacious room.",romanization:"neolbeun bangeul chatgo isseoyo.",note:"넓다 (consonant stem) → 넓은 modifies 방; the adjective comes before the noun."},{ko:"깨끗한 집이 좋아요.",en:"I like a clean house.",romanization:"kkaekkeutan jibi joayo.",note:"하다-adjective 깨끗하다 → 깨끗한 (drop the vowel-ending 하, add ㄴ)."}],more:{func:"Turn a descriptive adjective into a word that modifies a noun, like 'a spacious room' or 'a clean house.' This is how you describe homes: 넓은 방, 깨끗한 집, 조용한 동네.",funcLead:"Turn a descriptive adjective into a word that modifies a noun, like 'a spacious room' or 'a clean house.' This is how you describe homes: 넓은 방, 깨끗한 집, 조용한 동네.",keyPoint:{label:"넓은 방 vs 방이 넓어요",body:"Modifier form goes BEFORE the noun: 넓은 방 ('a spacious room' — naming a thing). Predicate form ends the sentence: 방이 넓어요 ('the room is spacious' — stating a fact). Use 넓은 방 when the adjective is labeling the noun."},pronunciation:"넓다 is pronounced [널따], and 넓은 links the ㄼ cluster as [널븐]. 가까운 and 깨끗한 link smoothly.",pitfall:{wrong:"나는 넓어요 방을 원해요.",right:"나는 넓은 방을 원해요.",explanation:"Don't put a sentence-ending form in the modifier slot. 넓어요 ends a sentence; to attach it to 방 you must change it to the modifier form 넓은. English uses 'wide' in both spots, but Korean changes the ending."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I'm looking for a spacious room."`,tokens:["넓은","방을","찾고","있어요."],correct:"넓은 방을 찾고 있어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I like a clean house."',tokens:["깨끗한","집이","좋아요."],correct:"깨끗한 집이 좋아요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"나는 넓은 방을 원해요.",ok:!0},{text:"나는 넓어요 방을 원해요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Don't put a sentence-ending form in the modifier slot. 넓어요 ends a sentence; to attach it to 방 you must change it to the modifier form 넓은. English uses 'wide' in both spots, but Korean changes the ending."}],canDo:"State a housing condition with -(으)면 (싸면 계약할 거예요)",id:"chapter-49-b6",chapterId:"chapter-49",index:5},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A/V-(으)면",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"A/V-(으)면",sub:"stating a housing condition",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"방이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"너무",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"비싸",mid:"면",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"다른",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"집을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"볼게요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"방이 너무 비싸면 다른 집을 볼게요.",en:"If the room is too expensive, I'll look at another place.",romanization:"bangi neomu bissamyeon dareun jibeul bolgeyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"지하철역에서",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가까우",mid:"면",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"좋아요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"지하철역에서 가까우면 좋아요.",en:"It'd be nice if it's close to the subway station.",romanization:"jihacheoryeogeseo gakkaumyeon joayo."}],rule:{name:"A/V-(으)면",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"+ 으면",ex:"좋다 → 좋으면 (if it's good)"},{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ 면",ex:"비싸다 → 비싸면 (if it's expensive)"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem (keep ㄹ, add 면)",add:"+ 면",ex:"멀다 → 멀면 (if it's far)"}]},more:{func:"-(으)면 means 'if / when.' Use it to set a condition about a home: 'if it's too expensive...', 'if I like the place...', 'when I sign the contract...'",funcLead:"-(으)면 means 'if / when.' Use it to set a condition about a home: 'if it's too expensive...', 'if I like the place...', 'when I sign the contract...'",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)면 vs -아/어서",body:"-(으)면 is a hypothetical 'if/when' — the result depends on the condition. -아/어서 gives an actual reason that already holds. 비싸면 안 사요 = 'if it's expensive, I won't buy.' 비싸서 안 사요 = 'I'm not buying because it's (actually) expensive.'"},pronunciation:"After a 받침, 으면 links: 좋으면 sounds like [조으면] (ㅎ weakens between vowels). The ㄹ-stem 들면 is simply [들면].",pitfall:{wrong:"집이 마음에 들으면 계약할 거예요.",right:"집이 마음에 들면 계약할 거예요.",explanation:"ㄹ-final stems do NOT add 으; they keep ㄹ and add 면 directly: 들다 → 들면. Learners over-apply 으면 to every consonant ending, but ㄹ is special here."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"When you sign the contract, you pay the deposit.",sentence:"계약하___ 보증금을 내요.",options:[{text:"있어요",ok:!1},{text:"없어요",ok:!1},{text:"면",ok:!0}],explanation:"계약하다 → 계약하면; here -(으)면 means 'when/once' for a routine step."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"If I like the house, I'll sign the contract.",sentence:"집이 마음에 들___ 계약할 거예요.",options:[{text:"면",ok:!0},{text:"있어요",ok:!1},{text:"없어요",ok:!1}],explanation:"들다 is ㄹ-final → 마음에 들면 (keep ㄹ, add 면); 마음에 들다 = 'to like.'"},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "If the room is too expensive, I'll look at another place."`,tokens:["방이","너무","비싸면","다른","집을","볼게요."],correct:"방이 너무 비싸면 다른 집을 볼게요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "It'd be nice if it's close to the subway station." (지하철역에서 시작 · Start with 지하철역에서.)`,tokens:["지하철역에서","가까우면","좋아요."],correct:"지하철역에서 가까우면 좋아요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Say where I live and where I'm moving with N에 살아요 / N(으)로 이사 가요",id:"chapter-49-b7",chapterId:"chapter-49",index:6},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-(으)ㄹ 수 있어요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-(으)ㄹ 수 있어요",sub:"what's possible/allowed at home",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"여기에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"주차할",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"수",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"있어요",post:"?",hit:!0}],ko:"여기에 주차할 수 있어요?",en:"Can I park here?",romanization:"yeogie juchahal su isseoyo?"},{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"집에서",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"강아지를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"키울",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"수",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"있어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"이 집에서 강아지를 키울 수 있어요.",en:"You can keep a dog in this house.",romanization:"i jibeseo gangajireul kiul su isseoyo."}],rule:{name:"V-(으)ㄹ 수 있어요",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"+ 을 수 있어요",ex:"살다 → 살 수 있어요 (can live)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ ㄹ 수 있어요",ex:"주차하다 → 주차할 수 있어요 (can park)"},{when:"Negative 'cannot'",add:"(으)ㄹ 수 없어요",ex:"키우다 → 키울 수 없어요 (cannot keep a pet)"}]},more:{func:"Express that something is possible or allowed in a home: you can park, you can keep a pet, you can move in next week. The opposite, 'cannot,' is V-(으)ㄹ 수 없어요.",funcLead:"Express that something is possible or allowed in a home: you can park, you can keep a pet, you can move in next week.",keyPoint:{label:"있어요 → 없어요 for 'cannot'",body:"To make V-(으)ㄹ 수 있어요 negative, swap 있어요 for 없어요, not 안: 살 수 있어요 (can live) → 살 수 없어요 (cannot live). The 수 (way/possibility) stays; only the existence verb flips.",drill:{instruction:"Make 'can do' with V-(으)ㄹ 수 있어요.",model:"주차하다 → 주차할 수 있어요",items:["살다 → ____","키우다 → ____","이사하다 → ____"]}},pronunciation:"수 있어요 is pronounced [수 이써요], and the ㄹ before 수 stays light: 살 수 → [살 쑤] (the ㅅ tenses to ㅆ after the ㄹ).",pitfall:{wrong:"여기에서 주차 안 할 수 있어요.",right:"여기에서 주차할 수 없어요.",explanation:"To say 'cannot park,' don't insert 안 into the can-phrase. The negative of (으)ㄹ 수 있어요 is (으)ㄹ 수 없어요. 안 주차하다 would mean 'choose not to park,' which is different from 'not allowed/able to park.'"}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Can I park here?" (여기에 시작 · Start with 여기에.)',tokens:["여기에","주차할","수","있어요?"],correct:"여기에 주차할 수 있어요?",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "You can keep a dog in this house." (이 시작 · Start with 이.)',tokens:["이","집에서","강아지를","키울","수","있어요."],correct:"이 집에서 강아지를 키울 수 있어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"여기에서 주차할 수 없어요.",ok:!0},{text:"여기에서 주차 안 할 수 있어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"To say 'cannot park,' don't insert 안 into the can-phrase. The negative of (으)ㄹ 수 있어요 is (으)ㄹ 수 없어요. 안 주차하다 would mean 'choose not to park,' which is different from 'not allowed/able to park.'"}],canDo:"Say what a home has or lacks with N이/가 있어요/없어요 (주차장이 없어요)",id:"chapter-49-b8",chapterId:"chapter-49",index:7},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N에 살아요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N에 살아요",sub:"saying where you live",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"저는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"부산",mid:"에",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"살아요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"저는 부산에 살아요.",en:"I live in Busan.",romanization:"jeoneun busane sarayo."},{tokens:[{pre:"지금",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"원룸",mid:"에",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"살아요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"지금 원룸에 살아요.",en:"I live in a studio apartment right now.",romanization:"jigeum wollume sarayo."}],rule:{name:"N에 살아요",rows:[{when:"Place + 에 살아요",add:"N + 에 살아요",ex:"부산에 살아요 (I live in Busan)"},{when:"Building/room type",add:"N + 에 살아요",ex:"아파트에 살아요 (I live in an apartment)"},{when:"Floor (N층)",add:"숫자 + 층에 살아요",ex:"3층에 살아요 (I live on the 3rd floor)"}]},more:{func:"Use 에 + 살아요 to say where you live: in a city, a neighborhood, an apartment, or on a particular floor. 에 marks the place of residence.",funcLead:"Use 에 + 살아요 to say where you live: in a city, a neighborhood, an apartment, or on a particular floor.",keyPoint:{label:"에 살아요 vs 에서 + action",body:"For living (a state), use 에: 서울에 살아요. For an action that happens at a place, use 에서: 집에서 일해요 ('I work at home'). With 살다 specifically, Korean uses 에, not 에서."},pronunciation:"살아요 links the ㄹ into the next vowel: [사라요]. 부산에 살아요 runs together as [부사네 사라요].",pitfall:{wrong:"저는 부산에서 살아요.",right:"저는 부산에 살아요.",explanation:"With 살다 (to live/reside), the standard particle is 에, not 에서. English 'live in/at' tempts learners toward 에서, but residence as a state takes 에."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I live in Busan." (저는 시작 · Start with 저는.)',tokens:["저는","부산에","살아요."],correct:"저는 부산에 살아요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I live in a studio apartment right now." (지금 시작 · Start with 지금.)',tokens:["지금","원룸에","살아요."],correct:"지금 원룸에 살아요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"저는 부산에 살아요.",ok:!0},{text:"저는 부산에서 살아요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"With 살다 (to live/reside), the standard particle is 에, not 에서. English 'live in/at' tempts learners toward 에서, but residence as a state takes 에."}],canDo:"Describe a home with adjective + noun (넓은 방, 깨끗한 집, 조용한 동네)",id:"chapter-49-b9",chapterId:"chapter-49",index:8},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N(으)로 이사 가요/와요",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"N(으)로 이사 가요/와요",sub:"moving house",rows:[{when:"Place ends in a consonant (받침)",add:"+ 으로 이사 가요",ex:"부산 → 부산으로 이사 가요"},{when:"Place ends in a vowel or ㄹ",add:"+ 로 이사 가요",ex:"제주 → 제주로… / 서울 → 서울로…"},{when:"Moving here (toward speaker)",add:"+ (으)로 이사 와요",ex:"새집으로 이사 와요 (move into a new house)"}],examples:[{ko:"다음 달에 새집으로 이사 가요.",en:"I'm moving to a new house next month.",romanization:"daeum dare saejibeuro isa gayo.",note:"새집 'new house' + 으로 (받침 ㅂ) + 이사 가요; (으)로 marks the destination."},{ko:"부산으로 이사 갈 거예요.",en:"I'm going to move to Busan.",romanization:"busaneuro isa gal geoyeyo.",note:"부산 ends in ㄴ → 으로; 이사 갈 거예요 = future 'will move.'"}],more:{func:"Use (으)로 to mark the destination you move TO when changing homes: 이사 가다 'to move (away to)' and 이사 오다 'to move (here to).' (으)로 means 'to / toward.'",funcLead:"Use (으)로 to mark the destination you move TO when changing homes: 이사 가다 'to move (away to)' and 이사 오다 'to move (here to).' (으)로 means 'to / toward.'",keyPoint:{label:"이사 가요 vs 이사 와요",body:"이사 가다 = move away (the speaker is not at the destination). 이사 오다 = move here (toward the speaker's location). 서울로 이사 가요 = 'I'm moving to Seoul'; 여기로 이사 왔어요 = 'I moved here.'"},pronunciation:"이사 가요 is [이사 가요]; 으로 links its ㄹ into 이사: 새집으로 → [새지브로]. 이사 왔어요 sounds like [이사 와써요].",pitfall:{wrong:"저는 새집에 이사 가요.",right:"저는 새집으로 이사 가요.",explanation:"For the destination of a move, Korean prefers (으)로 ('toward/to') with 이사 가다, not 에. English 'move to a new house' suggests 에, but the directional (으)로 is the natural choice here."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I'm moving to a new house next month." (다음 시작 · Start with 다음.)`,tokens:["다음","달에","새집으로","이사","가요."],correct:"다음 달에 새집으로 이사 가요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I'm going to move to Busan."`,tokens:["부산으로","이사","갈","거예요."],correct:"부산으로 이사 갈 거예요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"저는 새집에 이사 가요.",ok:!1},{text:"저는 새집으로 이사 가요.",ok:!0}],explanation:"For the destination of a move, Korean prefers (으)로 ('toward/to') with 이사 가다, not 에. English 'move to a new house' suggests 에, but the directional (으)로 is the natural choice here."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"빈칸을 채우세요 · Complete the form — Place ends in a consonant (받침)",sentence:"부산___",options:[{text:"으로 이사 가요",ok:!0},{text:"로…",ok:!1}],explanation:"부산 → 부산으로 이사 가요"}],canDo:"State a housing condition with -(으)면 (싸면 계약할 거예요)",id:"chapter-49-b10",chapterId:"chapter-49",index:9},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A tenant checks rent, deposit, utilities, and contract preparation at a real-estate office.",lines:[{speaker:"손님",ko:"이 원룸은 보증금이 얼마예요?",en:"How much is the deposit for this studio?",romanization:"i wollumeun bojeunggeumi eolmayeyo?"},{speaker:"중개사",ko:"보증금은 500만 원이고 월세는 70만 원이에요.",en:"The deposit is 5 million won and the monthly rent is 700,000 won.",romanization:"bojeunggeumeun 500man wonigo wolseneun 70man wonieyo."},{speaker:"손님",ko:"관리비는 따로예요? 인터넷도 포함돼 있어요?",en:"Is the maintenance fee separate? Is internet included too?",romanization:"gwallibineun ttaroyeyo? inteonesdo pohamdwae isseoyo?"},{speaker:"중개사",ko:"관리비는 7만 원이고 인터넷은 포함돼 있어요.",en:"The maintenance fee is 70,000 won, and internet is included.",romanization:"gwallibineun 7man wonigo inteones-eun pohamdwae isseoyo."},{speaker:"손님",ko:"이사하기 전에 계약서를 확인해 두면 되죠?",en:"I should check the contract before moving, right?",romanization:"isahagi jeone gyeyakseoreul hwaginhae dumyeon doejyo?"},{speaker:"중개사",ko:"네. 마음에 들면 오늘 계약할 수 있어요.",en:"Yes. If you like it, you can sign the contract today.",romanization:"ne. maeume deulmyeon oneul gyeyakal su isseoyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'Is internet included?'",tokens:["인터넷이","포함돼요?"],correct:"인터넷이 포함돼요?",explanation:"인터넷이 is the subject of being included. 포함돼요? is the natural real-estate question."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"누가 한 말일까요? · Who said this?",sentence:"보증금은 500만 원이고 월세는 70만 원이에요.",options:[{text:"손님",ok:!1},{text:"중개사",ok:!0}],explanation:"The deposit is 5 million won and the monthly rent is 700,000 won."},{kind:"drill",prompt:'무엇이 들어갈까요? · "How much is the deposit for this studio?"',sentence:"이 원룸은 ___이 얼마예요?",options:[{text:"부동산",ok:!1},{text:"월세",ok:!1},{text:"보증금",ok:!0}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Say what a home has or lacks with N이/가 있어요/없어요 (주차장이 없어요)",id:"chapter-49-b11",chapterId:"chapter-49",index:10},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 관리비에 인터넷이 포함돼 있어요?",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"관리비에 인터넷이 포함돼 있어요?",chunks:["하나는 이사하기 위해 부동산에 갔어요. 공인중개사는 넓은 원룸 하나를 보여 줬어요. 보증금은 500만 원이고 월세는 70만 원이었어요.","하나는 관리비가 따로인지, 인터넷이 관리비에 포함돼 있는지 물어봤어요. 베란다는 없었지만 부엌에 냉장고와 세탁기가 있었고 화장실도 깨끗했어요. 마음에 들면 오늘 계약할 수 있다고 해서, 하나는 계약하기 전에 계약서를 다시 확인해 두었어요."],translation:"Hana went to a real-estate office to move. The licensed agent showed her a spacious studio. The deposit was 5 million won and the monthly rent was 700,000 won. Hana asked whether the maintenance fee was separate and whether internet was included in the fee. There was no balcony, but there was a refrigerator and washing machine in the kitchen, and the bathroom was clean. Since she could sign the contract that day if she liked it, Hana checked the contract again before signing.",qas:[{q:"하나는 왜 부동산에 갔어요?",a:"이사하기 위해 부동산에 갔어요."},{q:"하나가 확인한 비용 두 가지는 뭐예요?",a:"보증금과 월세를 확인했어요."},{q:"계약하기 전에 무엇을 확인해 두었어요?",a:"계약서를 다시 확인해 두었어요."}]},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"하나는 이사하기 위해 ___에 갔어요.",options:[{text:"전세",ok:!1},{text:"거실",ok:!1},{text:"부동산",ok:!0}],explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"___는 넓은 원룸 하나를 보여 줬어요.",options:[{text:"전세",ok:!1},{text:"공인중개사",ok:!0},{text:"거실",ok:!1}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Describe a home with adjective + noun (넓은 방, 깨끗한 집, 조용한 동네)",id:"chapter-49-b12",chapterId:"chapter-49",index:11},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"At a real-estate office, which pair belongs together?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"보증금 500만 원 / 월세 70만 원",ok:!0},{text:"관리비 500만 원 / 월세 70만 원",ok:!1},{text:"매매가 500만 원 / 전세금 70만 원",ok:!1}],explanation:"보증금 and 월세 are core housing price terms."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which question checks whether a fee is separate?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"관리비는 따로예요?",ok:!0},{text:"관리비는 같이 나와요?",ok:!1},{text:"관리비는 보증금 먹어요?",ok:!1}],explanation:"따로 means separately and is essential for fee questions."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence asks whether internet is included?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"인터넷이 관리비에 포함돼 있어요?",ok:!0},{text:"인터넷을 관리비가 포함해요?",ok:!1},{text:"인터넷을 관리비에 포함돼 있어요?",ok:!1}],explanation:"The included item is the subject, and 관리비 takes 에."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Before moving, the prepared-action sentence is:",sentence:null,options:[{text:"계약서를 확인해 두세요.",ok:!0},{text:"계약서를 확인할 리가 없어요.",ok:!1},{text:"계약서를 확인하는군요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-아/어 두세요 means do it in advance and leave it prepared."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence uses possibility naturally?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"오늘 계약할 수 있어요.",ok:!0},{text:"오늘 계약할 리가 없어요.",ok:!1},{text:"오늘 계약서 때문에 포함돼요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Vㄹ/을 수 있어요 expresses practical possibility."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"관리비___ 포함돼요? (Is the maintenance fee included?)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"가",ok:!0},{text:"를",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"포함되다 is passive/included, so 관리비 is the subject and takes 가. 를 would fit an active verb like 포함하다."}],canDo:"State a housing condition with -(으)면 (싸면 계약할 거예요)",id:"chapter-49-b13",chapterId:"chapter-49",index:12}]},{id:"chapter-50",number:50,title:"A/V-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 · V/A-(으)ㄹ 텐데",goal:"Use 날씨, 일기예보, 비, 눈, 태풍, 미세먼지, and 우산 in forecast-based plan talk.",biteCount:11,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 날씨~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"날씨",romanization:"nalssi",en:"weather",pos:"noun",nuance:"날씨 is the pure-Korean word for the overall feel of the weather right now and is the single most common small-talk opener in Korea. Distinguish it from the Sino-Korean 기온(氣溫, 'air-temperature'), which is the measured number, and from 일기(日氣) / 기상(氣象), which are bookish words used in 일기예보 ('weather forecast'). You almost always pair 날씨 with the descriptive adjectives 좋다, 나쁘다, 춥다, 덥다, 맑다, 흐리다. The English-speaker pitfall is double: first, English says 'the weather IS cold' so learners reach for 이다, but Korean needs the adjective verb directly — 날씨가 추워요, never 날씨가 춥다이에요. Second, when stating a plain fact about the weather, default to the subject particle 가 (날씨가 좋아요), not the topic 는; 날씨는 좋아요 sounds like you are contrasting the weather against something else that is bad."},sentence:{ko:"오늘 날씨가 어때요?",en:"How is the weather today?"},target:"날씨",options:["weather","rain","snow"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-073."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"일기예보",romanization:"ilgiyebo",en:"weather forecast",pos:"noun",nuance:"일기예보 is the official weather forecast you get from the news or an app (日氣豫報, literally 일기 'the day's air = weather' + 예보 'fore-report'), so it names the prediction itself, not the weather. Contrast it with plain 날씨, which is the actual weather happening now: you say 날씨가 좋아요 for today's nice weather but 일기예보가 좋아요 only when the predicted outlook is good. Koreans habitually 일기예보를 보다 (watch/read it) and judge it as 맞다 right or 틀리다 wrong, and they quote it with 일기예보에서 ~고 했어요. The English-speaker pitfall is treating it as a verb ('to forecast') — it is a noun, so attach a verb like 보다 or 확인하다, and use 에서 (the source particle) rather than 에 when reporting what it announced."},sentence:{ko:"주말 일기예보 봤어요?",en:"Did you see the weekend forecast?"},target:"일기예보",options:["rain","snow","weather forecast"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-2002."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"맑다",romanization:"malkda",en:"to be clear; sunny",pos:"adjective",nuance:"맑다 means CLEAR or CLEAN in the sense of pure and transparent — clear see-through water (맑은 물), a cloudless sky (맑은 하늘 = sunny), fresh unpolluted air (맑은 공기), a clear ringing sound or a clear alert mind (정신이 맑다). Its opposite is 흐리다 (cloudy/murky). The key English-speaker pitfall is using 맑다 for a tidy ROOM: English 'clean' covers both purity and tidiness, but a clean, tidy room is 깨끗하다 (방이 깨끗하다), not 맑다. Reserve 맑다 for natural clearness/transparency (water, sky, air, sound) and use 깨끗하다 for cleanliness/neatness of objects and spaces. As a weather word it pairs with 날씨 and 하늘 and is the standard term in forecasts: 내일은 맑겠습니다 (it will be clear/sunny tomorrow). Pronunciation: the ㄺ surfaces as g before a vowel, so 맑아요 is 'malg-a-yo' and 맑게 is 'mak-ge.' As a descriptive adjective it never takes 있다: 물이 맑아요, not 맑아 있어요."},sentence:{ko:"날씨가 맑아질 때 다시 예약해요.",en:"Let's book again when the weather clears."},target:"맑아",options:["to be cloudy; overcast","weather","to be clear; sunny"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1279."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"흐리다",romanization:"heurida",en:"to be cloudy; overcast",pos:"adjective",nuance:"흐리다 is the all-purpose word for 'unclear,' covering CLOUDY weather (흐린 날 an overcast day), MURKY water (흐린 물), and BLURRY/out-of-focus images or hazy memories (사진이 흐리다, 기억이 흐리다). Its opposite is 맑다 (clear), and for weather these two are the everyday pair: 맑아요 (clear/sunny) ↔ 흐려요 (cloudy). The English-speaker pitfall is reaching for 흐리다 to mean DIRTY: murky water that's hard to see through is 흐리다, but water or a surface that is dirty/filthy is 더럽다 — 흐리다 is about lack of CLARITY, not presence of grime. Note also a near-synonym for blurriness: 뿌옇다/희미하다 also describe a hazy, faint look, but 흐리다 is the plain default learners should master first. The conjugation trap: like other ㅣ-stems, 흐리다 contracts in the 해요-form to 흐려요 (not 흐리어요), past 흐렸어요. As a descriptive adjective it does not take 있다: 하늘이 흐려요, not 흐려 있어요."},sentence:{ko:"내일은 흐릴 것 같아요.",en:"I think it will be cloudy tomorrow."},target:"흐릴",options:["to be clear; sunny","to be cloudy; overcast","weather"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1280."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"비",romanization:"bi",en:"rain",pos:"noun",nuance:"Korean has no single verb meaning 'to rain'; instead precipitation 'arrives', so you say 비가 와요 (rain comes) using 오다. A more formal, written variant is 비가 내리다 ('rain falls'), favored in forecasts and lyrics, while 비가 오다 dominates everyday speech. To say rain stops, use the dedicated verb 그치다 (비가 그쳤어요), not 끝나다. Note 비 also forms many compounds: 소나기 (sudden shower), 장마 (the summer monsoon rains), 가랑비 (drizzle). The big English-speaker pitfall is inventing a verb: learners say 비가 해요 or 비해요 by analogy with English 'it rains', but 하다 never combines with 비 — the natural pairing is strictly 비가 오다 / 비가 내리다. A second trap is 비를 맞다 ('to take the rain on your body', i.e. get drenched), which uses 맞다, not 받다."},sentence:{ko:"비가 올 것 같아요.",en:"I think it will rain."},target:"비",options:["weather","weather forecast","rain"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-074."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"눈",romanization:"nun",en:"snow",pos:"noun",nuance:"눈 can mean snow or eye, and context normally makes the meaning clear. Some dictionaries mark a traditional vowel-length distinction, but many modern speakers do not preserve it consistently, so do not rely on pitch or length alone."},sentence:{ko:"눈이 올 때 길이 미끄러워요.",en:"When it snows, the roads are slippery."},target:"눈",options:["weather","weather forecast","snow"],note:"Linked to weather card word-ext-075, not the body-part card for eye."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"오다",romanization:"oda",en:"to come; to fall for rain/snow",pos:"verb",nuance:"오다 is from the speaker’s point of view. If someone moves toward you or your place, 오다 is usually right."},sentence:{ko:"비가 오고 눈도 와요.",en:"It rains and it also snows."},target:"오고",options:["to cancel","weather","to come; to fall for rain/snow"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-verb-004."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"주말 일기예보 봤어요?",en:"Did you see the weekend forecast?"},hl:"일기예보"}],canDo:"Make a soft weather guess from what I see (비가 올 것 같아요)",id:"chapter-50-b1",chapterId:"chapter-50",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A/V-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"A/V-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",sub:`"I think it'll..." (a soft guess about the weather)`,lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"오늘",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"오후에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"비가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"올",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"것",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"같아요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"오늘 오후에 비가 올 것 같아요.",en:"I think it's going to rain this afternoon.",romanization:"oneul ohue biga ol geot gatayo."},{tokens:[{pre:"하늘을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"보니까",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"눈이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"올",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"것",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"같아요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"하늘을 보니까 눈이 올 것 같아요.",en:"Looking at the sky, I think it's going to snow.",romanization:"haneureul bonikka nuni ol geot gatayo."}],rule:{name:"A/V-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel (오다)",add:"-ㄹ 것 같아요",ex:"오다 → 올 것 같아요 (it looks like it'll rain)"},{when:"Verb/adjective stem ends in a consonant (맑다, 좋다)",add:"-을 것 같아요",ex:"맑다 → 맑을 것 같아요 (I think it'll be clear)"},{when:"ㅂ-irregular adjective (춥다 → 추우-, 덥다 → 더우-)",add:"-ㄹ 것 같아요 (on the 우 form)",ex:"춥다 → 추울 것 같아요 (I think it'll be cold)"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem (얼다, 불다) — ㄹ stays, no extra 으",add:"-ㄹ 것 같아요",ex:"불다 → 불 것 같아요 (it looks like the wind will blow)"}]},more:{func:`Use -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 to make a soft prediction about the weather from signs you can see — clouds, a dark sky, a dropping temperature. It is the everyday way to say "I think it's going to rain / snow / be cold." Because it is a guess, not a fact, it sounds careful and polite rather than absolute.`,funcLead:"Use -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 to make a soft prediction about the weather from signs you can see — clouds, a dark sky, a dropping temperature.",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 vs. -아/어요",body:"-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 is a guess about something not yet certain (비가 올 것 같아요 = I think it'll rain), while plain 비가 와요 states it as a present fact (it's raining). Reach for 것 같아요 when you're reading the signs, not reporting what's already true."},pronunciation:'것 같아요 is pronounced [걷 까타요]: the final ㅅ of 것 becomes a [t] stop, and the following ㄱ tenses to [ㄲ], so it sounds like "geot kkatayo."',pitfall:{wrong:"비가 올 거예요 같아요.",right:"비가 올 것 같아요.",explanation:'English speakers try to glue "will" (-ㄹ 거예요) onto "seems" (같아요). In Korean the single chunk is -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 — the noun 것, not the sentence-ending 거예요, attaches before 같아요.'}}},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I think it's going to rain this afternoon." (오늘 시작 · Start with 오늘.)`,tokens:["오늘","오후에","비가","올","것","같아요."],correct:"오늘 오후에 비가 올 것 같아요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Looking at the sky, I think it's going to snow."`,tokens:["하늘을","보니까","눈이","올","것","같아요."],correct:"하늘을 보니까 눈이 올 것 같아요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"비가 올 거예요 같아요.",ok:!1},{text:"비가 올 것 같아요.",ok:!0}],explanation:'English speakers try to glue "will" (-ㄹ 거예요) onto "seems" (같아요). In Korean the single chunk is -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 — the noun 것, not the sentence-ending 거예요, attaches before 같아요.'}],canDo:"Make a soft weather guess from what I see (비가 올 것 같아요)",id:"chapter-50-b2",chapterId:"chapter-50",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 태풍~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"태풍",romanization:"taepung",en:"typhoon",pos:"noun",nuance:"태풍 is a typhoon, the large tropical storm system that strikes Korea mainly in late summer and early autumn (颱風, literally 태 'typhoon' + 풍 'wind'). Even though the second character means wind, 태풍 is conceived as a moving body of weather, so it travels with 오다 come, 지나가다 pass, and the news verb 북상하다 move north — it does not 'blow.' That is the key contrast with plain 바람 wind, which 불다 blows: you must not say 태풍이 불어요. Distinguish it too from 폭풍 (a general violent storm, including at sea) and 돌풍 (a sudden gust); 태풍 specifically names the seasonal tropical cyclone, each one given an official name. Strength is described with 강하다/세다. The English-speaker pitfall is mapping 'the wind blows' onto 태풍 and producing 태풍이 불어요 — say instead 태풍이 와요 / 태풍이 지나가요."},sentence:{ko:"태풍이 올 것 같아서 비행기가 늦을 수도 있어요.",en:"It looks like a typhoon may come, so the plane might be late."},target:"태풍",options:["typhoon","weather","weather forecast"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-2004."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"황사",romanization:"hwangsa",en:"yellow dust",pos:"noun",nuance:"황사 is Sino-Korean, 黃沙 'yellow + sand', and names the seasonal haze of desert sand carried on the wind from China and Mongolia, peaking in spring. The crucial contrast is with 미세먼지 ('fine dust', fine particulate air pollution): 황사 is coarse natural desert sand, a centuries-old phenomenon, whereas 미세먼지 is man-made fine pollution measured as PM10/PM2.5 — news reports list them together but they are different things, and conflating them is the classic learner error. Note also that 황사 is described with 심하다 (severe) and the wind verb 불다/불어오다, not with 많다 or 오다. The English-speaker pitfall, beyond the 황사/미세먼지 mix-up, is reaching for 황사가 와요; in news-register Korean 황사가 심해요 or 황사가 불어와요 sounds far more natural."},sentence:{ko:"오늘은 황사가 심해요.",en:"The yellow dust is bad today."},target:"황사",options:["weather","yellow dust","weather forecast"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-816."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"미세먼지",romanization:"misemeonji",en:"fine dust; air pollution particles",pos:"noun",nuance:"미세먼지 is not ordinary dust in your room. It refers to air pollution particles reported in forecasts and apps."},sentence:{ko:"미세먼지가 심하면 일정은 취소하는 게 좋겠어요.",en:"If the fine dust is bad, it would be better to cancel the plan."},target:"미세먼지",options:["weather","weather forecast","fine dust; air pollution particles"],note:"Fine dust often changes outdoor plans in Korea."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"장마",romanization:"jangma",en:"rainy season",pos:"noun",nuance:"장마 is Korea's long early-summer monsoon, the roughly three-to-four-week stretch around late June and July when a stationary rain front parks over the peninsula and brings steady, humid rain day after day. It is a whole climatic period, not a single rainfall, so it contrasts sharply with 비 (one instance of rain) and with 소나기 (a sudden short downpour): you can have 비 during 장마, but 장마 names the season. The word is treated as native Korean (it is not a hanja compound), and it lives in fixed phrases — 장마가 시작되다/끝나다 and the compound 장마철 'rainy-season time'. The pitfall for English speakers is calling any rainy day 장마; reserve it for the seasonal monsoon, and note that 장마 takes 시작되다/끝나다 rather than the falling verbs 오다/내리다 that plain rain uses."},sentence:{ko:"장마라서 비가 많이 올 텐데 우산 챙기세요.",en:"It is rainy season, so it will probably rain a lot. Bring an umbrella."},target:"장마",options:["weather","weather forecast","rainy season"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-2003."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"영하",romanization:"yeongha",en:"below zero",pos:"noun",nuance:"영하(零下) is Sino-Korean — 零 'zero' + 下 'below' — meaning below 0°C. Its exact opposite is 영상(零上, 'above zero'), used for plus temperatures (영상 3도 = +3°C). The crucial structural fact, opposite to English, is word order: the marker comes BEFORE the number, so 'minus five degrees' is 영하 5도 (literally 'below-zero five degrees'), never 5도 영하. English speakers also say 'negative five' / 'minus five,' but Korean does not use 마이너스 in weather speech — it is always 영하. 영하 most often appears in winter forecasts and pairs with 떨어지다 (plunge below) and 내려가다 (drop), plus the handy forecaster word 영하권 (the subzero range). The pitfalls: (1) putting the number first (5도 영하 ✗); (2) confusing 영하 (below zero) with 영상 (above zero) — they look and sound similar but are opposites; (3) saying 마이너스 5도 in everyday weather talk, which sounds like a math/English calque rather than natural 영하 5도."},sentence:{ko:"기온이 영하로 떨어질 텐데 따뜻하게 입으세요.",en:"The temperature will probably drop below zero, so dress warmly."},target:"영하",options:["below zero","weather","weather forecast"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1615."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"기온",romanization:"gion",en:"air temperature",pos:"noun",nuance:"기온 is the air temperature expressed as a number of degrees (氣溫, 기 'air/atmosphere' + 온 'warmth'), so it is the measurable reading the forecaster gives, whereas 날씨 is the whole overall feel of the weather (sunny, humid, windy) and 온도 is the neutral word for the temperature of any object or liquid like 물 온도 water temperature or 체온 body temperature. Use 기온 only for outdoor air. It pairs with 높다 high and 낮다 low, and with the motion verbs 오르다 rise and 떨어지다 fall; the big English-speaker trap is reaching for 크다/작다 (big/small) the way English lets you say a 'big' difference, but a temperature itself is 높다/낮다, and only the gap 일교차 or 차이 can be 크다."},sentence:{ko:"기온이 떨어질 것 같아요.",en:"I think the temperature will drop."},target:"기온",options:["weather","weather forecast","air temperature"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-2001."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"소나기",romanization:"sonagi",en:"sudden shower",pos:"noun",nuance:"소나기 is a pure native Korean word for a brief, intense burst of rain that arrives and departs suddenly — the classic summer-afternoon downpour. The dividing line from plain 비 is duration and character: 비 is rain in general (steady, all-day, or light), while 소나기 is specifically short-lived and abrupt, so Koreans typically wait it out under an awning rather than buy an umbrella. It contrasts too with 장맛비 (the long monsoon rains) and 이슬비 (drizzle). Natural partners are 내리다/오다, 그치다 (the proper verb for rain stopping — not 끝나다), and 만나다 ('get caught in'). The English-speaker pitfall is using 소나기 for any rain: it must be brief and sudden, and remember it takes the ordinary rain verbs 오다/내리다, with no special verb of its own."},sentence:{ko:"오후에 소나기가 올 수도 있어요.",en:"There may be a sudden shower in the afternoon."},target:"소나기",options:["weather","sudden shower","weather forecast"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-817."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"태풍이 올 것 같아서 비행기가 늦을 수도 있어요.",en:"It looks like a typhoon may come, so the plane might be late."},hl:"태풍"}],canDo:"Forecast a likely situation and add advice (추울 텐데 우산을 가져오세요)",id:"chapter-50-b3",chapterId:"chapter-50",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V/A-(으)ㄹ 텐데",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V/A-(으)ㄹ 텐데",sub:`"it'll probably... so" (a likely situation plus concern)`,lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"밖에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"추울",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"텐데",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"코트를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"입으세요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"밖에 추울 텐데 코트를 입으세요.",en:"It'll probably be cold outside, so please wear a coat.",romanization:"bakke chuul tende koteureul ibeuseyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"오후에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"비가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"올",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"텐데",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"우산을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가져가세요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"오후에 비가 올 텐데 우산을 가져가세요.",en:"It'll probably rain in the afternoon, so take an umbrella.",romanization:"ohue biga ol tende usaneul gajyeogaseyo."}],rule:{name:"V/A-(으)ㄹ 텐데",rows:[{when:"Verb/adjective, vowel-ending stem (오다)",add:"-ㄹ 텐데",ex:"오다 → 올 텐데 (it'll probably rain, so...)"},{when:"Verb/adjective, consonant-ending stem (좋다, 많다)",add:"-을 텐데",ex:"많다 → 많을 텐데 (there'll probably be a lot, so...)"},{when:"ㅂ-irregular adjective (춥다 → 추우-, 덥다 → 더우-)",add:"-ㄹ 텐데 (on the 우 form)",ex:"춥다 → 추울 텐데 (it'll probably be cold, so...)"},{when:"Past situation (already likely happened)",add:"-았/었을 텐데",ex:"얼다 → 얼었을 텐데 (it must have frozen, so...)"}]},more:{func:`Use -(으)ㄹ 텐데 to predict a likely situation and then add worry, advice, or a suggestion about it. It is the warm "it'll probably rain, so (take an umbrella)" pattern — you forecast something and immediately care about its effect on the listener. The 텐데 part leaves the sentence half-open, inviting a follow-up clause.`,funcLead:"Use -(으)ㄹ 텐데 to predict a likely situation and then add worry, advice, or a suggestion about it.",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄹ 텐데 vs. -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",body:"Both can present an expectation. -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 comfortably ends after the guess: 비가 올 것 같아요. -(으)ㄹ 텐데 often leads into a consequence, concern, or advice: 비가 올 텐데 우산을 가져가세요. In real conversation it can also end the turn by itself when the missing thought is obvious, such as 비가 올 텐데… ('But it may rain…')."},pronunciation:'올 텐데 links smoothly into one breath group — "ol-ten-de," no pause — so the forecast flows straight into the advice. 텐데 itself is read [텐데] as written.',pitfall:{wrong:"Sentence-final -(으)ㄹ 텐데 is always incomplete and wrong.",right:"비가 올 텐데 우산을 가져가세요. / 비가 올 텐데…",explanation:"Include the following clause when you need to state the advice clearly. In conversation, a final 텐데 can deliberately leave concern, hesitation, or disagreement unstated because the listener can infer it. The trailing form is a real conversational tool, not a grammar error."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"It'll probably snow a lot, so drive carefully.",sentence:"눈이 많이 올 ___ 운전 조심하세요.",options:[{text:"텐데",ok:!0},{text:"으면",ok:!1},{text:"때문에",ok:!1}],explanation:"Shows 텐데 carrying genuine concern (조심하세요) about a weather outcome."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"It'll probably be hot tomorrow, so drink a lot of water.",sentence:"내일은 더울 ___ 물을 많이 드세요.",options:[{text:"으면",ok:!1},{text:"텐데",ok:!0},{text:"때문에",ok:!1}],explanation:"덥다 → 더우- + ㄹ 텐데; summer-heat advice, everyday seasonal vocabulary."},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "It'll probably be cold outside, so please wear a coat." (밖에 시작 · Start with 밖에.)`,tokens:["밖에","추울","텐데","코트를","입으세요."],correct:"밖에 추울 텐데 코트를 입으세요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "It'll probably rain in the afternoon, so take an umbrella." (오후에 시작 · Start with 오후에.)`,tokens:["오후에","비가","올","텐데","우산을","가져가세요."],correct:"오후에 비가 올 텐데 우산을 가져가세요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Forecast a likely situation and add advice (추울 텐데 우산을 가져오세요)",id:"chapter-50-b4",chapterId:"chapter-50",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 안개~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"안개",romanization:"angae",en:"fog",pos:"noun",nuance:"안개 is fog or mist — the cloud of tiny water droplets near the ground that cuts visibility. Its defining grammar is the verb 끼다, the same verb used for clouds 구름이 끼다, meaning the haze 'settles in,' so 안개가 끼다 is the standard way to say it gets foggy and 안개가 걷히다 that it lifts. Density is expressed with 짙다 thick and 옅다 thin — never the physical-thickness word 두껍다 — and a thick fog is 짙은 안개. 안개 is a native Korean noun (no hanja), and a close relative is 안개비 'misty drizzle.' The crucial pitfall for English speakers is the verb: do not say 안개가 와요 (treating fog like rain) and do not say 안개가 두꺼워요 by translating 'thick fog' literally; say 안개가 끼다 and 안개가 짙다."},sentence:{ko:"안개 때문에 앞이 잘 안 보여요.",en:"I cannot see well ahead because of the fog."},target:"안개",options:["weather","weather forecast","fog"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-2006."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"우산",romanization:"usan",en:"umbrella",pos:"noun",nuance:"우산 is Sino-Korean 雨傘, literally 雨 'rain' + 傘 'umbrella/parasol' — so the word transparently means 'rain-umbrella', which is your built-in reminder it's for rain. The verb is the deceptively busy 쓰다: to put up/use an umbrella is 우산을 쓰다, the same 쓰다 that covers wearing a hat (모자를 쓰다) and glasses (안경을 쓰다) — Korean groups 'things over your head/face' under 쓰다, distinct from 입다 (clothes on the torso/legs) and 신다 (footwear). The contrast to file away is 우산 vs 양산: both are 傘, but 우산 (雨傘) is the rain umbrella while 양산 (陽傘, 'sun-shade') is a parasol against sunlight — different words for different weather. Useful collocations: 우산을 쓰다/접다/펴다 (put up / fold / open an umbrella), 우산을 챙기다 (remember to grab an umbrella), 우산을 깜빡하다 (forget the umbrella). A folding umbrella is 접는 우산 or 3단 우산, a long one 장우산. Pitfall: English 'wear/carry an umbrella' must become 쓰다 (use over the head), never 입다, and don't reuse 우산 for a sun parasol — that's 양산."},sentence:{ko:"비가 오면 우산을 가져가세요.",en:"If it rains, take an umbrella."},target:"우산",options:["weather","umbrella","weather forecast"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1385."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"일정",romanization:"iljeong",en:"schedule; plan",pos:"noun",nuance:"일정 is the planned schedule of where you go and what you do, day by day — for a trip, but equally for work or events. The hanja 日程 is 日 (day) + 程 (journey/measure), an 'apportioning of the days'. Distinguish it from 시간 (time, clock time) and 계획 (plan): 시간 is just the hour on the clock, 계획 is the broad intention ('I plan to save money'), while 일정 is the concrete time-tabled sequence of items. Note the signature verb 짜다 — you 일정을 짜다 (literally 'weave a schedule'), the same verb used for 계획을 짜다. Watch the adjective 빡빡하다 / 바쁘다 for a packed itinerary. English-speaker pitfall: don't reach for 시간 when you mean the agenda — '오늘 시간이 어때요?' asks if someone is free, but '오늘 일정이 어때요?' asks about the day's plan."},sentence:{ko:"미세먼지 때문에 일정을 취소했어요.",en:"We canceled the plan because of fine dust."},target:"일정",options:["weather","schedule; plan","weather forecast"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-440."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"취소하다",romanization:"chwisohada",en:"to cancel",pos:"verb",nuance:"취소하다 (取消하다, 取 'take' + 消 'extinguish/erase') means to cancel — to call off or undo something already arranged: a reservation, an order, an appointment, a plan. The neat way to remember it: it's the natural opposite of 예약하다/신청하다 (book/apply). Distinguish it from 그만두다, which is 'to quit/stop' an ongoing activity or job (회사를 그만두다, 'quit the company'; 운동을 그만두다, 'stop exercising'): you 취소해요 a future arrangement, but you 그만둬요 something already in progress. It also differs from 미루다 ('postpone', 약속을 미루다, push the appointment later rather than scrap it) — canceling removes it, postponing reschedules it. As a noun+하다 verb it's regular: 취소하 + 여요 → 취소해요, past 취소했어요, with 안/못 before 하다 (취소 안 해요, 취소 못 해요). English-speaker pitfall: 'cancel my subscription / unsubscribe' is more often 해지하다 (解止, end a contract) than 취소하다, and don't use 취소하다 for 'quit a job' — that's 그만두다."},sentence:{ko:"미세먼지가 심하면 일정을 취소해요.",en:"If the fine dust is bad, we cancel the plan."},target:"취소해",options:["to come; to fall for rain/snow","to cancel","weather"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-verb-483."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"비행기",romanization:"bihaenggi",en:"airplane; flight",pos:"noun",nuance:"비행기 (飛行機, literally 'fly-go-machine') is the everyday spoken word for an airplane — the aircraft you board for trips abroad or across the country. Its near-synonym 항공기 means the same machine but is a stiffer, technical/news register ('aircraft'), and the ticket has its own formal twin: spoken 비행기 표 vs. written/official 항공권. It collocates with 타다 (board), 표/항공권 (ticket), 예약하다 (reserve), 연착되다 (be delayed), 환승하다 (transfer), and 공항 (airport). As with all vehicles, the pitfall is the particle: 'get on the plane' tempts 비행기에 타요, but Korean wants the object marker — 비행기를 타요. A second subtle trap is 타다 vs 내리다: you board with 비행기를 타다 (object) but disembark with 비행기에서 내리다 (source), so the particle flips between getting on and getting off."},sentence:{ko:"태풍 때문에 비행기가 늦었어요.",en:"The plane was late because of the typhoon."},target:"비행기",options:["weather","airplane; flight","weather forecast"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-146."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"태풍이 올 것 같아서 비행기가 늦을 수도 있어요.",en:"It looks like a typhoon may come, so the plane might be late."},hl:"비행기"}],canDo:"Set up a weather condition and its result (비가 오면 집에 있어요)",id:"chapter-50-b5",chapterId:"chapter-50",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A/V-(으)면",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"A/V-(으)면",sub:'"if / when the weather is like this" (condition)',lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"비가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"오",mid:"면",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"집에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"있을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"거예요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"비가 오면 집에 있을 거예요.",en:"If it rains, I'll stay home.",romanization:"biga omyeon jibe isseul geoyeyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"봄이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"오",mid:"면",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"꽃이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"펴요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"봄이 오면 꽃이 펴요.",en:"When spring comes, flowers bloom.",romanization:"bomi omyeon kkochi pyeoyo."}],rule:{name:"A/V-(으)면",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel (오다, 따뜻하다)",add:"-면",ex:"오다 → 오면 (if/when it rains)"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant (좋다, 많다)",add:"-으면",ex:"좋다 → 좋으면 (if the weather is good)"},{when:"ㅂ-irregular adjective (덥다 → 더우-, 춥다 → 추우-)",add:"-면 (on the 우 form)",ex:"덥다 → 더우면 (if it's hot)"},{when:"Past/completed condition",add:"-았/었으면",ex:"그치다 → 그쳤으면 (if the rain/snow has stopped)"}]},more:{func:`Use -(으)면 to set up a weather condition and its result: "if it rains, I'll stay home," "when spring comes, flowers bloom." It covers both English "if" (a possibility) and "when" (a regular trigger) — exactly how Koreans talk about weather shaping everyday decisions.`,funcLead:`Use -(으)면 to set up a weather condition and its result: "if it rains, I'll stay home," "when spring comes, flowers bloom." It covers both English "if" (a possi…`,keyPoint:{label:"-(으)면 vs. -(으)니까",body:"-(으)면 introduces a condition that may or may not happen (비가 오면 = if/when it rains — uncertain). -(으)니까 gives a reason that is already true (비가 오니까 = since it's raining — a fact right now). Use -면 for forecasts and rules, -니까 for what's actually happening."},pronunciation:`좋으면 is pronounced [조으면]: the ㅎ of 좋 goes silent (ㅎ-탈락) before the vowel 으, so it's "jo-eu-myeon," not "joh-eu-myeon."`,pitfall:{wrong:"비가 오면 and 비가 올 거면 are interchangeable in every context.",right:"비가 오면 집에 있을 거예요. (neutral condition) / 비가 올 거면 일정을 바꿔요. (if rain is what is expected)",explanation:"Plain V-(으)면 is the neutral everyday conditional. V-(으)ㄹ 거면 is also grammatical and sets up an expected plan or premise: 'if it is going to V / if that is what will happen.' Use it when that future premise itself matters."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"When it's hot, I turn on the air conditioner.",sentence:"날씨가 더우___ 에어컨을 켜요.",options:[{text:"면",ok:!0},{text:"텐데",ok:!1},{text:"때문에",ok:!1}],explanation:"덥다 → 더우- + 면; a habitual weather-triggered action."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"If it snows a lot, we don't go to school.",sentence:"눈이 많이 오___ 학교에 안 가요.",options:[{text:"면",ok:!0},{text:"텐데",ok:!1},{text:"때문에",ok:!1}],explanation:"Shows a weather condition deciding an everyday outcome (no school)."},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "If it rains, I'll stay home." (비가 시작 · Start with 비가.)`,tokens:["비가","오면","집에","있을","거예요."],correct:"비가 오면 집에 있을 거예요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "When spring comes, flowers bloom."',tokens:["봄이","오면","꽃이","펴요."],correct:"봄이 오면 꽃이 펴요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Set up a weather condition and its result (비가 오면 집에 있어요)",id:"chapter-50-b6",chapterId:"chapter-50",index:5},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N 때문에",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N 때문에",sub:'"because of..." (a weather cause)',lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"비",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"때문에",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"소풍이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"취소됐어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"비 때문에 소풍이 취소됐어요.",en:"The picnic was cancelled because of the rain.",romanization:"bi ttaemune sopungi chwisodwaesseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"더위",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"때문에",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"잠을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"못",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"잤어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"더위 때문에 잠을 못 잤어요.",en:"I couldn't sleep because of the heat.",romanization:"deowi ttaemune jameul mot jasseoyo."}],rule:{name:"N 때문에",rows:[{when:"Noun ending in a vowel (비, 더위)",add:"+ 때문에",ex:"비 → 비 때문에 (because of the rain)"},{when:"Noun ending in a consonant (태풍, 눈)",add:"+ 때문에 (no change)",ex:"태풍 → 태풍 때문에 (because of the typhoon)"},{when:'"because it is N" (noun as reason-state)',add:"+ (이)기 때문에",ex:"여름 → 여름이기 때문에 (because it's summer)"},{when:"Adjective/verb cause (the related form)",add:"-기 때문에",ex:"춥다 → 춥기 때문에 (because it's cold)"}]},more:{func:'Use N 때문에 to name a noun — usually a weather event — as the cause of something, especially something inconvenient: "because of the rain," "because of the heat." It attaches straight to a noun and is the everyday way to blame the weather for a delay, a cancellation, or how you feel.',funcLead:'Use N 때문에 to name a noun — usually a weather event — as the cause of something, especially something inconvenient: "because of the rain," "because of the heat.…',keyPoint:{label:"N 때문에 vs. N 덕분에",body:`때문에 is neutral-to-negative — you usually blame it for trouble (비 때문에 취소됐어요 = cancelled because of the rain). For a good outcome, Koreans switch to 덕분에 "thanks to" (좋은 날씨 덕분에 = thanks to the nice weather). Don't use 때문에 to thank the weather.`},pronunciation:'때문에 is pronounced [때무네]: the ㄴ of 문 links into the following 에 (연음), so it sounds like "ttae-mu-ne," run together as one word after the noun.',pitfall:{wrong:"비 때문에 좋은 날씨를 즐겼어요.",right:"좋은 날씨 덕분에 산책했어요.",explanation:'English "because of" is neutral, so learners use 때문에 for good things too. But 때문에 carries a blaming nuance; for a positive cause Korean uses 덕분에 (thanks to). Saving 때문에 for problems keeps you natural.'}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"The plane is late because of the typhoon.",sentence:"태풍 ___ 비행기가 늦어요.",options:[{text:"때문에",ok:!0},{text:"텐데",ok:!1},{text:"으면",ok:!1}],explanation:"태풍 ends in a consonant; 때문에 attaches with no change."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"The roads are really backed up because of the snow.",sentence:"눈 ___ 길이 많이 막혀요.",options:[{text:"때문에",ok:!0},{text:"텐데",ok:!1},{text:"으면",ok:!1}],explanation:"Everyday winter complaint; 눈 (snow) named as the cause of traffic."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "The picnic was cancelled because of the rain." (비 시작 · Start with 비.)',tokens:["비","때문에","소풍이","취소됐어요."],correct:"비 때문에 소풍이 취소됐어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I couldn't sleep because of the heat." (더위 시작 · Start with 더위.)`,tokens:["더위","때문에","잠을","못","잤어요."],correct:"더위 때문에 잠을 못 잤어요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Name a weather event as the cause (비 때문에, 황사 때문에)",id:"chapter-50-b7",chapterId:"chapter-50",index:6},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V/A-(으)ㄹ 때",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"V/A-(으)ㄹ 때",sub:"when (a time or situation)",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"-ㄹ 때",ex:"오다 → 올 때 (when it comes/rains)"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"-을 때",ex:"춥다 → 추울 때 (when it's cold)"},{when:"A past time",add:"-았/었을 때",ex:"비가 왔을 때 (when it rained)"}],examples:[{ko:"비가 올 때 우산을 써요.",en:"When it rains, I use an umbrella.",romanization:"biga ol ttae usaneul sseoyo.",note:"오다 → 올 때. The 때 clause sets the time for the main action."},{ko:"날씨가 추울 때 집에 있어요.",en:"When the weather is cold, I stay home.",romanization:"nalssiga chuul ttae jibe isseoyo.",note:"춥다 (ㅂ-irregular) → 추울 때."}],more:{func:`-(으)ㄹ 때 marks the time or situation when something happens: "when it rains," "when it's cold." Attach it to a verb or adjective stem; for a past time, use -았/었을 때.`,funcLead:`-(으)ㄹ 때 marks the time or situation when something happens: "when it rains," "when it's cold." Attach it to a verb or adjective stem; for a past time, use -았/었…`,keyPoint:{label:"때 is a noun, so use the -(으)ㄹ modifier",body:'때 means "time," so the verb in front must be in the noun-modifying -(으)ㄹ form: 올 때, 추울 때 — never 와 때 or 춥어 때. For a finished past time, use -았/었을 때 (왔을 때).'},pronunciation:"때 begins with the tense consonant ㄸ — say it hard: 올 때 is [올 때], not a soft [대].",pitfall:{wrong:"비가 와 때 / 비가 오을 때",right:"비가 올 때",explanation:"때 needs the -(으)ㄹ modifier form. 오다 ends in a vowel, so it's 올 때 (not 와 때, not 오을 때). A consonant stem takes -을 때 (추울 때)."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "When it rains, I use an umbrella."',tokens:["비가","올","때","우산을","써요."],correct:"비가 올 때 우산을 써요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "When the weather is cold, I stay home." (날씨가 시작 · Start with 날씨가.)',tokens:["날씨가","추울","때","집에","있어요."],correct:"날씨가 추울 때 집에 있어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"비가 와 때 / 비가 오을 때",ok:!1},{text:"비가 올 때",ok:!0}],explanation:"때 needs the -(으)ㄹ modifier form. 오다 ends in a vowel, so it's 올 때 (not 와 때, not 오을 때). A consonant stem takes -을 때 (추울 때)."}],canDo:"Point to a time or situation when something happens (추울 때)",id:"chapter-50-b8",chapterId:"chapter-50",index:7},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Two friends check the forecast before a weekend trip and decide what to do with their plan.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"주말 일기예보 봤어요?",en:"Did you see the weekend forecast?",romanization:"jumal ilgiyebo bwasseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"태풍이 올 것 같아서 비행기가 늦을 수도 있어요.",en:"It looks like a typhoon may come, so the plane might be late.",romanization:"taepungi ol geot gataseo bihaenggiga neujeul sudo isseoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"미세먼지가 심하면 일정은 취소하는 게 좋겠어요.",en:"If the fine dust is bad, it would be better to cancel the plan.",romanization:"misemeonjiga simhamyeon iljeongeun chwisohaneun ge jotgesseoyo."},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"날씨가 맑아질 때 다시 예약해요.",en:"Let's book again when the weather clears.",romanization:"nalssiga malgajil ttae dasi yeyakhaeyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'Because of fine dust, I canceled the plan.'",tokens:["미세먼지 때문에","일정을 취소했어요"],correct:"미세먼지 때문에 일정을 취소했어요",explanation:"N 때문에 names the weather or pollution cause. 일정을 취소했어요 gives the concrete decision."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"누가 한 말일까요? · Who said this?",sentence:"태풍이 올 것 같아서 비행기가 늦을 수도 있어요.",options:[{text:"지훈",ok:!0},{text:"민서",ok:!1}],explanation:"It looks like a typhoon may come, so the plane might be late."},{kind:"drill",prompt:'무엇이 들어갈까요? · "Did you see the weekend forecast?"',sentence:"주말 ___ 봤어요?",options:[{text:"날씨",ok:!1},{text:"일기예보",ok:!0},{text:"비",ok:!1}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Make a soft weather guess from what I see (비가 올 것 같아요)",id:"chapter-50-b9",chapterId:"chapter-50",index:8},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 태풍이 올 것 같아서 일정을 바꿔요",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"태풍이 올 것 같아서 일정을 바꿔요",chunks:["민서는 주말 일기예보를 봤어요. 태풍이 올 것 같아서 비행기가 늦을 수도 있었어요. 또 미세먼지가 심하면 밖에서 오래 있기 어려워요.","그래서 친구에게 '미세먼지가 심하면 일정은 취소하는 게 좋겠어요'라고 보냈어요. 친구는 날씨가 맑아질 때 다시 예약하자고 했어요. 이처럼 날씨 표현은 단순히 하늘을 설명하는 말이 아니라, 약속과 여행 계획을 바꾸는 말로 자주 쓰여요."],translation:"Minseo checked the weekend forecast. A typhoon might come, so the plane could be late. If fine dust is bad, it is hard to stay outside for long. Minseo texted that it would be better to cancel the plan if the fine dust was bad. The friend suggested booking again when the weather clears. Weather expressions often change appointments and travel plans.",qas:[{q:"민서는 무엇을 봤어요?",a:"주말 일기예보를 봤어요."},{q:"태풍이 오면 무엇이 늦을 수도 있어요?",a:"비행기가 늦을 수도 있어요."},{q:"언제 다시 예약하기로 했어요?",a:"날씨가 맑아질 때 다시 예약하기로 했어요."}]},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"민서는 주말 ___를 봤어요.",options:[{text:"일기예보",ok:!0},{text:"눈",ok:!1},{text:"황사",ok:!1}],explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"태풍이 올 것 같아서 ___가 늦을 수도 있었어요.",options:[{text:"비행기",ok:!0},{text:"눈",ok:!1},{text:"황사",ok:!1}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Forecast a likely situation and add advice (추울 텐데 우산을 가져오세요)",id:"chapter-50-b10",chapterId:"chapter-50",index:9},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence gives a cautious weather forecast?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"비가 올 것 같아요.",ok:!0},{text:"비가 때문에 올 것 같아요.",ok:!1},{text:"비에게 올 것 같아요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 makes a cautious guess, and 비 uses the subject particle 가."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence correctly uses 눈 as weather?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"눈이 올 때 길이 미끄러워요.",ok:!0},{text:"눈이 아플 때 길이 미끄러워요.",ok:!1},{text:"눈을 볼 때 눈이 와요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"In this chapter, 눈 is snow. 눈이 오다 means 'it snows.'"},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence names fine dust as the cause of canceling?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"미세먼지 때문에 취소해요.",ok:!0},{text:"미세먼지가 때문에 취소해요.",ok:!1},{text:"미세먼지를 때문에 취소해요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Use noun plus 때문에 without adding 가 or 를."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"비___ 오면 우산을 가져가요. (If it rains, take an umbrella.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"가",ok:!0},{text:"를",ok:!1},{text:"에서",ok:!1}],explanation:"비 is the subject of 오다, so it takes 가. 를 would make rain an object."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence delays a plan because the weather may clear later?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"날씨가 맑아질 때 다시 예약해요.",ok:!0},{text:"날씨가 맑은 때문에 예약해요.",ok:!1},{text:"날씨를 맑으면 예약해요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-ㄹ 때 marks the situation/time when the weather clears. The other options mix the weather word with the wrong particles or endings."}],canDo:"Set up a weather condition and its result (비가 오면 집에 있어요)",id:"chapter-50-b11",chapterId:"chapter-50",index:10}]},{id:"chapter-51",number:51,title:"V-아/어 보세요 · V-아/어 주시겠어요?",goal:"Use clothing words with 입다, 신다, 사이즈가 맞다, and 잘 어울리다.",biteCount:11,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 옷~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"옷",romanization:"ot",en:"clothes",pos:"noun",nuance:"옷 is a pure native Korean word (no hanja) meaning clothes/clothing for the body, and the verb you MUST pair it with is 입다 (to wear on the torso/body) — never 신다, which is reserved for footwear on the feet (신발을 신다), nor 쓰다 (hats/glasses) nor 끼다 (gloves/rings). English 'wear/put on' covers every garment with one verb, so this split is the central pitfall: 옷을 신어요 is a classic beginner error. To take clothes off is 벗다, to change them 갈아입다. For 'these clothes look good on you,' Korean says 옷이 잘 어울려요 (the clothes suit you), not a direct translation of 'you look good.' Note 옷 is treated as singular/uncountable grammatically (no plural needed): one outfit or a wardrobe full is still just 옷. The dressier counterpart 의상 (costume/attire, 衣裳) is for performances or formal contexts. Register is neutral and everyday."},sentence:{ko:"이 옷이 잘 어울려요.",en:"This outfit suits you well."},target:"옷",options:["shirt","T-shirt","clothes"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-067."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"셔츠",romanization:"syeocheu",en:"shirt",pos:"noun",nuance:"셔츠 is the loanword for a collared upper-body shirt; the general Sino-Korean cover term for any top is 상의 (上衣, 'upper garment'), and a knit pullover is 티셔츠/티. Because a shirt covers the torso it ALWAYS takes 입다 ('put on the body'), the same family of dressing verbs that splits by body zone in Korean: 입다 for trunk and legs, 신다 for feet, 쓰다 for the head/face, 끼다 for hands. The single biggest English-speaker pitfall is treating 'wear' as one verb the way English does — you cannot say 셔츠를 써요 or 셔츠를 신어요. Register is neutral and the word fits any politeness level. Natural collocations: 셔츠를 입다/벗다 (put on/take off), 셔츠를 다리다 (iron a shirt), 셔츠가 (안) 맞다 (it does (not) fit). Note that to take a shirt off you switch to 벗다, not a negated 입다."},sentence:{ko:"이 셔츠 입어 봐도 돼요?",en:"May I try on this shirt?"},target:"셔츠",options:["clothes","shirt","pants"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-229."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"티셔츠",romanization:"tisyeocheu",en:"T-shirt",pos:"noun",nuance:"티셔츠 is a direct loanword from English 'T-shirt' (named for its T shape), and it specifically means a pull-over knit top — most often short-sleeved cotton. The key contrast English speakers miss is 티셔츠 vs 셔츠: in Korean 셔츠 by itself implies a button-up collared shirt (a dress shirt / 와이셔츠), so a casual tee must keep the 티 to stay distinct. Sleeve length is built right into the noun phrase: 반팔 티셔츠 (half-sleeve) vs 긴팔 티셔츠 (long-sleeve). Register is casual and neutral. The pitfalls: (1) the verb is 입다, never 신다; (2) thin tees are frequently counted with 장 ('흰 티 한 장' = one plain white tee), though 벌 also works for an outfit; and (3) Koreans clip it to 티 in speech (흰 티, 검은 티), so don't be thrown when the 셔츠 disappears."},sentence:{ko:"여름에는 티셔츠를 자주 입어요.",en:"I often wear T-shirts in summer."},target:"티셔츠",options:["clothes","T-shirt","pants"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1887."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"바지",romanization:"baji",en:"pants",pos:"noun",nuance:"바지 covers the lower body, so despite English treating 'pants' as plural, Korean treats it as a single uncountable garment: there is no plural ending, no 'a pair' built into the word, and the verb is 입다 just like a shirt. The dividing line with footwear matters — legs use 입다 but the feet that stick out below use 신다, so you 입다 your 바지 but 신다 your 양말. Jeans are 청바지 (靑—, 'blue-color pants'), shorts are 반바지 ('half pants'), and dress slacks are often just called 슬랙스. The classic English-speaker error is the literal plural reflex — saying 바지들이 길어요 sounds as odd as saying 'pantses'. Natural collocations: 바지를 입다/벗다, 바지를 갈아입다 (change into other pants), 바지 길이를 줄이다 (shorten), 바지가 끼다 (the pants are tight). Note 끼다 here means 'feel tight', a different sense from putting gloves on."},sentence:{ko:"바지가 조금 커 보여요.",en:"The pants look a little big."},target:"바지",options:["clothes","pants","shirt"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-230."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"치마",romanization:"chima",en:"skirt",pos:"noun",nuance:"치마 is the native Korean word for a skirt and is purely neutral; the English loanword 스커트 exists but 치마 is what people actually say in daily life, and a traditional hanbok skirt is also 치마. Like all lower-body clothing it takes 입다, and to take it off you switch to 벗다 — never a negated 입다. The trap for English speakers is again the one-word-'wear' habit plus assuming a fancier loanword is more polite; here the plain native 치마 is the unmarked, fully appropriate choice in every register. A very useful partner verb is 어울리다 ('to suit/match'), used with the marker -이/가 for the item and -에게/한테 for the person: 그 치마가 너한테 잘 어울려. Natural collocations: 치마를 입다/벗다, 짧은/긴 치마 (short/long skirt), 치마가 어울리다, 치마를 갈아입다. Do not confuse 어울리다 (looks good on) with 맞다 (fits in size) — a skirt can 맞다 yet not 어울리다."},sentence:{ko:"이 구두가 치마하고 잘 어울려요.",en:"These dress shoes go well with the skirt."},target:"치마",options:["clothes","skirt","shirt"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-231."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"원피스",romanization:"wonpiseu",en:"dress",pos:"noun",nuance:"원피스 is a loanword from English 'one-piece', and that literal sense is the whole point: it's a dress where top and skirt are a single connected garment, as opposed to a 투피스 ('two-piece', a matching top-and-skirt set). The sharp contrast English speakers must learn is 원피스 vs 드레스: everyday casual or work dresses are 원피스, while 드레스 in Korean is reserved for a formal gown — a wedding dress (웨딩드레스) or evening gown — so calling your sundress a 드레스 sounds like you're going to a gala. The word is essentially female-coded. Register is neutral. Pitfalls: (1) the verb is 입다; (2) compliment the fit with 어울리다 ('it suits you'), the single most useful clothes-shopping verb; (3) count with 벌, and (4) never split a 원피스 into separates — that's literally a 투피스."},sentence:{ko:"이 원피스는 면접에는 조금 화려해 보여요.",en:"This dress looks a little flashy for an interview."},target:"원피스",options:["clothes","shirt","dress"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1889."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"코트",romanization:"koteu",en:"coat",pos:"noun",nuance:"코트 is a loanword for a warm outer layer; the broader native umbrella term for any outerwear is 외투 (外套, 'outer wrap'), and a padded winter jacket is 패딩 or 점퍼. Even though it is the outermost layer, 코트 still takes 입다 because the rule keys off the body zone (trunk), not how many layers are underneath; you put it on with 입다 and take it off with 벗다. The English-speaker pitfall is pronunciation-driven spelling and the 'wear' reflex: 코트 is two clean syllables (ko-teu) and is never said with 쓰다 or 차다. A second subtlety: when you mean 'put a coat ON over other clothes' Koreans often say 코트를 걸치다 ('throw on lightly'), a nuance 입다 alone doesn't carry. Natural collocations: 코트를 입다/벗다, 코트를 걸치다 (drape on), 두꺼운/긴 코트, 코트 단추를 잠그다 (button up the coat). Note 걸치다 implies a casual, partial wearing, unlike the full 입다."},sentence:{ko:"이 코트는 비싸 보여요.",en:"This coat looks expensive."},target:"코트",options:["coat","clothes","shirt"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-232."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"손님",ko:"이 셔츠 입어 봐도 돼요?",en:"May I try on this shirt?"},hl:"셔츠"}],canDo:"Invite someone to try something on (한번 입어 보세요)",id:"chapter-51-b1",chapterId:"chapter-51",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-아/어 보세요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-아/어 보세요",sub:"Try doing it",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"원피스",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"한번",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"입",mid:"어",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"보세요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"이 원피스 한번 입어 보세요.",en:"Try this dress on.",romanization:"i wonpiseu hanbeon ibeo boseyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"모자도",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"써",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"보세요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"이 모자도 써 보세요.",en:"Try this hat on too.",romanization:"i moja-do sseo boseyo."}],rule:{name:"V-아/어 보세요",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ends in ㅏ or ㅗ",add:"-아 보세요",ex:"사다 → 사 보세요 (try buying it)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in any other vowel or a consonant",add:"-어 보세요",ex:"입다 → 입어 보세요 (try wearing it), 신다 → 신어 보세요 (try them on)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"해 보세요",ex:"착용하다 → 착용해 보세요 (try wearing it on)"}]},more:{func:"Suggest that the listener try an action to see how it goes — perfect for inviting someone to try a piece of clothing on, try a color, or give a style a go. It softens a plain command into friendly encouragement: go ahead and try it.",funcLead:"Suggest that the listener try an action to see how it goes — perfect for inviting someone to try a piece of clothing on, try a color, or give a style a go.",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)세요 vs -아/어 보세요",body:"입으세요 = Put it on (a plain polite command). 입어 보세요 = Try it on (and see) — it adds the nuance of testing or experiencing, which is why a shop clerk says 입어 보세요, not 입으세요."},pronunciation:"",pitfall:{wrong:"이 코트 시도해 보세요.",right:"이 코트 입어 보세요.",explanation:"English try on makes learners reach for a word meaning try/attempt (시도하다). In Korean you keep the real clothing verb (입다/신다/쓰다) and just attach 보세요; the try meaning comes entirely from 보세요."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Try these shoes on.",sentence:"이 신발 신___.",options:[{text:"어 보세요",ok:!0},{text:"보세요",ok:!1}],explanation:"신다 = to wear on the feet. Each clothing verb is different, but all combine the same way with 보세요."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Try picking the red one.",sentence:"빨간색으로 한번 골라 ___.",options:[{text:"주시겠어요",ok:!1},{text:"보세요",ok:!0},{text:"보이다",ok:!1}],explanation:"고르다 is a 르-irregular, so 고르 → 골라 before -아; you are inviting them to try choosing."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Try this dress on."',tokens:["이","원피스","한번","입어","보세요."],correct:"이 원피스 한번 입어 보세요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Try this hat on too."',tokens:["이","모자도","써","보세요."],correct:"이 모자도 써 보세요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Invite someone to try something on (한번 입어 보세요)",id:"chapter-51-b2",chapterId:"chapter-51",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 운동화~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"운동화",romanization:"undonghwa",en:"sneakers",pos:"noun",nuance:"운동화 (運動靴, 運動 'exercise/movement' + 靴 'shoe', literally 'exercise-shoe') means sneakers/trainers, and like all 신발 (footwear) it takes 신다, the foot-zone verb shared with socks; it never takes 입다. The near-synonym to keep straight is 구두 (dress/leather shoes): both are shoes and both use 신다 and 켤레, but 운동화 is casual/athletic while 구두 is formal — so the choice signals register, not grammar. A pair is counted with 켤레. The English-speaker pitfalls: the single-'wear' habit, and forgetting that the generic word for any shoe is 신발, with 운동화 being one specific type. To take shoes off — a daily act before entering Korean homes — you use 벗다 (신발을 벗다). Natural collocations: 운동화를 신다/벗다, 운동화 한 켤레, 운동화 끈을 묶다 (tie the laces), 운동화가 편하다/낡다 (comfy/worn out). Note 끈을 묶다 ('tie the laces') uses 묶다, and 낡다 ('wear out with age') is the adjective for worn shoes."},sentence:{ko:"이 운동화도 신어 봐도 돼요?",en:"May I try on these sneakers too?"},target:"운동화",options:["clothes","shirt","sneakers"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-239."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"구두",romanization:"gudu",en:"dress shoes",pos:"noun",nuance:"구두 is the native everyday word for formal leather shoes (often dress/heeled shoes), and like all footwear it takes 신다 and is counted with 켤레; it never takes 입다. The key contrast is with 운동화 (sneakers): grammatically identical — both 신다, both 켤레 — but 구두 carries a formal, dressed-up register (worn with a suit, 정장, to work or interviews) while 운동화 is casual and athletic, so picking the right noun signals the occasion. The broad cover term for any shoe remains 신발. English-speaker pitfalls: the 'wear = one verb' reflex, and assuming 'shoes' is one word — Korean forces you to choose 구두 vs 운동화 vs the generic 신발 by formality. Because Koreans remove shoes indoors, 벗다 (신발/구두를 벗다) is a verb you will use daily, and 갈아 신다 means swapping footwear. Natural collocations: 구두를 신다/벗다, 구두 한 켤레, 구두를 닦다 (polish dress shoes), 구두 굽 (the heel of the shoe). Note 닦다 ('shine/polish') pairs especially with leather 구두, and 굽 is the heel."},sentence:{ko:"면접에는 운동화보다 구두가 더 단정해 보여요.",en:"For an interview, dress shoes look more presentable than sneakers."},target:"구두",options:["clothes","dress shoes","shirt"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-240."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"색깔",romanization:"saekkkal",en:"color",pos:"noun",nuance:"색깔 is the everyday, standalone word for 'color,' built on the Sino-Korean root 색 (色 'color') plus a native suffix that makes it feel fuller and more conversational. Its near-synonym is just 색: the dividing line is grammatical, not meaning — 색깔 stands on its own (색깔이 예뻐요, 무슨 색깔?), while 색 is the building block that fuses to a specific shade name (파란색 blue, 빨간색 red, 노란색 yellow). A more formal, technical synonym is 색상, common in design and shopping ('color option'). The main English-speaker pitfall is over-using 색깔 by sticking it onto shade names — '파란색깔' is wrong; the named colors take 색, giving 파란색. Also, to ask 'what color,' the natural question word is 무슨 색깔, not 어떤 색깔 (which leans toward 'what kind/sort of color')."},sentence:{ko:"다른 색깔도 보여 주시겠어요?",en:"Could you show me another color too?"},target:"색깔",options:["clothes","color","shirt"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1638."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"남색",romanization:"namsaek",en:"navy; deep blue",pos:"noun",nuance:"남색 is the deep, near-ink blue of school uniforms, business suits, and dark denim. The 남 is Sino-Korean 藍 ('indigo', the dye plant), so the word literally means 'indigo-color' — a good reminder that it lives at the DARK end of blue, the opposite pole from 하늘색 (sky blue). Against everyday 파란색 (a saturated mid-blue), 남색 reads as more formal and 차분하다 ('calm, composed'), which is exactly why it dominates uniforms and interview wear; younger shoppers often just say the loanword 네이비. Two English-speaker pitfalls: first, don't merge 남색 with 파란색 — they are not interchangeable, and a navy suit described as 파란색 정장 will sound like a bright-blue suit. Second, when stating 'navy is more X', the comparison particle goes on the thing you compare against: 남색이 파란색보다 (navy, compared-to blue), not 남색보다 파란색이 unless you mean the reverse."},sentence:{ko:"면접에는 남색 셔츠가 단정해 보여요.",en:"For an interview, a navy shirt looks neat and presentable."},target:"남색",options:["clothes","shirt","navy; deep blue"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1019."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"사이즈",romanization:"saijeu",en:"size",pos:"noun",nuance:"사이즈 is the size label or fit. Use 맞다 for whether it fits, and 어울리다 for whether it looks good on someone."},sentence:{ko:"사이즈가 조금 커서 한 치수 작은 것도 보여 주시겠어요?",en:"The size is a little big, so could you show me one size smaller too?"},target:"사이즈",options:["clothes","size","shirt"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch51-saijeu."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"치수",romanization:"chisu",en:"size; measurement",pos:"noun",nuance:"사이즈 is the everyday label; 치수 is the size measurement or one-step comparison used in 한 치수 큰/작은."},sentence:{ko:"한 치수 작은 것도 입어 봐도 돼요?",en:"May I try on one size smaller too?"},target:"치수",options:["clothes","size; measurement","shirt"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch51-chisu."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"피팅룸",romanization:"pitingrum",en:"fitting room",pos:"noun",nuance:"피팅룸 is used in many stores, but 탈의실 also means changing room. For clothing shopping, 피팅룸 is natural and easy to understand."},sentence:{ko:"피팅룸은 오른쪽에 있어요.",en:"The fitting room is on the right."},target:"피팅룸",options:["fitting room","clothes","shirt"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch51-pitingrum."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"직원",ko:"네, 피팅룸은 오른쪽에 있어요.",en:"Yes, the fitting room is on the right."},hl:"피팅룸"}],canDo:"Ask a clerk politely for help (다른 사이즈를 보여 주시겠어요?)",id:"chapter-51-b3",chapterId:"chapter-51",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-아/어 주시겠어요?",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-아/어 주시겠어요?",sub:"Could you please do it for me?",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"더",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"큰",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"사이즈로",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가져다",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"주시겠어요",post:"?",hit:!0}],ko:"더 큰 사이즈로 가져다 주시겠어요?",en:"Could you bring me a bigger size?",romanization:"deo keun saijeuro gajyeoda jusigesseoyo?"},{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"셔츠",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"좀",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"찾",mid:"아",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"주시겠어요",post:"?",hit:!0}],ko:"이 셔츠 좀 찾아 주시겠어요?",en:"Could you find this shirt for me?",romanization:"i syeocheu jom chaja jusigesseoyo?"}],rule:{name:"V-아/어 주시겠어요?",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in ㅏ/ㅗ",add:"-아 주시겠어요?",ex:"찾다 → 찾아 주시겠어요? (could you find it for me?)"},{when:"Stem ends in another vowel or consonant",add:"-어 주시겠어요?",ex:"보이다 → 보여 주시겠어요? (could you show me?)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"해 주시겠어요?",ex:"추천하다 → 추천해 주시겠어요? (could you recommend one?)"}]},more:{func:"A very polite request asking someone to do something for your benefit. In a shop or fitting room it is how you ask the clerk to bring another size, show you a color, or give you a hand. -주다 adds for me, and -시겠어요? makes it gentle and respectful.",funcLead:"A very polite request asking someone to do something for your benefit.",keyPoint:{label:"-아/어 주세요 vs -아/어 주시겠어요?",body:"가져다 주세요 = Please bring it (polite request). 가져다 주시겠어요? = Could you bring it? — the -시겠어요? question form is one step more deferential and indirect, the safest choice with a stranger or staff."},pronunciation:"",pitfall:{wrong:"더 큰 사이즈로 가져다 주시겠어요 나를 위해?",right:"더 큰 사이즈로 가져다 주시겠어요?",explanation:"Learners add for me as a separate phrase (나를 위해) by analogy with English. The benefit is already built into 주다, so adding 나를 위해 is redundant and unnatural — just use 주시겠어요?."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Could you show me another color too?",sentence:"다른 색깔도 보여 ___?",options:[{text:"주시겠어요",ok:!0},{text:"아 주시겠어요",ok:!1}],explanation:"보이다 → 보여; 보여 주다 literally show for me, the natural way to ask to be shown an item."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Could you bring me a bigger size?"',tokens:["더","큰","사이즈로","가져다","주시겠어요?"],correct:"더 큰 사이즈로 가져다 주시겠어요?",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Could you find this shirt for me?"',tokens:["이","셔츠","좀","찾아","주시겠어요?"],correct:"이 셔츠 좀 찾아 주시겠어요?",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"더 큰 사이즈로 가져다 주시겠어요 나를 위해?",ok:!1},{text:"더 큰 사이즈로 가져다 주시겠어요?",ok:!0}],explanation:"Learners add for me as a separate phrase (나를 위해) by analogy with English. The benefit is already built into 주다, so adding 나를 위해 is redundant and unnatural — just use 주시겠어요?."}],canDo:"Ask a clerk politely for help (다른 사이즈를 보여 주시겠어요?)",id:"chapter-51-b4",chapterId:"chapter-51",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 단정하다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"단정하다",romanization:"danjeonghada",en:"to be neat; presentable",pos:"adjective",nuance:"단정하다 means neat, orderly, and socially appropriate. It is not the same as stylish or trendy; it is often safer for interviews, school, and work."},sentence:{ko:"남색 셔츠가 더 단정해 보여요.",en:"The navy shirt looks neater."},target:"단정해",options:["to be neat; presentable","clothes","shirt"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch51-danjeonghada."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"어울리다",romanization:"eoullida",en:"to suit; to look good on",pos:"verb",nuance:"어울리다 carries two everyday meanings bound by one idea — 'to harmonize / fit together well': (1) to suit or look good on someone (이 옷이 잘 어울려요 = this suits you), and (2) to mingle, blend in, or get along with people (친구들하고 잘 어울려요). The particle pattern is the crux for English speakers, because English 'suits you' makes 'you' the object: in Korean the thing that suits is the SUBJECT and takes 이/가 (옷이 어울려요, never 옷을 어울려요), the person it suits is unmarked or takes 한테/에게, and a matching partner or group takes 하고/와/과 (치마하고 어울리다, 사람들하고 어울리다); a setting it fits takes 에 (분위기에 어울리다). Conjugation is the regular ㅣ-vowel contraction, not an irregular: 어울리 + 어 fuses to 어울려, giving polite 어울려요 and past 어울렸어요 (not 어울리어요). A useful contrast: 어울리다 ('look good / harmonize') vs 맞다 ('fit in size / be correct') — clothes that 어울리다 flatter you, clothes that 맞다 are the right size, and the two are not the same compliment."},sentence:{ko:"이 옷이 잘 어울려요.",en:"This outfit suits you well."},target:"어울려",options:["clothes","to suit; to look good on","shirt"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-verb-304."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"교환",romanization:"gyohwan",en:"exchange",pos:"noun",nuance:"교환 means swapping a purchased item for a different size, color, or model of the same kind of product, from the hanja 交換 (交 'to cross/mutual' + 換 'to change'), literally 'mutually change.' The crucial shopping contrast is with 반품, a 'return' that gives your money back: 교환 keeps the transaction (you walk out with goods), 반품 undoes it (you walk out with a refund). Counters typically post '교환·반품 안내' together. Note 교환 is also used more broadly than shopping — 의견 교환 (exchanging opinions), 정보 교환 (exchanging information) — so the word isn't shop-only, but here it's the item-swap sense. The English-speaker pitfall: English 'exchange money' tempts a literal 돈을 교환하다, but currency exchange is 환전 (a different word). To swap into the replacement item, use 로/으로 (큰 사이즈로 교환), and remember the verb is 교환하다, so 'I want to exchange' is 교환하고 싶어요, not 교환을 싶어요."},sentence:{ko:"교환도 돼요?",en:"Can I exchange it too?"},target:"교환",options:["clothes","exchange","shirt"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1971 and expression expr-765."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"환불",romanization:"hwanbul",en:"refund",pos:"noun",nuance:"환불(還拂) is Sino-Korean — 還 'return/give back' + 拂 'pay out' — literally 'pay back', i.e. returning the buyer's money for a returned item. The contrast learners must nail is 환불 vs 교환: 환불 = money back, 교환(交換 'exchange') = swap for a different item/size/color, no money returned. A related word is 반품(返品) 'returning the goods' — the physical act of sending the product back, which usually triggers a 환불. So you 반품 the item and receive a 환불. Voice matters as with 할인: from your side you say 환불을 받다 ('receive a refund'); to request it you say 환불해 주세요 ('refund it for me'); about whether it's allowed you say 환불이 되다/환불되다 ('a refund is possible'). The English-speaker pitfall is wanting an exchange but saying 환불 — if you actually want a different size, ask 교환돼요? / 교환해 주세요, because 환불 will give you cash back, not a swap. Note Korean also uses 결제 (결제하다) for 'make a payment/settle by card', often in the same conversation. Collocations: 환불을 받다, 환불해 주다, 환불되다, 환불 정책 (refund policy), 환불 규정."},sentence:{ko:"환불도 돼요?",en:"Can I get a refund too?"},target:"환불",options:["clothes","shirt","refund"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-143 and expression expr-764."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"면접",romanization:"myeonjeop",en:"job interview",pos:"noun",nuance:"면접 (面接, 'face-contact/meet') literally means meeting face-to-face, and refers to the job interview where you sit before evaluators. The most important thing to lock in is the FIXED verb: 면접을 보다, literally 'to see an interview,' which uses the same 보다 as 시험을 보다 (take a test) — Korean treats undergoing an evaluation as 'seeing' it. The synonym contrast: 면접 is the oral, face-to-face interview, while 시험 (試驗) is a written test/exam, and 면담 (面談) is a softer counseling-style talk (e.g., with a professor), not a hiring screen. After a 면접 you hope to 합격하다 (pass) rather than 떨어지다 (fall/fail) or 불합격하다. Register: neutral, used in any job-search context. The English-speaker pitfall is reaching for 하다: 면접을 하다 sounds off from the candidate's side — you 면접을 보다 (take it); the company that conducts it may 면접을 진행하다. Also note you pass 면접에 (with 에), patterning like 시험에 합격하다."},sentence:{ko:"면접에는 단정한 셔츠가 좋아요.",en:"For an interview, a neat shirt is good."},target:"면접",options:["job interview","clothes","shirt"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1627."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"직원",ko:"네. 면접에는 이 셔츠보다 남색 셔츠가 더 단정해 보여요.",en:"Sure. For an interview, the navy shirt looks neater than this shirt."},hl:"면접"}],canDo:"Say how clothes or a person look (따뜻해 보여요, 예뻐 보여요)",id:"chapter-51-b5",chapterId:"chapter-51",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A-아/어 보이다",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"A-아/어 보이다",sub:"It looks / appears",rows:[{when:"Adjective stem ends in ㅏ/ㅗ",add:"-아 보이다",ex:"좋다 → 좋아 보여요 (looks good)"},{when:"Stem ends in another vowel or consonant",add:"-어 보이다",ex:"예쁘다 → 예뻐 보여요 (looks pretty)"},{when:"하다 adjectives",add:"-해 보이다",ex:"깔끔하다 → 깔끔해 보여요 (looks neat)"}],examples:[{ko:"그 원피스 정말 예뻐 보여요.",en:"That dress really looks pretty.",romanization:"geu wonpiseu jeongmal yeppeo boyeoyo.",note:"예쁘다 → 예뻐 (ㅡ drops before -어); the headline example for this pattern."},{ko:"이 코트는 좀 비싸 보여요.",en:"This coat looks a bit expensive.",romanization:"i koteuneun jom bissa boyeoyo.",note:"비싸다 ends in ㅏ, so the -아 merges in: 비싸 보여요; commenting on apparent price."}],more:{func:"Describe how something or someone appears to the observer. Attached to an adjective it means looks (pretty/big/expensive); it is the everyday way to compliment an outfit or comment on how clothes look on someone.",funcLead:"Describe how something or someone appears to the observer.",keyPoint:{label:"예뻐요 vs 예뻐 보여요",body:"예뻐요 = It is pretty (your judgment of the thing itself). 예뻐 보여요 = It looks pretty (how it appears, possibly more than it really is). Use 보이다 when you are describing an impression or the effect an outfit gives."},pronunciation:"보여요 is pronounced with a smooth y-glide [bo-yeo-yo]; learners often clip it to [bo-eo-yo] — keep the ㅕ. In 좋아 보여요, ㅎ is silent before a vowel, so 좋아 links as [jo-a].",pitfall:{wrong:"그 원피스는 예쁜 것처럼 보여요.",right:"그 원피스는 예뻐 보여요.",explanation:"English looks like it is pretty tempts learners to build a heavy ~것처럼 보이다 clause. For a simple adjective impression, Korean just fuses the adjective with 보이다: 예뻐 보여요."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "That dress really looks pretty."',tokens:["그","원피스","정말","예뻐","보여요."],correct:"그 원피스 정말 예뻐 보여요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "This coat looks a bit expensive."',tokens:["이","코트는","좀","비싸","보여요."],correct:"이 코트는 좀 비싸 보여요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"그 원피스는 예쁜 것처럼 보여요.",ok:!1},{text:"그 원피스는 예뻐 보여요.",ok:!0}],explanation:"English looks like it is pretty tempts learners to build a heavy ~것처럼 보이다 clause. For a simple adjective impression, Korean just fuses the adjective with 보이다: 예뻐 보여요."}],canDo:"Say how clothes or a person look (따뜻해 보여요, 예뻐 보여요)",id:"chapter-51-b6",chapterId:"chapter-51",index:5},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A-(으)ㄴ N",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"A-(으)ㄴ N",sub:"An adjective before a noun",rows:[{when:"Adjective stem ends in a vowel",add:"-ㄴ + N",ex:"예쁘다 → 예쁜 원피스 (a pretty dress)"},{when:"Adjective stem ends in a consonant",add:"-은 + N",ex:"작다 → 작은 신발 (small shoes)"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem (ㄹ drops)",add:"-ㄴ + N",ex:"길다 → 긴 치마 (a long skirt)"},{when:"있다/없다 type",add:"-는 + N",ex:"멋있다 → 멋있는 옷 (cool-looking clothes)"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 긴 치마를 좋아해요.",en:"I like long skirts.",romanization:"jeoneun gin chimareul joahaeyo.",note:"길다 is a ㄹ-irregular: ㄹ drops and -ㄴ attaches → 긴. A classic irregular to flag for clothing length."},{ko:"검은색 구두를 신었어요.",en:"I wore black dress shoes.",romanization:"geomeunsaek gudureul sineosseoyo.",note:"검다 → 검은; consonant-final stems take -은. 검은색 (black color) is everyday clothing vocabulary."}],more:{func:"Turn a descriptive adjective into a modifier that sits directly in front of a noun — the basic way to say a pretty dress, a long skirt, black shoes. Essential for describing clothing and appearance with detail.",funcLead:"Turn a descriptive adjective into a modifier that sits directly in front of a noun — the basic way to say a pretty dress, a long skirt, black shoes.",keyPoint:{label:"치마가 길어요 vs 긴 치마",body:"치마가 길어요 = The skirt is long (adjective as predicate, sentence-final). 긴 치마 = a long skirt (adjective modifying the noun, mid-sentence). The same adjective takes -아/어요 to end a sentence but -(으)ㄴ to sit before a noun."},pronunciation:"작은 links as [ja-geun] — the final ㄱ of 작 moves onto the -은 (resyllabification). Likewise 검은 → [geo-meun], 좋은 → [jo-eun] (ㅎ silent).",pitfall:{wrong:"긴은 치마",right:"긴 치마",explanation:"With ㄹ-irregular adjectives like 길다, learners try to add -은 to a stem that already kept ㄹ. Instead the ㄹ drops and only -ㄴ attaches: 길다 → 긴 치마, not 길은/긴은 치마."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I like long skirts."',tokens:["저는","긴","치마를","좋아해요."],correct:"저는 긴 치마를 좋아해요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I wore black dress shoes."',tokens:["검은색","구두를","신었어요."],correct:"검은색 구두를 신었어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"긴 치마",ok:!0},{text:"긴은 치마",ok:!1}],explanation:"With ㄹ-irregular adjectives like 길다, learners try to add -은 to a stem that already kept ㄹ. Instead the ㄹ drops and only -ㄴ attaches: 길다 → 긴 치마, not 길은/긴은 치마."}],canDo:"Put a descriptive adjective before a noun (예쁜 치마, 긴 바지)",id:"chapter-51-b7",chapterId:"chapter-51",index:6},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N보다 (더)",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N보다 (더)",sub:"More ... than N",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"원피스가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"저",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"원피스",mid:"보다",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"예뻐요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"이 원피스가 저 원피스보다 예뻐요.",en:"This dress is prettier than that one.",romanization:"i wonpiseuga jeo wonpiseuboda yeppeoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"검은색",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"구두가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"흰색",mid:"보다",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"더",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"잘",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"어울려요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"검은색 구두가 흰색보다 더 잘 어울려요.",en:"Black shoes suit you better than white ones.",romanization:"geomeunsaek guduga huinsaekboda deo jal eoullyeoyo."}],rule:{name:"N보다 (더)",rows:[{when:"After any noun (the thing compared against)",add:"N보다",ex:"이 치마가 저 치마보다 길어요 (this skirt is longer than that one)"},{when:"Add 더 before the adjective for emphasis",add:"N보다 더 + A",ex:"이 코트가 저 코트보다 더 비싸요 (this coat is more expensive than that one)"},{when:"Opposite direction uses 덜 (less)",add:"N보다 덜 + A",ex:"이 신발이 저 신발보다 덜 편해요 (these shoes are less comfortable than those)"}]},more:{func:"Compare two things: mark the standard of comparison (the thing you measure against) with 보다, and add 더 (more) for emphasis. Ideal for clothing and appearance — saying one skirt is longer than another, or one coat is more expensive than another.",funcLead:"Compare two things: mark the standard of comparison (the thing you measure against) with 보다, and add 더 (more) for emphasis.",keyPoint:{label:"Where 보다 attaches",body:"보다 attaches to the standard (the thing you measure against), not the subject. 이 치마가 저 치마보다 길어요 = this skirt is longer than that skirt. The 가-marked noun is the winner; the 보다-marked noun is what it beats."},pronunciation:"보다 is always pronounced [bo-da] and attaches directly with no space in writing: 저 치마보다. 더 [deo] is a separate word with a clear ㅓ vowel — do not reduce it to [deu].",pitfall:{wrong:"이 치마가 길어요 저 치마보다.",right:"이 치마가 저 치마보다 길어요.",explanation:"English puts than that one at the end, so learners tack 저 치마보다 on after the adjective. In Korean the 보다 phrase must come before the adjective: subject + 보다-phrase + 더 + adjective."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"These pants are shorter than those.",sentence:"이 바지가 저 바지___ 짧아요.",options:[{text:"보세요",ok:!1},{text:"주시겠어요",ok:!1},{text:"보다",ok:!0}],explanation:"짧다 → 짧아요; comparing length is a very common clothing comment."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "This dress is prettier than that one."',tokens:["이","원피스가","저","원피스보다","예뻐요."],correct:"이 원피스가 저 원피스보다 예뻐요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Black shoes suit you better than white ones."',tokens:["검은색","구두가","흰색보다","더","잘","어울려요."],correct:"검은색 구두가 흰색보다 더 잘 어울려요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"이 치마가 저 치마보다 길어요.",ok:!0},{text:"이 치마가 길어요 저 치마보다.",ok:!1}],explanation:"English puts than that one at the end, so learners tack 저 치마보다 on after the adjective. In Korean the 보다 phrase must come before the adjective: subject + 보다-phrase + 더 + adjective."}],canDo:"Compare two items (이 옷이 저 옷보다 더 좋아요)",id:"chapter-51-b8",chapterId:"chapter-51",index:7},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A shopper tries on a shirt, asks for another size, and chooses an interview outfit.",lines:[{speaker:"손님",ko:"이 셔츠 입어 봐도 돼요?",en:"May I try on this shirt?",romanization:"i syeocheu ibeo bwado dwaeyo?"},{speaker:"직원",ko:"네, 피팅룸은 오른쪽에 있어요.",en:"Yes, the fitting room is on the right.",romanization:"ne, pitingrumeun oreunjjoge isseoyo."},{speaker:"손님",ko:"조금 커 보여요. 한 치수 작은 것도 보여 주시겠어요?",en:"It looks a little big. Could you show me one size smaller too?",romanization:"jogeum keo boyeoyo. han chisu jageun geotdo boyeo jusigesseoyo?"},{speaker:"직원",ko:"네. 면접에는 이 셔츠보다 남색 셔츠가 더 단정해 보여요.",en:"Sure. For an interview, the navy shirt looks neater than this shirt.",romanization:"ne. myeonjeobeneun i syeocheuboda namsaek syeocheuga deo danjeonghae boyeoyo."},{speaker:"손님",ko:"그럼 이걸로 할게요. 교환이나 환불도 돼요?",en:"Then I'll take this one. Can I exchange or refund it too?",romanization:"geureom igeollo halgeyo. gyohwanina hwanbuldo dwaeyo?"}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'Could you show me a smaller size?'",tokens:["한 사이즈 작은 거","보여 주시겠어요?"],correct:"한 사이즈 작은 거 보여 주시겠어요?",explanation:"작은 거 describes the smaller item you want. -주시겠어요? is a polite staff-facing request."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"누가 한 말일까요? · Who said this?",sentence:"네. 면접에는 이 셔츠보다 남색 셔츠가 더 단정해 보여요.",options:[{text:"손님",ok:!1},{text:"직원",ok:!0}],explanation:"Sure. For an interview, the navy shirt looks neater than this shirt."},{kind:"drill",prompt:'무엇이 들어갈까요? · "May I try on this shirt?"',sentence:"이 ___ 입어 봐도 돼요?",options:[{text:"셔츠",ok:!0},{text:"옷",ok:!1},{text:"바지",ok:!1}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Invite someone to try something on (한번 입어 보세요)",id:"chapter-51-b9",chapterId:"chapter-51",index:8},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 한 치수 작은 것도 보여 주시겠어요?",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"한 치수 작은 것도 보여 주시겠어요?",chunks:["민서는 면접에 입을 셔츠를 사러 갔어요. 먼저 직원에게 '이 셔츠 입어 봐도 돼요?'라고 물었어요. 피팅룸에서 입어 보니 사이즈가 조금 커 보였어요. 그래서 한 치수 작은 것도 보여 달라고 했어요.","직원은 면접에는 이 셔츠보다 남색 셔츠가 더 단정해 보인다고 말했어요. 민서는 그 셔츠가 잘 어울리고 사이즈도 맞아서 샀어요. 마지막으로 교환이나 환불이 되는지도 확인했어요."],translation:"Minseo went to buy a shirt for an interview. First, Minseo asked a staff member if trying on the shirt was allowed. After trying it on in the fitting room, the size looked a little big, so Minseo asked to see one size smaller. The staff member said a navy shirt looked neater for an interview. It suited Minseo and the size fit, so Minseo bought it. Finally, Minseo checked whether exchange or refund was possible.",qas:[{q:"민서는 무엇을 사러 갔어요?",a:"면접에 입을 셔츠를 사러 갔어요."},{q:"셔츠는 어떻게 보였어요?",a:"사이즈가 조금 커 보였어요."},{q:"마지막으로 무엇을 확인했어요?",a:"교환이나 환불이 되는지 확인했어요."}]},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"민서는 면접에 입을 ___를 사러 갔어요.",options:[{text:"옷",ok:!1},{text:"바지",ok:!1},{text:"셔츠",ok:!0}],explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"피팅룸에서 입어 보니 ___가 조금 커 보였어요.",options:[{text:"옷",ok:!1},{text:"티셔츠",ok:!1},{text:"사이즈",ok:!0}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Ask a clerk politely for help (다른 사이즈를 보여 주시겠어요?)",id:"chapter-51-b10",chapterId:"chapter-51",index:9},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence asks permission to try on a shirt?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"이 셔츠 입어 봐도 돼요?",ok:!0},{text:"이 셔츠 신어 봐도 돼요?",ok:!1},{text:"이 셔츠 입어 보세요?",ok:!1}],explanation:"입다 is for clothing, and -아/어 봐도 돼요? asks permission."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence asks for one size smaller most deferentially?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"한 치수 작은 것도 보여 주시겠어요?",ok:!0},{text:"한 치수 작은 것도 보여 주세요.",ok:!1},{text:"한 치수 작은 것도 잘 어울려요?",ok:!1}],explanation:"Both 보여 주세요 and 보여 주시겠어요? are polite; the indirect -주시겠어요? form is more deferential."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence is about style, not size?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"이 옷이 잘 어울려요.",ok:!0},{text:"사이즈가 맞아요.",ok:!1},{text:"한 치수 커요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"어울리다 describes whether something suits someone. 맞다 is about fit."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"셔츠___ 입어 봐도 돼요? (May I try on the shirt?)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"를",ok:!0},{text:"가",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"입다 takes the clothing item as an object, so 셔츠를 fits. 가 would make the shirt the subject."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence means “It looks too big”?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"너무 커 보여요.",ok:!0},{text:"너무 크게 입어요.",ok:!1},{text:"너무 큰 봐요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-아/어 보이다 describes how something appears. 커 보여요 is the natural “looks big.”"}],canDo:"Say how clothes or a person look (따뜻해 보여요, 예뻐 보여요)",id:"chapter-51-b11",chapterId:"chapter-51",index:10}]},{id:"chapter-52",number:52,title:"N 주세요 · V-아/어 주세요",goal:"Order Korean barbecue with 인분, 공기밥, 밑반찬, and N 주세요.",biteCount:11,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 삼겹살~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"삼겹살",romanization:"samgyeopsal",en:"grilled pork belly",pos:"noun",nuance:"삼겹살 literally breaks down as 삼(三, three) + 겹(layer) + 살(flesh) — 'three-layered meat' — naming the alternating stripes of fat and lean in pork belly, and it is the single most iconic Korean BBQ cut and a default casual group meal. Don't confuse it with 목살 (pork neck/collar, leaner) or 오겹살 (a five-layer belly cut with the skin on); waiters list these as separate menu lines. Register-wise the word itself is neutral, but the situation is firmly casual — friends, 회식 work dinners, late nights with soju — never a formal banquet. The pitfall for English speakers is the counter: 'a piece of pork belly' tempts you into 삼겹살 한 개, but you must order by 인분 (삼겹살 2인분), and you say 구워 먹다 (grill-and-eat) rather than the English 'have a barbecue,' because here you do the grilling yourself."},sentence:{ko:"삼겹살 두 인분 주세요.",en:"Please give us two servings of samgyeopsal."},target:"삼겹살",options:["grilled pork belly","bowl of rice","basic side dishes"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-802."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"인분",romanization:"inbun",en:"serving; portion",pos:"counter",nuance:"명 counts people; 인분 counts portions. 두 명 왔어요 says two people came, but 두 인분 주세요 orders two servings."},sentence:{ko:"삼겹살 두 인분하고 공기밥 하나 주세요.",en:"Please give us two servings of samgyeopsal and one bowl of rice."},target:"인분",options:["serving; portion","grilled pork belly","bowl of rice"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch52-inbun."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"공기밥",romanization:"gonggibap",en:"bowl of rice",pos:"noun",nuance:"밥 can mean cooked rice or a meal. 공기밥 is the restaurant item, a separate bowl of rice."},sentence:{ko:"공기밥 하나 주세요.",en:"Please give us one bowl of rice."},target:"공기밥",options:["bowl of rice","grilled pork belly","basic side dishes"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch52-gonggibap."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"밑반찬",romanization:"mitbanchan",en:"basic side dishes",pos:"noun",nuance:"밑반찬 is not the main dish. It refers to small supporting dishes such as kimchi, vegetables, or pickles served with the meal."},sentence:{ko:"밑반찬도 좀 더 주세요.",en:"Please give us a little more side dishes too."},target:"밑반찬",options:["grilled pork belly","bowl of rice","basic side dishes"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch52-mitbanchan."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"고기",romanization:"gogi",en:"meat",pos:"noun",nuance:"In everyday speech 고기 by default means red land meat — usually 돼지고기(pork) or 소고기(beef) — so 고기 먹으러 가자 implies a BBQ, not seafood. To be specific you compound the animal onto 고기: 닭고기(chicken), 소고기(beef), 돼지고기(pork). The words to contrast are 생선(fish as food) and 해산물(seafood), which Koreans do NOT file under 고기; chicken sits on the edge, so people usually say 닭고기 rather than bare 고기 for it. The set cooking verb is 굽다, a ㅂ-irregular that becomes 구워요/구웠어요 (not 'gubeoyo'). The English-speaker pitfall is the English umbrella sense of 'meat' that includes fish and poultry — in Korean, unmarked 고기 leans strongly toward pork/beef, and you should name the animal when you mean otherwise."},sentence:{ko:"고기를 굽고 마늘도 같이 구워요.",en:"We grill the meat and the garlic together too."},target:"고기",options:["meat","grilled pork belly","bowl of rice"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-040."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"굽다",romanization:"gupda",en:"to grill; roast; bake",pos:"verb",nuance:"굽다 is a ㅂ-irregular verb: the dictionary ㅂ becomes 우 before any vowel ending, so the 요-form is 구워요 (not the textbook-looking 굽어요), past 구웠어요, future 구울 거예요 — memorize the 요-form and the rest follows. Meaning-wise it is dry-heat cooking with direct radiant heat: grilling meat, roasting, AND oven-baking bread all fall under this one word, which surprises English speakers who keep three separate verbs. Contrast it sharply with its kitchen neighbours: 굽다 uses no water and little-to-no oil (just the surface), whereas 볶다 tosses food in a little oil and 튀기다 submerges it in lots of oil. It is the single most useful cooking verb for a foreigner here because 구이 (grilled dishes) and Korean BBQ are everywhere. The classic learner trap is writing 굽어요/굽었어요 by analogy with regular verbs — say 구워요/구웠어요."},sentence:{ko:"고기를 굽고 마늘도 같이 구워요.",en:"We grill the meat and the garlic together too."},target:"굽고",options:["to be cooked; done","to cut","to grill; roast; bake"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-verb-086."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"삼겹살 두 인분하고 공기밥 하나 주세요.",en:"Please give us two servings of samgyeopsal and one bowl of rice."},hl:"삼겹살"}],canDo:"Order an item with a quantity (삼겹살 이 인분 주세요)",id:"chapter-52-b1",chapterId:"chapter-52",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N 주세요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N 주세요",sub:"Ordering an Item",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"여기",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"김치찌개",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"하나",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"주세요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"여기 김치찌개 하나 주세요.",en:"One kimchi stew here, please.",romanization:"yeogi gimchijjigae hana juseyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"이거",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"주세요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"이거 주세요.",en:"This one, please.",romanization:"igeo juseyo."}],rule:{name:"N 주세요",rows:[{when:"Item alone",add:"N + 주세요",ex:"물 → 물 주세요 (Water, please.)"},{when:"Item + amount",add:"N + 수량 + 주세요",ex:"커피 두 잔 → 커피 두 잔 주세요 (Two coffees, please.)"},{when:"Pointing at the menu",add:"이거 + 주세요",ex:"이거 → 이거 주세요 (This one, please.)"},{when:'Softer "could you give me"',add:"N + 좀 + 주세요",ex:"물 → 물 좀 주세요 (Some water, please.)"}]},more:{func:'Use N 주세요 to ask someone to give you a thing. In a restaurant or café this is your basic ordering phrase: name the item, optionally with a number, then add 주세요 ("please give me"). It is polite and works with any server.',funcLead:"Use N 주세요 to ask someone to give you a thing.",keyPoint:{label:"주세요 vs. 주문할게요",body:`주세요 literally means "give me," so you attach it to the THING (커피 주세요). If you want to say "I'll order," that's the verb 주문하다 → 주문할게요. Beginners only need N 주세요 to order; save 주문할게요 for flagging the server that you're ready.`},pronunciation:"주세요 is read [주세요], no special linking. Watch the counter 한 개: say 한 / 개 as two clear beats — learners squash it into 항개. Keep the ㄴ of 한 crisp before 개.",pitfall:{wrong:"저는 커피 주세요.",right:"커피 주세요.",explanation:'English speakers add "I" (저는) out of habit, but 주세요 already means "give ME," so 저는 is redundant and sounds odd. Just name the item + 주세요.'}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Two colas, please.",sentence:"콜라 두 개 ___.",options:[{text:"고",ok:!1},{text:"아서",ok:!1},{text:"주세요",ok:!0}],explanation:"개 is the all-purpose counter for canned/bottled drinks; 잔 is for glasses you pour."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "One kimchi stew here, please."',tokens:["여기","김치찌개","하나","주세요."],correct:"여기 김치찌개 하나 주세요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"커피 주세요.",ok:!0},{text:"저는 커피 주세요.",ok:!1}],explanation:'English speakers add "I" (저는) out of habit, but 주세요 already means "give ME," so 저는 is redundant and sounds odd. Just name the item + 주세요.'}],canDo:"Order an item with a quantity (삼겹살 이 인분 주세요)",id:"chapter-52-b2",chapterId:"chapter-52",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 익다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"익다",romanization:"ikda",en:"to be cooked; done",pos:"verb",nuance:"익다 is intransitive: 고기가 익어요. 익히다 is transitive: 고기를 익혀요, to cook something through."},sentence:{ko:"고기가 익었으니까 이제 잘라 주세요.",en:"The meat is cooked now, so please cut it."},target:"익었",options:["to grill; roast; bake","to cut","to be cooked; done"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch52-ikda."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"자르다",romanization:"jareuda",en:"to cut",pos:"verb",nuance:"자르다 means to cut THROUGH and separate something with a blade or scissors — paper, hair, cake, rope, nails — i.e. to sever into pieces. Distinguish it from 썰다, which is 'to slice' food into many pieces on a board (양파를 썰다, 'chop an onion'), and from 끊다, which is 'to cut off / break' a connection, line, or supply (전화를 끊다 'hang up', 담배를 끊다 'quit smoking') — note 머리를 자르다 (cut hair) but never 머리를 끊다. There's also 깎다 for shaving/peeling/paring (사과를 깎다 'peel an apple', 수염을 깎다 'shave'). It's a 르-irregular: 자르 + 아요 → 잘라요 (ㅡ drops, ㄹ doubles), past 잘랐어요, but consonant endings keep it whole (자르고, 자를게요, 자릅니다). The big pitfall is 자르아요 (wrong) instead of 잘라요. Also, English 'cut prices' or 'cut down' does not map to 자르다 — reducing an amount is 줄이다, and cutting off a service is 끊다."},sentence:{ko:"고기가 익었으니까 이제 잘라 주세요.",en:"The meat is cooked now, so please cut it."},target:"잘라",options:["to cut","to grill; roast; bake","to be cooked; done"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-verb-479."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"마늘",romanization:"maneul",en:"garlic",pos:"noun",nuance:"마늘 is a native Korean word for garlic, and few foods are more central to Korean cooking — it's the aromatic base (with 양파 and 파) of countless dishes, eaten raw with grilled meat, pickled, or 다지다 (minced) into almost everything. Crucially, a single clove is counted with 쪽 (마늘 한 쪽), while a whole bulb/head is 마늘 한 통 — using 개 for garlic sounds wrong, so learn 쪽 (clove) vs 통 (head). It ends in ㄹ, so particles are 마늘을 / 마늘이, and say it as two clean syllables 'ma-neul.' The English-speaker pitfall is the counter (defaulting to 개 instead of 쪽/통), pronouncing 맵다 regularly (the correct form is 마늘이 매워요, ㅂ-irregular), and over-pluralizing (no 마늘들). It collocates with 다지다 (mince), 까다 (to peel a clove), 넣다 (add), and 빻다 (to crush/pound)."},sentence:{ko:"마늘도 같이 구워요.",en:"We grill the garlic together too."},target:"마늘",options:["grilled pork belly","bowl of rice","garlic"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-203."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"상추",romanization:"sangchu",en:"lettuce for wraps",pos:"noun",nuance:"상추 is the soft, floppy leaf lettuce used to wrap grilled meat as 쌈 — and the sharp line to draw is 상추 vs 양배추 vs 양상추: 상추 is the tender wrapping lettuce, 양배추 is the round head cabbage, and 양상추 is crunchy iceberg/head lettuce for salads. They are different vegetables, so the 양- prefix (a 'Western/foreign' marker, as in 양파 onion, 양식 Western food) is doing real work — 상추 vs 양상추 is not just casual vs formal, it's two distinct plants. Register is plain everyday vocabulary; the only social note is the BBQ ritual phrase. The single most useful collocation a resident learns is '상추 좀 더 주세요' — refills of 상추 are free at almost every 고깃집, so this one line earns its keep nightly. Other collocations: 상추쌈, 상추를 씻다, 상추가 아삭하다/싱싱하다. English-speaker pitfall: 'lettuce' in English defaults to the crunchy salad iceberg, but 상추 is the soft wrap leaf, so don't expect a Caesar-salad crunch; and English speakers often hesitate to ask for more because in many countries refills cost extra — in Korea, just say 더 주세요. 상추 is a native Korean word, no hanja."},sentence:{ko:"상추하고 쌈장도 주세요.",en:"Please give us lettuce and ssamjang too."},target:"상추",options:["grilled pork belly","lettuce for wraps","bowl of rice"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-532."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"쌈장",romanization:"ssamjang",en:"ssamjang sauce",pos:"noun",nuance:"쌈장 is not the same as 고추장. It is a mixed paste commonly used for wraps with grilled meat."},sentence:{ko:"쌈장이 짜니까 조금만 넣으세요.",en:"The ssamjang is salty, so put in only a little."},target:"쌈장",options:["grilled pork belly","ssamjang sauce","bowl of rice"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch52-ssamjang."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"맵다",romanization:"maepda",en:"to be spicy",pos:"adjective",nuance:"맵다 is ㅂ irregular: 매워요, 매운 음식. It is especially useful for food restrictions, because 매운 음식은 못 먹어요 is a survival sentence."},sentence:{ko:"양념이 매워서 공기밥을 같이 먹어요.",en:"The seasoning is spicy, so I eat it with rice."},target:"매워",options:["to be salty","to be spicy","grilled pork belly"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-adjective-035."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"고기를 굽고 마늘도 같이 구워요.",en:"Let's grill the meat and the garlic together too."},hl:"마늘"}],canDo:"Ask the server to do something (잘라 주세요, 바꿔 주세요)",id:"chapter-52-b3",chapterId:"chapter-52",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-아/어 주세요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-아/어 주세요",sub:"Asking for an Action",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"반찬",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"좀",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"더",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"갖다",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"주세요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"반찬 좀 더 갖다 주세요.",en:"Could you bring more side dishes, please.",romanization:"banchan jom deo gatda juseyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"고기",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"좀",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"잘라",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"주세요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"고기 좀 잘라 주세요.",en:"Please cut the meat for us.",romanization:"gogi jom jalla juseyo."}],rule:{name:"V-아/어 주세요",rows:[{when:"Stem vowel ㅏ/ㅗ",add:"stem + 아 주세요",ex:"닦다 → 닦아 주세요 (Please wipe it.)"},{when:"Other stem vowels",add:"stem + 어 주세요",ex:"바꾸다 → 바꿔 주세요 (Please change it.)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"해 주세요",ex:"계산하다 → 계산해 주세요 (Please ring it up.)"},{when:"ㅂ irregular",add:"ㅂ→우, then 어",ex:"굽다 → 구워 주세요 (Please grill it.)"}]},more:{func:"Use V-아/어 주세요 to ask someone to DO something for you, not just hand you a thing. Take a verb stem, add the 아/어 connector, then 주세요. In a restaurant this is how you ask the server to bring, change, cut, or wrap something.",funcLead:"Use V-아/어 주세요 to ask someone to DO something for you, not just hand you a thing.",keyPoint:{label:"N 주세요 vs. V-아/어 주세요",body:`N 주세요 asks for a THING (물 주세요 = give me water). V-아/어 주세요 asks for an ACTION (포장해 주세요 = pack it). If the English word is a noun ("water," "the menu"), use N 주세요; if it's a verb ("cut it," "wrap it," "bring it"), use V-아/어 주세요.`},pronunciation:"After a stem ending in ㄹ like 만들다, the form is 만들어 주세요 [만드러 주세요] — the ㄹ links into the next syllable. Say it smoothly as 만드러, not 만들-어.",pitfall:{wrong:"포장하 주세요.",right:"포장해 주세요.",explanation:"Learners attach 주세요 to the bare stem (포장하-), but you must first conjugate with 아/어: 하다 → 해. The connector vowel is not optional — 하 주세요 is ungrammatical; it must be 해 주세요."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Please wrap this to go.",sentence:"이거 포장해 ___.",options:[{text:"고",ok:!1},{text:"주세요",ok:!0},{text:"아서",ok:!1}],explanation:'포장하다 ("to pack") → 포장해 주세요 is the standard way to ask for takeout.'},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Please make it less spicy.",sentence:"덜 맵게 해 ___.",options:[{text:"고",ok:!1},{text:"아서",ok:!1},{text:"주세요",ok:!0}],explanation:'하다 → 해 주세요; 덜 맵게 ("less spicily") tells the kitchen how to cook it.'},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Could you bring more side dishes, please."',tokens:["반찬","좀","더","갖다","주세요."],correct:"반찬 좀 더 갖다 주세요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Please cut the meat for us."',tokens:["고기","좀","잘라","주세요."],correct:"고기 좀 잘라 주세요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Ask the server to do something (잘라 주세요, 바꿔 주세요)",id:"chapter-52-b4",chapterId:"chapter-52",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 짜다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"짜다",romanization:"jjada",en:"to be salty",pos:"adjective",nuance:"짜다 means having a strong salt taste, and the practical contrast for cooking is 짜다 vs 간이 맞다: 짜다 says there is too much salt, while 간이 맞다 means the seasoning is just right (and 싱겁다 means too little). The salty thing takes 이/가 (국이 짜요). Conjugation is regular for an ㅏ-stem: 짜 + 아요 contracts to 짜요 (not 짜아요), past 짰어요. Two pitfalls for English speakers: first, never write 짜아요. Second, 짜다 has a very common figurative meaning — 'stingy / tightfisted' — so 그 사람 진짜 짜 means 'that person is so cheap', nothing to do with food; English 'salty' meaning 'bitter/annoyed' does NOT transfer to Korean 짜다, so do not use it that way. When you want to talk about how salty you eat in general, use the adverb 짜게 (짜게 먹다), which health advice constantly repeats."},sentence:{ko:"쌈장이 좀 짜요.",en:"The ssamjang is a bit salty."},target:"짜요",options:["to be spicy","to be salty","grilled pork belly"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1221."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"양념",romanization:"yangnyeom",en:"seasoning; marinade",pos:"noun",nuance:"양념 is the native-Korean word for a whole blend of seasonings — soy sauce, garlic, sesame oil, sugar, chili — mixed to flavor a dish, NOT a single spice. This is the key contrast: one spice is a 향신료 or just its name (소금, 후추), but the prepared mixture you rub or pour on is 양념. The word also signals a style: 양념치킨 is chicken coated in sweet-spicy 양념 sauce, opposite 후라이드 (plain fried). 양념 collocates with 만들다 (make), 무치다 (toss/dress vegetables in it), 재우다 (marinate), 바르다 (brush on), and the verb 양념하다 (to season). The English-speaker pitfall is assuming 양념 = one spice; if you only added salt, that's not really 양념 — 양념 implies a composed blend, and you marinate IN it with 에 (양념에 재우다)."},sentence:{ko:"양념이 매워서 공기밥을 같이 먹어요.",en:"The seasoning is spicy, so I eat it with rice."},target:"양념",options:["seasoning; marinade","grilled pork belly","bowl of rice"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1915."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"국물",romanization:"gungmul",en:"broth; soup liquid",pos:"noun",nuance:"국물 is the liquid part of soup or stew, contrasted with the solid 건더기. Both 국물을 먹다 and 국물을 마시다 are natural: 먹다 treats it as part of the meal, while 마시다 focuses on drinking the liquid."},sentence:{ko:"국물이 뜨거우니까 천천히 드세요.",en:"The broth is hot, so eat slowly."},target:"국물",options:["grilled pork belly","bowl of rice","broth; soup liquid"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1916."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"포장해 주세요",romanization:"pojanghae juseyo",en:"Please pack it to go.",pos:"expression",nuance:"포장해 주세요 is the all-purpose 'to go' or 'pack it up' request — for a café drink ordered fresh, a bakery item, or restaurant leftovers. 포장 (包裝) literally means 'wrapping/packaging.' Plain polite -요 for all staff; the even shorter clipped form 포장이요 works as a quick answer to a binary dine-in question; 반말 포장해 줘 is friends-only. The near alternative is 가져갈게요 ('I'll take it away') — the dividing line is who does the action: 포장해 주세요 asks staff to actively pack the item, while 가져갈게요 simply announces you're taking it; in practice both achieve takeout but 포장해 주세요 is more specific. Another alternative is 싸 주세요 ('wrap it up') — homey and natural for restaurant leftovers but slightly informal for a fresh café order. The English-speaker mistake is defaulting to 테이크아웃 해 주세요 — understood at chains but 포장 is the fully native word, and 테이크아웃 컵 refers specifically to the cup type, not the whole transaction."},sentence:{ko:"남은 건 포장해 주세요.",en:"Please pack the leftovers to go."},target:"포장해 주세요",options:["grilled pork belly","Please pack it to go.","serving; portion"],note:"Linked to expression card expr-014."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"남은 건 포장해 주세요.",en:"Please pack the leftovers to go."},hl:"포장해 주세요"}],canDo:"Chain food actions in order (고기를 굽고 자르고)",id:"chapter-52-b5",chapterId:"chapter-52",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-고",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-고",sub:"Linking Food Actions in Order",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"밥을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"먹",mid:"고",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"커피를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"마셔요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"밥을 먹고 커피를 마셔요.",en:"I eat a meal and (then) drink coffee.",romanization:"babeul meokgo keopireul masyeoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"손을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"씻",mid:"고",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"밥을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"먹어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"손을 씻고 밥을 먹어요.",en:"I wash my hands and eat.",romanization:"soneul ssitgo babeul meogeoyo."}],rule:{name:"V-고",rows:[{when:"Vowel-ending stem",add:"stem + 고",ex:"마시다 → 마시고 (drink and...)"},{when:"Consonant-ending stem",add:"stem + 고",ex:"먹다 → 먹고 (eat and...)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"하 + 고",ex:"요리하다 → 요리하고 (cook and...)"},{when:"Tense goes on the LAST verb only",add:"V-고 + V-past/future",ex:"먹고 잤어요 (ate and slept)"}]},more:{func:`Use V-고 to chain two verbs done by the same person, usually in sequence: "do A and (then) B." Attach 고 directly to the verb stem — no vowel changes, no tense on the first verb. It's the everyday way to describe eating-and-doing routines.`,funcLead:'Use V-고 to chain two verbs done by the same person, usually in sequence: "do A and (then) B." Attach 고 directly to the verb stem — no vowel changes, no tense o…',keyPoint:{label:"V-고 vs. V-아서/어서 (sequence)",body:'Both can mean "and then," but 고 just lists separate actions in order (먹고 마셔요 = eat, then drink — different objects). 아서/어서 is used when action B builds on A with the same object (사서 먹어요 = buy it and eat it). For loosely sequenced food steps, 고 is the safe default.'},pronunciation:"After a stem ending in ㄱ, like 먹다, 먹고 is pronounced [먹꼬] — the 고 tenses to 꼬. Likewise 씻고 [씯꼬]. The 고 itself never changes spelling.",pitfall:{wrong:"Past tense may appear only on the final verb with -고.",right:"밥을 먹고 커피를 마셨어요. / 밥을 먹었고 커피도 마셨어요.",explanation:"Both are grammatical. In a simple sequence, the final past ending can set the time for both actions, so 먹고 … 마셨어요 is concise and common. When listing completed facts separately or emphasizing each event, 먹었고 … 마셨어요 is also natural."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I cooked ramyeon and took out the kimchi.",sentence:"라면을 끓이___ 김치를 꺼냈어요.",options:[{text:"고",ok:!0},{text:"주세요",ok:!1},{text:"아서",ok:!1}],explanation:"Past tense 꺼냈어요 sits only on the last verb; 끓이고 carries no -었-."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"We grill the meat and wrap it in lettuce to eat.",sentence:"고기를 굽___ 상추에 싸 먹어요.",options:[{text:"주세요",ok:!1},{text:"아서",ok:!1},{text:"고",ok:!0}],explanation:"Describes the two-step BBQ routine; 굽고 links the grilling to the eating."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I eat a meal and (then) drink coffee."',tokens:["밥을","먹고","커피를","마셔요."],correct:"밥을 먹고 커피를 마셔요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I wash my hands and eat."',tokens:["손을","씻고","밥을","먹어요."],correct:"손을 씻고 밥을 먹어요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Chain food actions in order (고기를 굽고 자르고)",id:"chapter-52-b6",chapterId:"chapter-52",index:5},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A/V-아서/어서",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"A/V-아서/어서",sub:"Reason and Result",rows:[{when:"Stem vowel ㅏ/ㅗ",add:"stem + 아서",ex:"비싸다 → 비싸서 (because it's expensive)"},{when:"Other stem vowels",add:"stem + 어서",ex:"맛있다 → 맛있어서 (because it's tasty)"},{when:"하다 words",add:"해서",ex:"매콤하다 → 매콤해서 (because it's nicely spicy)"},{when:"Past/future reason",add:"NO tense on the -아서/어서 clause",ex:"배가 고파서 먹었어요 (not 고팠어서)"}],examples:[{ko:"너무 매워서 못 먹겠어요.",en:"It's so spicy that I can't eat it.",romanization:"neomu maewoseo mot meokgesseoyo.",note:"맵다 → 매워서 (ㅂ irregular); the spiciness is the reason for not eating."},{ko:"이 집 김치찌개가 맛있어서 자주 와요.",en:"This place's kimchi stew is delicious, so I come often.",romanization:"i jip gimchijjigaega masisseoseo jaju wayo.",note:"맛있어서 explains the reason for the result 자주 와요 (come often)."}],more:{func:`Use A/V-아서/어서 to give a reason: "because A, B." The first clause is the cause, the second is the result. Attach the 아/어 connector + 서 to the stem. With food you use it to explain why you can or can't eat something, or why you like a dish.`,funcLead:'Use A/V-아서/어서 to give a reason: "because A, B." The first clause is the cause, the second is the result.',keyPoint:{label:"-아서/어서 vs. -(으)니까 (reason)",body:`Both mean "because," but -아서/어서 cannot be followed by a command or suggestion, and never takes past/future tense in the reason clause. Use -아서/어서 for a plain reason → result (매워서 못 먹어요). When you want to follow with a command or suggestion ("It's spicy, so order something else"), switch to -(으)니까.`},pronunciation:"맛있어서 is pronounced [마시써서] — the ㅅ of 맛 links forward and the double ㅆ stays tense. Say it as one smooth 마시써서, not 맛-있-어-서.",pitfall:{wrong:"배가 고팠어서 라면을 먹었어요.",right:"배가 고파서 라면을 먹었어요.",explanation:'English puts past tense on both clauses ("was hungry, so ate"), so learners write 고팠어서. The -아서/어서 reason clause never carries -었-; the tense lives only in the result clause (먹었어요).'}}},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "It's so spicy that I can't eat it."`,tokens:["너무","매워서","못","먹겠어요."],correct:"너무 매워서 못 먹겠어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "This place's kimchi stew is delicious, so I come often."`,tokens:["이","집","김치찌개가","맛있어서","자주","와요."],correct:"이 집 김치찌개가 맛있어서 자주 와요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"배가 고팠어서 라면을 먹었어요.",ok:!1},{text:"배가 고파서 라면을 먹었어요.",ok:!0}],explanation:'English puts past tense on both clauses ("was hungry, so ate"), so learners write 고팠어서. The -아서/어서 reason clause never carries -었-; the tense lives only in the result clause (먹었어요).'}],canDo:"Give a food reason and result (매워서 물을 마셔요)",id:"chapter-52-b7",chapterId:"chapter-52",index:6},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-(으)ㄹ게요",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"V-(으)ㄹ게요",sub:"Saying What You'll Have",rows:[{when:"Vowel-ending stem",add:"stem + ㄹ게요",ex:"시키다 → 시킬게요 (I'll order it.)"},{when:"Consonant-ending stem",add:"stem + 을게요",ex:"먹다 → 먹을게요 (I'll have it.)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"할게요",ex:"주문하다 → 주문할게요 (I'll order.)"},{when:"ㄹ-ending stem (drop nothing extra)",add:"stem + 게요",ex:"만들다 → 만들게요 (I'll make it.)"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 비빔밥 먹을게요.",en:"I'll have bibimbap.",romanization:"jeoneun bibimbap meogeulgeyo.",note:'Announcing your own choice at the table; 저는 marks "as for me" when each person picks a dish.'},{ko:"저기요, 주문할게요.",en:"Excuse me, we're ready to order.",romanization:"jeogiyo, jumunhalgeyo.",note:"저기요 calls the server; 주문할게요 signals you've decided and want to order now."}],more:{func:`Use V-(으)ㄹ게요 to announce a decision you're making on the spot — "I'll have / I'll do ___." At the table it's how you tell the server or your friends what you've chosen, with a nuance of committing to it right now. Add -ㄹ게요 to a vowel-ending stem, -을게요 to a consonant-ending stem.`,funcLead:`Use V-(으)ㄹ게요 to announce a decision you're making on the spot — "I'll have / I'll do ___." At the table it's how you tell the server or your friends what you'v…`,keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄹ게요 vs. -(으)ㄹ래요",body:"-(으)ㄹ게요 is you committing to do something, often for the listener's sake (저는 비빔밥 먹을게요 = I've decided, I'll have bibimbap). -(으)ㄹ래요 is more about your wish/preference and can sound like you're asking or stating want (비빔밥 먹을래요 = I want bibimbap / shall I?). To simply state your order, -(으)ㄹ게요 is the smoother choice."},pronunciation:"-ㄹ게요 is pronounced [ㄹ께요] — the ㄱ tenses to ㄲ. So 먹을게요 is [머글께요] and 갈게요 is [갈께요]. The spelling stays 게, but you say 께.",pitfall:{wrong:"저는 비빔밥 먹을 거예요.",right:"저는 비빔밥 먹을게요.",explanation:`-(으)ㄹ 거예요 states a future plan as information ("I'm going to eat bibimbap") and sounds detached when ordering. -(으)ㄹ게요 directs the decision at the listener — the server or your friends — which is what ordering needs. For "I'll have ___" at the table, use -(으)ㄹ게요.`}}},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I'll have bibimbap."`,tokens:["저는","비빔밥","먹을게요."],correct:"저는 비빔밥 먹을게요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"저는 비빔밥 먹을게요.",ok:!0},{text:"저는 비빔밥 먹을 거예요.",ok:!1}],explanation:`-(으)ㄹ 거예요 states a future plan as information ("I'm going to eat bibimbap") and sounds detached when ordering. -(으)ㄹ게요 directs the decision at the listener — the server or your friends — which is what ordering needs. For "I'll have ___" at the table, use -(으)ㄹ게요.`}],canDo:"Announce what you'll have on the spot (저는 비빔밥을 먹을게요)",id:"chapter-52-b8",chapterId:"chapter-52",index:7},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Coworkers order samgyeopsal and manage the table grill.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"삼겹살 두 인분하고 공기밥 하나 주세요.",en:"Please give us two servings of samgyeopsal and one bowl of rice.",romanization:"samgyeopsal du inbunhago gonggibap hana juseyo."},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"밑반찬도 좀 더 주세요.",en:"Please give us a little more side dishes too.",romanization:"mitbanchando jom deo juseyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"고기를 굽고 마늘도 같이 구워요.",en:"Let's grill the meat and the garlic together too.",romanization:"gogireul gupgo maneuldo gachi guwoyo."},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"고기가 익었으니까 이제 잘라 주세요.",en:"The meat is cooked now, so please cut it.",romanization:"gogiga igeosseunikka ije jalla juseyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"남은 건 포장해 주세요.",en:"Please pack the leftovers to go.",romanization:"nameun geon pojanghae juseyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'When the meat is cooked, please cut it.'",tokens:["고기가 익으면","잘라 주세요"],correct:"고기가 익으면 잘라 주세요",explanation:"-으면 sets the condition. 익으면 means when it is cooked, and 잘라 주세요 is the request."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"누가 한 말일까요? · Who said this?",sentence:"삼겹살 두 인분하고 공기밥 하나 주세요.",options:[{text:"지훈",ok:!1},{text:"민서",ok:!0}],explanation:"Please give us two servings of samgyeopsal and one bowl of rice."},{kind:"drill",prompt:'무엇이 들어갈까요? · "Please give us a little more side dishes too."',sentence:"___도 좀 더 주세요.",options:[{text:"밑반찬",ok:!0},{text:"삼겹살",ok:!1},{text:"공기밥",ok:!1}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Order an item with a quantity (삼겹살 이 인분 주세요)",id:"chapter-52-b9",chapterId:"chapter-52",index:8},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 삼겹살 두 인분 주세요",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"삼겹살 두 인분 주세요",chunks:["민서는 회사 사람들과 삼겹살집에 갔어요. 먼저 삼겹살 두 인분하고 공기밥 하나를 주문했어요. 밑반찬이 부족해서 직원에게 밑반찬도 좀 더 달라고 했어요. 고기를 굽고 마늘도 같이 구웠어요.","고기가 익었으니까 먹기 좋게 잘라 달라고 했어요. 양념이 조금 매워서 공기밥을 같이 먹었고, 쌈장은 짜니까 조금만 넣었어요. 마지막에는 남은 음식을 포장해 달라고 했어요."],translation:"Minseo went to a samgyeopsal restaurant with coworkers. First, Minseo ordered two servings of samgyeopsal and one bowl of rice. Because the side dishes were not enough, Minseo asked the staff for more. They grilled the meat and garlic together. When the meat was cooked, Minseo asked someone to cut it into easy-to-eat pieces. The seasoning was a little spicy, so they ate it with rice, and since the ssamjang was salty, they added only a little. At the end, they asked to pack the leftovers.",qas:[{q:"민서는 무엇을 주문했어요?",a:"삼겹살 두 인분하고 공기밥 하나를 주문했어요."},{q:"고기가 익었을 때 무엇을 부탁했어요?",a:"고기를 잘라 달라고 했어요."},{q:"왜 공기밥을 같이 먹었어요?",a:"양념이 조금 매워서 공기밥을 같이 먹었어요."}]},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"민서는 회사 사람들과 ___집에 갔어요.",options:[{text:"상추",ok:!1},{text:"국물",ok:!1},{text:"삼겹살",ok:!0}],explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"___이 부족해서 직원에게 밑반찬도 좀 더 달라고 했어요.",options:[{text:"밑반찬",ok:!0},{text:"상추",ok:!1},{text:"국물",ok:!1}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Ask the server to do something (잘라 주세요, 바꿔 주세요)",id:"chapter-52-b10",chapterId:"chapter-52",index:9},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which counter fits restaurant servings?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"삼겹살 두 인분",ok:!0},{text:"삼겹살 두 권",ok:!1},{text:"삼겹살 두 명",ok:!1}],explanation:"인분 counts food servings. 명 counts people."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence correctly separates cooking action and result?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"고기를 굽고, 고기가 익어요.",ok:!0},{text:"고기가 굽고, 고기를 익어요.",ok:!1},{text:"고기를 익고, 고기가 구워요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"굽다 takes the meat as object; 익다 uses the food as subject."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence gives a reason before a request?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"고기가 익었으니까 잘라 주세요.",ok:!0},{text:"고기가 익어서 잘라 주세요.",ok:!1},{text:"고기가 익고 잘라 주세요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-(으)니까 naturally supports a following request."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"삼겹살 2인분___ 주세요. (Two servings of pork belly, please.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"을",ok:!0},{text:"이",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"The ordered amount is the object of 주세요, so 을 is grammatical when the particle is shown. In restaurant speech, this particle is often dropped."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which counter fits Korean barbecue servings?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"인분",ok:!0},{text:"잔",ok:!1},{text:"권",ok:!1}],explanation:"Restaurant servings use 인분. 잔 counts cups, and 권 counts books."}],canDo:"Chain food actions in order (고기를 굽고 자르고)",id:"chapter-52-b11",chapterId:"chapter-52",index:10}]},{id:"chapter-53",number:53,title:"V-(으)ㄴ 적이 있어요 · A/V-는 것 같아요",goal:"Ask about watching experience with V(으)ㄴ 적이 있어요.",biteCount:13,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 드라마~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"드라마",romanization:"deurama",en:"TV drama; series",pos:"noun",nuance:"드라마 usually means a scripted series, not a one-off movie. 영화 is movie; 예능 is variety show."},sentence:{ko:"이 드라마 본 적 있어요?",en:"Have you watched this drama before?"},target:"드라마",options:["actor","TV drama; series","comment"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch53-deurama."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"배우",romanization:"baeu",en:"actor",pos:"noun",nuance:"배우 is 俳優 (both 俳 and 優 historically meant 'performer/entertainer'), an old Sino-Korean compound that already covers actors of any gender in film, TV drama, and theater — so there is no need to mark gender, and the English loan 액터 is essentially never used in normal speech. The thing an actor does is 연기 'acting' (연기를 하다 / 연기를 잘하다), not '배우를 하다'; that 연기 vs 배우 split (craft vs person) mirrors 요리 vs 요리사 and trips learners up. Distinguish 배우 from 가수 'singer', 개그맨/코미디언 'comedian', and the broader 연예인 'celebrity/entertainer', which includes all of them. The verb 나오다 ('come out') is the idiomatic way to say an actor 'appears in' a film or show. After the vowel, the copula is 배우예요, and one 배우가 된다."},sentence:{ko:"이 배우가 좋거든요.",en:"I like this actor, you see."},target:"배우",options:["TV drama; series","actor","comment"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-102."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"재미있다",romanization:"jaemiitda",en:"to be fun; interesting",pos:"adjective",nuance:"재미있다 is literally 재미 (fun, enjoyment) + 있다 (to exist) — 'fun exists [in it]' — which is exactly why it conjugates like 있다 and gives 재미있어요, and why the noun-modifying form is the 있다-style 재미있는 N (재미있는 영화), never 재미있은. It spans both English 'fun' and 'interesting': a party, a game, or a class can all be 재미있어요, so let context choose the English. The near-synonym to separate is 재미있다 vs 흥미롭다: 재미있다 is the warm, everyday 'enjoyable/entertaining' you say about a movie or a friend, while 흥미롭다 is a cooler, more bookish 'intriguing/of interest' you might use for a research finding or a phenomenon — calling a thrilling roller coaster 흥미로워요 sounds oddly academic. Its direct opposite is the single word 재미없다 (재미 + 없다, 'fun does not exist') = boring; do not say 안 재미있다 as your default negative — 재미없어요 is the natural 'it's boring', and 안 재미있어요 reads more like 'it's not [particularly] fun'. The big English-speaker pitfall is the conjugation: because the headword ends in 있다, learners over-apply -하다 or vowel rules and produce 재미있다요 or 재미있아요; the only correct polite form is 재미있어요, with past 재미있었어요."},sentence:{ko:"이 드라마 재미있는 것 같아요.",en:"I think this drama is fun."},target:"재미있",options:["TV drama; series","actor","to be fun; interesting"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1328."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"댓글",romanization:"daetgeul",en:"comment",pos:"noun",nuance:"댓글 is online. For a spoken comment or opinion, 말 or 의견 may fit better."},sentence:{ko:"댓글이 진짜 재미있거든요.",en:"The comments are really fun, you know."},target:"댓글",options:["comment","TV drama; series","actor"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch53-daetgeul."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"자막",romanization:"jamak",en:"subtitles; captions",pos:"noun",nuance:"자막 can mean subtitles for translation or captions on video. In streaming apps, 자막 설정 is subtitle settings."},sentence:{ko:"친구가 자막이 좋다고 했어요.",en:"My friend said the subtitles are good."},target:"자막",options:["subtitles; captions","TV drama; series","actor"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch53-jamak."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"이 드라마 본 적 있어요?",en:"Have you watched this drama before?"},hl:"드라마"}],canDo:"Say whether I have ever done something (본 적이 있어요/없어요)",id:"chapter-53-b1",chapterId:"chapter-53",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-(으)ㄴ 적이 있어요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-(으)ㄴ 적이 있어요",sub:"Have you ever…? (past experience)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"저는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"그",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"드라마를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"본",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"적이",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"있어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"저는 그 드라마를 본 적이 있어요.",en:"I have seen that drama before.",romanization:"jeoneun geu deuramareul bon jeogi isseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"그",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가수",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"콘서트에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"간",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"적이",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"있어요",post:"?",hit:!0}],ko:"그 가수 콘서트에 간 적이 있어요?",en:"Have you ever been to that singer's concert?",romanization:"geu gasu konseoteue gan jeogi isseoyo?"}],rule:{name:"V-(으)ㄴ 적이 있어요",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant",add:"+ -은 적이 있어요",ex:"듣다 → 들은 적이 있어요 (have heard it before)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ -ㄴ 적이 있어요",ex:"보다 → 본 적이 있어요 (have seen it before)"},{when:"Negative (never done it)",add:"replace 있어요 with 없어요",ex:"가다 → 간 적이 없어요 (have never gone)"}]},more:{func:"Says you have had the experience of doing something at least once. The negative, V-(으)ㄴ 적이 없어요, means you have never done it. It is about whether something has ever happened, not about one specific past event.",funcLead:"Says you have had the experience of doing something at least once.",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄴ 적이 있어요 vs. plain past -았/었어요",body:"Plain past 봤어요 reports one specific event ('I watched it yesterday'). 본 적이 있어요 reports the experience itself ('I have watched it at some point'). Use -(으)ㄴ 적이 있어요 when the time doesn't matter, only whether it has ever happened.",when:"",add:"",ex:""},pronunciation:"적이 is pronounced [저기] (jeogi): the final ㄱ of 적 links onto the 이 of the next syllable.",pitfall:{wrong:"저는 그 영화를 봤은 적이 있어요.",right:"저는 그 영화를 본 적이 있어요.",explanation:"English speakers add 었 because the meaning feels 'past.' But -(으)ㄴ already carries the past sense; attach it straight to the plain stem (보 → 본), never to the -았/었 form."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I have seen that drama before."',tokens:["저는","그","드라마를","본","적이","있어요."],correct:"저는 그 드라마를 본 적이 있어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Have you ever been to that singer's concert?" (그 시작 · Start with 그.)`,tokens:["그","가수","콘서트에","간","적이","있어요?"],correct:"그 가수 콘서트에 간 적이 있어요?",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"저는 그 영화를 본 적이 있어요.",ok:!0},{text:"저는 그 영화를 봤은 적이 있어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"English speakers add 었 because the meaning feels 'past.' But -(으)ㄴ already carries the past sense; attach it straight to the plain stem (보 → 본), never to the -았/었 form."}],canDo:"Say whether I have ever done something (본 적이 있어요/없어요)",id:"chapter-53-b2",chapterId:"chapter-53",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 영상~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"영상",romanization:"yeongsang",en:"video",pos:"noun",nuance:"영상 is Sino-Korean: 映 (영, 'reflect/project') + 像 (상, 'image'), literally a 'projected image', and it means a video clip — the kind you watch online, on your phone, or record yourself. Draw the line carefully against 영화 (映畫, 'projected picture'), a full cinema movie: a YouTube clip is an 영상, never an 영화. It also contrasts with the casual loanword 비디오, which now sounds dated (it evoked old videotapes) and with 동영상 (動映像, 'moving image'), a near-identical, slightly more technical synonym you'll see on menus and settings. The verb pairing is the pitfall: you watch a video with 보다 + object (영상을 보다), and crucially you 'shoot/take' one with 찍다 — 영상을 찍다 — not 만들다 unless you mean editing/producing it. English 'make a video' tempts learners into 영상을 만들다, but for simply recording, 찍다 is the natural choice."},sentence:{ko:"영상이 계속 끊기는데요.",en:"The video keeps cutting out, though."},target:"영상",options:["TV drama; series","video","actor"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-163."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"공유하다",romanization:"gongyuhada",en:"to share",pos:"verb",nuance:"공유하다 is broader than sending. 보내다 is to send; 공유하다 emphasizes making content available or passing it along."},sentence:{ko:"영상도 공유했어요?",en:"Did you share the video too?"},target:"공유했",options:["to share","to cut out; disconnect; buffer","to connect"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch53-gongyuhada and expression expr-565."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"끊기다",romanization:"kkeunhgida",en:"to cut out; disconnect; buffer",pos:"verb",nuance:"끊기다 is passive/intransitive: the video or connection cuts out. 끊다 is what someone actively cuts or ends."},sentence:{ko:"영상이 계속 끊기는데요.",en:"The video keeps cutting out, though."},target:"끊기",options:["to cut out; disconnect; buffer","to share","to connect"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch53-kkeunhgida."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"와이파이",romanization:"waipai",en:"Wi-Fi",pos:"noun",nuance:"와이파이 is the loanword for local wireless internet (the spoken form follows the English 'Wi-Fi'), and the key distinction English speakers must hold is 와이파이 vs 데이터: 와이파이 is the free signal you join from a router, while 데이터 is your paid mobile-data allowance — running out of one doesn't affect the other. It also differs from 인터넷, the network as a whole; 와이파이 is just one way to reach it. The pitfall is the connection particle: you connect 와이파이에 (to the wifi, a destination) with 연결하다, not 와이파이로, because here the wifi is where you're attaching, not a tool you act through. And like other tech that simply stops responding, a dead signal is 와이파이가 안 돼요 (it won't function), not 와이파이가 없어요, which claims there's no network at all."},sentence:{ko:"와이파이를 다시 연결해 보세요.",en:"Try reconnecting the Wi-Fi."},target:"와이파이",options:["Wi-Fi","TV drama; series","actor"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-162."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"연결하다",romanization:"yeongyeolhada",en:"to connect",pos:"verb",nuance:"연결하다 is active: you connect something. 연결되다 means something is connected."},sentence:{ko:"와이파이를 다시 연결해 보세요.",en:"Try reconnecting the Wi-Fi."},target:"연결해",options:["to share","to cut out; disconnect; buffer","to connect"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch53-yeongyeolhada."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"친구가 자막이 좋다고 했어요. 영상도 공유했어요?",en:"My friend said the subtitles are good. Did you share the video too?"},hl:"영상"}],canDo:"Give a soft impression or guess (재미있는 것 같아요)",id:"chapter-53-b3",chapterId:"chapter-53",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A/V-는 것 같아요",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"A/V-는 것 같아요",sub:"I think… / It seems… (soft impression)",rows:[{when:"Verb, present",add:"+ -는 것 같아요",ex:"보다 → 보는 것 같아요 (seems to be watching)"},{when:"Adjective, present",add:"+ -(으)ㄴ 것 같아요",ex:"재미있다 → 재미있는 것 같아요 / 좋다 → 좋은 것 같아요 (seems good)"},{when:"Verb, past",add:"+ -(으)ㄴ 것 같아요",ex:"끝나다 → 끝난 것 같아요 (seems to have ended)"},{when:"Any, future/guess",add:"+ -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",ex:"재미있다 → 재미있을 것 같아요 (looks like it'll be fun)"}],examples:[{ko:"이 드라마가 요즘 제일 인기 있는 것 같아요.",en:"I think this drama is the most popular these days.",romanization:"i deuramaga yojeum jeil ingi inneun geot gatayo.",note:"있다 takes the verb-style -는 것 같아요; softens a strong claim into an opinion."},{ko:"새 앨범이 정말 좋은 것 같아요.",en:"I think the new album is really good.",romanization:"sae aelbeomi jeongmal joeun geot gatayo.",note:"좋다 is an adjective → -은 것 같아요; gives a gentle personal opinion."}],more:{func:"Softens a statement into an impression or guess: 'it seems,' 'I think,' 'probably.' It makes you sound less blunt and more polite, which is why Korean speakers use it constantly even when fairly sure.",funcLead:"Softens a statement into an impression or guess: 'it seems,' 'I think,' 'probably.' It makes you sound less blunt and more polite, which is why Korean speakers…",keyPoint:{label:"Present -는 것 같아요 vs. future -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요",body:"Use -는 것 같아요 for something happening or true now ('seems to be ~ing'). Use -(으)ㄹ 것 같아요 for a prediction about something not yet visible ('will probably ~'). Same softener, different time: 보는 것 같아요 = 'seems to be watching it'; 볼 것 같아요 = 'will probably watch it.'",when:"",add:"",ex:""},pronunciation:"것 같아요 is pronounced [걷 가타요] → naturally [걷까타요]: the final ㅅ becomes [t] and tenses the following ㄱ to [ㄲ].",pitfall:{wrong:"이 드라마가 재미있은 것 같아요.",right:"이 드라마가 재미있는 것 같아요.",explanation:"재미있다 and 맛있다 look like adjectives but contain 있다, so they pattern as verbs here: use -는 것 같아요, not the adjective -은 것 같아요."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I think the new album is really good."',tokens:["새","앨범이","정말","좋은","것","같아요."],correct:"새 앨범이 정말 좋은 것 같아요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"이 드라마가 재미있는 것 같아요.",ok:!0},{text:"이 드라마가 재미있은 것 같아요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"재미있다 and 맛있다 look like adjectives but contain 있다, so they pattern as verbs here: use -는 것 같아요, not the adjective -은 것 같아요."}],canDo:"Give a soft impression or guess (재미있는 것 같아요)",id:"chapter-53-b4",chapterId:"chapter-53",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 누르다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"누르다",romanization:"nureuda",en:"to press; push",pos:"verb",nuance:"누르다 is the go-to verb for pressing or pushing down — elevator buttons, doorbells, app icons, keypad keys, the 'like' button — and modern Korean life is so full of buttons that nailing it instantly sounds fluent. It is a textbook 르-irregular verb: before a vowel ending, the 르 transforms and an extra ㄹ appears, so the stem becomes 눌ㄹ-, giving 눌러요 and past 눌렀어요, while consonant endings stay regular (누릅니다, 누르고, 누르지 마세요). The meaning contrast to lock in is with 밀다 and 당기다: 누르다 is pressing DOWN on a surface or button, 밀다 is pushing a door/object away from you (문을 밀다 'push the door'), and 당기다 is pulling it toward you (문을 당기다) — so a door says 미세요/당기세요, never 누르세요, but a doorbell beside it says 누르세요. The English-speaker pitfall is twofold: writing the un-irregular '누르어요' instead of 눌러요, and over-extending 'press/push' to doors, where Korean insists on 밀다 rather than 누르다."},sentence:{ko:"좋아요도 눌렀어요?",en:"Did you hit like too?"},target:"눌렀",options:["to share","to cut out; disconnect; buffer","to press; push"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-verb-108 and expression expr-549."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"ㅋㅋ",romanization:"kk",en:"haha; lol",pos:"expression",nuance:"ㅋㅋ is the texted shape of the snicker 크크/큭큭 (ㅋ is the consonant for the 'k' giggle), and among Korean 20-30s it is the default 'I'm-relaxed-and-friendly' marker — even more than a real laugh, it signals you're at ease, so chats feel cold without it. Length is meaning: ㅋㅋ is a polite chuckle, ㅋㅋㅋㅋㅋ is genuine laughter, and you stretch the string to match how funny something is (it helps that ㅋ sits under one key, so spamming it is effortless). Its near-rival ㅎㅎ is the softer cousin (a smile, not a laugh), so swapping them shifts tone — ㅋㅋ is funny, ㅎㅎ is gentle/warm. The sharp English-speaker pitfall is the lone ㅋ: in English a solo 'lol' is harmless, but a single ㅋ (or ㅋ.. / ㅋ?) can read as cold, sarcastic, even passive-aggressive — the texting equivalent of a flat 'ha,' so never go below ㅋㅋ. And keep all of it out of messages to a boss, professor, or stranger, where laughter letters look unserious; there you write plain-polite sentences with no ㅋ at all."},sentence:{ko:"댓글에 ㅋㅋ가 많아요.",en:"There is a lot of lol in the comments."},target:"ㅋㅋ",options:["awesome; wow; no way","whoa; OMG; no way","haha; lol"],note:"Linked to expression card expr-031."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"대박",romanization:"daebak",en:"awesome; wow; no way",pos:"expression",nuance:"대박 literally means 'a big hit / jackpot' (대 大 'big' + 박 = jackpot), and as a reaction it explodes out for anything extreme — amazingly good, but also shocking or unbelievably bad. It is youth slang and firmly casual: 대박 alone is banmal among friends; for even a little politeness say 대박이에요, though that still reads as informal in flavor. The variant ladder is 대박 → 대박이다 → 대박이에요 → 완전 대박 (intensified). The dividing line against 헐 is emotional direction: 헐 leans toward shock/dismay ('oh no, whoa'), while 대박 leans toward 'wow, that's enormous' — though both can appear together as 헐 대박. Because it's casual slang, the English-speaker pitfall is using it in formal or hierarchical settings — to a boss or elder, 대박 sounds flippant and disrespectful; use 대단하네요 or 굉장하네요 instead. A second pitfall is assuming it's always positive: Koreans say 대박 at a car crash too, meaning 'unbelievable/shocking.'"},sentence:{ko:"그 아이돌 무대가 대박이라고 했어요.",en:"They said that idol's stage was amazing."},target:"대박",options:["haha; lol","awesome; wow; no way","TV drama; series"],note:"Linked to expression card expr-646."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"헐",romanization:"heol",en:"whoa; OMG; no way",pos:"expression",nuance:"헐 is a pure interjection of shock — the involuntary sound that comes out when something startling, dismaying, or unbelievable hits you. It's hugely common in texting and casual conversation among friends and younger people. Because it's a raw exclamation there is no polite form: there is no 헐요 or 헐이에요; it lives entirely in banmal territory. The dividing line against 대박 is emotional direction: 헐 carries surprise often tinged with 'oh no' or dismay, while 대박 leans toward 'wow, that's enormous' — that's why 헐 어떡해 (shock + worry) is a natural pair but 대박 어떡해 is odd. Also don't confuse 헐 with 헉: 헐 is drawn-out disbelief ('whoa, no way'), while 헉 is a sharp one-syllable gasp of fright or physical surprise. The English-speaker pitfall is using 헐 in a professional or respectful setting — to an elder or boss it sounds childish and rude; in those moments use 어머 for neutral surprise or a full 정말요? 그런 일이. A second pitfall is trying to write 헐요 to be polite — simply switch words."},sentence:{ko:"헐, 영상이 또 끊겼어요.",en:"Whoa, the video cut out again."},target:"헐",options:["whoa; OMG; no way","haha; lol","TV drama; series"],note:"Linked to expression card expr-647."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"아이돌",romanization:"aidol",en:"idol; K-pop idol",pos:"noun",nuance:"아이돌 can refer to a solo idol or group member. For the whole group, 그룹 or 아이돌 그룹 is clearer."},sentence:{ko:"어떤 아이돌 좋아해요?",en:"Which idol do you like?"},target:"아이돌",options:["TV drama; series","idol; K-pop idol","actor"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch53-aidol."}],canDo:"Relay what someone said (좋다고 했어요)",id:"chapter-53-b5",chapterId:"chapter-53",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V/A-다고 했어요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V/A-다고 했어요",sub:"They said that… (reported speech)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"친구가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"그",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"영화",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"진짜",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"재미있",mid:"다고",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"했어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"친구가 그 영화 진짜 재미있다고 했어요.",en:"My friend said that movie is really fun.",romanization:"chinguga geu yeonghwa jinjja jaemiitdago haesseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"동생이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"새",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"게임을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"한",mid:"다고",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"했어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"동생이 새 게임을 한다고 했어요.",en:"My younger sibling said they're going to play a new game.",romanization:"dongsaengi sae geimeul handago haesseoyo."}],rule:{name:"V/A-다고 했어요",rows:[{when:"Verb, present",add:"+ -ㄴ/는다고 했어요",ex:"보다 → 본다고 했어요 / 먹다 → 먹는다고 했어요 (said they watch/eat)"},{when:"Adjective, present",add:"+ -다고 했어요",ex:"재미있다 → 재미있다고 했어요 (said it's fun)"},{when:"Past (verb or adjective)",add:"+ -았/었다고 했어요",ex:"끝나다 → 끝났다고 했어요 (said it ended)"},{when:"Noun statement",add:"이다 → -(이)라고 했어요",ex:"가수 → 가수라고 했어요 (said they're a singer)"}]},more:{func:"Reports what someone else said (or what you read or heard). It quotes a statement indirectly: 'He said that it's fun.' Used constantly for relaying news, comments, and messages.",funcLead:"Reports what someone else said (or what you read or heard).",keyPoint:{label:"Verb -ㄴ/는다고 vs. adjective -다고",body:"The trap is the present tense. Verbs insert 는/ㄴ: 먹는다고, 본다고. Adjectives do NOT: 좋다고, 재미있다고. Past tense erases the difference — both verbs and adjectives use -았/었다고 (봤다고, 좋았다고).",when:"",add:"",ex:""},pronunciation:"-다고 했어요: 했어요 is said [해써요], and the whole reporting tail comes out quickly and flatly, lower in pitch than the quoted part.",pitfall:{wrong:"친구가 영화를 본다고 말했어요 라고 했어요.",right:"친구가 영화를 본다고 했어요.",explanation:"English 'said that' makes learners reach for an extra verb. In Korean, -다고 했어요 already contains 'said.' Don't double it up; just attach -다고 to the quoted clause and end with 했어요."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"The news said that singer made a comeback.",sentence:"뉴스에서 그 가수가 컴백했___.",options:[{text:"라고 했어요",ok:!1},{text:"다고 했어요",ok:!0}],explanation:"Past tense 했 + -다고 했어요; the source (뉴스에서) is marked with 에서."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "My friend said that movie is really fun."',tokens:["친구가","그","영화","진짜","재미있다고","했어요."],correct:"친구가 그 영화 진짜 재미있다고 했어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "My younger sibling said they're going to play a new game."`,tokens:["동생이","새","게임을","한다고","했어요."],correct:"동생이 새 게임을 한다고 했어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"친구가 영화를 본다고 말했어요 라고 했어요.",ok:!1},{text:"친구가 영화를 본다고 했어요.",ok:!0}],explanation:"English 'said that' makes learners reach for an extra verb. In Korean, -다고 했어요 already contains 'said.' Don't double it up; just attach -다고 to the quoted clause and end with 했어요."}],canDo:"Relay what someone said (좋다고 했어요)",id:"chapter-53-b6",chapterId:"chapter-53",index:5},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A/V-지만",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"A/V-지만",sub:"but / although (clean contrast)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"드라마는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"길",mid:"지만",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"정말",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"재미있어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"이 드라마는 길지만 정말 재미있어요.",en:"This drama is long, but it's really fun.",romanization:"i deuramaneun giljiman jeongmal jaemiisseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"그",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"게임을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"좋아하",mid:"지만",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"시간이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"없어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"그 게임을 좋아하지만 시간이 없어요.",en:"I like that game, but I don't have time.",romanization:"geu geimeul joahajiman sigani eopseoyo."}],rule:{name:"A/V-지만",rows:[{when:"Verb or adjective stem",add:"+ -지만",ex:"재미있다 → 재미있지만 (is fun, but)"},{when:"Past tense",add:"-았/었 + -지만",ex:"봤다 → 봤지만 (watched it, but)"},{when:"Noun + 이다",add:"+ -(이)지만",ex:"가수 → 가수지만 (is a singer, but)"}]},more:{func:"Joins two clauses in a straightforward 'A, but B' contrast. Neutral and clear, it works in both speech and writing, much like English 'but' or 'although.'",funcLead:"Joins two clauses in a straightforward 'A, but B' contrast.",keyPoint:{label:"-지만 (one sentence) vs. 하지만 (sentence-starter)",body:"-지만 is a connector glued onto a verb or adjective inside one sentence: 좋지만 비싸요. 하지만 is a standalone conjunction that starts a new sentence: 좋아요. 하지만 비싸요. Same meaning 'but,' different position — don't put -지만 at the start of a sentence or 하지만 in the middle of one.",when:"",add:"",ex:""},pronunciation:"-지만 is said evenly as [지만]; in 봤지만 the ㅅ tenses the ㅈ, giving [봗찌만].",pitfall:{wrong:"이 드라마는 길어요지만 재미있어요.",right:"이 드라마는 길지만 재미있어요.",explanation:"Learners attach -지만 to the full polite form (길어요지만). -지만 goes on the plain stem instead: 길다 → 길지만. For past, use 봤지만, not 봤어요지만."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I booked the ticket, but I couldn't go.",sentence:"표를 예매했___ 못 갔어요.",options:[{text:"으ㄹ게요",ok:!1},{text:"지만",ok:!0},{text:"주세요",ok:!1}],explanation:"Past -했 + 지만 contrasts a completed action with the result."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"That person is a famous singer, but I don't know their songs.",sentence:"그 사람은 유명한 가수___ 저는 노래를 몰라요.",options:[{text:"으ㄹ게요",ok:!1},{text:"지만",ok:!0},{text:"주세요",ok:!1}],explanation:"Noun 가수 + 지만; contrasts fame with the speaker's unfamiliarity."},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "This drama is long, but it's really fun."`,tokens:["이","드라마는","길지만","정말","재미있어요."],correct:"이 드라마는 길지만 정말 재미있어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I like that game, but I don't have time."`,tokens:["그","게임을","좋아하지만","시간이","없어요."],correct:"그 게임을 좋아하지만 시간이 없어요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Make a clean contrast (자막은 없지만 재미있어요)",id:"chapter-53-b7",chapterId:"chapter-53",index:6},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-(으)ㄹ게요",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"V-(으)ㄹ게요",sub:"I'll do it (promise / casual intention)",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ -ㄹ게요",ex:"보다 → 볼게요 (I'll watch it)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant",add:"+ -을게요",ex:"찾다 → 찾을게요 (I'll look it up)"},{when:"ㄹ-final stem (drop nothing, add 게요)",add:"+ -게요",ex:"만들다 → 만들게요 (I'll make it)"}],examples:[{ko:"그 드라마 오늘 밤에 볼게요.",en:"I'll watch that drama tonight.",romanization:"geu deurama oneul bame bolgeyo.",note:"보다 ends in a vowel → 볼게요; a personal decision about what I'll do."},{ko:"링크 보내 줄게요.",en:"I'll send you the link.",romanization:"lingkeu bonae julgeyo.",note:"Very common in chat; 보내 주다 → 보내 줄게요, promising to do it for the listener."}],more:{func:"States what the speaker will do, often as a light promise or a response to the listener: 'I'll watch it,' 'I'll send it to you.' It is speaker-only (you can't use it for someone else) and signals you're deciding in reaction to the situation. Extremely common in texting and chatting.",funcLead:"States what the speaker will do, often as a light promise or a response to the listener: 'I'll watch it,' 'I'll send it to you.' It is speaker-only (you can't…",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄹ게요 vs. -(으)ㄹ 거예요",body:"-(으)ㄹ게요 is a promise made to the listener, decided on the spot ('okay, I'll do it'). -(으)ㄹ 거예요 is a plain statement of plan or prediction and can describe anyone ('I/he will probably do it'). Use 볼게요 when responding to someone; 볼 거예요 when just stating your plan.",when:"",add:"",ex:""},pronunciation:"-ㄹ게요 is pronounced [ㄹ께요]: the ㄱ is tensed to [ㄲ], so 볼게요 sounds like [볼께요].",pitfall:{wrong:"친구가 그 드라마를 볼게요.",right:"친구가 그 드라마를 볼 거예요.",explanation:"-(으)ㄹ게요 is only for the speaker's own promise. To talk about what someone else will do, switch to -(으)ㄹ 거예요. You can't promise on another person's behalf with -ㄹ게요."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I'll watch that drama tonight." (그 시작 · Start with 그.)`,tokens:["그","드라마","오늘","밤에","볼게요."],correct:"그 드라마 오늘 밤에 볼게요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I'll send you the link."`,tokens:["링크","보내","줄게요."],correct:"링크 보내 줄게요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"친구가 그 드라마를 볼 거예요.",ok:!0},{text:"친구가 그 드라마를 볼게요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-(으)ㄹ게요 is only for the speaker's own promise. To talk about what someone else will do, switch to -(으)ㄹ 거예요. You can't promise on another person's behalf with -ㄹ게요."}],canDo:"Promise to do something and give the background (볼게요, 보고 싶었거든요)",id:"chapter-53-b8",chapterId:"chapter-53",index:7},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-아/어 주세요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-아/어 주세요",sub:"Please do it (for me)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"재미있는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"드라마",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"좀",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"추천해",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"주세요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"재미있는 드라마 좀 추천해 주세요.",en:"Please recommend a fun drama.",romanization:"jaemiinneun deurama jom chucheonhae juseyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"비밀번호",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"좀",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"찾",mid:"아",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"주세요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"비밀번호 좀 찾아 주세요.",en:"Please find the password for me.",romanization:"bimilbeonho jom chaja juseyo."}],rule:{name:"V-아/어 주세요",rows:[{when:"Stem vowel ㅏ/ㅗ",add:"+ -아 주세요",ex:"찾다 → 찾아 주세요 (please find it)"},{when:"Other stem vowels",add:"+ -어 주세요",ex:"보내다 → 보내 주세요 (please send it)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"하 → 해 주세요",ex:"추천하다 → 추천해 주세요 (please recommend one)"}]},more:{func:"Asks someone to do something for you, politely: 'please send it,' 'please recommend one.' The 주다 ('give') part adds the 'for me / for my benefit' nuance, making it warmer than a bare command. The everyday way to make digital-life requests.",funcLead:"Asks someone to do something for you, politely: 'please send it,' 'please recommend one.' The 주다 ('give') part adds the 'for me / for my benefit' nuance, makin…",keyPoint:{label:"-아/어 주세요 (do it for me) vs. -(으)세요 (plain polite command)",body:"-(으)세요 is a polite instruction with no 'for me' feeling: 여기 앉으세요 ('please sit here'). Adding 주다 → -아/어 주세요 frames it as a favor done for the speaker: 사진 보내 주세요 ('send me the photo, please'). For requests where someone helps you, use -아/어 주세요.",when:"",add:"",ex:""},pronunciation:"주세요 is said smoothly as [주세요]; keep the 아/어 and 주 as two clear beats (보내 주세요 = bo-nae ju-se-yo), not crushed together.",pitfall:{wrong:"드라마 추천 주세요.",right:"드라마 추천해 주세요.",explanation:"Learners drop the verb and attach 주세요 to a noun. 주세요 needs an -아/어 verb form in front: 추천하다 → 추천해 주세요. (주세요 by itself only works with a noun meaning 'please give me ~,' e.g. 물 주세요.)"}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Please send me that video link.",sentence:"그 영상 링크 보내 ___.",options:[{text:"주세요",ok:!0},{text:"아 주세요",ok:!1}],explanation:"보내다 → 보내 주세요; a typical chat request to do something for you."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Please turn the music off for a moment.",sentence:"잠깐만 음악 좀 꺼 ___.",options:[{text:"지만",ok:!1},{text:"주세요",ok:!0},{text:"으ㄹ게요",ok:!1}],explanation:"끄다 → 꺼 주세요 (ㅡ drops before 어); asking someone to do it for your sake."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Please recommend a fun drama."',tokens:["재미있는","드라마","좀","추천해","주세요."],correct:"재미있는 드라마 좀 추천해 주세요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Please find the password for me."',tokens:["비밀번호","좀","찾아","주세요."],correct:"비밀번호 좀 찾아 주세요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Say whether I have ever done something (본 적이 있어요/없어요)",id:"chapter-53-b9",chapterId:"chapter-53",index:8},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V/A-거든요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V/A-거든요",sub:"you see, … (a reason or background)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"드라마",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"꼭",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"보세요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"정말",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"재미있",mid:"거든요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"이 드라마 꼭 보세요. 정말 재미있거든요.",en:"You should definitely watch this drama — it's really fun, you see.",romanization:"i deurama kkok boseyo. jeongmal jaemiitgeodeunyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"그",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"노래",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"알아요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"제가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"그",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가수",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"팬이",mid:"거든요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"그 노래 알아요. 제가 그 가수 팬이거든요.",en:"I know that song — I'm a fan of that singer, you see.",romanization:"geu norae arayo. jega geu gasu paenigeodeunyo."}],rule:{name:"V/A-거든요",rows:[{when:"Verb/adjective stem (present)",add:"-거든요",ex:"재미있다 → 재미있거든요 · 좋아하다 → 좋아하거든요"},{when:"Past",add:"-았/었거든요",ex:"보다 → 봤거든요 (I watched it, you see)"},{when:"Noun + (이)거든요",add:"(이)거든요",ex:"팬이다 → 팬이거든요 (I'm a fan, you see)"}]},more:{func:`-거든요 ends a sentence to offer a reason or new background the listener doesn't know yet — like "you see" or "because…". It often answers a real or implied "why?"`,funcLead:`-거든요 ends a sentence to offer a reason or new background the listener doesn't know yet — like "you see" or "because…".`,keyPoint:{label:"거든요 (new info) vs 잖아요 (shared info)",body:"Both give a reason, but 거든요 tells the listener something NEW they didn't know (재미있거든요), while 잖아요 appeals to something you BOTH already know (재미있잖아요). Use 거든요 to introduce a reason, 잖아요 to remind."},pronunciation:"재미있거든요: the ㅆ+ㄱ tightens and the ending links — roughly [재미읻꺼든뇨].",pitfall:{wrong:"재미있거든요? (rising, like a question)",right:"재미있거든요. (falling, a statement)",explanation:"거든요 is not a question. Say it with a falling, confiding tone — it sets up a reason or a follow-up, not a yes/no question."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I watched that movie yesterday, you see — it was really sad.",sentence:"어제 그 영화 봤___. 진짜 슬펐어요.",options:[{text:"지만",ok:!1},{text:"거든요",ok:!0},{text:"으ㄹ게요",ok:!1}],explanation:"Past 봤거든요 sets up the next comment."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I can't talk right now — I'm in the middle of a movie, you see.",sentence:"지금 통화 못 해요. 영화 보는 중이___.",options:[{text:"지만",ok:!1},{text:"으ㄹ게요",ok:!1},{text:"거든요",ok:!0}],explanation:"거든요 explains the refusal."},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "You should definitely watch this drama — it's really fun, you see."`,tokens:["이","드라마","꼭","보세요.","정말","재미있거든요."],correct:"이 드라마 꼭 보세요. 정말 재미있거든요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"재미있거든요. (falling, a statement)",ok:!0},{text:"재미있거든요? (rising, like a question)",ok:!1}],explanation:"거든요 is not a question. Say it with a falling, confiding tone — it sets up a reason or a follow-up, not a yes/no question."}],canDo:"Give a soft impression or guess (재미있는 것 같아요)",id:"chapter-53-b10",chapterId:"chapter-53",index:9},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Two friends chat about a drama and fix a streaming problem.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"이 드라마 본 적 있어요?",en:"Have you watched this drama before?",romanization:"i deurama bon jeok isseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네. 배우도 좋지만 댓글이 진짜 재미있거든요.",en:"Yes. The actor is good too, but the comments are really fun, you know.",romanization:"ne. baeudo jojiman daetgeuri jinjja jaemiitgeodeunyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"친구가 자막이 좋다고 했어요. 영상도 공유했어요?",en:"My friend said the subtitles are good. Did you share the video too?",romanization:"chinguga jamagi jotago haesseoyo. yeongsangdo gongyuhaesseoyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"공유했는데요, 영상이 계속 끊겨요.",en:"I shared it, but the video keeps cutting out.",romanization:"gongyuhaenneundeyo, yeongsangi gyesok kkeunhgyeoyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"와이파이를 다시 연결해 보세요. 좋아요도 눌렀어요?",en:"Try reconnecting the Wi-Fi. Did you hit like too?",romanization:"waipaireul dasi yeongyeolhae boseyo. joayodo nulleosseoyo?"}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'The video keeps cutting out.'",tokens:["영상이","계속 끊겨요"],correct:"영상이 계속 끊겨요",explanation:"끊기다 describes the stream or video cutting out. 계속 shows it keeps happening."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"누가 한 말일까요? · Who said this?",sentence:"와이파이를 다시 연결해 보세요. 좋아요도 눌렀어요?",options:[{text:"민서",ok:!0},{text:"지훈",ok:!1}],explanation:"Try reconnecting the Wi-Fi. Did you hit like too?"},{kind:"drill",prompt:'무엇이 들어갈까요? · "Have you watched this drama before?"',sentence:"이 ___ 본 적 있어요?",options:[{text:"배우",ok:!1},{text:"댓글",ok:!1},{text:"드라마",ok:!0}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Say whether I have ever done something (본 적이 있어요/없어요)",id:"chapter-53-b11",chapterId:"chapter-53",index:10},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 영상이 계속 끊기는데요",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"영상이 계속 끊기는데요",chunks:["민서는 친구에게 새 드라마를 추천했어요. 먼저 '이 드라마 본 적 있어요?'라고 물었어요. 친구는 배우도 좋지만 댓글이 진짜 재미있다고 했어요. 또 자막이 좋아서 한국어 공부에도 도움이 되는 것 같다고 했어요.","민서는 영상을 공유했지만, 친구는 영상이 계속 끊긴다고 했어요. 그래서 민서는 와이파이를 다시 연결해 보라고 했어요. 마지막에는 좋아요도 눌렀는지 물었어요."],translation:"Minseo recommended a new drama to a friend. First, Minseo asked whether the friend had watched it before. The friend said the actor was good, but the comments were really fun. The friend also said the subtitles were good and seemed helpful for Korean study. Minseo shared the video, but the friend said the video kept cutting out. So Minseo told the friend to try reconnecting the Wi-Fi. At the end, Minseo asked whether the friend had also hit like.",qas:[{q:"민서는 친구에게 무엇을 추천했어요?",a:"새 드라마를 추천했어요."},{q:"친구는 무엇이 재미있다고 했어요?",a:"댓글이 진짜 재미있다고 했어요."},{q:"영상이 끊길 때 민서는 무엇을 해 보라고 했어요?",a:"와이파이를 다시 연결해 보라고 했어요."}]},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"민서는 친구에게 새 ___를 추천했어요.",options:[{text:"아이돌",ok:!1},{text:"드라마",ok:!0},{text:"공유하다",ok:!1}],explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"친구는 배우도 좋지만 댓글이 진짜 ___고 했어요.",options:[{text:"공유하다",ok:!1},{text:"끊기다",ok:!1},{text:"재미있다",ok:!0}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Give a soft impression or guess (재미있는 것 같아요)",id:"chapter-53-b12",chapterId:"chapter-53",index:11},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence asks about experience with a drama?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"이 드라마 본 적 있어요?",ok:!0},{text:"이 드라마 볼 수밖에 없어요?",ok:!1},{text:"이 드라마 보고 있겠어요?",ok:!1}],explanation:"-ㄴ 적이 있어요 asks whether someone has done something before."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence reports a friend's opinion?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"친구가 자막이 좋다고 했어요.",ok:!0},{text:"친구가 자막을 좋아요 했어요.",ok:!1},{text:"친구가 자막을 켜라고 했어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Adjectives use -다고 했어요 in reported speech."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence naturally describes buffering?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"영상이 계속 끊기는데요.",ok:!0},{text:"영상을 계속 끊는데요.",ok:!1},{text:"영상이 계속 공유하는데요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"끊기다 describes the video or connection cutting out."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"그 드라마___ 본 적 있어요? (Have you ever watched that drama?)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"를",ok:!0},{text:"가",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"보다 takes the thing watched as an object, so 드라마를 fits. 본 적 있어요 asks about experience."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Your friend said the subtitles are good. Which report fits?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"친구가 자막이 좋다고 했어요.",ok:!0},{text:"친구가 자막이 좋냐고 했어요.",ok:!1},{text:"친구가 자막을 좋아요 했어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-다고 했어요 reports a statement. -냐고 would report a question."}],canDo:"Relay what someone said (좋다고 했어요)",id:"chapter-53-b13",chapterId:"chapter-53",index:12}]},{id:"chapter-54",number:54,title:"V-아/어야 해요 · V-기로 했어요",goal:"Talk about assignments, presentations, midterms, finals, semesters, grades, and study plans.",biteCount:12,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 과제~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"과제",romanization:"gwaje",en:"assignment; homework task",pos:"noun",nuance:"숙제 is homework, often younger-school or daily work. 과제 sounds more academic and can include reports, presentations, and projects."},sentence:{ko:"중간고사 전에 발표 과제를 끝내야 해요.",en:"We have to finish the presentation assignment before the midterm."},target:"과제",options:["assignment; homework task","midterm exam","final exam"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch54-gwaje."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"중간고사",romanization:"junggangosa",en:"midterm exam",pos:"noun",nuance:"중간 means middle; 고사 is exam. In daily student talk, 시험 is more general, while 중간고사 is specifically the midterm."},sentence:{ko:"중간고사 전에 과제를 끝내기로 했어요.",en:"We decided to finish the assignment before the midterm."},target:"중간고사",options:["assignment; homework task","midterm exam","semester; school term"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch54-junggangosa."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"기말고사",romanization:"gimalgosa",en:"final exam",pos:"noun",nuance:"기말 means the end of the term. 기말고사 is final exam; 중간고사 is midterm exam."},sentence:{ko:"기말고사에서 좋은 성적을 받기 위해 매일 공부해야 해요.",en:"I have to study every day in order to get a good grade on the final exam."},target:"기말고사",options:["assignment; homework task","semester; school term","final exam"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch54-gimalgosa."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"학기",romanization:"hakgi",en:"semester; school term",pos:"noun",nuance:"학기 is an academic term; 방학 is vacation/break between terms."},sentence:{ko:"이번 학기 동안 매일 복습했어요.",en:"I reviewed every day during this semester."},target:"학기",options:["assignment; homework task","midterm exam","semester; school term"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch54-hakgi."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"교수님",romanization:"gyosunim",en:"professor",pos:"noun",nuance:"Use 선생님 for school teachers or language teachers, and 교수님 for university professors."},sentence:{ko:"교수님께 질문하기로 했어요.",en:"I decided to ask the professor."},target:"교수님",options:["professor","assignment; homework task","midterm exam"],note:"Linked to chapter card word-ext-ch54-gyosunim."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"중간고사 전에 발표 과제를 끝내야 해요?",en:"Do we have to finish the presentation assignment before the midterm?"},hl:"과제"}],canDo:"Say what I have to do for school (과제를 내야 해요)",id:"chapter-54-b1",chapterId:"chapter-54",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-아/어야 해요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-아/어야 해요",sub:"have to / must",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"내일까지",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"숙제를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"내야",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"해요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"내일까지 숙제를 내야 해요.",en:"I have to hand in the homework by tomorrow.",romanization:"naeilkkaji sukjereul naeya haeyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"수업",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"시간에는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"휴대폰을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"꺼야",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"해요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"수업 시간에는 휴대폰을 꺼야 해요.",en:"During class you have to turn off your phone.",romanization:"sueop siganeneun hyudaeponeul kkeoya haeyo."}],rule:{name:"V-아/어야 해요",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ends in ㅏ or ㅗ",add:"+ -아야 해요",ex:"가다 → 가야 해요 (have to go)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in any other vowel",add:"+ -어야 해요",ex:"먹다 → 먹어야 해요 (have to eat)"},{when:"하다 verbs",add:"하 → 해야 해요",ex:"공부하다 → 공부해야 해요 (have to study)"},{when:"Spoken variant, same meaning",add:"+ -아/어야 돼요",ex:"내다 → 내야 돼요 (have to hand it in)"}]},more:{func:"Expresses obligation or necessity: something you have to do. In school life it covers class rules, assignments, deadlines, and things teachers require of students.",funcLead:"Expresses obligation or necessity: something you have to do.",keyPoint:{label:"-아/어야 해요 vs. -아/어야 돼요",body:"Both mean 'have to,' and in everyday speech they are interchangeable. -아/어야 돼요 sounds a touch more colloquial and is very common when talking; -아/어야 해요 is a little more neutral and shows up more in writing. Pick whichever you hear most — the meaning does not change."},pronunciation:"말해야 is pronounced [마래야]: the ㅎ weakens between vowels. 해야 해요 keeps two clear 해 syllables — don't blur them into one.",pitfall:{wrong:"숙제를 내야 하는 게 필요해요.",right:"숙제를 내야 해요.",explanation:"English 'I need to / it's necessary' tempts learners to add 필요해요. Korean already builds the necessity into -아/어야 해요, so adding 필요하다 on top is redundant and unnatural."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I have a test, so I have to go to the library today.",sentence:"시험이 있어서 오늘 도서관에 가야 ___.",options:[{text:"동안",ok:!1},{text:"해요",ok:!0},{text:"으니까",ok:!1}],explanation:"가다 ends in ㅏ, so it takes -아야; 도서관 keeps it in the school setting."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"In the classroom you have to speak in Korean.",sentence:"교실에서는 한국어로 말해야 ___.",options:[{text:"동안",ok:!1},{text:"으니까",ok:!1},{text:"해요",ok:!0}],explanation:"말하다 is a 하다 verb → 해야; a typical classroom rule."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I have to hand in the homework by tomorrow." (내일까지 시작 · Start with 내일까지.)',tokens:["내일까지","숙제를","내야","해요."],correct:"내일까지 숙제를 내야 해요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "During class you have to turn off your phone." (수업 시작 · Start with 수업.)',tokens:["수업","시간에는","휴대폰을","꺼야","해요."],correct:"수업 시간에는 휴대폰을 꺼야 해요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Say what I have to do for school (과제를 내야 해요)",id:"chapter-54-b2",chapterId:"chapter-54",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 수업~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"수업",romanization:"sueop",en:"class; lesson",pos:"noun",nuance:"수업 is Sino-Korean 授業 — 授 'give/confer' + 業 'work/study', literally 'conferring instruction', i.e. the lesson or class session itself (the activity and time period). The set verb is 수업을 듣다 'listen to a class' = attend/take it; from the teacher's side it is 수업을 하다 'give/teach a class'. Three near-neighbors confuse learners: 수업 (the lesson event) vs 교실(教室, the room it happens in) vs 과목(科目, the subject like 'math' or 'history') vs 강의(講義, a university lecture). So 'I have three classes today' = 수업 세 개, but 'I take three subjects' = 과목 세 개. The English-speaker pitfall is using 하다 to mean 'take a class' (수업을 하다 actually means 'to teach', which is what a professor does) — as a student you 듣다 a class. Also, 'go to class' is 수업에 가다 (event as destination with 에)."},sentence:{ko:"내일 수업에서 발표해야 해요.",en:"I have to present in class tomorrow."},target:"수업",options:["class; lesson","assignment; homework task","midterm exam"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-112."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"발표하다",romanization:"balpyohada",en:"to present",pos:"verb",nuance:"발표하다 is Sino-Korean 發表 — 發 'issue/emit' + 表 'express/show outwardly' — so 'issue something into the open'. It has two senses: (1) give a presentation/talk in front of an audience (수업에서 발표하다), and (2) officially announce/release information (정부가 결과를 발표하다 — the government announced the results). Contrast it with 설명하다 (說明, 'explain'): 설명하다 is clarifying so someone understands a point, while 발표하다 is the public act of presenting or announcing as a whole. Also distinguish 발표하다 from 말하다 'to say' — 발표하다 implies prepared, formal, to-an-audience delivery. A particle nuance: in the 'present' sense the location uses 에서 (수업에서), but the content shown is an object (자료를 발표하다). As a noun+하다 verb it conjugates on 하다 (발표해요, 발표했어요, formal 발표합니다); negative/impossible split the noun (발표 안 해요, 발표 못 해요)."},sentence:{ko:"내일 발표해야 해요.",en:"I have to present tomorrow."},target:"발표해",options:["assignment; homework task","midterm exam","to present"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-verb-492."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"시험",romanization:"siheom",en:"exam",pos:"noun",nuance:"시험 is Sino-Korean 試驗 — 試 'try/test' + 驗 'examine', literally 'a trial examination'. The collocation English speakers must memorize is 시험을 보다 — Koreans 'see' an exam, they do not 'do' it, so 시험을 하다 is wrong. Passing and failing are vivid metaphors with the particle 에: 시험에 붙다 (literally 'stick to the exam') = pass, 시험에 떨어지다 ('fall off the exam') = fail — note these take 에, not 을. Contrast 시험 with 퀴즈(quiz, a small in-class test) and with 검사(檢査, an inspection/medical test like 건강 검사): a graded school test is always 시험, never 퀴즈 unless it is a tiny informal one. The classic 직역 trap is 'take an exam' → 시험을 가지다/취하다 ✗ (English 'take' never transfers); and learners often write 시험을 통과하다, which is understandable but bookish — the everyday word is 붙다."},sentence:{ko:"시험 기간 동안 도서관에서 공부할 거예요.",en:"During exam period, I will study at the library."},target:"시험",options:["exam","assignment; homework task","semester; school term"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-111."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"도서관",romanization:"doseogwan",en:"library",pos:"noun",nuance:"도서관 is Sino-Korean 圖書館, literally 'pictures-books-building' (圖 picture + 書 writing/book + 館 hall), a public place to read, study, and borrow books where quiet is expected. The sharp contrast learners must hold is 도서관 vs 서점: a 도서관 is a LIBRARY where you borrow (빌리다) for free, while a 서점 is a BOOKSTORE where you buy (사다) — English 'library' and Spanish/French false friends like 'librería' push learners to mix these up. Note also 도서관 (the building) vs 도서 (books as items/collection). Register is neutral. Natural collocations: 도서관에서 공부하다, 책을 빌리다/반납하다, 도서관에 자리가 없다. The English-speaker pitfall, beyond the library/bookstore mix-up, is the particle split: study or read AT a library is 에서 (도서관에서), but going TO it is 에 (도서관에 가다)."},sentence:{ko:"오늘부터 도서관에서 공부하기로 했어요.",en:"I decided to study at the library starting today."},target:"도서관",options:["assignment; homework task","library","midterm exam"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-315."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"성적",romanization:"seongjeok",en:"grades",pos:"noun",nuance:"성적 means academic grades, marks, or results, written 成績 (成 'to accomplish, achieve' + 績 'achievement, merit'), literally 'accomplished achievements' — the outcome of your effort. Distinguish it from 점수, a single numeric 'score' on one test (시험 점수 90점), and from 시험, the 'exam' itself: you take a 시험, get a 점수 on it, and your overall 성적 reflects the term. Note the directional verbs that pair with it: 성적이 나오다 (results 'come out'/are released), 성적을 받다 (you 'receive' them), 성적이 오르다/떨어지다 (they rise/fall). The English-speaker pitfall is using 시험 where you mean the resulting grade ('my exam was good' → say 성적이 좋아요), and choosing the wrong particle: with descriptive 좋다 or motion 나오다/오르다 it's 이/가, only with 받다 is it 을/를."},sentence:{ko:"장학금을 받기 위해 좋은 성적이 필요해요.",en:"Good grades are necessary in order to get a scholarship."},target:"성적",options:["assignment; homework task","midterm exam","grades"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-403."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네, 이번 학기 성적에 들어가니까 준비해야 해요.",en:"Yes, it counts toward this semester's grade, so we have to prepare."},hl:"성적"}],canDo:"State a decision or study plan (같이 공부하기로 했어요)",id:"chapter-54-b3",chapterId:"chapter-54",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-기로 했어요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-기로 했어요",sub:"decided to V",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"친구들하고",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"같이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"도서관에서",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"공부하",mid:"기로",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"했어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"친구들하고 같이 도서관에서 공부하기로 했어요.",en:"I decided to study with my friends at the library.",romanization:"chingudeulhago gachi doseogwaneseo gongbuhagiro haesseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"이번",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"학기에는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"결석하지",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"않",mid:"기로",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"했어요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"이번 학기에는 결석하지 않기로 했어요.",en:"This semester I decided not to be absent.",romanization:"ibeon hakgieneun gyeolseokhaji anki-ro haesseoyo."}],rule:{name:"V-기로 했어요",rows:[{when:"Any verb, attach to the plain stem",add:"+ -기로 했어요",ex:"공부하다 → 공부하기로 했어요 (decided to study)"},{when:"Consonant-ending stem (no change)",add:"+ -기로 했어요",ex:"읽다 → 읽기로 했어요 (decided to read)"},{when:"Negative decision",add:"안 V / V-지 않 + -기로 했어요",ex:"지각 안 하기로 했어요 (decided not to be late)"},{when:"A standing rule (let's make it a habit)",add:"+ -기로 해요",ex:"매일 복습하기로 해요 (let's review every day)"}]},more:{func:"States a decision, plan, or promise that you (or a group) have already made. Common when classmates agree on a study plan or you resolve to do something for school.",funcLead:"States a decision, plan, or promise that you (or a group) have already made.",keyPoint:{label:"-기로 했어요 vs. -(으)ㄹ 거예요",body:"-기로 했어요 highlights that a decision was made (often jointly or as a resolution). -(으)ㄹ 거예요 just states a future plan or prediction. Use -기로 했어요 when the point is 'we agreed/decided,' not merely 'I will.'"},pronunciation:"했어요 is pronounced [해써요] with a tense ㅆ. 않기로 is read [안키로]: the ㅎ of 않 fuses with the following ㄱ into an aspirated [키].",pitfall:{wrong:"공부하는 것을 결정했어요.",right:"공부하기로 했어요.",explanation:"Learners translate 'decided to study' literally with 결정하다 + a nominalized clause. Native speakers package the decision into -기로 하다 instead. Use the idiomatic pattern, not a word-for-word 'decide that.'"}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I decided to study with my friends at the library." (친구들하고 시작 · Start with 친구들하고.)',tokens:["친구들하고","같이","도서관에서","공부하기로","했어요."],correct:"친구들하고 같이 도서관에서 공부하기로 했어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "This semester I decided not to be absent." (이번 시작 · Start with 이번.)',tokens:["이번","학기에는","결석하지","않기로","했어요."],correct:"이번 학기에는 결석하지 않기로 했어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"공부하는 것을 결정했어요.",ok:!1},{text:"공부하기로 했어요.",ok:!0}],explanation:"Learners translate 'decided to study' literally with 결정하다 + a nominalized clause. Native speakers package the decision into -기로 하다 instead. Use the idiomatic pattern, not a word-for-word 'decide that.'"}],canDo:"State a decision or study plan (같이 공부하기로 했어요)",id:"chapter-54-b4",chapterId:"chapter-54",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 장학금~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"장학금",romanization:"janghakgeum",en:"scholarship",pos:"noun",nuance:"장학금 is the award money granted to a student to support their studies — for merit (성적 장학금), need, or special talent — money you do not repay, unlike a loan. The hanja 奬學金 is 奬 (encourage/reward) + 學 (study/learning) + 金 (money/gold), literally 'study-encouraging money', so the very word frames it as funds that reward and enable learning. Distinguish it sharply from 학자금 (學資金, 'study-capital'), which is the broader pool of money for education and often appears in 학자금 대출 (a student loan you must repay): a 장학금 is given to you, whereas 학자금 대출 is borrowed. Also unlike a plain 돈 (money) or 용돈 (allowance), 장학금 specifically denotes an academic award. The natural verbs are 장학금을 받다 (formally receive) and the colloquial 장학금을 타다 (win/get one), plus 장학금을 신청하다 (apply) and 장학금이 나오다 (it comes through). The English-speaker pitfall is calling it just 돈 — saying 학교에서 돈을 받았어요 to mean a scholarship loses the meaning; the award is 장학금, and confusing it with the repayable 학자금 대출 reverses who gives money to whom."},sentence:{ko:"장학금을 받기 위해 좋은 성적이 필요해요.",en:"Good grades are necessary in order to get a scholarship."},target:"장학금",options:["assignment; homework task","midterm exam","scholarship"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1963."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"전공",romanization:"jeongong",en:"major",pos:"noun",nuance:"전공 is your academic major — the single specialized field you concentrate on at university — written 專攻 (專 'exclusive, sole' + 攻 'to attack, devote effort to'), literally 'to devote oneself exclusively', i.e. to specialize. It can also act as a verb with 하다: 한국어를 전공하다 ('to major in Korean'). The key contrast is 전공 vs 과목: a 과목 is any one of the many courses on your timetable, whereas 전공 is the one field you commit to — you take many 과목 but have one 전공. It also differs from 학과, the 'academic department' that houses the major. The English-speaker pitfall is calling a major 과목 (제 과목은..., wrong) and forgetting that with the verb form the field takes 을/를: not 한국어 전공해요 but 한국어를 전공해요; as a plain noun, though, 전공이 뭐예요 uses 이/가."},sentence:{ko:"전공을 바꾸기 위해 교수님께 질문했어요.",en:"I asked the professor a question in order to change my major."},target:"전공",options:["assignment; homework task","major","midterm exam"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-405."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"학원",romanization:"hagwon",en:"private academy",pos:"noun",nuance:"학원 is a private after-school academy (cram school) where students pay for extra lessons — English, math, piano, taekwondo — written 學院 (學 'study' + 院 'institution, hall'). The 院 'institution' element links it to 병원 (病院 hospital), 대학원 (大學院 graduate school), and 미용원/미장원, so the family is learnable. Sharply distinguish 학원 (a supplementary private institute) from 학교 (學校, the regular public/formal school) and from 대학원 (graduate school) — a child attends 학교 by day and 학원 in the evening. The verb of choice is 다니다, which implies habitual commuting/membership, with the 에 particle: 학원에 다니다 ('attend an academy regularly'), not the one-off 가다. The English-speaker pitfall is using 학교 for any place of learning, or marking the destination with 를 (학원를 다녀요, wrong) instead of 에."},sentence:{ko:"한국어를 잘하기 위해 학원에 다녀요.",en:"I go to an academy in order to get good at Korean."},target:"학원",options:["assignment; homework task","midterm exam","private academy"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-406."}],canDo:"Say when an action started (공부하기 시작했어요)",id:"chapter-54-b5",chapterId:"chapter-54",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-기 시작하다",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-기 시작하다",sub:"start doing",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"작년부터",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"한국어를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"배우",mid:"기",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"시작",post:"했어요.",hit:!0}],ko:"작년부터 한국어를 배우기 시작했어요.",en:"I started learning Korean last year.",romanization:"jangnyeonbuteo hangugeoreul baeugi sijakaesseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"수업이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"끝나고",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"숙제를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"하",mid:"기",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"시작",post:"했어요.",hit:!0}],ko:"수업이 끝나고 숙제를 하기 시작했어요.",en:"After class ended, I started doing my homework.",romanization:"sueobi kkeunnago sukjereul hagi sijakaesseoyo."}],rule:{name:"V-기 시작하다",rows:[{when:"Any verb stem + 시작하다",add:"+ -기 시작하다",ex:"공부하다 → 공부하기 시작하다 (start studying)"},{when:"Consonant-ending stem (no change)",add:"+ -기 시작하다",ex:"읽다 → 읽기 시작하다 (start reading)"},{when:"Past (started, and it continues)",add:"+ -기 시작했어요",ex:"배우다 → 배우기 시작했어요 (started learning)"},{when:"Present / general statement",add:"+ -기 시작해요",ex:"듣다 → 듣기 시작해요 (start listening)"}]},more:{func:"Says that an action begins or has begun. The verb is nominalized with -기 and followed by 시작하다 (to start). Useful for saying when you started studying, learning, or a new routine.",funcLead:"Says that an action begins or has begun.",keyPoint:{label:"V-기 시작하다 vs. N을/를 시작하다",body:"Use V-기 시작하다 to start an action (a verb): 공부하기 시작했어요. To start a thing (a noun), use N을/를 시작하다: 수업을 시작해요 (start the class). Don't say 수업하기 시작하다 when you simply mean 수업을 시작하다."},pronunciation:"시작했어요 is read [시자캐써요]: the ㄱ of 작 + the ㅎ of 했 fuse into an aspirated [ㅋ], and 했어요 becomes [캐써요].",pitfall:{wrong:"한국어를 배우는 것을 시작했어요.",right:"한국어를 배우기 시작했어요.",explanation:"English speakers reach for the heavier -는 것 nominalizer for 'started to learn.' With 시작하다, Korean prefers the lighter -기. -는 것을 시작하다 sounds clumsy and over-literal."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I started learning Korean last year." (작년부터 시작 · Start with 작년부터.)',tokens:["작년부터","한국어를","배우기","시작했어요."],correct:"작년부터 한국어를 배우기 시작했어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "After class ended, I started doing my homework."',tokens:["수업이","끝나고","숙제를","하기","시작했어요."],correct:"수업이 끝나고 숙제를 하기 시작했어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"한국어를 배우는 것을 시작했어요.",ok:!1},{text:"한국어를 배우기 시작했어요.",ok:!0}],explanation:"English speakers reach for the heavier -는 것 nominalizer for 'started to learn.' With 시작하다, Korean prefers the lighter -기. -는 것을 시작하다 sounds clumsy and over-literal."}],canDo:"Say when an action started (공부하기 시작했어요)",id:"chapter-54-b6",chapterId:"chapter-54",index:5},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N 동안",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N 동안",sub:"for / during (a period)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"두",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"시간",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"동안",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"도서관에서",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"공부했어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"두 시간 동안 도서관에서 공부했어요.",en:"I studied at the library for two hours.",romanization:"du sigan dongan doseogwaneseo gongbuhaesseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"방학",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"동안",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"한국어",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"학원에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"다녔어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"방학 동안 한국어 학원에 다녔어요.",en:"During vacation I went to a Korean language academy.",romanization:"banghak dongan hangugeo hagwone danyeosseoyo."}],rule:{name:"N 동안",rows:[{when:"After a duration (length of time)",add:"N 동안 = 'for N'",ex:"한 시간 동안 (for one hour)"},{when:"After a named period",add:"N 동안 = 'during N'",ex:"방학 동안 (during the vacation)"},{when:"With a verb ('while V-ing')",add:"V-는 동안",ex:"공부하는 동안 (while studying)"},{when:"No particle before 동안",add:"(do NOT add 에/을)",ex:"삼 년 동안 (for three years), not 삼 년에 동안"}]},more:{func:"Marks a span of time over which something happens: 'for' a duration or 'during' a named period. Essential for saying how long you studied, or that something happened over vacation or a semester.",funcLead:"Marks a span of time over which something happens: 'for' a duration or 'during' a named period.",keyPoint:{label:"N 동안 (a stretch) vs. N에 (a point)",body:"Use 동안 for a span of time you do something over: 세 시간 동안 (for three hours). Use 에 for a single point in time: 세 시에 (at three o'clock). Don't mix them — 세 시간에 or 세 시간에 동안 are both wrong."},pronunciation:"동안 is two even syllables [동안]. 공부했어요 is [공부해써요], with the tense ㅆ in 했.",pitfall:{wrong:"세 시간에 동안 공부했어요.",right:"세 시간 동안 공부했어요.",explanation:"English 'for three hours' makes learners insert a particle, but 동안 attaches straight to the time noun with nothing in between. No 에, no 을 before 동안."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"While taking the test, you can't use your phone.",sentence:"시험을 보는 ___ 휴대폰을 쓸 수 없어요.",options:[{text:"동안",ok:!0},{text:"해요",ok:!1},{text:"으니까",ok:!1}],explanation:"With a verb, use -는 동안 for 'while'; 시험을 보다 (to take a test) sets the school scene."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I studied Korean for three years.",sentence:"삼 년 ___ 한국어를 공부했어요.",options:[{text:"해요",ok:!1},{text:"동안",ok:!0},{text:"으니까",ok:!1}],explanation:"삼 년 (three years) is a duration → 'for'; again no particle before 동안."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I studied at the library for two hours." (두 시작 · Start with 두.)',tokens:["두","시간","동안","도서관에서","공부했어요."],correct:"두 시간 동안 도서관에서 공부했어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "During vacation I went to a Korean language academy." (방학 시작 · Start with 방학.)',tokens:["방학","동안","한국어","학원에","다녔어요."],correct:"방학 동안 한국어 학원에 다녔어요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Give a reason that leads to a plan (시험이 있으니까 도서관에 가요)",id:"chapter-54-b7",chapterId:"chapter-54",index:6},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V/A-(으)니까",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V/A-(으)니까",sub:"because / so (reason before a plan)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"성적이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"중요하",mid:"니까",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"미리",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"준비해요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"성적이 중요하니까 미리 준비해요.",en:"Grades are important, so let's prepare in advance.",romanization:"seongjeogi jungyohanikka miri junbihaeyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"숙제가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"많",mid:"으니까",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"오늘",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"시작해요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"숙제가 많으니까 오늘 시작해요.",en:"There's a lot of homework, so let's start today.",romanization:"sukjega maneunikka oneul sijakaeyo."}],rule:{name:"V/A-(으)니까",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"+ -니까",ex:"바쁘다 → 바쁘니까 (since I'm busy)"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"+ -으니까",ex:"많다 → 많으니까 (since there are many)"},{when:"하다 verbs/adjectives",add:"하 + -니까",ex:"중요하다 → 중요하니까 (since it's important)"},{when:"Past tense",add:"-았/었 + -으니까",ex:"끝났다 → 끝났으니까 (since it ended)"}]},more:{func:"Gives a reason that leads into a request, suggestion, or plan: 'since A, (let's) B.' In school talk it links why something matters to what you should do next.",funcLead:"Gives a reason that leads into a request, suggestion, or plan: 'since A, (let's) B.' In school talk it links why something matters to what you should do next.",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)니까 vs. -아/어서 for 'because'",body:"Both give reasons, but only -(으)니까 can lead into a command, suggestion, or 'let's' (시험이 가까우니까 공부해요). -아/어서 cannot: you can't say 시험이 가까워서 공부합시다. Use -(으)니까 when the next clause is a request or proposal."},pronunciation:"많으니까 is read [마느니까]: the ㅎ in 많 is silent before a vowel. 끝났으니까 is [끈나쓰니까].",pitfall:{wrong:"비가 와서 우산을 가져가세요.",right:"비가 오니까 우산을 가져가세요.",explanation:"When the second clause is a command or suggestion ('take an umbrella'), Korean needs -(으)니까, not -아/어서. -아/어서 only works before a plain statement, not before an order or 'let's.'"}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"The exam is near, so I study at the library.",sentence:"시험이 가까우___ 도서관에서 공부해요.",options:[{text:"니까",ok:!0},{text:"으니까",ok:!1}],explanation:"가깝다 is ㅂ-irregular: 가까우니까."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Class is over, so let's go eat together.",sentence:"수업이 끝났___ 같이 밥 먹으러 가요.",options:[{text:"해요",ok:!1},{text:"으니까",ok:!0},{text:"동안",ok:!1}],explanation:"Past 끝났 + -으니까; reason plus an invitation."},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Grades are important, so let's prepare in advance."`,tokens:["성적이","중요하니까","미리","준비해요."],correct:"성적이 중요하니까 미리 준비해요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "There's a lot of homework, so let's start today."`,tokens:["숙제가","많으니까","오늘","시작해요."],correct:"숙제가 많으니까 오늘 시작해요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"State an academic purpose (장학금을 받기 위해서 공부해요)",id:"chapter-54-b8",chapterId:"chapter-54",index:7},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-기 위해서",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-기 위해서",sub:"in order to (purpose)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"좋은",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"성적을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"받",mid:"기",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"위해서",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"매일",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"공부해요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"좋은 성적을 받기 위해서 매일 공부해요.",en:"I study every day in order to get good grades.",romanization:"joeun seongjeogeul batgi wihaeseo maeil gongbuhaeyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"시험에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"합격하",mid:"기",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"위해서",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"학원에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"다녀요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"시험에 합격하기 위해서 학원에 다녀요.",en:"I go to an academy in order to pass the exam.",romanization:"siheome hapgyeokhagi wihaeseo hagwone danyeoyo."}],rule:{name:"V-기 위해서",rows:[{when:"Any verb, attach to the stem",add:"+ -기 위해서",ex:"합격하다 → 합격하기 위해서 (in order to pass)"},{when:"Common short form",add:"+ -기 위해",ex:"받다 → 받기 위해 (in order to get)"},{when:"With a noun goal",add:"N을/를 위해서",ex:"시험을 위해서 (for the exam)"},{when:"Negative goal (use 지 않기 위해서)",add:"V-지 않기 위해서",ex:"늦지 않기 위해서 (in order not to be late)"}]},more:{func:"Marks a deliberate goal: you do B in order to achieve A. Good for academic goals like passing, getting a scholarship, raising your grade, or getting into a program. Often shortened to -기 위해.",funcLead:"Marks a deliberate goal: you do B in order to achieve A.",keyPoint:{label:"V-기 위해서 vs. V-(으)러",body:"Both express purpose, but -(으)러 only attaches to motion verbs like 가다/오다 (공부하러 가요 = go to study). -기 위해서 has no such limit and works with any main verb (장학금을 받기 위해서 공부해요). For a goal that isn't 'going somewhere to do it,' use -기 위해서."},pronunciation:"받기 위해서 is read [받끼 위해서]: the ㄷ tenses the following ㄱ to [ㄲ]. 합격하기 is [합껴카기].",pitfall:{wrong:"장학금을 받으려고 위해서 공부해요.",right:"장학금을 받기 위해서 공부해요.",explanation:"Learners mix two purpose patterns. Either use -(으)려고 (받으려고 공부해요) OR -기 위해서 (받기 위해서 공부해요), never both together. Don't stack 위해서 after a -(으)려고 ending."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I study every day in order to get good grades."',tokens:["좋은","성적을","받기","위해서","매일","공부해요."],correct:"좋은 성적을 받기 위해서 매일 공부해요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I go to an academy in order to pass the exam." (시험에 시작 · Start with 시험에.)',tokens:["시험에","합격하기","위해서","학원에","다녀요."],correct:"시험에 합격하기 위해서 학원에 다녀요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"장학금을 받기 위해서 공부해요.",ok:!0},{text:"장학금을 받으려고 위해서 공부해요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Learners mix two purpose patterns. Either use -(으)려고 (받으려고 공부해요) OR -기 위해서 (받기 위해서 공부해요), never both together. Don't stack 위해서 after a -(으)려고 ending."}],canDo:"Say what I have to do for school (과제를 내야 해요)",id:"chapter-54-b9",chapterId:"chapter-54",index:8},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Classmates plan around a presentation assignment and upcoming exams.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"중간고사 전에 발표 과제를 끝내야 해요?",en:"Do we have to finish the presentation assignment before the midterm?",romanization:"junggangosa jeone balpyo gwajereul kkeutnaeya haeyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"네, 이번 학기 성적에 들어가니까 준비해야 해요.",en:"Yes, it counts toward this semester's grade, so we have to prepare.",romanization:"ne, ibeon hakgi seongjeoge deureoganikka junbihaeya haeyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"그럼 오늘부터 도서관에서 공부하기 시작하기로 했어요.",en:"Then I decided to start studying at the library from today.",romanization:"geureom oneulbuteo doseogwaneseo gongbuhagi sijakagiro haesseoyo."},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"기말고사에서 좋은 성적을 받기 위해 시험 기간 동안 매일 공부해야 해요.",en:"In order to get a good grade on the final exam, we have to study every day during exam period.",romanization:"gimalgosaeseo joeun seongjeogeul batgi wihae siheom gigan dongan maeil gongbuhaeya haeyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'I decided to study at the library during exam period.' (Start with 시험 기간 동안.)",tokens:["시험 기간 동안","도서관에서","공부하기로 했어요"],correct:"시험 기간 동안 도서관에서 공부하기로 했어요",explanation:"동안 gives the period, and 도서관에서 gives the study place. -기로 했어요 states the decision."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"누가 한 말일까요? · Who said this?",sentence:"기말고사에서 좋은 성적을 받기 위해 시험 기간 동안 매일 공부해야 해요.",options:[{text:"지훈",ok:!0},{text:"민서",ok:!1}],explanation:"In order to get a good grade on the final exam, we have to study every day during exam period."},{kind:"drill",prompt:'무엇이 들어갈까요? · "Do we have to finish the presentation assignment before the midterm?"',sentence:"___ 전에 발표 과제를 끝내야 해요?",options:[{text:"중간고사",ok:!0},{text:"기말고사",ok:!1},{text:"학기",ok:!1}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Say what I have to do for school (과제를 내야 해요)",id:"chapter-54-b10",chapterId:"chapter-54",index:9},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 중간고사 전에 발표 과제를 끝내야 해요",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"중간고사 전에 발표 과제를 끝내야 해요",chunks:["민서는 이번 학기에 한국어 수업을 듣고 있어요. 중간고사 전에 발표 과제를 끝내야 해서 친구와 일정을 정했어요. 발표가 성적에 들어가니까 오늘부터 준비하기 시작하기로 했어요.","시험 기간 동안은 도서관에서 매일 공부할 거예요. 기말고사에서 좋은 성적을 받기 위해 복습도 하고, 모르는 것은 교수님께 질문하기로 했어요. 장학금을 받으려면 좋은 성적이 필요하니까 이번 학기에는 더 꾸준히 공부해야 해요."],translation:"Minseo is taking a Korean class this semester. Because the presentation assignment must be finished before the midterm, Minseo made a schedule with a friend. Since the presentation counts toward the grade, they decided to start preparing from today. During exam period, Minseo will study at the library every day. In order to get a good grade on the final, Minseo will review and ask the professor about things they do not know. Since good grades are needed for a scholarship, Minseo has to study more consistently this semester.",qas:[{q:"민서는 언제까지 발표 과제를 끝내야 해요?",a:"중간고사 전에 끝내야 해요."},{q:"시험 기간 동안 어디에서 공부할 거예요?",a:"도서관에서 공부할 거예요."},{q:"무엇을 받으려면 좋은 성적이 필요해요?",a:"장학금을 받으려면 좋은 성적이 필요해요."}]},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"민서는 이번 ___에 한국어 수업을 듣고 있어요.",options:[{text:"전공",ok:!1},{text:"학기",ok:!0},{text:"학원",ok:!1}],explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"___ 전에 발표 과제를 끝내야 해서 친구와 일정을 정했어요.",options:[{text:"전공",ok:!1},{text:"중간고사",ok:!0},{text:"학원",ok:!1}],explanation:""}],canDo:"State a decision or study plan (같이 공부하기로 했어요)",id:"chapter-54-b11",chapterId:"chapter-54",index:10},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence gives a school obligation?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"중간고사 전에 발표 과제를 끝내야 해요.",ok:!0},{text:"중간고사 전에 발표 과제를 끝낼 리가 없어요.",ok:!1},{text:"중간고사 전에 발표 과제를 끝내더라고요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-아/어야 해요 means have to."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence states a study decision?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"도서관에서 공부하기로 했어요.",ok:!0},{text:"도서관에서 공부한 척했어요.",ok:!1},{text:"도서관에서 공부할 뻔했어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-기로 했어요 states what was decided."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence uses 동안 correctly?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"시험 기간 동안 매일 공부해요.",ok:!0},{text:"시험 기간에 동안 매일 공부해요.",ok:!1},{text:"시험 기간을 동안 매일 공부해요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Put 동안 directly after the time span noun."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"과제___ 해야 해요. (I have to do the assignment.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"를",ok:!0},{text:"가",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"하다 takes the assignment as an object, so 과제를 fits. 가 would make the assignment the subject."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence states a personal study decision?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"오늘부터 도서관에서 공부하기로 했어요.",ok:!0},{text:"오늘부터 공부해야 해요.",ok:!1},{text:"오늘부터 공부하는 동안이에요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"-기로 했어요 marks a decision. -아/어야 해요 marks obligation instead."}],canDo:"Say when an action started (공부하기 시작했어요)",id:"chapter-54-b12",chapterId:"chapter-54",index:11}]},{id:"chapter-55",number:55,title:"N(으)로 · V-(으)러 가요",goal:"Ask and answer subway transfer questions with 호선, 환승, 갈아타다, and 승강장.",biteCount:12,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 환승~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"환승",romanization:"hwanseung",en:"transfer",pos:"noun",nuance:"환승 means transferring between buses and subways. If you tap the same transit card, transfers within a time limit are usually discounted or free."},sentence:{ko:"서울역에 가려면 어디서 환승해요?",en:"Where do I transfer if I want to get to Seoul Station?"},target:"환승",options:["subway line number","transfer","platform"],note:"Official transit noun; 갈아타다 is the everyday action verb."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"갈아타다",romanization:"garatada",en:"to transfer / change vehicles",pos:"verb",nuance:"갈아타다 is a transparent compound of 갈다 'to change/replace' + 타다 'to ride', so it literally means 'change-ride' — you get off one vehicle and board a different one. The dividing line learners must feel is against plain 타다 (just 'to board/get on') and against 환승하다, the Sino-Korean twin (換乘, 換 'exchange' + 乘 'ride', literally 'change-ride') printed on every subway sign and announcement: 갈아타다 is the warm spoken word a friend uses ('여기서 갈아타'), while 환승하다 is the signage/official register ('환승 게이트'). The grammar trap is the particle: the vehicle you move TO takes 로/으로 (지하철로 갈아타다), the same directional 로 as 'change INTO', and the station where you do it takes 에서 — English 'transfer to the subway' tempts you to drop the particle or use 에. Because 타다 is regular, every form is regular too: the stem 타- already ends in ㅏ, so the 아 of 아요 merges in and disappears, giving 갈아타요 (not 갈아타아요), and the past is 갈아탔어요. Habitual collocations: 다른 노선으로 갈아타다, 같은 방향으로 갈아타다."},sentence:{ko:"시청역에서 1호선으로 갈아타세요.",en:"Transfer to Line 1 at City Hall Station."},target:"갈아타",options:["to transfer / change vehicles","to book a ticket in advance","subway line number"],note:"Linked to verb card word-verb-378."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"호선",romanization:"hoseon",en:"subway line number",pos:"noun",nuance:"호선 is for subway line labels, not for bus routes. Say 2호선 for Line 2, but 버스 노선 for a bus route."},sentence:{ko:"서울역에 가려면 몇 호선으로 갈아타요?",en:"Which line do I transfer to if I want to get to Seoul Station?"},target:"호선",options:["transfer","platform","subway line number"],note:"Chapter card word-ext-ch55-hoseon."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"승강장",romanization:"seunggangjang",en:"platform",pos:"noun",nuance:"승강장 乘降場 stacks 乘 (board/get on) + 降 (alight/get off) + 場 (place/field) = 'the place where you board and get off', i.e. the platform along the tracks. It is the formal term on signage and in announcements; the everyday spoken equivalent is 플랫폼 (the English loanword), and 타는 곳 ('boarding place') is the plain-Korean label posted in many stations — all three point to the same spot. The crucial contrast is with 정류장 停留場 (a bus/tram STOP at the roadside) and 역 (the whole STATION building): the 승강장 is the specific edge where you stand beside the train, inside the 역. As a 場 place-noun it takes 에서 for waiting (승강장에서 기다려요) and 으로 for moving toward it (승강장으로 가요). The English-speaker pitfall is treating the platform as something you wait 'for': you wait for the TRAIN on the platform, so it's 승강장에서 기차를 기다려요, never 승강장을 기다려요."},sentence:{ko:"3번 승강장으로 가세요.",en:"Please go to platform 3."},target:"승강장",options:["transfer","subway line number","platform"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1597."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"노선",romanization:"noseon",en:"route, transit line",pos:"noun",nuance:"노선(路線) is Sino-Korean 路 'road' + 線 'line' — literally a 'road-line' — meaning a transit route/line for both buses and subways; the famous 노선도(路線圖) 'route map' (圖 = diagram) is the colored subway map newcomers squint at. The key usage split: for a specific subway line Koreans say the number + 호선 (2호선 'Line 2'), while 노선 is the general word for a route's path, used heavily for buses (버스 노선). Pitfalls: don't conflate 노선 'the route itself' with 노선도 'the map of routes' — one is the line, the other the diagram. And don't say 2노선 for Line 2; the counter is 호선. Apps like 카카오맵/네이버지도 list every 노선 and timetable."},sentence:{ko:"이 노선은 배차 간격이 길어요.",en:"This route has a long service interval."},target:"노선",options:["route, transit line","transfer","platform"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-631."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"교통카드",romanization:"gyotongkadeu",en:"transit card",pos:"noun",nuance:"교통카드(交通+card) literally welds the Sino-Korean 교통 'traffic/transport' onto the English 'card', and names the rechargeable tap-to-ride card (T머니, 캐시비, or a 후불 credit card) for buses, the subway, and even some taxis. The make-or-break verb is 찍다 'to tap/stamp', never 누르다 'to press a button' — you lay the card flat on the reader, you don't push anything. A prepaid card you 충전 'top up' at convenience stores; the gate beeps your 잔액 'remaining balance'. Don't confuse it with a plain 카드, which to a Korean means a credit/debit card for shopping; the tap-to-ride one is always the full 교통카드. English 'swipe' also misleads — there is no magnetic swipe, only a contactless 찍다."},sentence:{ko:"교통카드를 충전하려고 해요.",en:"I am trying to top up my transit card."},target:"교통카드",options:["transfer","transit card","subway line number"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-622."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"지금 홍대입구역인데 서울역 가려면 어디서 환승해?",en:"I am at Hongik University Station now. Where do I transfer to get to Seoul Station?"},hl:"환승"}],canDo:"Say what I travel by or which way I go (버스로, 2호선으로)",id:"chapter-55-b1",chapterId:"chapter-55",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N(으)로",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N(으)로",sub:"by (a means of transport) / via (a route)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"저는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"버스",mid:"로",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"학교에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"저는 버스로 학교에 가요.",en:"I go to school by bus.",romanization:"jeoneun beoseuro hakgyoe gayo."},{tokens:[{pre:"지하철",mid:"로",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"가면",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"더",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"빨라요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"지하철로 가면 더 빨라요.",en:"It's faster if you go by subway.",romanization:"jihacheollo gamyeon deo ppallayo."}],rule:{name:"N(으)로",rows:[{when:"Noun ends in a vowel",add:"-로",ex:"택시 → 택시로 (by taxi)"},{when:"Noun ends in ㄹ",add:"-로",ex:"지하철 → 지하철로 (by subway)"},{when:"Noun ends in any other consonant",add:"-으로",ex:"오른쪽 → 오른쪽으로 (to the right)"}]},more:{func:"Attach (으)로 to a noun to say what you travel by (버스로 = by bus) or which way you go (이 길로 = this way). In transportation talk it is the everyday particle for naming the vehicle, the subway line, or the road you take.",funcLead:"Attach (으)로 to a noun to say what you travel by (버스로 = by bus) or which way you go (이 길로 = this way).",keyPoint:{label:"(으)로 가다 vs 을/를 타고 가다",body:"버스로 가요 and 버스를 타고 가요 both mean 'go by bus.' (으)로 simply labels the means in one word; 을/를 타고 literally says 'ride and go,' picturing you boarding. Use (으)로 for the quick, neutral 'by ___,' and 을/를 타고 when you want to stress actually getting on."},pronunciation:"After ㄹ the two ㄹ sounds link: 지하철로 is said [지하철로] as 'jihacheollo,' not 'jihacheoro.' Same for 이 길로 → 'gillo.'",pitfall:{wrong:"저는 버스에 가요.",right:"저는 버스로 가요.",explanation:"English 'by bus' tempts learners to grab 에. But the means of transport always takes (으)로, never 에. 버스에 would point to the bus as a destination, like 'into the bus.'"}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Go this way (via this road).",sentence:"이 길___ 가세요.",options:[{text:"로",ok:!0},{text:"으면",ok:!1},{text:"때문에",ok:!1}],explanation:"Here (으)로 marks a route, not a vehicle. 길 ends in ㄹ, so -로."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Shall we go to the airport by taxi?",sentence:"공항까지 택시___ 갈까요?",options:[{text:"으면",ok:!1},{text:"때문에",ok:!1},{text:"로",ok:!0}],explanation:"택시 → -로 names the vehicle; 까지 marks the destination 'all the way to'."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I go to school by bus." (저는 시작 · Start with 저는.)',tokens:["저는","버스로","학교에","가요."],correct:"저는 버스로 학교에 가요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "It's faster if you go by subway."`,tokens:["지하철로","가면","더","빨라요."],correct:"지하철로 가면 더 빨라요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Say what I travel by or which way I go (버스로, 2호선으로)",id:"chapter-55-b2",chapterId:"chapter-55",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 충전~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"충전",romanization:"chungjeon",en:"top-up / charge",pos:"noun",nuance:"For transit cards, 충전 is a top-up. For phones, it is battery charging. The object matters: 교통카드를 충전하다 versus 휴대폰을 충전하다."},sentence:{ko:"교통카드에 만 원 충전해 주세요.",en:"Please top up my transit card with 10,000 won."},target:"충전",options:["transfer","subway line number","top-up / charge"],note:"Transit-card top-up phrase; the card takes 에 when money is added."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"KTX",romanization:"KTX",en:"KTX high-speed train",pos:"noun",nuance:"KTX (Korea Train eXpress, spoken letter-by-letter as 케이티엑스) is the fast premium train Koreans set against three cheaper options: the 고속버스, the slower 무궁화호, and the mid-tier ITX-새마을. The boundary worth fixing is KTX vs SRT — both are bullet trains, but KTX is run by 코레일 (Korail) and SRT is a separate operator from 수서역, so the booking app differs. Note it behaves grammatically more like a proper noun than 버스: 'by KTX' is naturally KTX로/으로, where buses prefer 타고. It collocates with 타다, 예매하다, 표, and the seat tiers 특실/일반실/자유석/입석. The signature English-speaker pitfall is reading the letters in English; a Korean always says 케이티엑스, sounding each letter Korean-style. A second trap is booking on the wrong app — for KTX you need 코레일톡, not the SRT app."},sentence:{ko:"서울에서 부산까지 KTX로 가요.",en:"I go from Seoul to Busan by KTX."},target:"KTX",options:["transfer","subway line number","KTX high-speed train"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-993."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"표",romanization:"pyo",en:"ticket",pos:"noun",nuance:"표 is the general ticket word. 입장권 is more specifically an admission ticket for entering a venue, so KTX 표 should not be replaced with 입장권."},sentence:{ko:"부산 가는 표 있어요?",en:"Are there tickets to Busan?"},target:"표",options:["transfer","ticket","subway line number"],note:"General ticket word for KTX, buses, movies, and events."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"매표소",romanization:"maepyoso",en:"ticket counter / ticket office",pos:"noun",nuance:"매표소 is the selling place for tickets. 표 is the ticket itself, and 예매하다 is the booking action."},sentence:{ko:"KTX 표 예매하러 매표소에 가요.",en:"I am going to the ticket counter to book a KTX ticket."},target:"매표소",options:["ticket counter / ticket office","transfer","subway line number"],note:"Chapter card word-ext-ch55-maepyoso."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"예매하다",romanization:"yemaehada",en:"to book a ticket in advance",pos:"verb",nuance:"예매하다 is ticket-centered and usually means the seat or ticket is secured before departure or showtime. 예약하다 is broader and can mean reserving a restaurant, hotel, appointment, or service."},sentence:{ko:"부산 가는 KTX 표를 예매하려고 해요.",en:"I am trying to book a KTX ticket to Busan."},target:"예매하",options:["to transfer / change vehicles","to book a ticket in advance","transfer"],note:"Chapter card word-ext-ch55-yemaehada."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"편도",romanization:"pyeondo",en:"one-way",pos:"noun",nuance:"편도 is the opposite of 왕복. It answers the ticket type question, not the destination question."},sentence:{ko:"부산 표는 편도로 예매할게요.",en:"I will book the Busan ticket one-way."},target:"편도",options:["one-way","transfer","subway line number"],note:"Chapter card word-ext-ch55-pyeondo."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"고마워. 부산 가는 KTX 표를 예매하려고 하는데 편도면 돼?",en:"Thanks. I am trying to book a KTX ticket to Busan. Is one-way okay?"},hl:"KTX"}],canDo:"Give the purpose of a trip (표를 사러 가요)",id:"chapter-55-b3",chapterId:"chapter-55",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-(으)러 가요",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"V-(으)러 가요",sub:"go (somewhere) in order to do something",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant",add:"-으러",ex:"표를 받다 → 표를 받으러 가요 (go to pick up the ticket)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel",add:"-러",ex:"타다 → 타러 가요 (go to catch/board it)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in ㄹ",add:"-러 (ㄹ stays)",ex:"(표를) 팔다 → 팔러 가요 (go to sell the ticket)"}],examples:[{ko:"기차표를 사러 역에 가요.",en:"I'm going to the station to buy a train ticket.",romanization:"gichapyoreul sareo yeoge gayo.",note:"사다 ends in a vowel → -러; the purpose (buying a ticket) comes before the movement verb 가요."},{ko:"친구를 만나러 부산에 가요.",en:"I'm going to Busan to meet a friend.",romanization:"chingureul mannareo busane gayo.",note:"만나다 → 만나러; the whole trip is for meeting the friend."}],more:{func:"Attach -(으)러 to a verb stem before a movement verb (가다 / 오다 / 다니다) to give the purpose of the trip: you go or come somewhere in order to do that action. Very common for travel errands like buying tickets or topping up a card.",funcLead:"Attach -(으)러 to a verb stem before a movement verb (가다 / 오다 / 다니다) to give the purpose of the trip: you go or come somewhere in order to do that action.",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)러 needs a 'go/come' verb",body:"-(으)러 must be followed by a movement verb (가다, 오다, 다니다). You cannot say 표를 사러 공부해요 — the main verb has to involve going or coming. If your main verb is not movement, use -(으)려고 instead (Note 3)."},pronunciation:"받으러 links the final ㄷ into the 으: say [바드러] 'badeureo,' not [받으러] with a hard stop.",pitfall:{wrong:"표를 사기 위해 가요.",right:"표를 사러 가요.",explanation:"Learners reach for -기 위해 because it maps onto English 'in order to.' But for a physical errand with go/come, -(으)러 is far more natural and shorter. Save -기 위해 for abstract or formal goals."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I'm going to the station to buy a train ticket." (기차표를 시작 · Start with 기차표를.)`,tokens:["기차표를","사러","역에","가요."],correct:"기차표를 사러 역에 가요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I'm going to Busan to meet a friend." (친구를 시작 · Start with 친구를.)`,tokens:["친구를","만나러","부산에","가요."],correct:"친구를 만나러 부산에 가요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"표를 사러 가요.",ok:!0},{text:"표를 사기 위해 가요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Learners reach for -기 위해 because it maps onto English 'in order to.' But for a physical errand with go/come, -(으)러 is far more natural and shorter. Save -기 위해 for abstract or formal goals."}],canDo:"Give the purpose of a trip (표를 사러 가요)",id:"chapter-55-b4",chapterId:"chapter-55",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 왕복~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"왕복",romanization:"wangbok",en:"round trip",pos:"noun",nuance:"왕복 is the ticket-plan pair of 편도. The question 편도예요, 왕복이에요? is a normal ticket-counter question."},sentence:{ko:"돌아오는 시간도 정했으면 왕복으로 예매하세요.",en:"If you have decided the return time too, book it round-trip."},target:"왕복",options:["round trip","transfer","subway line number"],note:"Chapter card word-ext-ch55-wangbok."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"매진",romanization:"maejin",en:"sold out for tickets/seats",pos:"noun",nuance:"매진 is for tickets and seats. 품절 is for products on a shelf or in an online store."},sentence:{ko:"KTX 표가 매진이면 다음 기차로 예매하세요.",en:"If the KTX tickets are sold out, book the next train."},target:"매진",options:["transfer","sold out for tickets/seats","subway line number"],note:"Chapter card word-ext-ch55-maejin."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"지연",romanization:"jiyeon",en:"delay",pos:"noun",nuance:"지연 sounds more official than 늦다. Use 늦다 for a person being late and 지연 for a service, schedule, or system delay."},sentence:{ko:"버스 지연 때문에 약속에 늦었어요.",en:"I was late to the appointment because of a bus delay."},target:"지연",options:["transfer","delay","subway line number"],note:"Chapter card word-ext-ch55-jiyeon."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"배차 간격",romanization:"baecha gangyeok",en:"service interval",pos:"noun",nuance:"배차 간격 is not the travel time. It is the gap between one bus or train and the next one."},sentence:{ko:"이 노선은 배차 간격이 길어서 택시로 갈게요.",en:"This route has a long service interval, so I will go by taxi."},target:"배차 간격",options:["service interval","transfer","subway line number"],note:"Chapter card word-ext-ch55-baecha-gangyeok."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"택시",romanization:"taeksi",en:"taxi",pos:"noun",nuance:"택시로 가요 and 택시를 타요 are both natural, but they answer different questions: method versus boarding action."},sentence:{ko:"버스가 지연되면 택시로 갈게요.",en:"If the bus is delayed, I will go by taxi."},target:"택시",options:["taxi","transfer","platform"],note:"Method phrase with 로: 택시로 가다."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"막차",romanization:"makcha",en:"last train or bus",pos:"noun",nuance:"막차 fuses the native prefix 막- 'last/final' with 차(車) 'vehicle', literally 'the last vehicle', and it dominates Korean nightlife because the subway stops near midnight and missing it means a pricey taxi. Its exact opposite is 첫차 'first vehicle'. Two collocations are everywhere: 막차 끊기다 'the last service is cut off (gone)' and 막차 놓치다 'miss the last one'. The key pitfall is the English verb 'miss' — Korean uses 놓치다 'let slip', NEVER 잃어버리다 'lose (an object)', so 막차를 잃어버렸어요 sounds like you misplaced a train. Also note 막차 시간 is the DEPARTURE time, so a learner who reads it as an arrival time will stroll up just as the doors close."},sentence:{ko:"막차를 놓치면 첫차를 타야 해요.",en:"If you miss the last train, you have to take the first train."},target:"막차",options:["transfer","last train or bus","subway line number"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-624."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"응, 돌아오는 시간 안 정했으면 편도로 해. 매진이면 다음 기차 확인하고.",en:"Yes, if you have not set the return time, book it one-way. If it is sold out, check the next train."},hl:"매진"}],canDo:"State a travel intention (예매하려고 해요)",id:"chapter-55-b5",chapterId:"chapter-55",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-(으)려고 해요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V-(으)려고 해요",sub:"be planning / intending to do something",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"이번",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"주말에는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"기차로",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"부산에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가",mid:"려고",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"해요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"이번 주말에는 기차로 부산에 가려고 해요.",en:"This weekend I'm planning to go to Busan by train.",romanization:"ibeon jumareneun gicharo busane garyeogo haeyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"차가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"막혀서",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"지하철을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"타",mid:"려고",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"",mid:"해요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"차가 막혀서 지하철을 타려고 해요.",en:"Traffic is bad, so I'm going to take the subway.",romanization:"chaga makhyeoseo jihacheoreul taryeogo haeyo."}],rule:{name:"V-(으)려고 해요",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant",add:"-으려고 해요",ex:"표를 찾다 → 찾으려고 해요 (I plan to pick up the ticket)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel",add:"-려고 해요",ex:"타다 → 타려고 해요 (I intend to take it)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in ㄹ",add:"-려고 해요 (ㄹ stays)",ex:"(표를) 팔다 → 팔려고 해요 (I plan to sell the ticket)"}]},more:{func:"Attach -(으)려고 하다 to a verb stem to say what you intend or plan to do. With travel it means 'I'm going to / I'm trying to' make the trip or booking. It states a decided intention, softer than the firm future -(으)ㄹ 거예요.",funcLead:"Attach -(으)려고 하다 to a verb stem to say what you intend or plan to do.",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)려고 vs -(으)러",body:"-(으)러 needs a movement main verb and means 'go/come TO DO something' (표를 사러 가요). -(으)려고 (해요) states the intention itself and works with ANY main verb (표를 사려고 해요 = 'I plan to buy a ticket'). If you can drop 가요/와요 and still have a full sentence, you want -(으)려고 해요."},pronunciation:"In casual speech people sometimes say 갈려고 with an extra ㄹ, but the standard form keeps one ㄹ: 가려고 'garyeogo,' not 갈려고.",pitfall:{wrong:"표를 사려고 가요. (meaning 'I plan to buy a ticket')",right:"표를 사려고 해요.",explanation:"To STATE the plan you need -(으)려고 해요 with 하다 at the end. 표를 사려고 가요 only works as 'I go in order to buy a ticket' (a purpose for going), overlapping with -(으)러. Decide: stating a plan → 해요; purpose for going → 가요/러."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "This weekend I'm planning to go to Busan by train." (이번 시작 · Start with 이번.)`,tokens:["이번","주말에는","기차로","부산에","가려고","해요."],correct:"이번 주말에는 기차로 부산에 가려고 해요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Traffic is bad, so I'm going to take the subway."`,tokens:["차가","막혀서","지하철을","타려고","해요."],correct:"차가 막혀서 지하철을 타려고 해요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"표를 사려고 해요.",ok:!0},{text:"표를 사려고 가요. (meaning 'I plan to buy a ticket')",ok:!1}],explanation:"To STATE the plan you need -(으)려고 해요 with 하다 at the end. 표를 사려고 가요 only works as 'I go in order to buy a ticket' (a purpose for going), overlapping with -(으)러. Decide: stating a plan → 해요; purpose for going → 가요/러."}],canDo:"State a travel intention (예매하려고 해요)",id:"chapter-55-b6",chapterId:"chapter-55",index:5},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A/V-(으)면",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"A/V-(으)면",sub:"if / when (a travel condition happens)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"길이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"막히",mid:"면",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"지하철을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"타세요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"길이 막히면 지하철을 타세요.",en:"If the road is jammed, take the subway.",romanization:"giri makhimyeon jihacheoreul taseyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"버스를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"놓치",mid:"면",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"다음",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"버스를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"기다려야",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"해요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"버스를 놓치면 다음 버스를 기다려야 해요.",en:"If you miss the bus, you have to wait for the next one.",romanization:"beoseureul notchimyeon daeum beoseureul gidaryeoya haeyo."}],rule:{name:"A/V-(으)면",rows:[{when:"Stem ends in a vowel",add:"-면",ex:"막히다 → 막히면 (if it's jammed)"},{when:"Stem ends in a consonant",add:"-으면",ex:"놓치다 is a vowel stem; consonant example: 없다 → 없으면 (if there isn't)"},{when:"Stem ends in ㄹ",add:"-면 (ㄹ stays)",ex:"길다 → 길면 (if it's long)"}]},more:{func:"Attach -(으)면 to a verb or adjective stem to set up a condition: 'if/when ___, then ___.' In transportation it covers fallback plans: if the bus is delayed, if you miss the last train, if tickets are sold out.",funcLead:"Attach -(으)면 to a verb or adjective stem to set up a condition: 'if/when ___, then ___.' In transportation it covers fallback plans: if the bus is delayed, if…",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)면 vs -(으)니까",body:"-(으)면 is hypothetical 'if' (막히면 = if it gets jammed — may or may not happen). -(으)니까 gives a real, already-true reason 'since/because' (막히니까 = since it's jammed right now). Don't use -(으)면 for a present fact you can already see."},pronunciation:"없으면 links the ㅄ cluster: say [업쓰면] 'eopsseumyeon.' After a consonant stem, the buffer 으 is heard clearly: 먹으면 [머그면]. 놓치다 ends in a vowel, so it simply becomes 놓치면.",pitfall:{wrong:"지금 막히면 지하철을 타요. (meaning 'since it's jammed now')",right:"지금 막히니까 지하철을 타요.",explanation:"When the situation is already true right now, Korean uses -(으)니까 (since/because), not -(으)면. -(으)면 is for hypothetical or repeated conditions, not a fact you're looking at this moment."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"If the tickets are sold out, check the next train.",sentence:"표가 매진이___ 다음 기차를 확인하세요.",options:[{text:"으로",ok:!1},{text:"면",ok:!0},{text:"때문에",ok:!1}],explanation:"Noun state 매진 + 이면; for nouns use 이면 after a consonant."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"If the service interval is long, a taxi is better.",sentence:"배차 간격이 길___ 택시가 나아요.",options:[{text:"면",ok:!0},{text:"으로",ok:!1},{text:"때문에",ok:!1}],explanation:"Adjective stem 길- (ends in ㄹ) → 길면, ㄹ stays."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "If the road is jammed, take the subway."',tokens:["길이","막히면","지하철을","타세요."],correct:"길이 막히면 지하철을 타세요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "If you miss the bus, you have to wait for the next one."',tokens:["버스를","놓치면","다음","버스를","기다려야","해요."],correct:"버스를 놓치면 다음 버스를 기다려야 해요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Make a fallback plan with a condition (늦으면 버스로 가요)",id:"chapter-55-b7",chapterId:"chapter-55",index:6},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · N 때문에",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"N 때문에",sub:"because of (a transport cause)",lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"버스",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"지연",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"때문에",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"약속에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"늦었어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"버스 지연 때문에 약속에 늦었어요.",en:"I was late for the appointment because of a bus delay.",romanization:"beoseu jiyeon ttaemune yaksoge neujeosseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"사고",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"",mid:"때문에",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"길이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"많이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"막혀요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"사고 때문에 길이 많이 막혀요.",en:"The road is very jammed because of an accident.",romanization:"sago ttaemune giri mani makhyeoyo."}],rule:{name:"N 때문에",rows:[{when:"Noun cause (any ending)",add:"N 때문에",ex:"지연 → 지연 때문에 (because of a delay)"},{when:"Do NOT add 이/가 or 을/를",add:"(no particle before 때문에)",ex:"사고 때문에, not 사고가 때문에"},{when:"For a verb/adjective reason instead",add:"use -아/어서 or -(으)니까",ex:"막혀서 늦었어요 (was late because it was jammed)"}]},more:{func:"Use N 때문에 to name the noun reason that something happened or that a plan changed. In transportation it explains delays and disruptions: 지연 때문에 (because of a delay), 사고 때문에 (because of an accident).",funcLead:"Use N 때문에 to name the noun reason that something happened or that a plan changed.",keyPoint:{label:"N 때문에 vs V/A-아서",body:"Use N 때문에 when the reason is a NOUN (지연 때문에). When the reason is a verb or adjective, switch to -아/어서: 지하철이 늦어서 (because the subway was late). You cannot put a full verb directly before 때문에 — you'd need to nominalize it (늦었기 때문에)."},pronunciation:"때문에 links the ㄴ into 에: say [때무네] 'ttaemune,' as one smooth phrase, not 'ttaemun-e' with a pause.",pitfall:{wrong:"지연이 때문에 늦었어요.",right:"지연 때문에 늦었어요.",explanation:"English 'because of the delay' has 'the,' which tempts learners to add a subject particle 이/가. But 때문에 attaches straight to the bare noun — no 이/가 and no 을/를 in front of it."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Because of the rain, I can't catch a taxi.",sentence:"비 ___ 택시가 안 잡혀요.",options:[{text:"으로",ok:!1},{text:"때문에",ok:!0},{text:"으면",ok:!1}],explanation:"비 (rain) + 때문에; a typical transport complaint."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Because of the last train, we have to leave now.",sentence:"막차 ___ 지금 출발해야 해요.",options:[{text:"으로",ok:!1},{text:"때문에",ok:!0},{text:"으면",ok:!1}],explanation:"The last-train schedule is the reason for hurrying."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I was late for the appointment because of a bus delay." (버스 시작 · Start with 버스.)',tokens:["버스","지연","때문에","약속에","늦었어요."],correct:"버스 지연 때문에 약속에 늦었어요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "The road is very jammed because of an accident." (사고 시작 · Start with 사고.)',tokens:["사고","때문에","길이","많이","막혀요."],correct:"사고 때문에 길이 많이 막혀요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Explain a delay's cause and tie a tip to a moment (사고 때문에, 갈아탈 때 조심하세요)",id:"chapter-55-b8",chapterId:"chapter-55",index:7},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V-(으)ㄹ 때",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"V-(으)ㄹ 때",sub:"when (the moment you do a travel action)",rows:[{when:"Verb stem ends in a vowel",add:"-ㄹ 때",ex:"타다 → 탈 때 (when you ride)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in a consonant",add:"-을 때",ex:"갈아타다 → 갈아탈 때 (when you transfer); 받다 → 받을 때 (when you receive)"},{when:"Verb stem ends in ㄹ",add:"-ㄹ 때 (one ㄹ, the stem ㄹ drops into it)",ex:"놀다 → 놀 때; here use 만들다 → 만들 때"}],examples:[{ko:"환승할 때 출구 번호도 확인하세요.",en:"When you transfer, check the exit number too.",romanization:"hwanseunghal ttae chulgu beonhodo hwaginhaseyo.",note:"환승하다 → 환승할 때; a real subway-navigation tip."},{ko:"표를 예매할 때 편도인지 왕복인지 확인하세요.",en:"When booking a ticket, check whether it's one-way or round-trip.",romanization:"pyoreul yemaehal ttae pyeondoinji wangboginji hwaginhaseyo.",note:"예매하다 → 예매할 때; a useful ticket-buying check."}],more:{func:"Attach -(으)ㄹ 때 to a verb stem to name the moment an action happens: 'when transferring,' 'when booking,' 'when you ride.' It is the everyday way to give travel tips and warnings tied to a specific moment.",funcLead:"Attach -(으)ㄹ 때 to a verb stem to name the moment an action happens: 'when transferring,' 'when booking,' 'when you ride.' It is the everyday way to give travel…",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)ㄹ 때 (present/general) vs -았/었을 때 (completed)",body:"Plain -(으)ㄹ 때 names the moment of an ongoing or general action: 탈 때 = 'when (in the act of) riding.' Add past -았/었을 때 for a moment after something finished: 내렸을 때 = 'when I had gotten off.' For habits and instructions you almost always want plain -(으)ㄹ 때."},pronunciation:"The 때 after -ㄹ tenses to a hard sound: 탈 때 is said [탈 떼] 'tal ttae,' and 할 때 is 'hal ttae,' with a tightened ㄸ.",pitfall:{wrong:"환승하는 때 출구 번호를 확인하세요.",right:"환승할 때 출구 번호를 확인하세요.",explanation:"Learners often attach the present modifier -는 to 때, copying English 'the time when you transfer.' But 때 takes the -(으)ㄹ modifier, not -는: 환승할 때, never 환승하는 때."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "When you transfer, check the exit number too."',tokens:["환승할","때","출구","번호도","확인하세요."],correct:"환승할 때 출구 번호도 확인하세요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "When booking a ticket, check whether it's one-way or round-trip."`,tokens:["표를","예매할","때","편도인지","왕복인지","확인하세요."],correct:"표를 예매할 때 편도인지 왕복인지 확인하세요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"환승할 때 출구 번호를 확인하세요.",ok:!0},{text:"환승하는 때 출구 번호를 확인하세요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Learners often attach the present modifier -는 to 때, copying English 'the time when you transfer.' But 때 takes the -(으)ㄹ modifier, not -는: 환승할 때, never 환승하는 때."}],canDo:"Say what I travel by or which way I go (버스로, 2호선으로)",id:"chapter-55-b9",chapterId:"chapter-55",index:8},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"A Kakao-style travel chat while Minseo transfers to Seoul Station and books a KTX ticket.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"지금 홍대입구역인데 서울역 가려면 어디서 환승해?",en:"I am at Hongik University Station now. Where do I transfer to get to Seoul Station?",romanization:"jigeum hongdaeipguyeoginde seouryeok garyeomyeon eodiseo hwanseunghae?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"2호선 타고 시청역에서 1호선으로 갈아타. 승강장은 오른쪽이야.",en:"Take Line 2 and transfer to Line 1 at City Hall. The platform is on the right.",romanization:"i hoseon tago sicheongyeogeseo il hoseoneuro garata. seunggangjangeun oreunjjogiya."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"고마워. 부산 가는 KTX 표를 예매하려고 하는데 편도면 돼?",en:"Thanks. I am trying to book a KTX ticket to Busan. Is one-way okay?",romanization:"gomawo. busan ganeun KTX pyoreul yemaeharyeogo haneunde pyeondomyeon dwae?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"응, 돌아오는 시간 안 정했으면 편도로 해. 매진이면 다음 기차 확인하고.",en:"Yes, if you have not set the return time, book it one-way. If it is sold out, check the next train.",romanization:"eung, doraoneun sigan an jeonghaesseumyeon pyeondoro hae. maejinimyeon daeum gicha hwaginhago."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"버스 지연 때문에 조금 늦으면 택시로 갈게.",en:"If I am a little late because of the bus delay, I will go by taxi.",romanization:"beoseu jiyeon ttaemune jogeum neujeumyeon taeksiro galge."},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"괜찮아. 환승할 때 출구 번호도 같이 확인해.",en:"No problem. When you transfer, check the exit number too.",romanization:"gwaenchana. hwanseunghal ttae chulgu beonhodo gachi hwaginhae."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'Transfer to Line 2 at Seoul Station.'",tokens:["서울역에서","2호선으로","갈아타세요"],correct:"서울역에서 2호선으로 갈아타세요",explanation:"에서 marks the transfer location, and (으)로 marks the line you transfer to. 갈아타세요 is the transit instruction."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"누가 한 말일까요? · Who said this?",sentence:"응, 돌아오는 시간 안 정했으면 편도로 해. 매진이면 다음 기차 확인하고.",options:[{text:"민서",ok:!1},{text:"지훈",ok:!0}],explanation:"Yes, if you have not set the return time, book it one-way. If it is sold out, check the next train."},{kind:"drill",prompt:'무엇이 들어갈까요? · "I am at Hongik University Station now. Where do I transfer to get to Seoul Station?"',sentence:"지금 홍대입구역인데 서울역 가려면 어디서 ___해?",options:[{text:"환승",ok:!0},{text:"호선",ok:!1},{text:"승강장",ok:!1}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Say what I travel by or which way I go (버스로, 2호선으로)",id:"chapter-55-b10",chapterId:"chapter-55",index:9},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 서울역에서 부산까지 가는 길",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"서울역에서 부산까지 가는 길",chunks:["민서는 홍대입구역에서 서울역으로 가려고 해요. 먼저 2호선을 타고 시청역에서 1호선으로 갈아타야 해요. 환승할 때는 승강장 방향과 출구 번호를 같이 확인하면 헷갈리지 않아요. 서울역에 도착하면 부산 가는 KTX 표를 예매할 거예요.","돌아오는 시간이 아직 정해지지 않아서 편도로 예매하려고 해요. 만약 표가 매진이면 다음 기차를 확인하면 돼요. 친구에게는 버스 지연 때문에 늦으면 택시로 가겠다고 메시지를 보냈어요."],translation:"Minseo is planning to go from Hongik University Station to Seoul Station. First, Minseo takes Line 2 and transfers to Line 1 at City Hall Station. When transferring, checking both the platform direction and exit number helps avoid confusion. After arriving at Seoul Station, Minseo will book a KTX ticket to Busan. Since the return time is not set yet, Minseo plans to book one-way. If the tickets are sold out, Minseo can check the next train. Minseo also messaged a friend that if they are late because of a bus delay, they will go by taxi.",qas:[{q:"민서는 어디에서 1호선으로 갈아타야 해요?",a:"시청역에서 갈아타야 해요."},{q:"돌아오는 시간이 정해지지 않았으면 어떤 표로 예매해요?",a:"편도로 예매해요."},{q:"버스 지연 때문에 늦으면 무엇으로 가겠다고 했어요?",a:"택시로 가겠다고 했어요."}]},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"먼저 2___을 타고 시청역에서 1호선으로 갈아타야 해요.",options:[{text:"노선",ok:!1},{text:"호선",ok:!0},{text:"교통카드",ok:!1}],explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"환승할 때는 ___ 방향과 출구 번호를 같이 확인하면 헷갈리지 않아요.",options:[{text:"노선",ok:!1},{text:"승강장",ok:!0},{text:"교통카드",ok:!1}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Give the purpose of a trip (표를 사러 가요)",id:"chapter-55-b11",chapterId:"chapter-55",index:10},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence gives a natural subway transfer instruction?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"시청역에서 1호선으로 갈아타세요.",ok:!0},{text:"시청역에게 1호선을 갈아타세요.",ok:!1},{text:"시청역을 1호선에 갈아타세요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Use 에서 for the transfer station and 으로 for the line you change to."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which word is natural when KTX tickets are sold out?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"매진",ok:!0},{text:"품절",ok:!1},{text:"고장",ok:!1}],explanation:"매진 is for tickets and seats; 품절 is usually for store products."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence explains a delay cause correctly?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"버스 지연 때문에 늦었어요.",ok:!0},{text:"버스 지연이 때문에 늦었어요.",ok:!1},{text:"버스 지연을 때문에 늦었어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Put the noun cause directly before 때문에 with no extra particle."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"KTX 표___ 예매했어요. (I booked a KTX ticket.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"를",ok:!0},{text:"가",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"예매하다 takes the ticket as an object, so 표를 fits. 가 would make the ticket the subject."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence says you will go by taxi?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"택시로 갈게요.",ok:!0},{text:"택시에 갈게요.",ok:!1},{text:"택시를 갈게요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Transport method takes (으)로. 택시에 would mean to a taxi, which is not the intended meaning."}],canDo:"State a travel intention (예매하려고 해요)",id:"chapter-55-b12",chapterId:"chapter-55",index:11}]},{id:"chapter-56",number:56,title:"V/A-잖아요 · V/A-거든요",goal:"Choose and explain housewarming gifts with 집들이, 선물, 휴지, and 세제.",biteCount:12,bites:[{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 1 · 집들이~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"집들이",romanization:"jipdeuri",en:"housewarming party",pos:"noun",nuance:"집들이 is a pure native-Korean word (no hanja): 집 'house' + 들이 (from 들이다, 'to bring/take in'), so it literally evokes 'bringing people into the house' — the housewarming party thrown after an 이사 (移徙, moving). Don't confuse the event with the move itself: you do 이사 first (the act of relocating), THEN 집들이 (the celebration). Also distinguish it from 집들이 선물 vs a generic 선물 — the classic gifts are pointed: 휴지 (toilet paper), because it 'unrolls smoothly' (everything in the new home going smoothly), and 세제 (laundry detergent), whose bubbles symbolize prosperity 'bubbling up.' These are warm, traditional choices, not cheap ones — an English speaker may misread practical household items as an awkward gift. It's a relaxed gathering where the host usually cooks or orders in for friends."},sentence:{ko:"주말에 회사 선배 집들이에 가요.",en:"I am going to a senior coworker's housewarming this weekend."},target:"집들이",options:["housewarming party","gift","toilet paper / tissue"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1109."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"선물",romanization:"seonmul",en:"gift",pos:"noun",nuance:"선물 can be the thing given, received, bought, or prepared. In this chapter, focus on the particle around the person and the particle attached to 선물 itself."},sentence:{ko:"집들이 선물로 세제를 가져가도 괜찮아요.",en:"It is okay to bring detergent as a housewarming gift."},target:"선물",options:["gift","housewarming party","toilet paper / tissue"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-ch42-seonmul."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"휴지",romanization:"hyuji",en:"toilet paper / tissue",pos:"noun",nuance:"휴지 is the hanja word 休紙 — literally '休 rest/discard + 紙 paper,' i.e. 'paper to be used up and tossed' — and in real life it covers BOTH rolled toilet paper and the flat table tissue you wipe your mouth with; context tells you which. The contrast to learn is 휴지 vs 티슈 vs 화장지: 티슈 is the soft pop-up/pocket pack you carry, 화장지 is the more formal/printed label for toilet roll, and 휴지 is the broad everyday word. Note 휴지통 ('휴지 + bin') means the wastebasket, so don't picture only paper. The pitfall is the particle and the verb: you wipe WITH it, so 휴지로 닦다 (means 로, vowel-final), never 휴지를 닦다 (which would mean wiping the paper itself). Also 휴지 is uncountable-feeling but a torn square is 한 칸 and a roll is 한 통/한 롤."},sentence:{ko:"휴지는 집들이 선물로 많이 가져가잖아요.",en:"People often bring toilet paper as a housewarming gift, you know."},target:"휴지",options:["housewarming party","gift","toilet paper / tissue"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-378."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"세제",romanization:"seje",en:"detergent",pos:"noun",nuance:"세제 is practical rather than fancy. In 집들이 culture, practical household gifts can feel considerate because they help the new home run smoothly."},sentence:{ko:"집들이 선물로 세제를 샀거든요.",en:"I bought detergent as the housewarming gift, you see."},target:"세제",options:["housewarming party","gift","detergent"],note:"Chapter card word-ext-ch56-seje."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"나이",romanization:"nai",en:"age",pos:"noun",nuance:"나이 is the native Korean word for a person's age, and it sits at the center of Korean social life because age sets who speaks formally to whom. Register is everything: to an adult, an elder, or a stranger you ask 나이가 어떻게 되세요? (or even more deferential 연세가 어떻게 되세요?, using the honorific 연세 for grandparents), while the blunt 몇 살이에요? is reserved for children or close friends — asking your boss 몇 살이에요? sounds rude. The near-synonym pair is 나이 vs 연세: same meaning, but 연세 (年歲, Sino-Korean) is the respectful 'years' you grant to someone older, so you raise the register by swapping the word, not by adding an ending. Crucially, age is measured with 많다/적다, not size words: 나이가 많아요 = 'is old', 나이가 적어요 = 'is young' — never 나이가 커요, a direct-translation trap from English 'big'. When you state an actual number, age takes NATIVE numbers + the counter 살: 스무 살 (20), 서른 살 (30) — saying 이십 살 with Sino-Korean numbers is a classic beginner error. One more pitfall: the verb is 되세요 (from 되다, 'to become/amount to'), so 어떻게 되세요? literally asks 'how does it come to be?' — treat the whole phrase as a fixed polite formula rather than translating it word by word."},sentence:{ko:"나이가 비슷하면 동갑일 수도 있어요.",en:"If your ages are similar, you might be the same age."},target:"나이",options:["housewarming party","gift","age"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1329."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"동갑",romanization:"donggap",en:"same age",pos:"noun",nuance:"동갑 is useful because Korean relationships often adjust around age. Still, being 동갑 does not mean you immediately use 반말; closeness and setting matter too."},sentence:{ko:"동갑이지만 처음엔 존댓말을 써요.",en:"We are the same age, but at first I use polite speech."},target:"동갑",options:["housewarming party","same age","gift"],note:"Chapter card word-ext-ch56-donggap."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"민서",ko:"집들이에 갈 때 휴지랑 세제 중에 뭐가 나아요?",en:"When going to a housewarming, which is better, toilet paper or detergent?"},hl:"집들이"}],canDo:"Remind someone of shared knowledge (선물하잖아요)",id:"chapter-56-b1",chapterId:"chapter-56",index:0},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V/A-잖아요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V/A-잖아요",sub:'"you know (we both know this)"',lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"윗사람한테는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"두",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"손으로",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"드리",mid:"잖아요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"윗사람한테는 두 손으로 드리잖아요.",en:"You hand things to elders with both hands, you know.",romanization:"witsaram-hante-neun du soneuro deurijanhayo."},{tokens:[{pre:"집들이에는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"보통",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"휴지나",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"세제를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"사",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가",mid:"잖아요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"집들이에는 보통 휴지나 세제를 사 가잖아요.",en:"You usually bring toilet paper or detergent to a housewarming, you know.",romanization:"jipdeurie-neun botong hyujina sejereul sa gajanhayo."}],rule:{name:"V/A-잖아요",rows:[{when:"Verb or Adjective stem (present)",add:"-잖아요",ex:"한국에서는 신발을 벗잖아요 (You take your shoes off in Korea, you know)"},{when:"Past tense (V & A)",add:"-았/었잖아요",ex:"지난번에 제가 말했잖아요 (I told you last time, remember)"},{when:"Noun + 이다",add:"(이)잖아요",ex:"오늘 어버이날이잖아요 (It's Parents' Day today, you know)"}]},more:{func:"Reminds the listener of something you assume you both already know, or gently corrects them with a fact they should remember. It softens a reminder by framing it as shared knowledge rather than news — very common when explaining everyday manners and customs.",funcLead:"Reminds the listener of something you assume you both already know, or gently corrects them with a fact they should remember.",keyPoint:{label:"-잖아요 vs. -거든요",body:"Use -잖아요 when you think the listener ALREADY knows it ('as you know'). Use -거든요 when it's NEW background the listener does not know yet ('the thing is…'). Same slot, opposite assumption about the listener."},pronunciation:"-잖아요 is pronounced [자나요]. The spelling keeps ㄶ, but the ㅎ is not heard before ㄴ; keep the ㄴ sound and use three clear syllables: 자-나-요.",pitfall:{wrong:"한국에서는 신발을 벗어요, 잖아요.",right:"한국에서는 신발을 벗잖아요.",explanation:"-잖아요 is a sentence ending attached straight to the stem (벗 + 잖아요). It is not a tag word you stick after a finished -아요/-어요 sentence the way English adds 'right?' at the end."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"We saw it together yesterday, remember? Don't you recall?",sentence:"어제 같이 봤___, 기억 안 나요?",options:[{text:"거든요",ok:!1},{text:"는데요",ok:!1},{text:"잖아요",ok:!0}],explanation:"Past form gently corrects the listener about a shared experience."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"You leave the priority seats empty on the subway, you know.",sentence:"지하철에서는 노약자석을 비워 두___.",options:[{text:"거든요",ok:!1},{text:"는데요",ok:!1},{text:"잖아요",ok:!0}],explanation:"Appeals to a publicly known norm to back up the speaker's point."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "You hand things to elders with both hands, you know."',tokens:["윗사람한테는","두","손으로","드리잖아요."],correct:"윗사람한테는 두 손으로 드리잖아요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "You usually bring toilet paper or detergent to a housewarming, you know." (집들이에는 시작 · Start with 집들이에는.)',tokens:["집들이에는","보통","휴지나","세제를","사","가잖아요."],correct:"집들이에는 보통 휴지나 세제를 사 가잖아요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Remind someone of shared knowledge (선물하잖아요)",id:"chapter-56-b2",chapterId:"chapter-56",index:1},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 2 · 선배~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"선배",romanization:"seonbae",en:"senior colleague or schoolmate",pos:"noun",nuance:"선배 (先輩, 'earlier-generation/fellow') means someone senior to you who entered the same school, university, company, or field BEFORE you — a relationship that structures enormous amounts of Korean social life. The sharp contrast: a 선배 is defined by entering EARLIER, while a 상사 is defined by RANK — your 선배 may or may not be your boss, and your boss may not be your 선배 if they transferred in. Also distinguish 선배 from 어른 (an elder/adult by age generally). Unlike 상사, you CAN and do address a 선배 directly, adding 님 for politeness: 선배님. A 선배 traditionally looks after their 후배 (junior), often picking up the meal tab. Register: warm but respectful, deeply tied to hierarchy. The English-speaker pitfall: English has no real equivalent and learners default to 'senior' = 'boss' — but 선배 is about timing, not authority; treating every 선배 as a 상사 (or vice versa) misreads the relationship. Mark whom you learn from with 한테/에게."},sentence:{ko:"처음엔 선배님이라고 부르는 편이에요.",en:"At first, I tend to call them seonbaenim."},target:"선배",options:["housewarming party","gift","senior colleague or schoolmate"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1624."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"후배",romanization:"hubae",en:"junior colleague or schoolmate",pos:"noun",nuance:"후배 means someone who entered the same school, company, or field later than you. 후배님 is possible as a respectful address in some workplaces or unfamiliar relationships; names, roles, and local workplace custom also affect the best form of address."},sentence:{ko:"후배라도 처음엔 존댓말을 쓰면 좋아요.",en:"Even with a junior, it is good to use polite speech at first."},target:"후배",options:["housewarming party","gift","junior colleague or schoolmate"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1625."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"호칭",romanization:"hoching",en:"form of address / title",pos:"noun",nuance:"호칭 is not just a word choice; it signals age, role, closeness, and respect. When unsure, asking 어떤 호칭을 쓰면 될까요? is safer than guessing too casually."},sentence:{ko:"어떤 호칭을 쓰면 될까요?",en:"What title should I use?"},target:"호칭",options:["housewarming party","form of address / title","gift"],note:"Chapter card word-ext-ch56-hoching."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"어르신",romanization:"eoreusin",en:"elder; older person respectfully",pos:"noun",nuance:"어르신 is respectful and warm. 어른 simply means adult or grown-up; 어르신 adds social respect for age."},sentence:{ko:"어르신도 계시면 말투를 조심하세요.",en:"If elders are there too, be careful with your tone."},target:"어르신",options:["housewarming party","gift","elder; older person respectfully"],note:"Chapter card word-ext-ch56-eoreusin."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"눈치",romanization:"nunchi",en:"social awareness / reading the room",pos:"noun",nuance:"눈치 is a native-Korean word (눈 'eye' + 치) and one of the most essential Korean social concepts: the radar for sensing what others feel and what's appropriate without being told. The distinctions learners trip on are between its set phrases: 눈치가 빠르다 ('quick eye') is high praise, 눈치가 없다 is a real social criticism, 눈치를 보다 means cautiously reading someone's mood (often a boss's, with a slightly self-conscious feel), and 눈치(를) 채다 means to catch on / notice something. So 보다 and 채다 are not interchangeable — one is ongoing wariness, the other is a moment of realization. The English-speaker pitfall is mapping 눈치 to 'eyes' or vision because of 눈; it has nothing to do with eyesight and everything to do with social perceptiveness. Living in Korea you'll use and need this word daily."},sentence:{ko:"눈치 있게 챙기면 분위기가 덜 어색해요.",en:"If you take care of things tactfully, the mood is less awkward."},target:"눈치",options:["housewarming party","social awareness / reading the room","gift"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-797."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"분위기",romanization:"bunwigi",en:"atmosphere; mood",pos:"noun",nuance:"분위기 (雰圍氣, 雰 'mist/vapor' + 圍 'surround' + 氣 'air/energy') is literally 'the air that surrounds you' — the collective mood of a place, gathering, or moment. The sharp contrast is 분위기 vs 기분: 분위기 is the SHARED vibe in the room (회식 분위기가 좋다), while 기분 is one person's INNER feeling (제 기분이 좋다 'I'm in a good mood'). Mixing them is the classic error: 'I'm in a good mood' is 기분이 좋아요, not 분위기가 좋아요 (which describes the room, not you). Habitual partners: 분위기가 좋다/편안하다/어색하다, 분위기를 띄우다/바꾸다, 분위기가 가라앉다, 분위기를 깨다 ('kill the mood'). 분위기 있다 colloquially means 'has a classy/romantic ambiance.' The English-speaker pitfall, beyond 기분 confusion, is the particle: 분위기를 좋다 ✗ → 분위기가 좋다 ✓."},sentence:{ko:"분위기가 어색하면 먼저 물어보세요.",en:"If the atmosphere is awkward, ask first."},target:"분위기",options:["housewarming party","atmosphere; mood","gift"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1692."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"그래도 처음엔 선배님 같은 호칭을 쓰는 편이에요.",en:"Even so, at first people tend to use a title like seonbaenim."},hl:"선배"}],canDo:"Explain background or a reason (좋은 뜻이 있거든요)",id:"chapter-56-b3",chapterId:"chapter-56",index:2},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V/A-거든요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V/A-거든요",sub:`"the thing is… (here's the background)"`,lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"오늘은",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"일찍",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"볼게요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"시댁에",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가야",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"하",mid:"거든요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"오늘은 일찍 가 볼게요. 시댁에 가야 하거든요.",en:"I'll head off early today. The thing is, I have to visit my in-laws.",romanization:"oneureun iljjik ga bolgeyo. sidaege gaya hageodeunyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"꽃을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"사",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"왔어요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"오늘",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"처음",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"인사",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"드리는",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"거",mid:"거든요",post:".",hit:!0}],ko:"꽃을 사 왔어요. 오늘 처음 인사 드리는 거거든요.",en:"I brought flowers. It's the first time I'm greeting you properly, you see.",romanization:"kkocheul sa wasseoyo. oneul cheoeum insa deurineun geogeodeunyo."}],rule:{name:"V/A-거든요",rows:[{when:"Verb or Adjective stem (present)",add:"-거든요",ex:"제가 매운 걸 못 먹거든요 (The thing is, I can't eat spicy food)"},{when:"Past tense (V & A)",add:"-았/었거든요",ex:"어제 늦게 잤거든요 (I went to bed late yesterday, you see)"},{when:"Noun + 이다",add:"(이)거든요",ex:"오늘이 제 생일이거든요 (Today's my birthday, you see)"}]},more:{func:"Gives a reason or piece of background the listener does not yet know, often answering a real or expected 'why?'. It signals 'the thing is…' and is a soft, polite way to explain yourself in conversation.",funcLead:"Gives a reason or piece of background the listener does not yet know, often answering a real or expected 'why?'.",keyPoint:{label:"-거든요 vs. -아서/어서",body:"Both give reasons. -아서/어서 joins the reason to a clause in the SAME sentence ('Because A, B'). -거든요 ends its own sentence and offers the reason as new information the listener didn't have, so it usually stands alone as a reply."},pronunciation:"-거든요 is [거드뇨] in quick speech, but the careful form is [거든뇨]→[거든요]. Keep the 든 clear.",pitfall:{wrong:"A stand-alone -아서요 reason is unnatural or incomplete.",right:"왜 안 가요? — 약속이 있어서요. / 약속이 있거든요.",explanation:"Both replies are natural. -아서요 gives a direct, polite reason and is extremely common in everyday answers. -거든요 presents the reason as background the listener may not know and can sound a little more explanatory. Choose the nuance instead of treating -아서요 as an error."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I won't drink. The thing is, I have to drive.",sentence:"술은 안 마실게요. 제가 운전해야 하___.",options:[{text:"거든요",ok:!0},{text:"잖아요",ok:!1},{text:"는데요",ok:!1}],explanation:"Declines politely by offering a reason, which is socially expected."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Sorry I couldn't reach you. My phone broke, you see.",sentence:"연락 못 드려서 죄송해요. 핸드폰이 고장 났___.",options:[{text:"잖아요",ok:!1},{text:"거든요",ok:!0},{text:"는데요",ok:!1}],explanation:"Past form gives the unknown cause behind an apology."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"A stand-alone -아서요 reason is unnatural or incomplete.",ok:!1},{text:"왜 안 가요? — 약속이 있어서요. / 약속이 있거든요.",ok:!0}],explanation:"Both replies are natural. -아서요 gives a direct, polite reason and is extremely common in everyday answers. -거든요 presents the reason as background the listener may not know and can sound a little more explanatory. Choose the nuance instead of treating -아서요 as an error."}],canDo:"Explain background or a reason (좋은 뜻이 있거든요)",id:"chapter-56-b4",chapterId:"chapter-56",index:3},{kind:"words",title:"단어 Words 3 · 어색하다~",cards:[{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"어색하다",romanization:"eosaekada",en:"to be awkward",pos:"adjective",nuance:"어색하다 traces to the Sino-Korean 語塞 (어 語 'speech' + 색 塞 'blocked/stuck') — the picture of words getting jammed — and names two feelings: the stiff, uneasy awkwardness between people who aren't yet close, and the sense that something looks or feels unnatural or out of place (a forced smile, a new haircut, an odd-sounding sentence). It is the antonym of 자연스럽다 (natural). Draw the line from 부끄럽다 and 창피하다: those are shyness/embarrassment about YOURSELF (blushing, shame), whereas 어색하다 is the awkwardness of an unfamiliar situation or relationship — no shame, just lack of ease; and 불편하다 is broader discomfort, while 어색하다 specifically flags social unfamiliarity. As a 하다 adjective it conjugates to 어색해요. English-speaker pitfall: don't use 어색하다 for personal embarrassment ('I was so embarrassed' is 창피했어요); reserve 어색하다 for that not-yet-comfortable-together or 'this looks unnatural' feeling."},sentence:{ko:"처음 만난 자리라서 조금 어색해요.",en:"It is a little awkward because it is the first meeting."},target:"어색해",options:["housewarming party","gift","to be awkward"],note:"Linked to dictionary card word-ext-1265."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"도와드리다",romanization:"dowadeurida",en:"to help politely",pos:"verb",nuance:"도와드리다 is the humble form of 도와주다 ('help (as a favour)'), where 돕다 (to help) becomes 도와- by the ㅂ-irregular (돕 + 아 → 도와), and the helper-benefit 주다 is upgraded to its humble twin 드리다. So it literally means 'humbly do the favour of helping' — you lower yourself to help someone above you: a customer, elder, or guest. Contrast the three levels you must keep straight: 돕다 (bare dictionary 'help'), 도와주다 (help someone, neutral/friendly), 도와드리다 (help a superior, humble). English flattens all of these to 'help', so the pitfall is using 도와주다 with a customer (too casual for service) or, conversely, 도와드리다 with a buddy (oddly stiff). The phrase 무엇을 도와드릴까요? is the fixed, must-know service line. Conjugation follows 드리다: 도와드려요, 도와드렸어요."},sentence:{ko:"도와드릴까요? 하고 먼저 물어보세요.",en:"Ask first, 'May I help?'"},target:"도와드릴",options:["to help politely","to prepare / take care of","housewarming party"],note:"Linked to verb card word-verb-265."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"챙기다",romanization:"chaenggida",en:"to prepare / take care of",pos:"verb",nuance:"챙기다 is one of the most useful 'caring' verbs in daily Korean, with a cluster of related senses: (1) gather/pack the things you need (짐을 챙기다), (2) make sure not to skip something, especially food (밥 챙겨 먹어 'make sure you eat'), and (3) look after a person attentively (동생 좀 챙겨 'keep an eye on your sibling'). Set it against 돌보다 (care for over time, like nursing a child or the sick): 챙기다 is quicker, more about 'making sure / not letting slip', while 돌보다 is sustained nurturing. It also contrasts with 챙겨 주다, where adding 주다 makes the care explicitly a favour for someone. The English trap is reaching for 들다 'lift' or 가지다 'have' when you mean 'grab/remember to bring' — that everyday act is 챙기다. Native warmth: 잘 챙겨 먹어요 is a standard way to say 'take care of yourself'."},sentence:{ko:"집들이 선물을 챙겼어요.",en:"I prepared a housewarming gift."},target:"챙겼",options:["to help politely","to prepare / take care of","housewarming party"],note:"Linked to verb card word-verb-262."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"수고하셨습니다",romanization:"sugohasyeotseumnida",en:"thank you for your hard work",pos:"expression",nuance:"수고하셨습니다 acknowledges finished effort. 수고(受苦 / loosely) means 'trouble, effort, hard work'; 하시— is the honorific stem of 하다 'to do'; and the past -셨- marks completion, so the phrase means '(you) have done hard work'. The make-or-break contrast is TENSE and DIRECTION. Tense: 수고하셨습니다 (past, work is finished) vs 수고하세요 (present, person is still at it — used as you leave). Mix these and you are either congratulating someone mid-task or urging a finished person to keep going. Direction: 수고하셨습니다 flows naturally downward (superior to junior) or sideways (peers), but upward to a clearly senior boss it can sound presumptuous, as if you are evaluating their effort from a position of authority you don't have — to a superior, 감사합니다 or 고생 많으셨습니다 is safer. The near-synonym 고생하셨습니다 (고생 'hardship/toil') feels warmer and more empathetic about the difficulty of the work, not just the fact of effort. Casual register: 수고했어 (반말, to a junior or close peer). The English-speaker pitfall is conflating it with a casual 'thanks' and dropping it on anyone regardless of rank or whether the work is done."},sentence:{ko:"오늘 초대해 주셔서 감사합니다. 수고하셨습니다.",en:"Thank you for inviting me today. Thank you for your hard work."},target:"수고하셨습니다",options:["thanks to","housewarming party","thank you for your hard work"],note:"Linked to expression card expr-125."},{kind:"guess",word:{ko:"덕분에",romanization:"deokbune",en:"thanks to",pos:"expression",nuance:"덕분에 is for welcome causes. 때문에 often sounds neutral or negative, so 선배님 때문에 잘 됐어요 can sound like blame even with a good result."},sentence:{ko:"선배님 덕분에 분위기가 편해졌어요.",en:"Thanks to you, the atmosphere became comfortable."},target:"덕분에",options:["thanks to","thank you for your hard work","housewarming party"],note:"Chapter card word-ext-ch56-deokbune."},{kind:"payoff",line:{speaker:"지훈",ko:"좋아요. 눈치 있게 챙기면 분위기가 덜 어색해요.",en:"Good. If you take care of things tactfully, the mood is less awkward."},hl:"챙기"}],canDo:"Soften a statement or careful question (조심하는 게 좋은데요)",id:"chapter-56-b5",chapterId:"chapter-56",index:4},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V/A-는데요",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V/A-는데요",sub:'"soften a careful statement or question"',lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"이거",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"집들이",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"선물로",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"드리려고",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"하",mid:"는데요",post:",",hit:!0},{pre:"괜찮을까요?",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"이거 집들이 선물로 드리려고 하는데요, 괜찮을까요?",en:"I was thinking of giving this as a housewarming gift… would that be okay?",romanization:"igeo jipdeuri seonmullo deuriryeogo haneundeyo, gwaenchaneulkkayo?"},{tokens:[{pre:"호칭을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"잘",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"모르",mid:"는데요",post:",",hit:!0},{pre:"뭐라고",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"부르면",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"될까요?",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"호칭을 잘 모르는데요, 뭐라고 부르면 될까요?",en:"I'm not really sure what to call you… what should I say?",romanization:"hochingeul jal moreuneundeyo, mworago bureumyeon doelkkayo?"}],rule:{name:"V/A-는데요",rows:[{when:"Verb stem (present)",add:"-는데요",ex:"지금 가는데요 (Well, I'm going now…)"},{when:"Adjective stem (present)",add:"-(으)ㄴ데요",ex:"좀 비싼데요 (It's a bit pricey, though…)"},{when:"Past tense (V & A)",add:"-았/었는데요",ex:"벌써 끝났는데요 (It's already finished, though…)"},{when:"Noun + 이다",add:"인데요",ex:"저는 처음인데요 (It's my first time, you see…)"}]},more:{func:"Ends a turn on an open, non-final note that leaves room for the listener to react. It softens statements, eases into careful questions, and sets up background before a request — essential for polite, indirect social talk.",funcLead:"Ends a turn on an open, non-final note that leaves room for the listener to react.",keyPoint:{label:"Adjective -(으)ㄴ데요 vs. Verb -는데요",body:"Verbs always take -는데요 (가는데요, 먹는데요). Adjectives take -(으)ㄴ데요 (비싼데요, 좋은데요). 있다/없다 act like verbs: 있는데요, 없는데요. Sorting verb from adjective is the part learners trip on most."},pronunciation:"-는데요 is [는데요]. After ㄹ-final verb stems the ㄹ drops: 살다 → 사는데요 (not 살는데요).",pitfall:{wrong:"좀 비싸는데요.",right:"좀 비싼데요.",explanation:"Learners over-apply the verb pattern -는데요 to adjectives. Adjectives (비싸다, 좋다, 크다) must use -(으)ㄴ데요, so it's 비싼데요, not 비싸는데요."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I did make a reservation, but… we might be a little late.",sentence:"예약은 했___, 시간이 좀 늦을 것 같아요.",options:[{text:"는데요",ok:!0},{text:"잖아요",ok:!1},{text:"거든요",ok:!1}],explanation:"Past -는데요 concedes one point before adding a softer caveat."},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I was thinking of giving this as a housewarming gift… would that be okay?"',tokens:["이거","집들이","선물로","드리려고","하는데요,","괜찮을까요?"],correct:"이거 집들이 선물로 드리려고 하는데요, 괜찮을까요?",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "I'm not really sure what to call you… what should I say?"`,tokens:["호칭을","잘","모르는데요,","뭐라고","부르면","될까요?"],correct:"호칭을 잘 모르는데요, 뭐라고 부르면 될까요?",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"좀 비싼데요.",ok:!0},{text:"좀 비싸는데요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Learners over-apply the verb pattern -는데요 to adjectives. Adjectives (비싸다, 좋다, 크다) must use -(으)ㄴ데요, so it's 비싼데요, not 비싸는데요."}],canDo:"Soften a statement or careful question (조심하는 게 좋은데요)",id:"chapter-56-b6",chapterId:"chapter-56",index:5},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V/A-(으)ㄴ/는 편이다",cards:[{kind:"teach",name:"V/A-(으)ㄴ/는 편이다",sub:`"I tend to / I'm rather …"`,rows:[{when:"Verb stem (present habit)",add:"-는 편이다",ex:"저는 일찍 가는 편이에요 (I tend to arrive early)"},{when:"Adjective stem (present)",add:"-(으)ㄴ 편이다",ex:"한국 사람들은 정이 많은 편이에요 (Koreans tend to be warm-hearted)"},{when:"Past verb (one-time result)",add:"-(으)ㄴ 편이다",ex:"선물을 많이 받은 편이에요 (I got rather a lot of gifts)"},{when:"Negative (verb)",add:"안/잘 안 …-는 편이다",ex:"술을 잘 안 마시는 편이에요 (I tend not to drink much)"}],examples:[{ko:"저는 처음엔 호칭을 조심하는 편이에요.",en:"At first I tend to be careful with how I address people.",romanization:"jeoneun cheoeumen hochingeul josimhaneun pyeonieyo.",note:"Hedges a self-description so it sounds modest, not absolute."},{ko:"요즘 젊은 사람들은 명절에 한복을 잘 안 입는 편이에요.",en:"These days young people tend not to wear hanbok on the holidays.",romanization:"yojeum jeolmeun saramdeureun myeongjeore hanbogeul jal an imneun pyeonieyo.",note:"A soft generalization about a changing custom, leaving room for exceptions."}],more:{func:"Describes a general tendency rather than a hard fact — 'on the … side', 'tends to be …'. It hedges a judgment, which sounds modest and natural when you comment on people, habits, or customs without being too absolute.",funcLead:"Describes a general tendency rather than a hard fact — 'on the … side', 'tends to be …'.",keyPoint:{label:"Verb 는 편 vs. Adjective (으)ㄴ 편",body:"With a verb, use -는 편이다 for a habit (가는 편, 챙기는 편). With an adjective, use -(으)ㄴ 편이다 for a quality (많은 편, 비싼 편). 편이다 means 'leans that way', so never use it for a one-time event like '오늘 갔어요'."},pronunciation:"편이에요 is [펴니에요]; the ㄴ links into the next syllable. Keep 편 short, not 'pyeon-i'.",pitfall:{wrong:"한국 사람들은 정이 많는 편이에요.",right:"한국 사람들은 정이 많은 편이에요.",explanation:"많다 is an adjective, so it takes -(으)ㄴ 편이다 → 많은 편이에요, not the verb ending -는 편. Check whether the word is a verb or an adjective before you pick 는 or (으)ㄴ."}}},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "At first I tend to be careful with how I address people."',tokens:["저는","처음엔","호칭을","조심하는","편이에요."],correct:"저는 처음엔 호칭을 조심하는 편이에요.",explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어느 쪽이 자연스러워요? · Which one is natural?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"한국 사람들은 정이 많은 편이에요.",ok:!0},{text:"한국 사람들은 정이 많는 편이에요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"많다 is an adjective, so it takes -(으)ㄴ 편이다 → 많은 편이에요, not the verb ending -는 편. Check whether the word is a verb or an adjective before you pick 는 or (으)ㄴ."}],canDo:"Describe a tendency modestly (나이가 많은 편이에요)",id:"chapter-56-b7",chapterId:"chapter-56",index:6},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · V/A-(으)니까",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"V/A-(으)니까",sub:`"since (so here's what to do)"`,lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"집들이",mid:"니까",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"작은",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"선물이라도",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"챙겨",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"가세요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"집들이니까 작은 선물이라도 챙겨 가세요.",en:"Since it's a housewarming, take at least a small gift.",romanization:"jipdeurinikka jageun seonmurirado chaenggyeo gaseyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"어르신도",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"계시",mid:"니까",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"말투를",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"조심해요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"어르신도 계시니까 말투를 조심해요.",en:"Since there are elders there too, be careful with your tone.",romanization:"eoreusindo gyesinikka maltureul josimhaeyo."}],rule:{name:"V/A-(으)니까",rows:[{when:"Verb/Adjective stem ending in a vowel",add:"-니까",ex:"바쁘다 → 바쁘니까 미리 챙기세요 (Since you're busy, prepare it in advance)"},{when:"Verb/Adjective stem ending in a consonant",add:"-으니까",ex:"많다 → 사람이 많으니까 조심하세요 (Since there are many people, be careful)"},{when:"Past tense (V & A)",add:"-았/었으니까",ex:"덕분에 잘 됐으니까 감사 인사를 하세요 (Since it went well thanks to them, say thank you)"},{when:"Noun + 이다",add:"(이)니까",ex:"오늘이 첫날이니까 일찍 가요 (Since today's the first day, let's go early)"}]},more:{func:"Gives a reason that leads into advice, a request, or a decision: 'since X, (let's / please / I'll) …'. Unlike -아서/어서, it's the natural choice when the second clause is a suggestion or command — very common when explaining the reason behind a social custom.",funcLead:"Gives a reason that leads into advice, a request, or a decision: 'since X, (let's / please / I'll) …'.",keyPoint:{label:"-(으)니까 vs. -아서/어서",body:"Both mean 'because', but only -(으)니까 can lead into a command or suggestion: 늦었으니까 빨리 가세요 (O), 늦어서 빨리 가세요 (X). Use -아서/어서 for plain cause-and-result, -(으)니까 when you're telling someone what to do because of the reason."},pronunciation:"-(으)니까 is [(으)니까] with a tense 까. Don't soften it to '니가'; the double ㄲ stays strong.",pitfall:{wrong:"늦어서 빨리 가세요.",right:"늦었으니까 빨리 가세요.",explanation:"When the second clause is a command or suggestion (가세요, 합시다, -ㄹ게요), Korean needs -(으)니까, not -아서/어서. -아서/어서 only works before a plain statement, so 'Since we're late, please hurry' must use 늦었으니까."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Since I'm not sure yet, I'll ask first how to address them.",sentence:"아직 잘 모르___ 호칭을 먼저 여쭤볼게요.",options:[{text:"니까",ok:!0},{text:"잖아요",ok:!1},{text:"거든요",ok:!1}],explanation:"Reason + the speaker's own decision (-ㄹ게요)."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Since the mood is a bit awkward, I'll be the one to break the ice.",sentence:"분위기가 좀 어색하___ 제가 먼저 말을 걸어 볼게요.",options:[{text:"잖아요",ok:!1},{text:"거든요",ok:!1},{text:"니까",ok:!0}],explanation:"-(으)니까 supports a decision to act."},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Since it's a housewarming, take at least a small gift."`,tokens:["집들이니까","작은","선물이라도","챙겨","가세요."],correct:"집들이니까 작은 선물이라도 챙겨 가세요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:'방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "Since there are elders there too, be careful with your tone."',tokens:["어르신도","계시니까","말투를","조심해요."],correct:"어르신도 계시니까 말투를 조심해요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Give a reason that leads into advice (선배니까 호칭을 조심하세요)",id:"chapter-56-b8",chapterId:"chapter-56",index:7},{kind:"pattern",title:"문법 Grammar · A/V-지만",cards:[{kind:"hunt",name:"A/V-지만",sub:`"… but (I'll still do the polite thing)"`,lines:[{tokens:[{pre:"동갑이",mid:"지만",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"처음엔",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"존댓말을",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"써요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"동갑이지만 처음엔 존댓말을 써요.",en:"We're the same age, but at first I use polite speech.",romanization:"donggabijiman cheoeumen jondaenmareul sseoyo."},{tokens:[{pre:"조금",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"어색하",mid:"지만",post:"",hit:!0},{pre:"제가",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"먼저",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1},{pre:"인사할게요.",mid:"",post:"",hit:!1}],ko:"조금 어색하지만 제가 먼저 인사할게요.",en:"It's a little awkward, but I'll greet them first.",romanization:"jogeum eosaekajiman jega meonjeo insahalgeyo."}],rule:{name:"A/V-지만",rows:[{when:"Verb or Adjective stem",add:"-지만",ex:"바쁘지만 갈게요 (I'm busy, but I'll go)"},{when:"Past tense (V & A)",add:"-았/었지만",ex:"늦었지만 인사는 드렸어요 (I was late, but I did greet them)"},{when:"Noun + 이다",add:"(이)지만",ex:"동갑이지만 존댓말을 써요 (We're the same age, but I use polite speech)"}]},more:{func:"Admits one fact, then turns to a contrasting choice: 'A, but B'. In social situations the second clause usually shows the careful or considerate option, so it's handy for explaining why you act politely even when you don't strictly have to.",funcLead:"Admits one fact, then turns to a contrasting choice: 'A, but B'.",keyPoint:{label:"-지만 vs. 그렇지만 / 그런데",body:"-지만 joins the two clauses inside one sentence (바쁘지만 갈게요). 그렇지만 / 그런데 do the same job but start a new sentence: 바빠요. 그렇지만 갈게요. Use -지만 to keep it as one smooth sentence."},pronunciation:"-지만 itself is [지만], but a preceding final obstruent can tense its ㅈ: 봤지만 is pronounced [봗찌만]. With 어렵지만, the ㅂ stays because -지만 begins with a consonant.",pitfall:{wrong:"동갑이에요지만 존댓말을 써요.",right:"동갑이지만 존댓말을 써요.",explanation:"-지만 attaches to the plain stem, not to a finished 해요/예요 form. With a noun it's 동갑 + 이지만 → 동갑이지만, never 동갑이에요 + 지만."}}},{kind:"drill",prompt:"I'm busy, but I prepared the housewarming gift ahead of time.",sentence:"바쁘___ 집들이 선물은 미리 챙겼어요.",options:[{text:"잖아요",ok:!1},{text:"거든요",ok:!1},{text:"지만",ok:!0}],explanation:"Effort despite being busy shows consideration."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"We're not close, but calling them seonbaenim is the safe choice.",sentence:"친하지 않___ 선배님이라고 부르는 게 안전해요.",options:[{text:"지만",ok:!0},{text:"잖아요",ok:!1},{text:"거든요",ok:!1}],explanation:"Title before closeness — the careful option."},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "We're the same age, but at first I use polite speech."`,tokens:["동갑이지만","처음엔","존댓말을","써요."],correct:"동갑이지만 처음엔 존댓말을 써요.",explanation:""},{kind:"order",prompt:`방금 본 문장을 다시 조립하세요 · Rebuild: "It's a little awkward, but I'll greet them first."`,tokens:["조금","어색하지만","제가","먼저","인사할게요."],correct:"조금 어색하지만 제가 먼저 인사할게요.",explanation:""}],canDo:"Remind someone of shared knowledge (선물하잖아요)",id:"chapter-56-b9",chapterId:"chapter-56",index:8},{kind:"dialogue",title:"대화 Dialogue · 진짜 한국어",cards:[{kind:"chat",setting:"Minseo asks how to behave at a coworker's housewarming.",lines:[{speaker:"민서",ko:"집들이에 갈 때 휴지랑 세제 중에 뭐가 나아요?",en:"When going to a housewarming, which is better, toilet paper or detergent?",romanization:"jipdeurie gal ttae hyujirang seje junge mwoga naayo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"둘 다 괜찮아요. 집들이 선물로 많이 가져가잖아요.",en:"Both are fine. People often bring them as housewarming gifts, you know.",romanization:"dul da gwaenchanayo. jipdeuri seonmullo manhi gajyeogajanhayo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"나이가 같으면 동갑이라고 말해도 돼요?",en:"If we are the same age, can I say that we are donggap?",romanization:"naiga biseutamyeon donggabirago malhaedo dwaeyo?"},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"그래도 처음엔 선배님 같은 호칭을 쓰는 편이에요.",en:"Even so, at first people tend to use a title like seonbaenim.",romanization:"geuraedo cheoeumen seonbaenim gateun hochingeul sseuneun pyeonieyo."},{speaker:"민서",ko:"어르신도 계시면 도와드릴까요? 하고 물어볼게요.",en:"If elders are there too, I will ask, 'May I help?'",romanization:"eoreusindo gyesimyeon dowadeurilkkayo? hago mureobolgeyo."},{speaker:"지훈",ko:"좋아요. 눈치 있게 챙기면 분위기가 덜 어색해요.",en:"Good. If you take care of things tactfully, the mood is less awkward.",romanization:"joayo. nunchi itge chaenggimyeon bunwigiga deol eosaekaeyo."}]},{kind:"order",prompt:"Build: 'Thanks to you, I had a good time.'",tokens:["덕분에","좋은 시간 보냈어요"],correct:"덕분에 좋은 시간 보냈어요",explanation:"덕분에 credits the other person as the positive reason. 좋은 시간 보냈어요 is a natural thanks-after-visit phrase."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"누가 한 말일까요? · Who said this?",sentence:"둘 다 괜찮아요. 집들이 선물로 많이 가져가잖아요.",options:[{text:"민서",ok:!1},{text:"지훈",ok:!0}],explanation:"Both are fine. People often bring them as housewarming gifts, you know."},{kind:"drill",prompt:'무엇이 들어갈까요? · "When going to a housewarming, which is better, toilet paper or detergent?"',sentence:"___에 갈 때 휴지랑 세제 중에 뭐가 나아요?",options:[{text:"선물",ok:!1},{text:"집들이",ok:!0},{text:"나이",ok:!1}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Remind someone of shared knowledge (선물하잖아요)",id:"chapter-56-b10",chapterId:"chapter-56",index:9},{kind:"reading",title:"읽기 Reading · 집들이에서 호칭과 눈치 챙기기",cards:[{kind:"read",title:"집들이에서 호칭과 눈치 챙기기",chunks:["민서는 회사 선배 집들이에 가려고 해요. 집들이 선물로 휴지와 세제 중에서 고민하다가, 실용적인 세제를 사기로 했어요. 처음 만나는 사람들이 있을 수 있으니까 나이와 호칭도 조심하려고 해요. 나이가 비슷해 보여도 바로 반말하지 않고, 처음에는 선배님 같은 호칭을 쓰는 편이에요.","어르신도 계시면 먼저 도와드릴까요? 하고 물어볼 거예요. 분위기가 어색하면 눈치 있게 선물을 건네고, 마지막에는 초대해 주셔서 감사합니다, 덕분에 편했어요라고 말하려고 해요."],translation:"Minseo is going to a senior coworker's housewarming. After thinking between toilet paper and detergent as a housewarming gift, Minseo decided to buy practical detergent. Since there may be people Minseo is meeting for the first time, Minseo plans to be careful with age and titles too. Even if someone seems close in age, Minseo will not switch to casual speech right away and tends to use a title like seonbaenim at first. If elders are there, Minseo will ask first, 'May I help?' If the atmosphere feels awkward, Minseo will give the gift tactfully and at the end say, 'Thank you for inviting me. I felt comfortable thanks to you.'",qas:[{q:"민서는 집들이 선물로 무엇을 사기로 했어요?",a:"세제를 사기로 했어요."},{q:"나이가 비슷해 보여도 처음에는 어떻게 해요?",a:"바로 반말하지 않고 선배님 같은 호칭을 쓰는 편이에요."},{q:"마지막에 어떤 감사 표현을 하려고 해요?",a:"초대해 주셔서 감사합니다, 덕분에 편했어요라고 말하려고 해요."}]},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"민서는 회사 선배 ___에 가려고 해요.",options:[{text:"동갑",ok:!1},{text:"후배",ok:!1},{text:"집들이",ok:!0}],explanation:""},{kind:"drill",prompt:"지문에서 뭐라고 했어요? · What did the passage say?",sentence:"처음 만나는 사람들이 있을 수 있으니까 ___와 호칭도 조심하려고 해요.",options:[{text:"동갑",ok:!1},{text:"후배",ok:!1},{text:"나이",ok:!0}],explanation:""}],canDo:"Explain background or a reason (좋은 뜻이 있거든요)",id:"chapter-56-b11",chapterId:"chapter-56",index:10},{kind:"boss",title:"보스 Boss bite · 다 걸어요",cards:[{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence reminds the listener of a familiar housewarming gift custom?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"집들이 선물로 휴지나 세제를 많이 가져가잖아요.",ok:!0},{text:"집들이 선물이 휴지나 세제를 많이 가져가잖아요.",ok:!1},{text:"집들이 선물로 휴지나 세제를 많이 가져가더라고요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"선물로 marks the role, and 잖아요 points to a familiar custom."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence is safest when you do not know the title?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"어떤 호칭을 쓰면 될까요?",ok:!0},{text:"너 이름 뭐야?",ok:!1},{text:"동갑이니까 바로 반말할게요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"Asking about 호칭 is safer than guessing too casually."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"Which sentence thanks someone with a positive cause?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"선배님 덕분에 분위기가 편해졌어요.",ok:!0},{text:"선배님 때문에 분위기가 편해졌어요.",ok:!1},{text:"선배님한테 분위기가 편해졌어요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"덕분에 is the warm positive-cause expression for gratitude."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"어르신___ 도와드렸어요. (I helped the elder.)",sentence:null,options:[{text:"을",ok:!0},{text:"이",ok:!1},{text:"에",ok:!1}],explanation:"도와드리다 still takes the person helped as an object. 드리다 adds respect toward the person receiving help."},{kind:"drill",prompt:"You do not know the safest title. Which strategy fits?",sentence:null,options:[{text:"정중하게 여쭤봐요.",ok:!0},{text:"무조건 반말을 써요.",ok:!1},{text:"이름만 불러요.",ok:!1}],explanation:"When title is unclear, asking politely is safer than guessing casually. Korean social titles matter in first meetings."}],canDo:"Soften a statement or careful question (조심하는 게 좋은데요)",id:"chapter-56-b12",chapterId:"chapter-56",index:11}]}],o={generatedFrom:e,chapters:t};export{t as chapters,o as default,e as generatedFrom};
